hadiths[1148]{hadithnumber,text}:
"12632","(12627) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Iyās informed me, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""My sister vowed to perform Hajj, but she died."" He (ﷺ) said: ""If she had a debt, would you have paid it?"" He said: ""Yes."" He (ﷺ) said: ""Then discharge the right of Allah, for He is more deserving of fulfilment.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, from Shuʿbah. [Authentic. Bukhārī 1852]"
"12633","(12628) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Ṣafwān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, Shuʿayb ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī, from Abū al-Ghawth ibn al-Ḥuṣayn al-Khathʿamī, who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, my father has been overtaken by the obligation of Allah regarding Hajj, and he is an old man who cannot maintain himself on a mount. What do you think about performing Hajj on his behalf?' He (ﷺ) said: 'Yes, perform Hajj on his behalf.' He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, likewise, if any of our family members die without having bequeathed Hajj, shall we perform Hajj on their behalf?' He (ﷺ) said: 'Yes, and you will be rewarded.' He said: 'And shall charity be given on their behalf and fasting be observed on their behalf?' He (ﷺ) said: 'Yes, and charity is more excellent.' And likewise in the case of vows and walking to the mosques.""

This is a mursal hadith between ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī and those above him, and its meaning is found in the established hadith preceding it.

[Weak. Previously referenced under number 8673]"
"12634","(12629) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed me, Ibn Wahb informed me, Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥumayyī, Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sālim, Mālik ibn Anas, and al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying: ""My mother has died suddenly, and I think that if she had spoken, she would have given charity. Is there any reward for her if I give charity on her behalf?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Yes."" Except that Mālik said in the hadith: ""I think that if she had spoken, she would have given charity. Shall I give charity on her behalf?"" He said: ""Yes.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Uways, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from other routes from Hishām. [Authentic. Bukhārī 1388]"
"12635","(12630) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Muḥammad ibn Muʾammal ibn Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us, al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr informed me, Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, that a man said to the Prophet (ﷺ): ""My mother has died suddenly, and I think that if she had spoken, she would have given charity. Is there any reward for her if I give charity on her behalf?"" He said: ""Yes."" The wording is that of the hadith of Abū ʿAbd Allāh alone.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn.

[Authentic]"
"12636","(12631) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Munādī narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, Yaʿlā informed me that he heard ʿIkrimah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, saying: Ibn ʿAbbās informed us that Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah's mother passed away while he was absent from her. He came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my mother has passed away and I was absent from her. Will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf?"" He (ﷺ) said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then I bear witness to you that my garden, al-Mikhraf, is charity on her behalf.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm, from Rūḥ. [Authentic. Bukhārī 2856]"
"12637","(12632) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Mālik ibn Anas informed us, from Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr ibn Sharḥīl, from his father, from his grandfather Saʿīd ibn Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, that Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah was with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on one of his military expeditions when the death of Saʿd's mother arrived. She was told: ""Make a bequest."" She said: ""In what shall I make a bequest? The wealth is Saʿd's wealth."" She died before Saʿd arrived. When Saʿd arrived and was informed of what had happened concerning his mother, he came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and informed him of what had happened concerning his mother, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Yes."" Saʿd said: ""Such-and-such a garden""—he named it—""is charity on her behalf."" [Authentic. Muwaṭṭāʾ 2211]"
"12638","(12633) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah al-Anṣārī sought a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding a vow that had been upon his mother, who had died before fulfilling it. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Fulfil it on her behalf.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Qutaybah. [Authentic. Previously referenced 12634]"
"12639","(12634) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Qutaybah, and ʿAlī ibn Ṭayfūr al-Nasawī narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me, Abū Yaʿlā informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that a man said to the Prophet (ﷺ): ""My father has died and left wealth, but he did not make a bequest. Will it expiate for him if I give charity on his behalf?"" He (ﷺ) said: ""Yes.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, Qutaybah, and ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr. [Muslim 1630]"
"12640","(12635) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl informed us, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When a person dies, his deeds are cut off except for three things: ongoing charity (ṣadaqah jāriyah), knowledge from which benefit is derived, or a righteous child who supplicates for him.""

[Good. Muslim 1631]"
"12641","(12636) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed me: Muhammad ibn Nahr narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Hu narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us — (h) and Abu Bakr: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Tusi al-Faqih informed us: Abu Bishr: Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Hadiri informed us: Abu al-'Abbas al-Sarraj narrated to us: Abu Hammam al-Sakuni and al-Husayn ibn al-Dahhak both said: Isma'il narrated to us from al-'Ala', and he mentioned it with its like. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn Hu and others. [Hasan]"
"12642","(12637) Abu 'Abd Allah: Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi informed us: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asam informed us: al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazyad informed us: my father informed me: al-Awza'i narrated to me: Hassan ibn 'Atiyyah narrated to me from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father from his grandfather: that al-'As ibn Wa'il al-Sahmi bequeathed that one hundred slaves be emancipated on his behalf. His son Hisham emancipated fifty slaves, and his son 'Amr wished to emancipate the remaining fifty on his behalf. He said: ""Until I ask the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him)."" He came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my father bequeathed that one hundred slaves be emancipated on his behalf, and Hisham has emancipated fifty, and fifty remain. Shall I emancipate them on his behalf?"" The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""If he had been a Muslim, and you had emancipated on his behalf, or given charity on his behalf, or performed Hajj on his behalf, it would have reached him."" [Hasan. Ahmad 2/181]"
"12643","(12638) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajan informed us: Abu Bakr: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Bushanj narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated to us from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Amrah al-Ansari from his mother: she intended to make a bequest but delayed it until morning, and she passed away. She had been resolved upon emancipation. 'Abd al-Rahman said: I said to al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: ""Would it benefit her if I emancipate on her behalf?"" Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad said: ""Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah said to the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him): 'My mother has passed away — would it benefit her if I emancipate on her behalf?' The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Yes, emancipate on her behalf.'"" [Mursal. Da'if]"
"12644","(12639) Hisham narrated it from al-Hasan from the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal narration with part of its meaning. Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us: Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah, meaning Ibn al-Munadi, narrated to us: Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us from al-Hasan: that Sa'd came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the mother of Sa'd used to love charity and love emancipation. Will she have reward if I give charity on her behalf or emancipate?"" He said: ""Yes."" [Da'if]"
"12645","(12640) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us in al-Mustadrak, saying: Abu 'Abd Allah: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Hilali narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid al-'Adani narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from 'Ata' from 'A'ishah, who said: A man said: ""Shall I emancipate on behalf of my father, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Yes."" Thus he informed with it, and it is an error. [Da'if]"
"12646","(12641) 'Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Hilali narrated it in the Jami' of al-Thawri from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid from al-Thawri from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah: that a man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall I emancipate on behalf of my father, who has died?"" He said: ""Yes."" Abu Bakr: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi informed us: Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, mentioning it as mursal. [Da'if]"
"12647","(12642) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us: Abu al-Hasan al-Karizi informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us: Abu 'Ubayd narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us from Yahya ibn Sa'id from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from 'A'ishah: that her brother died in his sleep, and 'A'ishah emancipated on his behalf his old slaves (tilad), meaning his long-held mamluks. And al-tilad is every property of long standing. [Sahih. 'Abd al-Razzaq 6345]"
"12648","(12643) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us: Abu al-Hasan al-Raffa' informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us: Khalaf ibn Musa ibn Khalaf al-'Ammi narrated to us: my father narrated to us from Qatadah from Musa ibn Salamah al-Hudhali, who said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas, saying: ""A man emancipates a slave on behalf of his parents — will there be reward for them in that?"" He said: ""Yes."" [Da'if]"
"12649","(12644) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Ahmad ibn Salih informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far from Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr from 'Urwah from 'A'ishah: that the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever dies while fasting is obligatory upon him, his guardian should fast on his behalf."" Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in their Sahihs from the hadith of Ibn Wahb, and it has already been mentioned in the Book of Fasting the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas and Buraydah ibn Husayb from the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) regarding fasting on behalf of the deceased. [Sahih. Bukhari 1952. Muslim 1147]"
"12650","(12645) Abu Muhammad: 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Ashbahani informed us orally: Abu Muhammad: 'Abd Allah ibn Ishaq al-Fakihi informed us: Abu Yahya ibn Abi Masarrat informed us: Badl ibn al-Muhabbar informed us: Shu'bah informed us: he said: I was informed by Sulayman al-A'mash, who said: I heard Muslim al-Batin narrating from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas: that a woman vowed to fast for a month but died. Her brother came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: ""Fast on her behalf."" [Sahih. al-Tayalisi 2752]"
"12651","(12646) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, ʿAbbās ibn Tamīm al-Sukkariyy informed us, Hudbah informed us, and they both said: Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us, Thābit informed us, from Anas, who said: When the verse {You will never attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love} was revealed, Abū Ṭalḥah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I see that our Lord is asking us for our wealth. I bear witness to you that I have made my land of Arīḥā for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Distribute it among your relatives."" So he divided it between Ḥassān ibn Thābit and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb.

Abū Dāwūd said: And it has reached me from al-Anṣārī, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: Abū Ṭalḥah is Zayd ibn Sahl ibn al-Aswad ibn Ḥarām ibn ʿAmr ibn Zayd Manāṭ ibn ʿAdī ibn ʿAmr ibn Mālik ibn al-Najjār, and Ḥassān ibn Thābit ibn al-Mundhir ibn Ḥarām — they both converge at Ḥarām, who is the third ancestor. And Ubayy ibn Kaʿb ibn Qays ibn ʿUbayd ibn Zayr ibn Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr ibn Mālik ibn al-Najjār — so ʿAmr gathers Ḥassān, Abū Ṭalḥah, and Ubayy together. Al-Anṣārī said: Between Ubayy and Abū Ṭalḥah there are six fathers.

The ḥadīth of Ḥammād from Thābit was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ, and al-Bukhārī mentioned it in the tarjamah (heading), then he said: And al-Anṣārī said: My father narrated to me, from Thumāmah, from Anas, with a narration similar to that of Thābit, in which he said: ""Distribute it among your poor relatives."" So he distributed it between Ḥassān and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 3/285]"
"12652","(12647) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdūs ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Simṣār informed us, Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Ḥanẓalī al-Rāzī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī informed us, my father informed me, from his uncle Thumāmah, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: When this verse was revealed {You will never attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love} and {Who is it that would loan Allah a goodly loan?}, Abū Ṭalḥah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my garden at such-and-such a place is for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. Had I been able to conceal it, I would not have announced it."" He said: ""Distribute it among your poor family members."" He said: So he distributed it between Ḥassān ibn Thābit and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12653","(12648) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us, Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Jakkānī informed us, and they both said: Abū al-Yamān informed us, Shuʿayb informed us, from al-Zuhrī, who said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab and Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me that Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up when Allah revealed upon him {And warn your nearest kinsfolk}, and he said: ""O assembly of Quraysh, secure yourselves from Allah; I will avail you nothing against Allah. O Banū ʿAbd Manāf, I will avail you nothing against Allah. O ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, I will avail you nothing against Allah. O Ṣafiyyah, aunt of the Messenger of Allah, I will avail you nothing against Allah. O Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, ask me whatever you wish; I will avail you nothing against Allah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from al-Zuhrī.

Thus it is established by this and what preceded it that the children of paternal uncles are included among the ""nearest kinsfolk."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2753]"
"12654","(12649) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Mihrajānī, Ibn Abī ʿAlī al-Saqqāʾ, informed us, Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad — he is Ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān — informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ informed us, from Hishām, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: When {And warn your nearest kinsfolk} was revealed, the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""O Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, O Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, I possess nothing for you from Allah. Ask me from my wealth whatever you wish.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr, from Wakīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 205]"
"12655","(12650) ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, and they both said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us, Sufyān informed us, from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, that Ṣafiyyah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), said to a Jewish brother of hers: ""Embrace Islam so that you may inherit from me."" His people heard of this and said: ""You would sell your religion for worldly gain."" So he refused to embrace Islam, and she bequeathed one-third to him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12656","(12651) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, and they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Ibn Lahīʿah informed me, from Bukayr ibn ʿAbdullāh, that Umm ʿAlqamah, the freed slave of ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), narrated to him that Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab, may Allah be pleased with her, bequeathed to a Jewish brother of hers, and she bequeathed to ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, one thousand dīnārs, and she appointed a son of ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar as her executor. When her brother's son heard of this, he embraced Islam in order to inherit from her, but he did not inherit from her. He sought what she had bequeathed to him and found that the son of ʿAbdullāh had corrupted it. So ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""Woe to him! Give him the one thousand dīnārs that his aunt bequeathed to me for him.""

And it has been narrated from Ibn ʿUmar that Ṣafiyyah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), may Allah be pleased with her, bequeathed to a Jewish relative of hers. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12657","(12652) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī, Abū Zakariyyā al-Muzakkī, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, and Abū Saʿīd Masʿūd ibn Muḥammad al-Jurjānī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us, Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj al-Ḥijāzī informed us, Baqiyyah informed us, Mubashshir ibn ʿUbayd informed us, from Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh, from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""A killer has no will (waṣiyyah).""

And likewise Muḥammad ibn Muṣaffā narrated it from Baqiyyah. Mubashshir ibn ʿUbayd al-Ḥimṣī is alone in narrating it, and he is accused of fabricating aḥādīth. I have mentioned this ḥadīth only so that its narrators may be known. And success is through Allah alone. [Mawḍūʿ]"
"12658","(12653) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us, Ibn Ḥammād informed me, ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me: I heard my father say: ""There is a shaykh called Mubashshir ibn ʿUbayd who used to be in Ḥimṣ. I think he was a Kūfan. Baqiyyah and Abū al-Mīrāth narrated from him. His aḥādīth are fabricated lies."" He said: And Ibn Ḥammād informed us, and said: Al-Bukhārī said: ""Mubashshir ibn ʿUbayd is munkar al-ḥadīth (rejected in ḥadīth)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12659","(12654) Abū Bakr al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us, Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr informed us, Abū Isḥāq informed us, from Ibn ʿAwn, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""Let a man write in his will: 'If any event of death befalls me before I change this will of mine.'""

And it has been narrated from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: ""A man may change his will as he wishes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12660","(12655) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū Bakr informed us, ʿAbd al-Aʿlā informed us, from Hishām, from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""When a man makes a will, he may change his will as he wishes."" It was said to him: ""What about manumission (al-ʿitāqah)?"" He said: ""Manumission and other than manumission."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12661","(12656) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Abū ʿAwānah narrated from Ḥuṣayn, from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, who said: I saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, days before he was struck in Madīnah. He mentioned the hadith regarding his stabbing. He said: He was carried to his house, and we went with him. He said: One person said, ""There is no harm,"" while another said, ""We fear for him."" Nabīdh was brought to him, and he drank it, and it came out from his wound. Then milk was brought to him, and he drank it, and it came out from his wound. So they knew that he was dying. Then he mentioned the hadith regarding his will and the matter of the Shūrā.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3700]"
"12662","(12657) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, from his father, that ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqī informed him that it was said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him: ""Here there is a young lad who has not yet reached puberty, from Ghassān, and his heir is in Shām. He has wealth, and he has no one here except a paternal cousin (bint ʿamm)."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Then let him bequeath for her."" So he bequeathed for her a property known as Biʾr Jusham. ʿAmr ibn Sulaym said: ""I sold that property for thirty thousand, and the paternal cousin for whom he bequeathed was Umm ʿAmr ibn Sulaym.""

It is narrated from Sharīḥ and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah that they both permitted the bequest of a minor and said: ""Whoever arrives at what is right, we permit it."" Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, conditioned the permissibility of his bequest and his tadbīr upon the establishment of the report regarding it from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him. The report is munqaṭiʿ, for ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqī did not meet ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him. However, he mentioned in the report his connection to the woman of the story. And Allah knows best.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik 1454]"
"12663","(12658) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Shabīb ibn Gharqadah, from Jundub, who said: Ṭahmān asked Ibn ʿAbbās: ""Can a slave make a bequest?"" He said: ""No."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq 16465]"
"12664","(12659) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me; ʿAbd al-Ghaffār ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Mawṣilī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Mushhir narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, who said: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad, and Muṭīʿ ibn al-Aswad, may Allah be pleased with them, appointed al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, as executor. He said to Muṭīʿ: ""I do not accept your executorship."" Muṭīʿ said to him: ""I adjure you by Allah and by kinship—I will not follow in that except the opinion of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him. I heard ʿUmar say: 'If I were to leave an estate or entrust a charge to anyone, I would entrust it to al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, for he is a pillar from among the pillars of the religion.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12665","(12660) Abū al-Ḥusayn informed us; ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us; Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated from Abū ʿUmays, from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd made a bequest, and he wrote: ""My bequest is to Allah, and to al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, and to his son ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr. They are free and unencumbered in whatever they oversee and decide regarding my estate. And none of my daughters shall be married except with their permission—Zaynab shall not be prevented from that."" His statement ""shall not be prevented"" means she shall not be barred from it and nothing shall be placed between her and it. This was stated by Abū ʿUbayd al-Qāsim. [Ḥasan. al-Arwāʾ 1663]"
"12666","(12661) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī and Ibrāhīm ibn Munqidh al-Khawlānī in Miṣr both said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar al-Qurashī, from Sālim ibn Abī Sālim al-Jayshānī, from his father, from Abū Dharr, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""O Abū Dharr, I see you as weak, and I love for you what I love for myself. Do not take command over two people, and do not assume guardianship over the property of an orphan."" This is the wording of al-Dūrī's hadith.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1826]"
"12667","(12662) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed me; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Ḥajabī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated. He said: I heard my father narrate from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""I and the guardian of an orphan will be in Paradise like these two."" And he indicated with his index finger and the one next to it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5304]"
"12668","(12663) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""I and the guardian of an orphan—whether for himself or for another—provided he fears Allah, will be in Paradise like these two."" And the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, indicated with his middle finger and the one next to the thumb. [Munkar. Mālik]"
"12669","(12664) Through this chain of narration from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym, raising it (marfūʿan): The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The one who strives for the widow and the needy is like the mujāhid in the cause of Allah, or like the one who fasts by day and stands in prayer by night.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ in this form as a mursal report from Ibn Abī Uways, from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5353]"
"12670","(12665) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to me; Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym narrated from a woman called Unaysah, from Umm Saʿīd bint Murrah al-Fihrī, from her father, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""I and the guardian of an orphan—whether for himself or for another—will be in Paradise like these two."" And Sufyān indicated with his two fingers. Al-Ḥumaydī said: It was said to Sufyān, ""ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī says that you are more correct in this hadith than Mālik."" Sufyān said: ""How would he know? Did he meet Ṣafwān?"" They said: ""No, but he said that Mālik reported it from Ṣafwān, from ʬāʾ ibn Yāsar, while you reported it from Unaysah, from Umm Saʿīd bint Murrah, from her father. Where did you obtain this chain of narration?"" Sufyān said: ""How excellent is what he said! Had Ṣafwān told us ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yāsar, it would have been easier for us than bringing this strong chain of narration."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12671","(12666) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik, from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dīlī, from Abū al-Ghayth, the freedman of Ibn Muṭīʿ, from Abū Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The one who strives on behalf of the widow and the poor is like the mujāhid in the cause of Allah."" Al-Qaʿnabī said: ""And I think he said: 'Like the one who stands in prayer without slackening and like the one who fasts without breaking his fast.'"" Narrated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12672","(12667) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Uwaysī narrated and said: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to me, from Thawr ibn Zayd, from Abū al-Ghayth, from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Avoid the seven destructive sins."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what are they?"" He said: ""Associating partners with Allah, magic, killing a soul which Allah has forbidden except with right, consuming ribā, consuming the wealth of the orphan, fleeing on the day of the march, and slandering chaste, believing women."" Narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Uwaysī, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2767]"
"12673","(12668) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā al-Ḥīrī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Salamah narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated from Hishām, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {And whoever is rich, let him abstain, and whoever is poor, let him consume with kindness} — she said: It was revealed concerning the guardian of an orphan, that if he is poor, he may consume from it in lieu of his maintenance of it, with kindness.

Narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Kurayb, both from Ibn Numayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2212]"
"12674","(12669) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ḥumayd ibn Masʿadah narrated that Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to them, saying: Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""I am poor and I have nothing, and I have an orphan (under my care)."" He said: ""Then consume from the wealth of your orphan, without being extravagant, without being hasty, and without hoarding."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 2/186]"
"12675","(12670) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad who said: A man came to Ibn ʿAbbās and said: ""O Ibn ʿAbbās, I have camels and I lend them out and I am poor, and I have an orphan under my care who also has camels. What is permissible for me from the camels of my orphan?"" He said: ""If you seek his lost camel, treat the mange of his camels, maintain his watering troughs, and tend to them, then drink from them without harming the offspring and without exhausting the milk.""

And we have narrated in the Book of Sales from ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās regarding the repayment of what was consumed from it when one becomes affluent, and this is the view of ʿAbīdah, Mujāhid, and Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, and from Abū al-ʿĀliyah. And we have narrated from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī and ʬāʾiẓ ibn Abī Rabāḥ that he does not repay it. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12676","(12671) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Zakariyyā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Jarīr narrated from ʬāʾib ibn al-Sāʾib, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: When Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And do not approach the wealth of the orphan except in the best manner} and {Indeed, those who consume the wealth of orphans unjustly are only consuming fire into their bellies, and they will be burned in a Blaze} — everyone who had an orphan separated his food and drink from his own food and drink. Whatever surplus remained of the orphan's food and drink would be kept until the orphan ate it or it spoiled. This became difficult for them, so they mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {They ask you about the orphans. Say: 'Setting their affairs right is best. And if you mix with them, they are your brothers.'} So they mixed their food and drink with their own food and drink. [Ḍaʿīf. Ḥākim 3184]"
"12677","(12672) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me, ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us, Yazīd ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth narrated to us, al-Ashjaʿī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Najīḥ, from al-Zubayr ibn Mūsā, from al-Ḥasan al-ʿUranī who said: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: ""I have an orphan under my care. May I strike him?"" He said: ""As you would strike your own child."" He said: ""May I eat (from his wealth)?"" He said: ""With kindness, without hoarding wealth, and without using his wealth to protect your own.""

This is a mursal narration. It has also been narrated through another route as a mawṣūl narration, but it is weak. Its mention has already passed in the Book of Sales. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12678","(12673) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ḥarb ibn Maymūn narrated to us, ʿAwf narrated from Abū Rajāʾ who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""May Allah have mercy on a man who disciplines an orphan with a slap."" [Ḥasan]"
"12679","(12674) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated and said: I found in my book from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh, my father narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Shumaysah who said: I asked ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) about disciplining an orphan. She said: ""I would strike one of them until he becomes cheerful."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhādī in al-Adab 142]"
"12680","(12675) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Manṣūr, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) had the wealth of an orphan with him, and he used to pay zakāh on it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12681","(12676) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān informed us from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, and ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn Abī al-Mukhāriq — all of them reporting from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, who said: ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) used to pay the zakāh on our funds, and they were being traded with in al-Baḥrayn. [Sahih. See 10986]"
"12682","(12677) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: Orphans' funds would be in his possession, and he would lend them out so as to preserve them from destruction, and he would pay their zakāh from their funds. [Sahih]"
"12683","(12678) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh informed us, my father informed us, Shuʿbah informed us from Abū Isḥāq, who said: I heard Ṣilah say: I was present with ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — when a man from Hamdān came to him on a piebald horse and said: A man has bequeathed [a horse] to me and left an orphan. Shall I purchase this horse or another horse from his wealth? ʿAbdullāh said: Do not purchase anything from his wealth. And in the book: Do not purchase anything from his wealth, and do not borrow anything from his wealth. [Sahih]"
"12684","(12679) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us by dictation, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-Muthannā informed us, Musaddad informed us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal informed us, Abū Muslimah informed us, Abū Naḍrah informed us, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: When the battle of Uḥud drew near, my father called me at night and said: I do not think I will be killed except among the first of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to be killed. And by Allah, I do not leave anyone after me dearer to me than you, after the soul of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). And I have a debt, so pay my debt on my behalf, and treat your sisters well. He said: We awoke in the morning, and he was the first to be killed. I buried him with another man in a single grave, but I was not comfortable leaving him with another man in a grave, so I exhumed him after six months. He was as he was on the day I had placed him, except for his ear. [Sahih. Al-Ḥākim 4913]"
"12685","(12680) Abū Saʿīd Khalīl ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Bustī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn al-Muẓaffar al-Bakrī informed us, Ibn Abī Khaythamah informed us, Khālid ibn Khidāsh informed us, Ghassān ibn Muḍar informed us, from Saʿīd ibn Yazīd, from Abū Naḍrah, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, who narrated it with the same meaning and said at the end of it: As on the day I buried him, except for a small portion near his head. [Sahih]"
"12686","(12681) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us, Abū ʿAwanah informed us, from Ḥuṣayn, from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, who mentioned the account of the killing of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), and in it, from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: O ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, see what debt I owe. They calculated it and found it to be eighty thousand or thereabouts. He said: If the wealth of the family of ʿUmar suffices for it, then pay it from their wealth. Otherwise, ask Banū ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb. If their wealth does not suffice, then ask Quraysh, and do not go beyond them to anyone else. Pay this wealth on my behalf. [Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā. Sahih. Bukhārī 3700]"
"12687","(12682) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā al-Ḥīrī informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Sawwār informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Salām al-Ṭarsūsī informed us, Abū Usāmah Ḥammād ibn Usāmah informed us, Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us, from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, who said: When al-Zubayr stood on the Day of the Camel, he called me, and I stood beside him. He said: O my son, today no one will be killed except an oppressor or one who is oppressed, and I believe I will be killed today as one who is oppressed. And the greatest of my concerns is my debt. Do you think our debt will leave anything of our wealth, O my son? Sell our wealth and pay my debt. And bequeath one-third, and one-third of that one-third, to the children of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr. If anything remains of our wealth after the debt is paid, then divide it into thirds for your children. Hishām said: Some of the children of ʿAbdullāh were of the same age as some of the children of al-Zubayr — Khubayb and ʿAbbād. He said: He had seven daughters at that time. ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr said: He began instructing me regarding his debt and saying: O my son, if you are unable to pay any of it, then seek help from my Mawlā. He said: By Allah, I did not understand what he meant until I said: O father, who is your Mawlā? He said: Allah. He said: By Allah, whenever I faced any difficulty regarding his debt, I would say: O Mawlā of al-Zubayr, pay it on his behalf, and He would discharge it. He said: Al-Zubayr was killed and did not leave a single dīnār or dirham except lands — among them the land of Ghābah, eleven houses in Madīnah, two houses in Baṣrah, a house in Kūfah, and a house in Miṣr. He said: His debt that he owed was only because a man would come to him with wealth and entrust it to him, and al-Zubayr would say: No, but it is a loan (salaf), for I fear its loss. He never held any governorship, nor any tax collection, nor any kharāj, nor anything at all, except that he participated in a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), or with Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, or ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with them). ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr said: I calculated what he owed in debt and found it to be two million and two hundred thousand. He said: Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām met ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr and said: O my nephew, how much debt does my brother owe? He said: He concealed it and said: A hundred thousand. Ḥakīm said: I do not think your wealth will cover that. ʿAbdullāh said to him: What if it is two million and two hundred thousand? He said: I do not think you can manage that. If you are unable to pay any of it, then seek my help. He said: Al-Zubayr had purchased Ghābah for one hundred and seventy thousand, and ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr sold it for one million six hundred thousand. Then he stood and said: Whoever has a debt owed by al-Zubayr, let him come to us at Ghābah. ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar came, and he had four hundred thousand owed by al-Zubayr. He said to ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr: If you wish, we will leave it for you. ʿAbdullāh said: No. He said: If you wish, you may include it in what you defer, if you defer anything. ʿAbdullāh said: No. He said: Then set aside a portion for me. ʿAbdullāh said: For you is from here to here. He said: He sold it to him from that and paid his debt in full. Four and a half shares remained. He said: He came to Muʿāwiyah, and with him were ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān, al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr, and Ibn Zamʿah. Muʿāwiyah said to him: How much was Ghābah valued at? He said: Six hundred thousand, or he said: Each share was a hundred thousand. He said: How much remains? He said: Four and a half shares. Al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr said: I will take a share for a hundred thousand. ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān said: I will take a share for a hundred thousand. Ibn Zamʿah said: I will take a share for a hundred thousand. Muʿāwiyah said: How much remains? He said: One and a half shares. He said: I will take it for a hundred and fifty thousand. ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar sold his share to Muʿāwiyah for six hundred thousand. When Ibn al-Zubayr finished paying his debt, the children of al-Zubayr said: Divide our inheritance among us. He said: No, by Allah, I will not divide it among you until I announce at the pilgrimage season for four years: Whoever has a debt owed by al-Zubayr, let me know so that we may pay it. He said: Every year he would announce at the pilgrimage season. When four years had passed, he divided their inheritance among them. He said: Al-Zubayr had four wives. He raised the one-third, and each woman received one million two hundred thousand. The total of his wealth was fifty million two hundred thousand. [Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Abū Usāmah. Sahih. Bukhārī 3129]"
"12688","(12683) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid informed us, Muḥammad ibn Zunbūr informed us, Fuḍayl ibn ʿIyāḍ informed us, from Hishām, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: They used to write at the beginning of their testaments: ""This is the testament of so-and-so, son of so-and-so. He testifies that there is no deity except Allah alone, with no partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, and that the Hour is coming, there is no doubt about it, and that Allah will raise those in the graves. And he enjoins those of his family who remain after him to fear Allah as He deserves to be feared, to set right their mutual affairs, and to obey Allah and His Messenger, if they are believers. And he enjoins upon them what Ibrāhīm enjoined upon his sons and Yaʿqūb: 'O my sons, indeed Allah has chosen for you the religion, so do not die except while you are Muslims.'"" [Sahih. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 16319]"
"12689","(12684) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Ibn ʿAwn informed us, saying: The testament of Ibn Sīrīn was: Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAmrah enjoined his children and the children of his family to fear Allah, to set right their mutual affairs, and to obey Allah and His Messenger, if they are believers. And he enjoined upon them what Ibrāhīm enjoined upon his sons and Yaʿqūb: ""O my sons, indeed Allah has chosen for you the religion, so do not die except while you are Muslims."" And he enjoined upon them that they not cease to be brothers of the Anṣār and their freedmen, for indeed chastity and truthfulness are more pious and more honorable than fornication and lying. And he enjoined regarding what he left behind, should any event befall him before he changes his testament. [Hasan]"
"12690","(12685) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, Abū Ḥayyān Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us, from his father, who said: Al-Rabīʿ ibn Khuthaym wrote his testament: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is what al-Rabīʿ ibn Khuthaym bequeaths. I call Allah as witness over it, and sufficient is Allah as a witness, as a recompenser for His righteous servants, and as a granter of reward. I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) as Prophet. I command myself and whoever obeys me that we worship Allah among the worshippers, praise Him among those who praise, and act for the good of the Muslim community."" [Weak. Al-Dārimī 3187]"
"12691","(12686) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Muzanī informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Abū al-Yamān informed us: Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""All of you are shepherds and each of you is responsible for his flock. The Imam is a shepherd and he is responsible for his flock. A man is a shepherd over his family and he is responsible for his flock. A woman is a shepherdess in her husband's house and she is responsible for her flock. A servant is a shepherd over the wealth of his master and he is responsible for his flock."" He (ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar) said: ""I heard these words from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and I think the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) also said: 'A man is a shepherd over the wealth of his son and he is responsible for his flock.' And all of you are shepherds and each of you is responsible for his flock."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2409, Muslim 1829]"
"12692","(12687) The Shaykh al-Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Ḥanafī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Maṭar narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr al-Ḥaddāʾ narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād al-Narsī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There are three traits: whoever possesses them is a hypocrite, even if he fasts, prays, and claims that he is a Muslim: when he speaks he lies, when he is entrusted he betrays, and when he makes a promise he breaks it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"
"12693","(12688) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bishr ibn Maṭar narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād and Abū Naṣr al-Tammār both said: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Naṣr al-Tammār and ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12694","(12689) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Nāfiʿ ibn Mālik ibn Abī ʿĀmir, Abū Suhayl, narrated from his father from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The sign of the hypocrite is three: when he speaks he lies, when he promises he breaks it, and when he is entrusted he betrays.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah and Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb from Ismāʿīl."
"12695","(12690) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd al-Nasawī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Abū Hilāl narrated from Qatādah from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""It was seldom that our Prophet (ﷺ) — or the Prophet (ﷺ) — addressed us without saying in his sermon: 'There is no faith for the one who has no trustworthiness, and there is no religion for the one who does not keep his pledge.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12696","(12691) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us by dictation: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Firās al-Mālikī narrated to us in Makkah: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Abū ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr from al-Muṭṭalib ibn Ḥanṭab from ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Guarantee me six things from yourselves and I will guarantee you Paradise: be truthful when you speak, fulfill your promises when you make them, render back what is entrusted to you when you are entrusted, guard your private parts, lower your gaze, and restrain your hands."" [Ḥasan li-ghayrih]"
"12697","(12692) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us: al-Aswad ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Sāʾib from Zādhān from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: ""Being killed in the path of Allah expiates every sin except trustworthiness (amānah). The one who was entrusted will be brought, even if he was killed in the path of Allah, and it will be said to him: 'Render back your trust.' He will say: 'O my Lord, the world has passed away — from where shall I render it?' It will be said: 'Take him to the Abyss.' When he is brought to the bottom of the Abyss, his trust will be presented to him as it was on the day it was entrusted to him. He will carry it on his neck and ascend with it into the Fire. When he sees that he has escaped from it, it will fall, and he will fall after it, forever and ever."" Then ʿAbd Allāh recited: {Indeed, Allah commands you to render trusts to their rightful owners.} [Ḥasan]"
"12698","(12693) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Dulāf from his father that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Do not look at anyone's prayer or fasting; rather look at the one who, when he speaks, speaks the truth, when he is entrusted, renders back the trust, and when he is cautious, is God-fearing."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12699","(12694) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Let not the prayer of a man nor his fasting deceive you — whoever wishes may fast and whoever wishes may pray. But there is no religion for the one who has no trustworthiness."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12700","(12695) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū Aḥmad Ḥamzah ibn al-ʿAbbās al-ʿAqabī narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Isḥāq al-Marwazī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Khālid ibn Yazīd from Ibn Abī Hilāl from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar from ʿUbayd ibn Abī Kilāb, who said: He heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was addressing the people, saying: ""Let not the loquaciousness of a man impress you. Rather, the one who renders back the trust and refrains from violating the honour of people — he is the man."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12701","(12696) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed me; Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Shuʿbah reported from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ from Shaddād ibn Maʿqil from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: ""The first thing you will lose of your religion is trustworthiness (al-amānah), and the last thing you will lose is the prayer (al-ṣalāh). And people will pray while having no religion.""

And in what Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bakkāʾī narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār, he said: ""One whom I do not suspect narrated to me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr from ʿĀʾishah regarding the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ, she said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) to remain behind in Makkah until he returned, on behalf of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the deposits (al-wadāʾiʿ) that were in his custody for the people.'"" [Ḥasan]"
"12702","(12697) And from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: ""Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr informed me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUwaym ibn Sāʿidah, who said: 'Men from my people, among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated to me,' and he mentioned the hadith regarding the departure of the Prophet ﷺ. He said in it: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed, and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) remained for three nights and their days, returning on behalf of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the deposits that were in his custody for the people. When he was finished with that, he caught up with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'""

And this is among what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from him, that Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed them; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Makhlad narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Zurārah narrated to us; Ziyād narrated, and he mentioned both of them. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12703","(12698) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Abū Shihāb reported from Ḥajjaj ibn Arṭāh from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir: ""That Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) ruled regarding a deposit that was in a bag and was lost through a tear in the bag: that there is no liability (ḍamān) in it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12704","(12699) Abū Bakr al-Ardistānī informed us; Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us; Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Sufyān reported from Jābir from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān: ""That ʿAlī and Ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: 'There is no liability upon one who is entrusted (a muʾtaman).'""

And it has been narrated from Sharīḥ: ""There is no liability upon the depositary (al-mustawdaʿ) other than one who commits treachery (al-mughill).""

And a musnad hadith has been narrated in that regard with a weak chain. [Very weak]"
"12705","(12700) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed him; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shabīb narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Yazīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik reported from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥajabī from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather: ""That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'There is no liability upon one who is entrusted (a muʾtaman).'""

And Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Whoever is entrusted with a deposit (wadīʿah), there is no liability upon him."" [Weak. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī, 3/41]"
"12706","(12701) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ in al-Kūfah informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Ḥammād reported from Asbāṭ from Simāk from Ḥanash: ""That two men deposited one hundred dinārs with a woman from Quraysh on the condition that she would not give it to either of them without the other being present, until they both came together. Then one of them came to her and said: 'My companion has died, so give me the money.' She refused. They disputed with her for three years and he interceded with her until she gave it to him. Then the other one came and said: 'Give me what is mine.' He went with her to ʿUmar, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: 'Do you have proof?' He said: 'She is my proof.' He said: 'I think you are liable (ḍāminah).' She said: 'I ask you, O Abū Fulān, to take us to Ibn Abī Ṭālib.' They came to him while he was plastering a basin for him in a garden, wearing a wrapper as a lower garment. They recounted the story to him, and he said: 'Bring me your companion, and your belongings are my responsibility.'"" [Very weak]"
"12707","(12702) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar and Abū al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj both said: ""Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān informed us; ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Shuʿbah reported from Qatādah from al-Naḍr ibn Anas from Anas ibn Mālik: ""That ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) held him liable for a deposit that was stolen from his house."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12708","(12703) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Khamb informed us; Abū Ismāʿīl al-Tirmidhī narrated to us; Ayyūb ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Uways narrated to me from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, who said: ""Yaḥyā said: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl, a man from the people of Baṣrah, narrated to me that Anas ibn Mālik narrated to him: 'That ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) held him liable for merchandise that was with him and was stolen or lost, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah, the Most High, be pleased with him) made him liable for it.'""

The Sheikh said: ""It is possible that he was negligent in it, so he held him liable due to the negligence. And Allah knows best."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12709","(12704) Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbāṣ al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Sinān narrated to us; Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us; Abū Yūnus al-Qawī said: ""I heard al-Shaʿbī from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: 'I was entrusted with wealth, so I placed it with my own wealth. It perished among my wealth. I was brought to ʿUmar, and he said: ""You are trustworthy in my view, but it perished among your wealth, so I hold you liable.""'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12710","(12705) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed me; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Maḥmūd al-Marwazī informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; al-Anṣārī narrated to us; Hishām reported from al-Ḥasan regarding a man who is entrusted with a deposit and he moves it, taking some of it: ""He used to say: 'When he moved it, he became liable (ḍamina).'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12711","(12706) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy upon him, informed us by dictation: Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar reported from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Spoils of war were not made lawful for those who came before us. That was because Allah saw our weakness and our incapacity, so He made them pure and lawful for us."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3124]"
"12712","(12707) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar reported from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""A Prophet from among the Prophets set out for battle. He said to his people: 'No man should accompany me who has married a woman and intends to consummate the marriage but has not yet done so; nor one who has built a house but has not yet raised its roof; nor one who has purchased sheep or pregnant camels and is waiting for them to give birth.' So he went forth and approached the village around the time of ʿAṣr prayer, or close to that time. He said to the sun: 'You are commanded, and I am commanded. O Allah, hold it back for us somewhat.' So it was held back for him until Allah granted him victory. They gathered the spoils of war, and the fire came to consume them but refused to eat them. He said: 'There is theft (ghulūl) among you. Let one man from each tribe pledge allegiance to me.' So they pledged allegiance to him, and the hand of one man stuck to his hand. He said: 'The theft is among your tribe. Let your tribe pledge allegiance to me.' So his tribe pledged allegiance, and the hands of two or three men stuck to his hand. He said: 'The theft is among you; you have committed ghulūl.' So they brought out something the size of a cow's head made of gold and placed it with the spoils, which were on open ground. Then the fire came and consumed them. Spoils of war were not made lawful for anyone before us. That was because Allah saw our weakness and our incapacity, so He made them pure and lawful for us."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Mubārak from Maʿmar, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12713","(12708) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us: Muḥāḍir narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated — (ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah reported from al-Aʿmash, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Spoils of war were not made lawful for any people with dark heads before you. The spoils would be gathered, and fire would descend from the sky and consume them. Then when the day of Badr came, the people rushed to take the spoils, so Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {Were it not for a decree from Allah that had preceded, there would have touched you, for what you took, a terrible punishment. So enjoy what you have taken of booty, lawful and good.} [al-Anfāl 8:69]"" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah. In the narration of Muḥāḍir it adds: ""When the day of Badr came, they rushed to take the spoils before they had been made lawful for them, so Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed…"" And he added at the end: ""So they were made lawful for them."" The remainder carries the same meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12714","(12709) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Hushaym reported from Sayyār from Yazīd al-Faqīr, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I have been given five things that no one before me was given: every Prophet was sent to his people specifically, but I have been sent to every red and black person; the spoils of war have been made lawful for me and were not made lawful for anyone before me; the earth has been made for me a pure place of purification and a place of prayer, so wherever a man is when the prayer time comes, let him pray wherever he is; I have been aided by terror (cast into the hearts of enemies) at a distance of one month's journey; and I have been given the right of intercession (al-shafāʿah)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Sinān from Hushaym, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 335, Muslim 521]"
"12715","(12710) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us in the year 337 AH: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us: Shuʿbah reported from Simāk, from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd, from Saʿd, who said: ""Four verses were revealed concerning me. I acquired a sword on the day of Badr and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, grant it to me as a nafal (bonus gift).' He said: 'Put it back where you took it.' Then I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, grant it to me.' He said: 'Put it back where you took it.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, grant it to me and make me like one who has no share.' He said: 'Put it back where you took it.' Then the verse was revealed: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger…} [al-Anfāl 8:1] to the end of the verse."" Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah, and he said: ""And make me like one who has no share."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1748]"
"12716","(12711) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Abū Bakr and ʿUthmān, the two sons of Abī Shaybah, narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh reported from ʿĀṣim, from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd, from his father, who said: ""I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) on the day of Badr with a sword and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, Allah has today cooled my chest toward the enemy, so give me this sword.' He said: 'This sword is neither mine nor yours.' I went away saying: 'Today it will be given to someone who has not been tested as I have been tested.' While I was in that state, the Messenger came to me and said: 'Come.' I thought that something had been revealed concerning me because of what I had said. So I came, and the Prophet (ﷺ) said to me: 'You asked me for this sword, and it was neither mine nor yours. But Allah has now placed it in my possession, so it is yours.' Then he recited: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger…} [al-Anfāl 8:1] to the end of the verse."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12717","(12712) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥīrī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah al-Wāsiṭī informed us: Khālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh reported from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on the day of Badr: ""Whoever does such and such shall have such and such as a nafal (bonus gift)."" He said: ""So the young men advanced and the elders held fast to the banners and did not leave them. When Allah granted them victory, the elders said: 'We were your support; if you had been defeated, you would have retreated to us. So do not take the spoils and leave us behind.' The young men refused and said: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave them to us.' So Allah, the Blessed and Most High, revealed: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger…} [al-Anfāl 8:1] up to His words: {Just as your Lord brought you out of your house with truth, and indeed a group of the believers were averse.} [al-Anfāl 8:5] He says: 'That was better for them. Likewise, obey me, for I know the outcome of this matter better than you.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12718","(12713) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Abū ʿImrān al-Tustarī narrated to us: Sahl ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah reported from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, mentioning it with the same meaning, and he added: ""So he divided it among them equally."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12719","(12714) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr reported from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā al-Ashdaq, from Makḥūl, from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, who said: ""I asked ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit about the spoils of war (anfāl). He said: 'It was revealed concerning us, the Companions of Badr. That was because when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) met the people at Badr, he granted each person what he had acquired. We were in three groups: one third fought the enemy and took captives; one third gathered the spoils; and one third stood guard over the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), fearing a counterattack from the enemy, serving as his protection. When the fighting ended, those who had acquired the spoils said: 'The nafal is ours, for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted each person what he had acquired.' Those who had been fighting and taking captives said: 'By Allah, you have no greater right to it than we do. We engaged the enemy on your behalf and stood between you and the spoils, so you have no greater right to it than we do.' And those who had been guarding the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'By Allah, you have no greater right to it than we do. We intended to kill the men when they had turned their backs to us, and we could have taken the spoils with no one to stop us. But we feared for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) a counterattack from the enemy, so we stood guard over him. You have no greater right to it than we do.' When we fell into disagreement and our conduct deteriorated, Allah took it from our hands and placed it with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he divided it among the people equitably. In that was the fear of Allah, obedience to Him, obedience to His Messenger (ﷺ), and the setting right of mutual relations.' Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger. So fear Allah and set right your mutual relations.} [al-Anfāl 8:1]"" Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq with the same meaning, though with a shorter chain of narration, and he said: ""He divided it equally among them; there was no khums (one-fifth share) in it at that time."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12720","(12715) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Dalaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijzī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jahḍam al-Khurāsānī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith reported from Sulaymān al-Ashdaq, from Makḥūl, from Abī Salām, from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to Badr and met the enemy. When Allah routed them, a group of the Muslims pursued them, killing them. A group surrounded the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and a group took control of the camp (and its contents). When Allah sufficed against the enemy and those who had killed them returned, they said: 'The nafal is ours; we killed the enemy, and through us Allah drove them away and routed them.' Those who had surrounded the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'By Allah, you have no greater right to it than we do. It is ours, for we surrounded the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) so that the enemy could not reach him by surprise.' And those who had taken control of the camp said: 'By Allah, you have no greater right to it than we do. We took control of the camp.' So Allah, the Blessed and Most High, revealed: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger. So fear Allah and set right your mutual relations…} [al-Anfāl 8:1] up to His words: {If you are believers.} [al-Anfāl 8:1] So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) divided it among them equitably."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12721","(12716) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū ʿĀwanah and Shaybān narrated from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Mawhab from Ibn ʿUmar that he said to a man: ""As for that which you asked me about—whether ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) witnessed Badr—he was occupied with the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allotted him his share."" And he mentioned the hadith. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the hadith of Abū ʿĀwanah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4066]"
"12722","(12717) Abū ʿAbdullāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baghdādī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Khālid narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Abū al-Aswad from ʿUrwah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12723","(12718) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAttāb narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mughīrah al-Jawharī informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to me: Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah narrated to me from his paternal uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, in the Maghāzī of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), regarding the naming of those who witnessed Badr and those who stayed behind, for whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allotted their share: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ stayed behind attending to his wife Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who was ill. He remained with her until she passed away on the day the people of Badr arrived in Madīnah. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allotted him his share. He said: ""And my reward, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""And your reward.""

Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh arrived from Shām after the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had returned from Badr, and he spoke to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about his share. He said: ""You have your share."" He said: ""And my reward, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""And your reward.""

Saʿīd ibn Zayd arrived from Shām after the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) returned from Badr, and he spoke to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about his share. He said: ""You have your share."" He said: ""And my reward, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""And your reward.""

Abū Lubābah went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to Badr, but he was sent back and appointed over Madīnah, and he was allotted a share along with the companions of Badr.

Khawwāt ibn Jubayr went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) until he reached al-Ṣafrāʾ, when a stone struck his lower leg, so he returned, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allotted him his share.

ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAdī—they claim—went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), but he was turned back. He returned from al-Rawḥāʾ, and he was allotted a share.

Al-Ḥārith ibn al-Ṣimmah was injured at al-Rawḥāʾ, and the Prophet (ﷺ) allotted him his share.

This is the wording of the hadith of Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, and the hadith of ʿUrwah carries the same meaning. And ʿUrwah's hadith adds: ""Al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥāṭib—he was turned back and allotted a share.""

Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālah from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding the verse in Sūrah al-Anfāl: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: 'The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger.'} He said: ""The spoils of war (al-anfāl) are the gains of war. They belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ); no one else had any share in them. Whatever the Muslim raiding parties brought back—whoever withheld from it a needle or thread, it was treachery (ghulūl). So they asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to give them some of it. Allah, the Blessed and Most High, said: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: 'The spoils of war'} are for Me; I have made them for My Messenger. You have no share in them. {So fear Allah and settle your disputes,} until His words: {If you are believers.} Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And know that whatever you obtain as spoils, then indeed one-fifth of it is for Allah and for the Messenger.} Then that one-fifth was divided for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), for the near relatives (i.e., the relatives of the Prophet ﷺ), the orphans, the poor, and those striving in the cause of Allah. And the remaining four-fifths were distributed among the people. The people are equal in it: for the horse, two shares; for its rider, one share; and for the foot soldier, one share."" Thus it occurs in the book: ""and those striving"" (al-mujāhidīn), but it is an error; it is actually ""the wayfarer"" (ibn al-sabīl). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12724","(12719) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ Maḥbūb ibn Mūsā informed us: Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shawdhab: ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to me from Ibn Buraydah from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: ""Whenever the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obtained war gains, he would command Bilāl, who would announce among the people. They would bring their spoils, and he would extract the one-fifth and divide it. Then after that, a man came with a rein made of hair and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, this is from what we obtained as war gains.' He said: 'Did you hear Bilāl call out three times?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'What prevented you from bringing it?' He offered an excuse. He said: 'You will bring it on the Day of Resurrection, and I will not accept it from you.'"" [Ḥasan]"
"12725","(12720) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Furak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Jamrah: ""I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: 'When the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he said: ""Who are these people?"" They said: ""From Rabīʿah."" He said: ""Welcome to the delegation, without disgrace or regret."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we are a tribe of Rabīʿah, and we come to you from a great distance. Between us and you is this tribe of the disbelievers of Muḍar, and we cannot reach you except in a sacred month. So command us with a clear matter by which we may call those behind us and by which we may enter Paradise."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I command you four things and forbid you four things. I command you with faith in Allah alone—do you know what faith in Allah is? Testifying that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, establishing the prayer, giving the zakah, fasting Ramadan, and giving one-fifth of the war gains. And I forbid you four things: the dubbāʾ, the ḥantam, the naqīr, and the muzaffat."" And sometimes he said: ""And the muqayyar."" ""So memorize them and call those behind you to them.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd from Shuʿbah, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 53. Muslim]"
"12726","(12721) Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī the Imam, Nāṣir ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Sharayḥ informed us: Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us: Shuʿbah narrated it with its chain and meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12727","(12722) Abū ʿAbdullāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Manāzil al-Taymī informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Idrīs al-Awdī narrated to us: Khālid ibn Abī Karīmah narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah from his father: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) sent his father—the grandfather of Muʿāwiyah—to a man who had married his father's wife. He struck his neck and took the one-fifth of his wealth."" [Ḥasan]"
"12728","(12723) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Khālid narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿĀʾidh narrated to us: al-Walīd narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to me from what was narrated to him by a son of ʿAdī ibn ʿAdī al-Kindī: ""That ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote that whoever asks about the places of the fayʾ (spoils of war obtained without fighting), it is what ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) ruled concerning it. The believers saw it as justice and in agreement with the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ): 'Allah has placed the truth upon the tongue and heart of ʿUmar.' He fixed the stipends (aṭiyyah) and granted the people of the religions a covenant (dhimmah) in exchange for the jizyah imposed upon them. He did not allocate any one-fifth or war gains from it.""

The narration of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) is discontinuous (munqaṭiʿ). And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12729","(12724) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""I heard Ibn ʿUyaynah narrate from al-Zuhrī that he heard Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān say: 'I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and al-ʿAbbās and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with them both) disputing before him regarding the wealth of the Prophet (ﷺ). ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The wealth of Banū al-Naḍīr was from that which Allah restored to His Messenger, over which the Muslims had no need to mobilize horses or camels. So it belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), to the exclusion of the Muslims. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would spend from it on his family for a year's provision. Whatever remained, he would place in weapons and mounts as preparation for the cause of Allah. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, and Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) took charge of it, managing it just as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had managed it. Then I took charge of it, managing it just as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) had managed it. Then you two asked me to entrust it to you, so I entrusted it to you on the condition that you manage it just as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then Abū Bakr, then I had managed it. Then you both came to me disputing. Do you want me to give each of you half? Do you want from me a judgment other than that which I previously rendered between you? No, by the One by whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, I will not judge between you with anything other than that. If you two are unable to manage it, return it to me, and I will suffice you in its management.'"" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Sufyān said to me: 'I did not hear it from al-Zuhrī, but ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed me from al-Zuhrī.' I said: 'As you have related?' He said: 'Yes.'"" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Ibn ʿUyaynah, in abridged form.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The meaning of ʿUmar's statement that it was exclusively for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is that it was not for those who mobilize (i.e., the four-fifths)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 4/140]"
"12730","(12725) Abū ʿAbdullāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from Usāmah ibn Zayd from Ibn Shihāb from Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān, who said: ""While I was sitting among my family as the day grew long, a messenger of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came and said: 'Respond to the Commander of the Faithful.' So I set out until I entered upon him."" Then he mentioned the lengthy hadith regarding the exchange between ʿAlī and al-ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both). ʿUmar said: ""Let me inform you about this matter. Allah distinguished His Messenger with a portion of this fayʾ that He gave to no one other than him."" Then he recited: {What Allah restored to His Messenger from them—you did not mobilize any horses or camels for it} until {And Allah is over all things competent.} ""So this was exclusively for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He did not withhold it from you nor give you preference over others. Rather, he gave it to you and distributed it among you, until this wealth remained. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would spend from it on his family for their year's provision, and then take what remained and treat it as the wealth of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did this throughout his lifetime."" Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Then Usāmah ibn Zayd said: ""Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir informed me from Mālik ibn Aws, similarly to this hadith."" Ibn Shihāb said: ""Mālik ibn Aws informed me that ʿUmar said, by way of argument: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had three exclusive properties: Banū al-Naḍīr, Khaybar, and Fadak. As for Banū al-Naḍīr, it was set aside for his emergencies. As for Fadak, it was for the wayfarer. As for Khaybar, he divided it into three parts: he distributed two parts among the Muslims and set aside one part for himself and his family's provision. Whatever exceeded his family's provision, he returned to the poor of the Muhājirīn.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12731","(12726) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd informed us that Ibn Thawr informed from Ma'mar from al-Zuhri regarding His statement {And whatever you made gain of war-horses or riding-camels}, he said: The Prophet ﷺ made peace with the people of Fadak and certain villages—the names of which he did not recall—while he was besieging another group of people. They sent him an offer of peace. He said {And whatever you made gain of war-horses or riding-camels}, meaning: without fighting. Al-Zuhri said: The property of Banu al-Nadir belonged exclusively to the Prophet ﷺ; he had not taken it by force, but rather he had conquered it by peace agreement. The Prophet ﷺ distributed it among the Muhajirun and gave nothing to the Ansar from it except two men who were in need.

Abd al-Razzaq also reported it from Ma'mar from al-Zuhri from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from a man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ with the same meaning. [Sahih]"
"12732","(12727) And Abu Bakr—Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi—informed us that Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah al-Asbahani informed us that Abu Ahmad ibn Fars informed us that Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari said: Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Muhammad told me, my father told me from my father, from Khudhayfah ibn Khudhayfah, who said: My uncle Ziyad ibn Sayfi told me from his father from his grandfather, Suhayb ibn Sinan, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Banu al-Nadir, Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, revealed to him {Whatever Allah restored to His Messenger from them, you did not urge on any horses or riding-camels}, and it was exclusively for the Prophet ﷺ. He distributed it among the Muhajirun and gave two men from the Ansar—Sahl ibn Hunayf and 'Abd al-Mundhir (meaning Abu Lubabah)—and he gave Abu Bakr, and he gave 'Umar ibn al-Khattab the well of Hazm, and he gave Suhayb, and he gave Sahl ibn Hunayf and Abu Dujanah the property of the two brothers, and he gave 'Abd al-Rahman the well—which is called the property of Sulayman—and he gave al-Zubayr the well, may Allah be pleased with them. [Da'if]"
"12733","(12728) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr—Ahmad ibn Ishaq—informed us that Abu al-Muthanna and Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Bushanji and Muhammad ibn Harun al-Azdi—

(Transition) And Dalij ibn Ahmad al-Sijzi and Abu Zakariyya—Yahya ibn Muhammad al-'Anbari and Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki—they said: Abu 'Abd Allah—Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi—informed us. They said: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' informed us that Juwayriyyah ibn Asma' informed us from Malik ibn Anas from Muhammad ibn Shihab al-Zuhri that Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadthanani informed him, saying: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent for me, so I came to him when the day had grown hot. I found him sitting in his house upon a bed, its covering spread out, leaning upon a leather cushion. He said to me: ""O Malik, people from your tribe have come to me, and I have been commanded to give them a gratuity. Take it and distribute it among them."" I said: ""If only you had commanded someone else to do this."" He said: ""Take it, O man."" Then Yarfa' came and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, do you grant permission for 'Umar and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf and al-Zubayr and Sa'd?"" 'Umar said: ""Yes, permit them."" So they entered. Then he came again and said: ""Do you grant permission for al-'Abbas and 'Ali?"" He said: ""Yes, permit them both."" Al-'Abbas said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, judge between me and this lying, sinful, treacherous, perfidious man."" Some of the people said: ""Yes, O Commander of the Faithful, judge between them and relieve them."" Malik ibn Aws said: ""It seemed to me that they had been sent ahead for this purpose."" 'Umar said: ""I adjure you by Allah, by whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity'?"" They said: ""Yes."" Then he turned to al-'Abbas and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: ""I adjure you both by Allah, by whose permission the heavens and the heavens stand, do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity'?"" They said: ""Yes."" 'Umar said: ""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, had distinguished the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a distinction that He gave to no one else. He said {Whatever Allah restored to His Messenger from the people of the towns is for Allah and for the Messenger and for the near relatives}. I do not know whether he recited the verse before it or not."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed Banu al-Nadir among you. By Allah, he did not prefer anyone over you, nor did he take it exclusively apart from you, until this remaining wealth was left. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to take from it his annual expenditure and then place what remained in the category of wealth. Then he said: ""I adjure you by Allah, by whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, do you know this?"" They said: ""Yes."" Then he adjured al-'Abbas and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, as he had adjured the people: ""Do you know this?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, Abu Bakr said: 'I am the guardian of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' You two came seeking your inheritance from your brother's son, and this one sought the inheritance of his wife from her father. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity.""' You two considered him to be a liar, sinful, treacherous, and perfidious, while Allah knows that he was truthful, righteous, upright, and a follower of the truth. Then Abu Bakr passed away, and I said: 'I am the guardian of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the guardian of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him.' You two considered me to be a liar, sinful, treacherous, and perfidious, while Allah knows that I am truthful, righteous, upright, and a follower of the truth. I assumed responsibility for it. Then you came to me—you two together, your matter being one—and you said: 'Hand it over to us.' I said: 'If you wish, I shall hand it over to you upon the condition that you are bound by the covenant of Allah to deal with it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to deal with it.' You took it upon that. He said: 'Is that so?' They said: ""Yes."" Then you both came to me that I might judge between you, and no, by Allah, I shall not judge between you by anything other than that until the Hour is established. If you are unable over it, then return it to me.""

Muslim reported it in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma', and al-Bukhari reported it from Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farawi from Malik. [Sahih; previously referenced as no. 12724]"
"12734","(12729) Abu Muhammad—'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari in Baghdad—informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us that Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi informed us that 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us that Ma'mar informed us from al-Zuhri from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadthanani, who said: A messenger from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to me, so I went to him. He said: ""People from your tribe have arrived in Madinah, and we have been commanded to give them a gratuity. Take it and distribute it."" I said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, command someone else to do it."" He said: ""Take it, O man."" While I was in that state, his attendant Yarfa' entered and said: ""'Uthman, 'Abd al-Rahman, al-Zubayr, and Sa'd—and I do not know whether he mentioned Talhah or not—are asking your permission."" He said: ""Permit them."" He remained a short while, then came and said: ""Al-'Abbas and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, are asking your permission."" He said: ""Permit them both."" They entered. Al-'Abbas said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, judge between me and this man."" The people said: ""Judge between them and relieve each of them of the other, for their dispute has grown long."" They were at that time disputing over what Allah had restored to His Messenger from the wealth of Banu al-Nadir. The people said: ""Yes, judge between them and relieve each of them of the other."" 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I adjure you by Allah, by whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity'?"" The people said: ""Yes, he said that."" Then he turned to them both, and they affirmed the same. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I shall inform you about this wealth: Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, distinguished His Prophet ﷺ with something He gave to no one else."" He said {Whatever Allah restored to His Messenger from them}—the verse. Then he said: ""By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not take it exclusively apart from you, nor did he prefer anyone over you. He distributed it among you and dispersed it among you, until this wealth remained. He used to spend from it upon his family for his year""—and Ma'mar sometimes said: ""He would withhold his family's provisions for a year""—""then he would place what remained in the category of the wealth of Allah, the Mighty and Sublime."" When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, Abu Bakr said: ""I am the guardian of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; I shall deal with it as he used to deal with it."" Then he turned to 'Ali and al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: ""You two claim that he was unjust in it, while Allah knows that he was truthful, righteous, and a follower of the truth."" Then I assumed responsibility for it after Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, for two years of my caliphate, and I dealt with it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abu Bakr had dealt with it. You two claim that I am unjust in it, while Allah knows that I am truthful, righteous, and a follower of the truth. Then you both came to me—this one, meaning al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, asking me for his inheritance from his brother's son, and this one, meaning 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, asking me for the inheritance of his wife from her father. I said to you both: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity.""' Then it occurred to me to hand it over to you, so I took from you the covenant of Allah and His pledge that you would deal with it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abu Bakr after him had dealt with it. I assumed responsibility for it. You said: 'Hand it over to us upon that condition.' You now seek from me a judgment other than this. By Allah, by whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, I shall not judge between you in it by any judgment other than this until the Hour is established. If you are unable over it, then hand it back to me."" He said: 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, overcame him regarding it, so it remained in the hands of 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, then in the hands of Hasan, then in the hands of Husayn, then in the hands of 'Ali ibn Husayn, then in the hands of Hasan ibn Hasan, then in the hands of Zayd ibn Hasan. Ma'mar said: Then it was in the hands of 'Abd Allah ibn Hasan until the Banu al-'Abbas took possession of it and seized it.

Muslim reported it in al-Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and others from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih]"
"12735","(12730) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me that Abu al-Hasan—'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Qurqub al-Tammar in Hamadan—informed us that Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn informed us that Abu al-Yaman informed us that Shu'ayb informed us from al-Zuhri. Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadthanani al-Nasri informed me that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, called him after the day had risen. He said: ""I entered upon him and found him sitting upon the covering of a bed, with no mattress between him and the covering, leaning upon a leather cushion. He said: 'O Malik, people from your tribe have come and have arrived in Madinah. I have been commanded to give them a gratuity. Take it and distribute it among them.' I said to him: 'O Commander of the Faithful, if only you had commanded someone else to do that.' He said: 'Take it, O man.' While I was with him, his chamberlain Yarfa' came and said: 'Do you grant permission for 'Uthman, 'Abd al-Rahman, al-Zubayr, and Sa'd?' He said: 'Yes, admit them.' He remained a short while, then he came and said: 'Do you grant permission for 'Ali and al-'Abbas?' He said: 'Yes, permit them both.' When they entered, al-'Abbas said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, judge between me and this man, meaning 'Ali, and they were disputing over what Allah had restored to His Messenger from the wealth of Banu al-Nadir.' The group said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, judge between them and relieve each of them of the other.' 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Be gentle. I adjure you by Allah, by whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, do you know that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity""?'—meaning himself. They said: ""He said that."" Then 'Umar turned to 'Ali and al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: ""I adjure you both by Allah, do you know that the Prophet ﷺ said that?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""I shall inform you about this matter: Allah was distinguishing His Messenger ﷺ with something from this fay' that He gave to no one else."" Allah said {Whatever Allah restored to His Messenger from them; you did not urge on any horses or riding-camels; but Allah gives authority to His messengers over whom He wills, and Allah is over all things competent}. And this was exclusively for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By Allah, he did not take it exclusively apart from you, nor did he prefer anyone over you. He gave it to you and dispersed it among you, until this wealth remained from it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to spend from it upon his family the expenditure of their year, then he would take what remained and place it in the category of the wealth of Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did this during his lifetime. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, and Abu Bakr said: ""I am the guardian of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" Abu Bakr took it and dealt with it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had dealt with it, and you were present at that time. Then 'Umar turned to 'Ali and al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: ""Do you remember regarding Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, what you two say? And Allah knows that regarding it he was truthful, righteous, upright, and a follower of the truth."" Then Allah caused Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, to pass away, and I said: ""I am the guardian of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and of my father Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."" I took it for two years of my caliphate and dealt with it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had dealt with it, and you were present at that time. Then 'Umar turned to 'Ali and al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: ""Do you remember regarding me what you two say? And Allah knows that regarding it I am truthful, upright, and a follower of the truth."" Then you both came to me—both of you together, your word being one and your matter being united. He came to me, meaning al-'Abbas, and I said to you both: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity.'"" Then when it became clear to me to hand it over to you, I said: ""If you wish, I shall hand it over to you upon the condition that upon you is the covenant of Allah and His pledge that you shall deal with it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, dealt with it, and as I have dealt with it since I assumed responsibility for it; otherwise, do not speak to me."" You said: ""Hand it over to us upon that."" So I handed it over to you. Do you now seek from me a judgment other than that? By Allah, by whose permission the heavens and the earth stand, I shall not judge in it by any judgment other than that until the Hour is established. If you are unable over it, then hand it back to me, and I shall be sufficient for you regarding it."" He said: I narrated this hadith to 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, and he said: ""Malik ibn Aws has spoken the truth. I heard 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, say: 'The wives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent 'Uthman to Abu Bakr asking him for their share of what Allah had restored to His Messenger ﷺ. I said: ""I shall turn them away from that."" I said to them: ""Do you not fear Allah? Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: 'We are not inherited from'—meaning himself—'whatever we leave is charity; the family of Muhammad may eat from this wealth'?"" The wives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ desisted from what he had informed them of."" Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""By the One in whose hand is my soul, my heirs shall not divide anything. Whatever we leave is charity."" So this charity was in the hands of 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and their dispute over it grew long. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, refused to divide it between them, until al-'Abbas withdrew from it. Then after 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, it was in the hands of Hasan ibn 'Ali, then in the hands of Husayn ibn 'Ali, then in the hands of 'Ali ibn Husayn and Hasan ibn Hasan—both of them used to alternate it—then in the hands of Zayd ibn Hasan, and it was truly the charity of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Al-Bukhari reported it in al-Sahih from Abu al-Yaman. [Sahih; previously referenced for him]"
"12736","(12731) Abu Bakr—Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak—informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us that Yunus ibn Habib informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that Shu'bah—

(Transition) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that 'Amr ibn Marzuq informed us that Shu'bah informed us from 'Amr ibn Murrah from Abu al-Bakhtari, who said: I heard a hadith from a man which impressed me, so I said: ""Write it down for me."" He brought it to me written on a scroll. Al-'Abbas and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, entered upon 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and with him were Talhah, al-Zubayr, Sa'd, and 'Abd al-Rahman, may Allah be pleased with them, and the two of them were disputing. 'Umar said to Talhah, al-Zubayr, 'Abd al-Rahman, and Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with them: ""Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Every prophet's wealth is charity except what he fed his family and clothed them; we are not inherited from'?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to spend from his wealth upon his family and give charity with what remained."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, and Abu Bakr assumed responsibility for it for two years, doing what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done.""—the wording being that of the hadith of 'Amr ibn Marzuq. The hadith of Abu Dawud does not contain ""then he passed away"" at its end. [Sahih]"
"12737","(12732) Abu Muhammad—'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar in Baghdad—informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us that Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us that 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us that Ma'mar informed us from al-Zuhri from 'Urwah from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Fatimah and al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, came to Abu Bakr seeking their inheritance from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. At that time, they were requesting his land in Fadak and his share in Khaybar. Abu Bakr said to them: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity. The family of Muhammad may eat from this wealth.' By Allah, I shall not leave any matter that I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ doing without doing it."" Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, became angry and forsook him, and she did not speak to him until she passed away. 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, buried her at night and did not inform Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""So 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, had a standing among the people during the lifetime of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her. When Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, passed away, the people's regard for him turned away at that point."" Ma'mar said: I asked al-Zuhri: ""How long did Fatimah remain after the Prophet ﷺ?"" He said: ""Six months."" A man said to al-Zuhri: ""Did 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, not pledge allegiance until Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, passed away?"" He said: ""No, nor any one of the Banu Hashim."" Al-Bukhari reported it in al-Sahih from two sources from Ma'mar, and Muslim reported it from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh and others from 'Abd al-Razzaq. Al-Zuhri's statement regarding 'Ali's withholding of the pledge of allegiance to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, until the death of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, is disconnected. And the hadith of Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding his pledge of allegiance to him when the general pledge was given at the Saqifah is more correct. And perhaps al-Zuhri intended his withholding of it after the pledge, then his rising to it a second time and his fulfillment of its obligations. And Allah knows best. [Sahih; al-Bukhari, Muslim]"
"12738","(12733) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me that Abu al-Nadr—Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Faqiin—informed us that 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi said: I said to Abu al-Yaman: ""Has Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed you from al-Zuhri?"" He said: ""He informed me that 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed him that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, sent to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, asking him for her inheritance from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from what Allah had restored to His Messenger ﷺ. And at that time, Fatimah was requesting the Prophet's charity that was in Madinah, Fadak, and what remained of the khums of Khaybar."" 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity. The family of Muhammad may eat from this wealth""'—meaning the wealth of Allah—'they have no right to increase beyond the eating. And by Allah, I shall not change the charities of the Prophet ﷺ from the state they were in during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, and I shall deal with them as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dealt with them.' Abu Bakr refused to give anything from it to Fatimah. Fatimah found that regarding Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both."" Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both: ""By the One in whose hand is my soul, the nearness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is more beloved to me than that I maintain ties of kinship with my own relatives. But as for that which arose between you and us regarding these charities, I shall not fall short in it regarding any good, and I shall not leave any matter that I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ doing in it without doing it."" Al-Bukhari reported it in al-Sahih from Abu al-Yaman. [Sahih]"
"12739","(12734) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Sahl—Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Qattan—informed us that Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sulami informed us that 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Uwaysi informed us that Ibrahim ibn Sa'd informed us from Salih from Ibn Shihab, who said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that 'A'ishah, Mother of the Faithful, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, asked Abu Bakr, after the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to divide her inheritance from what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had left from what Allah had restored. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to her: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity.'"" Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, became angry and forsook Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and continued to boycott him until she passed away. She lived after the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for six months. Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to ask Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, for her share of what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had left from Khaybar, Fadak, and his charity in Madinah. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, refused that to her. He said: ""I shall not leave anything that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do without doing it. I fear that if I leave anything of his affair, I shall deviate. As for her charity in Madinah, 'Umar gave it to 'Ali and al-'Abbas, and 'Ali overpowered it. As for Khaybar and Fadak, 'Umar withheld them and said: 'They are the charity of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; they were for his rights that befell him and their calamities. Their matter is for the guardian of the affair.' And they remain upon that until this day."" Al-Bukhari reported it in al-Sahih from 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Uwaysi, and Muslim reported it from another source from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd. [Sahih]"
"12740","(12735) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu 'Abd Allah—Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz—informed us that Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us that 'Abd ibn 'Uthman al-'Ataki in Nishapur informed us that Abu Damrah informed us that Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid informed us from al-Sha'bi, who said: When Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, fell ill, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, came to her and asked permission to enter. 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""O Fatimah, Abu Bakr is asking permission to enter upon you."" She said: ""Would you like me to grant him permission?"" He said: ""Yes."" So she granted him permission. He entered upon her, endeavored to please her, and said: ""By Allah, I have not left the house, the wealth, the family, or the clan except to seek the pleasure of Allah, the pleasure of His Messenger, and the pleasure of you, the People of the House."" Then he continued to endeavor to please her until she was pleased. This is a mursal hadith with a sound chain of narration. [Sahih]"
"12741","(12736) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from al-Mughīrah, who said: ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz gathered the Banū Marwān when he was appointed as caliph and said: ""Indeed, Fadak belonged to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, from which he used to spend and from which he used to provide for the young among Banū Hāshim and marry off their widows. Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with her) asked him to grant it to her, but he refused. Thus it remained during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he passed away. Then when Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed authority, he managed it as the Prophet ﷺ had managed it during his lifetime, until he passed away. Then when ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed authority, he managed it in the same manner as they both had done, until he passed away. Then Marwān was granted it as a fief, and it eventually came to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz."" ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: ""I have seen a matter which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ withheld from Fāṭimah — it is not my right. I bear witness to you that I have restored it to what it was, meaning to the state it was in during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""

The Sheikh said: Marwān was granted Fadak during the days of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him), and it is as though he interpreted in that regard what has been narrated from the Prophet ﷺ: ""When Allah grants a prophet a provision, it belongs to the one who succeeds him."" He was in no need of it due to his own wealth, so he made it for his relatives and thereby maintained ties of kinship with them. This was his interpretation according to many of the people of knowledge. Others held that what was meant by that was the administration and the cessation of inheritance flowing through it, and that it should be directed toward the interests of the Muslims, as Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to do, and as ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz deemed correct when he restored the matter of Fadak to what it had been. Those who held this position cited as evidence what we have narrated in the ḥadīth of al-Zuhrī. As for Khaybar and Fadak, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) withheld them and said: ""They are the ṣadaqah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; they were for his needs that befell him and his emergencies, and their administration belongs to the one in authority. Thus they remain to this day."" [Ḥasan]"
"12742","(12737) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Marwazī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated, saying: I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: ""When the Prophet ﷺ passed away, his wives resolved to send ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to ask him for their inheritance from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" ʿĀʾishah said to them: ""Did not the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is ṣadaqah'?"" In the narration of al-Qaʿnabī: ""…to ask him for their right."" Then ʿĀʾishah said to them…

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"12743","(12738) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Fāris narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us; Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from Usāmah ibn Zayd from Ibn Shihāb, with its chain, similarly. He said: ""Do you not fear Allah? Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is ṣadaqah'? This wealth of the Family of Muḥammad is for their emergencies and their guests. When I die, it goes to the one in authority after me."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
"12744","(12739) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsá informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī and Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī both said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated, saying: I read to Mālik from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Let my heirs not divide a dinar. Whatever I leave beyond the maintenance of my wives and the provision of my agent is ṣadaqah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, both from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2776]"
"12745","(12740) ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us; ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""Fāṭimah came to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them) seeking her inheritance. They both said: 'We heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is ṣadaqah.""'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12746","(12741) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, that Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with her) came to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""Who inherits from you?"" He said: ""My family and my children."" She said: ""Then why should I not inherit from the Prophet ﷺ?"" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'We are not inherited from.' However, I shall provide for those whom the Prophet ﷺ used to provide for, and I shall spend upon those upon whom the Prophet ﷺ used to spend."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12747","(12742) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan informed us; Yūsuf narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, from Abū Salamah, that Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with her)… and he mentioned the ḥadīth similarly, but did not mention Abū Hurayrah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12748","(12743) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī narrated to us; Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī narrated from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh, from Ḥudhayfah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""A prophet is not inherited from."" And Abū al-ʿAbbās said in another place: ""We are not inherited from."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12749","(12744) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Ibn Dāwūd narrated from Fuḍayl ibn Marzūq, who said: Zayd ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī said: ""As for me, had I been in the place of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), I would have judged as Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) judged regarding Fadak."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12750","(12745) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us. And he said: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us — and the wording is his — Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us. Both said: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Sufyān narrated from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir, and from ʿAmr, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, from Jābir — one of them adding to the other — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""When the wealth of al-Bahrayn arrives, I shall give you such and such and such."" He said: ""The wealth of al-Bahrayn did not arrive until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away. Then the wealth of al-Bahrayn came, and Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Whoever has a debt or a promise owed to him by the Prophet ﷺ, let him come forward.' So I came to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised me that when the wealth of al-Bahrayn arrived, he would give me such and such and such' — meaning three handfuls. He said: 'Take.' So I scooped, and he said: 'Count them.' They were five hundred. He said: 'Take the same number again.'"" Ibn al-Munkadir added in it: ""I came to him, meaning Abū Bakr, once and asked him, but he did not give me. Then I came to him a second time and asked him, but he did not give me. I said: 'I have asked you twice and you have not given me. Either you give me, or you are being miserly.' He said: 'You did not come to me once except that I intended to give you. What disease is worse than miserliness?'"" Isḥāq said: ""Thus Sufyān narrated to me, or something similar."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī from Sufyān, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2296]"
"12751","(12746) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-'Ataridi narrated to us: Ibn Fudayl narrated from al-Walid ibn Jumay' from Abu al-Tufayl, who said: Fatimah came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, and said: ""O successor of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), did you inherit from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) or did his family inherit?"" He said: ""No, rather his family inherited."" She said: ""Then what is the matter with the khums?"" He said: ""Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: 'When Allah provides a provision for a Prophet and then takes him, it goes to the one who succeeds him.' So when I became his successor, I decided to return it to the Muslims."" She said: ""You and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) know best."" Then she left. [Hasan. Ahmad 51]"
"12752","(12747) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated from Abu Ishaq, meaning al-Fazari, who narrated: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Ayyash narrated from Sulayman ibn Musa from Makhul from Abu Salama from Abu Umamah from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) took on the day of Khaybar a piece of dung from the side of a camel and said: ""O people, it is not lawful for me from what Allah has bestowed upon you even the equivalent of this, except the khums, and the khums is returned to you"" — meaning, and Allah knows best, it is returned in your interests. [Sahih]"
"12753","(12748) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub — and Abu al-Hasan al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us: Abu Hilal narrated from Abu Jamrah from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The delegation of 'Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, between us and you is this tribe of Mudar, and we cannot reach you except in the Sacred Month"" — or he said ""in Rajab"" — ""so command us with a matter by which we may adhere and call to it those behind us from our people."" He said: ""I command you with four things and forbid you four things: I command you to bear witness that there is no god but Allah, to establish the Salah, to give the Zakah, and to give from the spoils of war the share of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and the safiyy. And I forbid you from the dubba', the hantam, the muzaffat, and the naqir."" Abu Hilal al-Rasibi is singular in mentioning the safiyy in this narration. [Sahih]"
"12754","(12749) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us: Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us: I heard Yazid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Shikh-khir saying: We were sitting in the Murbad, and I was the youngest of the people or among the youngest of them. A man from the people of the desert came to us, and when we saw him we said: ""It is as though this man is not from this town."" He said: ""Yes."" And he had with him a letter on a piece of leather — and perhaps he said on a piece of hide — and he said: ""This is a letter that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) wrote for me."" And in it was: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad the Prophet to Banu Zuhayr ibn Uqaysh, a tribe of 'Ukl: If you establish the Salah, give the Zakah, separate from the polytheists, and give the khums from the spoils of war, then the share of the Prophet and the safiyy"" — and perhaps he said ""his safiyy"" — ""then you are secure by the security of Allah and the security of His Messenger."" They said: ""Come, narrate to us — may Allah set you right — what you heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: 'Fasting the month of patience and three days of every month removes much of the heat of the chest.'"" Qurrah said: I said to him: ""And the gharr of the chest?"" He said: ""The heat of the chest."" The people said: ""Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) narrating it?"" He reached for his scroll and took it and then departed quickly. Then he said: ""Beware — do you think I would lie upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)? By Allah, I shall not narrate any hadith to you today."" [Da'if]"
"12755","(12750) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated from his father from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utban ibn Mas'ud from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took his sword Dhu al-Faqar as his personal share of the spoils on the day of Badr. [Hasan]"
"12756","(12751) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Matirif from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) had a share called the sahm al-safiyy: if he wished, he would choose a male slave; if he wished, a female slave; and if he wished, a horse — selecting it before the khums was distributed. [Da'if]"
"12757","(12752) And Abu 'Ali informed us: Abu Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us: Abu 'Asim and Azhar both narrated to us: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us: I asked Muhammad about the share of the Prophet (ﷺ) and the safiyy. He said: ""He would be given a share along with the Muslims even if he was not present, and the safiyy would be taken for him — a head from the khums before everything else."" [Sahih. Al-Sijistani, 2994]"
"12758","(12753) And Abu 'Ali informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Mahmud ibn Khalid al-Sulami narrated to us: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Wahid narrated from Sa'id ibn Bashir from Qatadah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went on a military expedition, he had a distinguished share which he could take from wherever he wished. And Safiyyah was from that share. And when he did not go on an expedition himself, his share was allocated to him but he did not exercise choice. [Da'if. Al-Sijistani, 2993]"
"12759","(12754) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Saffar narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Qadi narrated to us: Abu Hudhayfah and Abu Nu'aym both narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwah from his father from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: Safiyyah was from the safiyy. [Sahih]"
"12760","(12755) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman — and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Ahmad ibn Sahl al-Faqih in Bukhari narrated to us: Qays ibn Unayf narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Ya'qub, meaning Ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated from 'Amr ibn Abi 'Amr from Anas ibn Malik that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to Abu Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him: ""Seek for me a young man from among your servants to serve me until I go out to Khaybar."" So Abu Talhah brought me out, riding behind him, and I was a young man approaching puberty. I would serve the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he dismounted, and I would hear him frequently saying: ""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from worry and grief, from incapacity and laziness, from miserliness and cowardice, from the burden of debt and the overpowering of men."" Then we arrived at Khaybar, and when Allah granted him victory over the fortress, the beauty of Safiyyah bint Huyayy ibn Akhtab was mentioned to him, and her husband had been killed and she was a bride. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) chose her for himself and set out with her until we reached Sad al-Sahba', where she became pure. He then consummated the marriage with her. Then he prepared hays on a small leather mat, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Announce to those around you."" That was the wedding feast of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for Safiyyah bint Huyayy. Then we set out for al-Madinah, and I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) drawing a cloak behind him for her, then sitting upon his camel and placing his knee so that Safiyyah would place her foot upon his knee until she could mount. We continued until, as we approached al-Madinah, he looked at Uhud and said: ""This is a mountain that loves us and we love it."" Then he looked at al-Madinah and said: ""O Allah, I declare sacred what is between its two lava fields, just as Ibrahim declared Makkah sacred. O Allah, bless them in their mudd and their sa'."" This is the wording of the hadith of Qutaybah. Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Qutaybah, and Muslim also narrated it from Sa'id ibn Mansur with the same wording in this narration from Anas. [Sahih. Muslim 1365]"
"12761","(12756) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Sakhtawayh al-'Adl narrated to us: Musa ibn al-Hasan, Muhammad ibn Ghalib, and Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Batha narrated to us, saying: 'Affan narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us: Thabit narrated on the authority of Anas ibn Malik, who said: A slave-girl fell into the share of Dihyah, and it was said, ""O Messenger of Allah, a beautiful slave-girl has fallen into the share of Dihyah."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) purchased her for seven heads (i.e., in exchange for seven captives), then handed her over to Umm Sulaym to prepare and equip her. He (the narrator) said: I think he added, ""She was observing her waiting period in his house, and she was Safiyyah ibn Huyayy.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from 'Affan. And Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated it from Thabit, who said: Anas narrated to us, saying: Safiyyah fell into the share of Dihyah in his portion, and they began praising her to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying, ""We have not seen the like of her among the captives."" He said: So he sent for Dihyah and gave him whatever he wanted in exchange for her, then handed her over to my mother and said, ""Prepare her.""

Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us: Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Hashim narrated to us: Bahz narrated to us: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated it. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Hashim.

Al-Shafi'i said: The matter upon which none of the people of knowledge among us have differed — and I have known this and we have preserved their statement regarding it — is that whatever the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) selected as his share of the war spoils (safiyy) belonged to no one other than him. [Sahih]"
"12762","(12757) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) proceeded, and when he emerged from a narrow pass called al-Safra', he came out upon a mound called Sayr, at a distance of one night's journey from Badr or more. There the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) distributed the additional shares (nafal) among the Muslims upon that mound.

Al-Shafi'i said: The area around Sayr and its inhabitants were polytheists. Al-Shafi'i said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) divided the wealth of Banu al-Mustaliq and their captives at the very place where he had seized them, before moving away from it, and all the surrounding land was the territory of polytheism. Most of what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and the commanders of his expeditions divided of the war spoils they acquired was done in the lands of the people of war. [Da'if]"
"12763","(12758) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated us: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us: Huyayy narrated on the authority of Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out on the day of Badr with three hundred and fifteen men. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O Allah, they are barefoot — so carry them. O Allah, they are naked — so clothe them. O Allah, they are hungry — so feed them."" Then Allah granted him victory on the day of Badr. When they returned, there was not a single man among them who did not come back with a camel or two, and they were clothed and satiated.

The Shaykh said: Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, repeated this issue in the book of Sirah, and we shall mention it in its entirety in its proper place in the book of Sirah, if Allah, the Most High, wills. [Da'if — narrated by al-Sijistani: 2747]"
"12764","(12759) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us: Abu 'Ali Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub ibn al-Majishun narrated to us. (H) And Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Hamshadh narrated to us: Abu al-Muthanna al-'Anbari narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated on the authority of Salih ibn Ibrahim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf on the authority of his father on the authority of his grandfather, who said: While I was standing in the ranks on the day of Badr, I looked to my right and to my left, and behold, I was between two young Ansari boys whose teeth were recently grown. I wished I were between two stronger men than they. Then one of them nudged me and said, ""O uncle, do you know Abu Jahl?"" I said, ""Yes, and what need do you have of him, O brother's son?"" He said, ""I have been told that he insults the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). By Him in whose hand is my soul, if I see him, my shadow shall not part from his until the quicker of us two dies."" I was astonished at that. Then the other nudged me and said the same to me. It was not long before I saw Abu Jahl moving among the people. I said to them, ""This is the one you are asking about."" They rushed upon him with their swords and struck him until they killed him. Then they went to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and informed him. He said, ""Which one of you killed him?"" Each one of them said, ""I killed him."" He said, ""Have you wiped your swords?"" They said, ""No."" He looked at the two swords and said, ""Both of you killed him."" He awarded his spoils to Mu'adh ibn 'Amr ibn al-Jamuh. The two were Mu'adh ibn 'Afrā' and Mu'adh ibn 'Amr ibn al-Jamuh.

[Sahih — al-Bukhari 3141, Muslim 1752]"
"12765","(12760) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad and Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us: Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated it.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya.

Arguing by this hadith in this issue is not sound, for the state of the war spoils on the day of Badr has already been discussed in our book, until the verse was revealed. The proof lies rather in the Prophet's (ﷺ) granting the spoils of the slain to the killer after the battle of Badr, and this is clear in the hadith of Abu Qatadah and others. [Sahih — previously cited]"
"12766","(12761) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, and I heard Malik ibn Anas say: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us.

(H) And Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan, and others informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Malik informed us.

(H) And Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Sa'id narrated to us: al-Qa'nabi narrated from Malik — from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Umar ibn Kathib ibn Aflah, from Abu Muhammad, the freedman of Abu Qatadah, from Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the year of Hunayn. When we met the enemy, the Muslims suffered a setback. I saw a polytheist man who had overpowered a Muslim man. I said: I went around behind him and struck him on the tendon of his shoulder blade with a blow. He turned toward me and seized me in a grip from which I felt the scent of death. Then death overtook him and he released me. I caught up with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said to him: ""What is the matter with the people?"" He said: ""The matter is Allah's."" Then the people returned. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever kills a slain person and has proof against him, his spoils are his."" I stood up and said: ""Who will testify for me?"" Then I sat down. He said it a second time, and I stood up and said: ""Who will testify for me?"" Then I sat down. He said it a third time, and I stood up the third time. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What is the matter with you, O Abu Qatadah?"" I recounted the story to him. A man from the people said: ""He has spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah, and the spoils of that slain man are with me. Satisfy him on my behalf."" Abu Bakr said: ""Far be it! By Allah, He will not direct one of the lions of Allah, who fights for Allah, to give you his spoils."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""He has spoken the truth. Give them to him."" Abu Qatadah said: He gave them to me. I sold the coat of mail and purchased with its price a garden among Banu Salamah. It was the first wealth I acquired in Islam.

Al-Shafi'i said: Malik said: al-Makhraf means date-palms. This is the wording of al-Shafi'i's hadith.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from Abu al-Tahir from Ibn Wahb. [Sahih — al-Bukhari, Muslim]"
"12767","(12762) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us.

(H) And Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdān informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: al-Kajji — meaning Abu Muslim — narrated to us: Hajjaj narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us: Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah narrated on the authority of Anas, who said: Hawazin came on the day of Hunayn with their women, children, camels, and sheep, and they arranged them in rows to make their numbers appear greater to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Muslims and the polytheists met, and the Muslims turned in retreat, as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O servants of Allah, I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger. O company of the Ansar, I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger."" Then Allah routed the polytheists, and no sword was struck and no spear was thrust. The Prophet (ﷺ) said that day: ""Whoever kills a disbeliever, his spoils are his,"" and he took them.

In the hadith of Abu Dawud: Abu Talhah killed twenty men that day and took their spoils. Abu Qatadah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I struck a man on the tendon of the shoulder blade, and he had a coat of mail on him. I hastened and did not take his spoils. See with whom it is and give it to me."" A man said: ""I have taken it. Satisfy him on my behalf and give it to me."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was silent. He used not to be asked for anything except that he gave it or remained silent. 'Umar said: ""By Allah, Allah, the Most High, will not return it upon one of His lions who fights for Allah and then give it to you."" The Prophet (ﷺ) laughed and said: ""'Umar has spoken the truth.""

Abu Talhah met Umm Sulaym, and she had a dagger with him. He said: ""O Umm Sulaym, what is this with you?"" She said: ""If any polytheist man comes near me, I will rip open his belly."" Abu Talhah informed the Prophet (ﷺ) of that. Umm Sulaym said: ""O Messenger of Allah, kill those who are behind us — the freed captives."" He said: ""O Umm Sulaym, Allah has sufficed and done well.""

Muslim narrated the end of this hadith regarding the story of Umm Sulaym, and it is sahih according to his conditions. [Sahih — Muslim 1809]"
"12768","(12763) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Saffar narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Abi 'Uthman al-Tayalisi al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ma'in and Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us: Abu Ayyub al-Afriqi narrated on the authority of Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah on the authority of Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever kills a slain person, his spoils are his."" [Sahih — previously cited]"
"12769","(12764) Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Ubayd al-Hurfi in Baghdad informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Ishaq ibn al-Hasan narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. (H) And Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Saffar narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Birti al-Qadi narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us: Abu al-'Umais narrated on the authority of Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' on the authority of his father, who said: A spy from among the polytheists came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was on a journey. He sat and conversed with his companions, then slipped away. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Seek him and kill him."" He said: ""I outran them to him, killed him, and took his spoils."" Al-Birti added in his narration: ""And he granted them to me as nafal (additional share).""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Nu'aym. [Sahih — al-Bukhari]"
"12770","(12765) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: al-Asfati — meaning al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl — narrated to us: Abu al-Walid narrated to us: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us: Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated on the authority of his father, who said: We went on campaign with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) against Hawazin. While we were taking our midday meal, most of us being on foot and among us being those who were weak, a man came on a red camel. He pulled a strap from the camel's hindquarters and tied his camel with it, then turned toward the people. When he saw their weakness, he released it, made it kneel, sat upon it, and then set off at a gallop. A man from Aslam followed him on a grey she-camel from among the people. I went out running and caught up with him, the head of the she-camel being level with the thigh of the other camel. I advanced until I took hold of the camel's reins and made it kneel. When its knees touched the ground, I drew my sword and struck it, and its head fell off. I came with his mount and what was upon it, driving them. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) met me among the people, coming forward, and said: ""Who killed the man?"" They said: ""Ibn al-Akwa'."" He said: ""All the spoils are his.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar. [Sahih — previously cited]"
"12771","(12766) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā ibn Abī Qumāsh narrated to us, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, and ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated the hadith with its meaning, except that he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted me as a special gift (naffalanī) his mount, what was upon it, and his weapons."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited.]"
"12772","(12767) And it has been narrated to us from Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from his father, who said: ""We encountered the enemy with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and I stabbed a man and killed him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted me his spoils (salabahu)."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported it to me; Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me; Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdūn al-Aʿmashī narrated to us from his original manuscript; Abū Saʿīd al-Ashajj narrated to us; Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated it. And this is a gharīb narration with this chain. It has also been narrated through another weak chain. [Ḥasan]"
"12773","(12768) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Maslamah ibn ʿAlī narrated to me, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, who said: ""I went out during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on a military expedition. We encountered the enemy, and I charged at a man and stabbed him, causing his blood to flow, and I took his spoils. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted them to me as a special gift (naffalanīhā)."" [Ḥasan li-ghayrih]"
"12774","(12769) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maʿqilī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb narrated to me; Abū Ṣakhr narrated to me, from Yazīd ibn Qusaīṭ al-Laythī, from Isḥāq ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, who said: My father narrated to me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh said on the day of Uḥud: ""Come, let us call upon Allah."" So we went aside to a corner, and Saʿd supplicated, saying: ""O Lord, when we meet the people tomorrow, grant me a man of intense fury and great strength, that I may fight him for Your sake and he may fight me, then grant me victory over him so that I may kill him and take his spoils."" ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh said ""Āmīn."" Then ʿAbd Allāh said: ""O Lord, grant me tomorrow a man of intense fury and great strength, that I may fight him for Your sake and he may fight me, then let him seize me and cut off my nose. So when I meet You tomorrow, You will say: 'O ʿAbd Allāh, why was your nose and your ear cut off?' And I will say: 'For Your sake and for the sake of Your Messenger.' And You will say: 'You have spoken the truth.'"" Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said: ""O my son, the supplication of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh was better than my supplication. I saw him at the end of the day, and his ear and his nose were hanging on a thread."" [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ḥākim 530]"
"12775","(12770) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Khaffāf narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Mundhir ibn Saʿīd al-Harawī narrated to us; Abū al-Zubayr ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim al-Makkī narrated to me; Hārūn ibn Yaḥyā ibn Hārūn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah al-Madanī narrated to me, saying: Abū Rabīʿah al-Ḥarrānī narrated to me, from ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Abī Anas, from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym, from Anas ibn Mālik, that he heard Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah say: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) came to me at Uḥud while he was in distress, and in the hand of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) was a shield containing water, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was washing his face with that water. Ḥāṭib said to him: 'Who did this to you?' He said: 'ʿUtbah ibn Abī Waqqāsh struck my face and broke my incisor with a stone he threw at me.' I said: 'I heard a caller crying out on the mountain: ""Muḥammad has been killed!"" So I came as though my soul had departed. I said: ""Where has ʿUtbah gone?"" He pointed to where he had gone. I went until I caught up with him, and I struck him with my sword and severed his head. I descended and took his head, his spoils, and his horse, and I brought them to the Prophet (ﷺ). He accepted them from me and supplicated for me, saying: 'May Allah be pleased with you, may Allah be pleased with you.'"" [Very weak]"
"12776","(12771) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Ibn Isḥāq narrated to me; Yazīd ibn Rūmān narrated to me, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, and Yazīd ibn Ziyād narrated to me, from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī and ʿUthmān ibn Yahūdhā, from men of his people, who said: ""He mentioned the story of the Battle of the Trench and the killing of ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Wudd by ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him). Then ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) came toward the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), his face beaming, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Why did you not strip his armor? For there is no armor among the Arabs better than it.' He said: 'I struck him and he shielded himself with his dark mass (of followers), and I felt shy before my paternal uncle to strip him.'"" [Weak]"
"12777","(12772) Through his chain, Ibn Isḥāq said: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from his father, who said: ""Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib was in the fort of Ḥassān ibn Thābit when the Prophet (ﷺ) dug the trench."" Ṣafiyyah said: ""A man from the Jews passed by us and began to circle the fort. I said to Ḥassān: 'This Jew is circling the fort, as you see, and I fear he may expose our vulnerability. Go down to him and kill him.' He said: 'May Allah forgive you, O daughter of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib! By Allah, you know well that I am not the one for this.' Ṣafiyyah said: 'When he said that, I prepared myself and took a pole, then I descended from the fort toward him and struck him with the pole until I killed him. Then I returned to the fort and said: 'O Ḥassān, go down and strip him, for nothing prevented me from stripping him except that he was a man.' He said: 'I have no need of his spoils, O daughter of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.'"" [Ḥasan. Al-Ḥākim 6969]"
"12778","(12773) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; Aḥmad informed us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, a similar narration, and he added in it: ""She was the first woman to kill a man from the polytheists."" [Weak]"
"12779","(12774) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Karīm, from ʿIkrimah, who said: A Jew said on the day of Qurayẓah: ""Who will come forth to duel?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Rise, O Zubayr."" Ṣafiyyah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I will be left alone."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whichever of the two overcomes his companion, let him kill him."" So al-Zubayr overcame him and killed him, and the Prophet (ﷺ) granted him his spoils. This is a mursal narration, and it has also been narrated as a musnad narration with the mention of Ibn ʿAbbās in it. [Weak]"
"12780","(12775) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Jahm narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; al-Wāqidī narrated to me; Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl narrated to me, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""Some Muslims were afflicted in the expedition of Muʾtah, and the Muslims captured some of the polytheists' belongings. Among what they captured was a ring. A man brought it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: 'I killed its owner that day.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted it to him as a special gift."" Al-Wāqidī said: And Bukayr ibn Misḥār narrated to me, from ʿUmarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thābit, from his father, who said: ""I was present at Muʾtah. A man from among them came out to duel me that day. I struck him, and he was wearing a helmet in which there was a ruby. My only interest was the ruby, so I took it. When I returned to Madīnah, I brought it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he granted it to me as a special gift. I sold it during the time of ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) for one hundred dinars, and with it I purchased a garden."" [Weak]"
"12781","(12776) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us, Abū al-Walīd narrated to us, Hishām narrated to us, Sharīk narrated from Ibn ʿAqīl, from Jābir, who said: ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah be pleased with him — engaged a man in single combat on the day of Muʾtah and killed him, so the Prophet ﷺ awarded him his sword and his shield. [Weak]"
"12782","(12777) He said, and Tamtām narrated to me, al-Walīd ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Naḥḥās narrated to us, Sharīk narrated from Ibn ʿAqīl, from Jābir — or it is from the ḥadīth of Jābir — who said: ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib engaged a man in single combat on the day of Muʾtah, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ awarded him his sword and his shield. [Weak. See the preceding.]"
"12783","(12778) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū Khaythamah narrated from Jābir, who said: A man from Banī Hāshim narrated to me that ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah be pleased with him — killed a man on the day of Muʾtah and obtained from him a ring with a red gemstone in it that bore an image. He brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who took it and looked at it and said: ""If only it did not have an image in it."" He then awarded it to him. He said: ""It is with us."" This indicates that the ḥadīth has an origin. The Jābir from whom Abū Khaythamah narrated is al-Juʿfī, and the one from whom Ibn ʿAqīl narrated is Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh — may Allah be pleased with him.

Abū Ḥamzah narrated it from Jābir al-Juʿfī, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl, and he mentioned it. Isḥāq al-Ḥanẓalī narrated it from Aḥmad ibn Ayyūb, from Abū Ḥamzah.

The Sheikh said: They differed regarding the killer of Marḥab. Some of them said that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah be pleased with him — killed him, and some of them said that Muḥammad ibn Maslamah al-Anṣārī — may Allah be pleased with him — killed him. [Weak]"
"12784","(12779) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Buṭṭah al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Jaham narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar — that is, al-Wāqidī — who said: It is said that Muḥammad ibn Maslamah struck the shins of Marḥab and cut them off. Marḥab said: ""Finish me off, O Muḥammad!"" Muḥammad said: ""Taste death, as my brother Maḥmūd tasted it."" He then left him and passed on. ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — came upon him and struck off his neck and took his spoils. The two of them brought their dispute over the spoils to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Muḥammad said: ""O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I did not cut off his legs and leave him except so that he might taste death, and I was fully capable of finishing him off."" ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — said: ""He has spoken the truth; I only struck off his neck after he had cut off his legs."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ awarded the spoils to Muḥammad ibn Maslamah — his sword, his coat of mail, his helmet, and his shield. The sword of Marḥab remained with the family of Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, upon which was an inscription that no one understood until a Jew from the Jews of Taymāʾ read it. Upon it was written: ""This is the sword of Marḥab; whoever tastes it shall perish."" [Very Weak]"
"12785","(12780) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Jaham narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, al-Wāqidī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥārithī narrated from Abī ʿUfayr Muḥammad ibn Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ moved to the flank — meaning at Khaybar — a man from the Jews came out and shouted: ""Who will engage in single combat?"" Abū Dujānah came forth, having bound his head with a red bandage over the helmet, swaggering in his gait. He struck him and cut off his legs, then finished him off and took his spoils — his coat of mail and his sword. He brought them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ awarded them to him.

This and the one before it are disconnected (munqaṭiʿ), and in the connected (mawṣūl) aḥādīth there is sufficient evidence. [Weak]"
"12786","(12781) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated, from Abī Isḥāq, from Abī Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, who said: Nuʿaym ibn Abī Hind narrated to us, and Ibn Samurah ibn Jundub narrated to me, from Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever kills a slain person, his spoils belong to him."" [Weak]"
"12787","(12782) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated, from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī and Khālid ibn al-Walīd, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ decreed that the spoils belong to the killer and did not take the khums from the spoils. [Authenticated. Narrated by al-Sijistānī, 2721]"
"12788","(12783) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to me, Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr narrated, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, who said: I went out with Zayd ibn Ḥārihah on the expedition of Muʾtah, and a reinforcement man from the people of Yemen accompanied me, who had nothing with him but his sword. A man from among the Muslims slaughtered a camel, and the reinforcement man asked him for a portion of its skin, so he gave it to him. He fashioned it into something like a shield. We proceeded and encountered the forces of the Romans, among whom was a man on a sorrel horse, upon which was a gilded saddle and gilded arms. The Roman began to tear through the Muslims, and the reinforcement man sat behind a rock. When the Roman passed by him, he hamstrung his horse, and it fell, and he overpowered him and killed him, and took his horse and his arms. When Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, granted victory to the Muslims, Khālid ibn al-Walīd sent to him and took the spoils from him. ʿAwf said: I came to him and said: ""O Khālid, do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ decreed that the spoils belong to the killer?"" He said: ""Yes, but I considered it too valuable."" I said: ""You must return it to him, or I shall certainly report you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" He refused to return it. ʿAwf said: We gathered before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I recounted to him the story of the reinforcement man and what Khālid had done. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Khālid, what prompted you to do what you did?"" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I considered it too valuable."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Khālid, return to him what you took from him."" ʿAwf said: I said: ""Here you are, O Khālid — did I not warn you?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""What is that?"" I informed him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry and said: ""O Khālid, do not return it to him. Are you leaving for me their commanders — for you is the best of their affairs and upon them is the burden?""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. [Authentic. Muslim, 1753]"
"12789","(12784) Abū ʿAlī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, al-Walīd said: I asked Thawr about this ḥadīth, and he narrated to me from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, something similar to it. [Authentic]"
"12790","(12785) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl informed me, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Sahl al-Ramlī narrated to us. Both said: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated, from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not take the khums from the spoils, and that a reinforcement man had been their companion on the expedition of Muʾtah. He mentioned the ḥadīth through both chains of narration in its entirety, with its meaning. [Authentic]"
"12791","(12786) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah al-Harawī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Hishām ibn Ḥassān from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from Anas ibn Mālik: that the first spoils (salab) upon which the khums (one-fifth) was levied in Islam were the spoils of al-Barāʾ ibn Mālik. He had charged upon the Marzubān and stabbed him, killing him, and his companions had dispersed from him. So he went down to him and took his belt and his two bracelets. When he returned, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) came walking until he reached Abū Ṭalḥah al-Anṣārī (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""O Abū Ṭalḥah, we used not to levy the khums upon spoils, but the spoils of al-Barāʾ ibn Mālik constitute wealth, and I am the one who shall take the fifth from it."" So they appraised the belt and the two bracelets at thirty thousand. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 33088]"
"12792","(12787) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb from Muḥammad from Anas ibn Mālik: that al-Barāʾ, meaning Ibn Mālik, confronted the Marzubān of al-Zārah and charged upon him with the spear, piercing his back, and took his two bracelets and his belt. Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in prayer one day and then said: ""Is Abū Ṭalḥah present? We used to grant a Muslim man the spoils of a man from the disbelievers when he killed him, but the spoils of al-Barāʾ have reached the level of wealth, and I see nothing but that I must take the fifth from it."" It was said to Muḥammad: ""So did he levy the khums upon it?"" He said: ""I do not know."" And it was narrated from another chain from Anas: that he did levy the khums upon it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12793","(12788) It was narrated to him by Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Sālim ibn Nūḥ narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn ʿĀmir narrated from Qatādah from Anas: that al-Barāʾ ibn Mālik killed one hundred men from the polytheists, except for one man, in single combat. When they went on the expedition to al-Zārah, the dihqān of al-Zārah came out, and a man confronted a man. So al-Barāʾ went out to him and they exchanged blows with their swords, then they grappled and embraced. Al-Barāʾ threw him down and sat upon his chest, then took the sword and slaughtered him. He took his weapons and his belt and brought them to ʿUmar, who granted him the weapons as a special gift (nafl) and appraised the belt at thirty thousand, then levied the khums upon it and said: ""It is wealth.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: This narration regarding the khums of spoils from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) is not from our narrations, and there is a narration from Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) during the time of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) that contradicts it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12794","(12789) Al-Shāfiʿī said: Ibn ʿUyaynah informed us from al-Aswad ibn Qays from a man from his people called Shabrum ibn ʿAlqamah, who said: ""I engaged in single combat with a man on the day of al-Qādisiyyah and killed him. His spoils amounted to twelve thousand, and Saʿd granted them to me as a special gift."" It was narrated to him by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us, and he mentioned it similarly.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Twelve thousand is a considerable amount, and regarding this matter there is a narration from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him)."""
"12795","(12790) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mīkāl informed us; ʿAbdān al-Ahwāzī narrated to us; Abū al-Sukayn Zakariyyāʾ ibn Yaḥyā al-Ṭāʾī narrated to us; my father's father Ẓaḥr ibn Ḥiṣn narrated to me; my grandfather Ḥumayd ibn Munhib narrated to me, who said: Khuraym ibn Aws ibn Ḥāribah ibn Lām said: ""No one was more hostile to the Arabs than Hurmuz. When we had finished with Musaylimah and his companions, he came toward the direction of al-Baṣrah. We met Hurmuz at Kāẓimah with a great host. Khālid ibn al-Walīd went out and called for single combat, so Hurmuz came out to him, and Khālid (may Allah be pleased with him) killed him. He wrote about that to Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), and he granted him his spoils as a special gift. The cap (qalansuwah) of Hurmuz was valued at one hundred thousand dirhams. The Persians, when a man among them attained distinction, would set his cap at one hundred thousand dirhams."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12796","(12791) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī and ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad al-Najjār al-Muqrīʾ in al-Kūfah informed us, both saying: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān from al-Awzāʿī from al-Zuhrī from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The spoils (salab) are from the special gift (nafl), and the special gift is from the khums (one-fifth).""

This is what Ibn ʿAbbās said, but the narrations from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding spoils indicate that it is taken from the principal body of the war gains (raʾs al-ghanīmah).

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""When something is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) — my father and mother be his ransom — it is not permissible to abandon it."" He said: ""And the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) did not make an exception for spoils whether little or much."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12797","(12792) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʤAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Mālik ibn Anas and other men of knowledge informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad and Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read to Mālik from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) sent a military expedition toward Najd, and I was among them. They acquired abundant camels as war gains, and their shares amounted to twelve camels or eleven camels, and they were granted an additional camel each as a special gift (nafal).""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And in the narration of Ibn Wahb and al-Shāfiʿī: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent a military expedition in which was ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, and the rest is similar. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3134]"
"12798","(12793) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent a military expedition toward Najd, and among them was ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, and that their shares reached twelve camels, and they were granted an additional camel each besides that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not alter it.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
"12799","(12794) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent an army toward Najd, and I was among them. Our shares reached twelve camels, twelve camels, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) granted us an additional camel each as a special gift, so we returned with thirteen camels, thirteen camels.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abū al-Nuʿmān from Ḥammād, except that he said: ""And we were granted an additional camel each,"" without mentioning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).

And likewise ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar and Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah and others narrated it from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) granted them the special gift. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12800","(12795) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed us; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated. He said: Nāfiʿ said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent an expedition toward Najd, and from that expedition he sent a raiding party in which was ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar."" ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar related that the shares of the expedition reached twelve camels, and the companions of the raiding party in which was ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar were granted an additional camel each besides that. So the companions of the raiding party had thirteen each, and the companions of the expedition had twelve each. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12801","(12796) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Razzāz informed us in Baghdad: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Jahm al-Simmārī informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us on a military expedition, and we acquired a large number of camels. Each one of us took a camel. When we returned, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave us our shares, and each man among us received twelve camels, in addition to the camel that had been given as a bonus (nafl). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not find fault with us, nor with the one who had given us that.

(T) And ʿAbdah narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq with a more complete chain, and he said: ""Our commander gave each person a camel as a bonus,"" then he mentioned the rest with the same meaning.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12802","(12797) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus ibn Yazīd, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: It reached me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a bonus (nafl) to one of his military expeditions that he sent towards Najd, granting them camels as a bonus in addition to their share of the war booty.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12803","(12798) And he narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ, from Yūnus, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us on a military expedition, and our shares amounted to such and such, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave us a bonus of one camel each. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Thaqafī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣalt Abū Yaʿlā informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ narrated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was mentioned before it.]"
"12804","(12799) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Shabīb al-Maʿmarī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Dhakwān and Maḥmūd ibn Khālid al-Dimashqī, both said: Marwān ibn Muḥammad al-Dimashqī informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah informed me: I heard Abū Wahb say: I heard Makhūl say: I was a slave in Egypt belonging to a woman from Hudhayl, and she manumitted me. I did not leave Egypt without having encompassed whatever knowledge was there, as far as I could. Then I came to al-Shām and sifted through it. Throughout all that, I asked about the bonus (nafl), but I did not find anyone who could inform me about anything concerning it, until I met an old man called Ziyād ibn Jāriyah al-Tamīmī. I said to him: ""Have you heard anything about the bonus (nafl)?"" He said: ""Yes, I heard Ḥabīb ibn Muslimah al-Fihrī say: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ give a quarter (of the booty) as a bonus at the outset, and a third upon the return."" Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from both of them. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12805","(12800) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr informed us: Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī informed us: Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed me: I heard Makhūl say: I heard Ziyād ibn Jāriyah al-Tamīmī say: I heard Ḥabīb ibn Muslimah say: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ give a third (of the booty) as a bonus. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12806","(12801) Saʿīd said: And Sulaymān ibn Mūsā narrated to me, from Makhūl, from Ziyād ibn Jāriyah, from Ḥabīb ibn Muslimah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a quarter as a bonus at the outset and a third upon the return. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12807","(12802) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī informed us: Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Tanūkhī, from Makhūl, from Ziyād ibn Jāriyah al-Tamīmī, from Ḥabīb ibn Muslimah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a third (of the booty) as a bonus. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12808","(12803) And with his chain of narration: Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Makhūl, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a quarter as a bonus at the beginning of the expedition, and when he returned, he gave a third. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12809","(12804) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī informed us: Sufyān narrated. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Maymūnī al-Raqqī informed us: al-Firyābī informed us: Sufyān, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, narrated to me: Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Makhūl, from Abī Salām, from Abī Umāmah, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give a quarter as a bonus at the beginning of a military campaign, and when he returned, a third. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12810","(12805) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us: al-Firyābī narrated. (Ḥ) And al-Dabarī narrated, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, both of them, from al-Thawrī, and he narrated it with his chain of narration similarly, adding: ""after the fifth (khums)."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12811","(12806) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Walīd Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth ibn Saʿd said: My father narrated to me, from his grandfather, from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to grant an additional share (nafl) to some of those whom he sent out on military expeditions, specifically for themselves, over and above the general division of the army, and the khums (one-fifth) was obligatory upon all of that.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr from al-Layth.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1750]"
"12812","(12807) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us; Aḥmad ibn Sayyār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Yazīd ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir al-Shāmī, from Makḥūl, from Ziyād ibn Jāriyah al-Tamīmī, from Ḥabīb ibn Maslamah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to grant one-third as an additional share after the khums.

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. In the narration of al-Zubayrī: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted one-third as an additional share — I think he said after the khums — then he granted what remained.

Abū Dāwūd narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12813","(12808) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Mayssarah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us; from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith, from Makḥūl, from Ibn Jāriyah, from Ḥabīb ibn Maslamah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to grant one-quarter as an additional share after the khums — I think he said one-third after the khums — when he returned.

In the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ: He used to grant an additional share when he set out on a military expedition — one-quarter after the khums — and he used to grant an additional share when he returned — one-third after the khums. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12814","(12809) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Abū Zakariyyā al-Ḥinnāʾī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Abū ʿAwanah narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb. He said: My father narrated to me — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us; from Abū Isḥāq, who is al-Fazārī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Abū Salām, from Abī Umāmah, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: The Prophet ﷺ, when he raided in the land of the enemy, would grant one-quarter as an additional share, and when he returned, having the people in his care, he would grant one-third. And he used to dislike additional shares (anfāl) and would say: ""Let the strong among the believers return [their share] to their weak.""

Abū al-Jawrīyah said: I found a green jar during the governorship of Muʿāwiyah in the land of the enemy, and over us was a man from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Banī Sulaym, called Maʿn ibn Yazīd. I brought it to him, and he divided it among the people and gave me the same as he gave any one of them. Then he said: ""Had I not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say — and I saw him do it — the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'There is no additional share except after the khums,' I would have given you [more]."" He began to offer me from his share, but I refused and said: ""I have no greater right to it than you.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 3/470]"
"12815","(12810) He said: And Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Abū ʿAwanah narrated to us; ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb narrated with his chain of narration, similar to it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12816","(12811) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us; Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥurr narrated to us; al-Ḥakam narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to grant an additional share before the obligation of the khums was revealed regarding the spoils of war. When the verse was revealed: {And know that whatever you obtain of anything, then indeed, for Allah is one-fifth of it} [al-Anfāl:41], he discontinued the additional share that he used to grant, and that became part of the khums of the khums — from the share of Allah and the share of the Prophet ﷺ. [Ḥasan]"
"12817","(12812) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Abū al-Zinād that he heard Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib say: The people used to be given the additional share from the khums. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12818","(12813) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn Ḥammād al-Barbariyy narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad al-Sukkariyy informed us; Abū Bakr al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Azhar narrated to us. They both said: al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Ghassān al-Ghallābī narrated to us; al-Wāqidī narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī al-Abyaḍ narrated to me; from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Miqsam, who said: I asked Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān about the additional share (nafl), and he said: ""I rode horses in the Jāhiliyyah, and I never found the people granting an additional share except from the khums."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12819","(12814) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us; from Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Abū Salām, from Abī Umāmah, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: The Prophet ﷺ, when he raided in the land of the enemy, would grant one-quarter as an additional share, and when he returned, having the people in his care, he would grant one-third. And he used to dislike additional shares and would say: ""Let the strong among the believers return [their share] to their weak."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12820","(12815) And it has been narrated from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Makḥūl, from Abū Salām. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us; Daʿlaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijzī informed us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jahḍam narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith, meaning Ibn ʿAyyāsh, narrated from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Makḥūl, from Abū Salām, and he mentioned it with its chain of narration and part of its meaning. And the ḥadīth of al-Fazārī is more complete. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12821","(12816) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā from Makḥūl from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, who said: I asked ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit about the spoils of war (al-anfāl), and he said: ""It was revealed concerning us, the Companions of Badr. That was when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) encountered the people at Badr — he granted each person whatever he had acquired."" Then he mentioned the hadith regarding the revelation of the verse and the division among them, and that has already been discussed earlier in this book.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Some of the people of knowledge said: When the Imam dispatches an army detachment or a military force and says to them before the encounter, 'Whoever gains something from the spoils shall have it after the one-fifth (khums) is taken out,' then that is permissible for them upon that condition, because they fought upon that basis and were content with it."" He went on to say: ""This is based on the fact that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said on the Day of Badr: 'Whoever takes something, it is his.' And that was before the revelation of the verse regarding the one-fifth (khums). And Allah knows best. And I do not know of anything established with us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to this effect.""

The Shaykh said: ""What has been narrated in this regard is as we have mentioned, and something contradicting it in wording has been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās."" [Weak]"
"12822","(12817) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Muʿtamir narrated, and he said: I heard Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind narrating from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said — meaning on the Day of Badr: ""Whoever does such-and-such and comes to such-and-such a place, for him is such-and-such."" So the young men hastened toward it, while the elders remained by the banners. When victory was granted to them, the young men came seeking what had been promised to them, but the elders said: ""Do not take it away from us, for we were your support (rid' — a backing and shield) behind you."" So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: **""They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger. So fear Allah and set right that which is between you.""** [Authentic]"
"12823","(12818) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Ziyād ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said on the Day of Badr: ""Whoever kills a fighter, for him is such-and-such, and whoever captures a prisoner, for him is such-and-such."" He said: Then he proceeded with the hadith. This differs from the first narration in the nature of the condition. And it has been narrated to us regarding the spoils of Badr that they were distributed before the revelation of the verse of the one-fifth (khums). Then the word of Allah was revealed: **""And know that whatever you obtain as spoils of war, then indeed, one-fifth of it is for Allah…""** (al-Anfāl: 41) — so the matter was referred to Him. And with Allah lies success. [Authentic]"
"12824","(12819) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Walīd Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Abū Dāwūd Sulaymān ibn Muḥammad al-Mubārakī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, and he said: Sahl ibn ʿUthmān al-ʿAskarī narrated to me: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us: Mujālid narrated from Ziyād ibn ʿIllāqah from Saʿd ibn Abī Wāqqāṣ, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) arrived in Madīnah, he sent us with a cavalry detachment, and he mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: ""And the booty (al-fayʾ) at that time was such that whoever took something, it was his."" Then he mentioned the hadith regarding his sending ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh al-Asadī against them, and he said: ""He was the first commander to be appointed in Islam.""

In this there is evidence that this was before the revelation of the verse regarding spoils of war (ghanīmah) and booty (fayʾ). [Weak]"
"12825","(12820) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abī Isḥāq al-Fazārī from Mālik ibn Anas, who said: Thawr narrated to me, who said: Sālim, the freed slave of Ibn Muṭīʿ, narrated to me that he heard Abū Hurayrah say: ""We conquered Khaybar and did not acquire any gold or silver as spoils. Rather, we acquired camels, cattle, goods, and orchards.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad from Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr. [Authentic. Bukhārī 6707]"
"12826","(12821) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Abū Khaythamah and ʿUbaydullāh al-Qawārīrī both said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Were it not that I fear leaving nothing for the people who come after me, whenever the Muslims conquered a village, I would divide it among them just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) divided Khaybar.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl and Abū Mūsā from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. [Authentic. Bukhārī 2334]"
"12827","(12822) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us that Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Madanī informed them, and he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me from his father that he heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, were it not that I fear leaving nothing for the people who come after me, not a single thing, whenever a village was conquered under my authority, I would divide it just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) divided Khaybar. However, I leave it for them as cultivated land.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam. [Authentic]"
"12828","(12823) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān al-Mu'adhdhin informed us: Asad ibn Mūsā informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā informed us: Sufyān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Bushayr ibn Yasār from Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) divided Khaybar into two halves: one half for his contingencies and needs, and one half among the Muslims, which he divided among them into eighteen shares."" [Authentic. Narrated by al-Sijistānī 3010]"
"12829","(12824) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us: Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Bushayr, the freed slave of the Anṣār, from men among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) conquered Khaybar, he divided it into thirty-six shares, each share comprising one hundred portions. Half of that was for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and the Muslims, and he set aside the remaining half for the delegations who would come to him, for matters and contingencies of the people. [Authentic]"
"12830","(12825) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd al-Kindī informed us: Abū Khālid — meaning Sulaymān — narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Bushayr ibn Yasār, who said: ""When Allah granted His Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) the conquest of Khaybar, he divided it into thirty-six shares, each share comprising one hundred portions. He set aside half of it for his contingencies and for what would come to him of delegations, al-Waṭīḥah, al-Katībah, and whatever was acquired along with them. And he set aside the other half and divided it among the Muslims — al-Shaqq, al-Naṭāh, and whatever was acquired along with them. And the share of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was in what was acquired along with them.""

The Shaykh said: ""The reason why part of it was not divided among the Muslims is that it was conquered by treaty (ṣulḥ), and thus it belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."" [Authentic. Preceded earlier]"
"12831","(12826) And that is clear from what Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, that Ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to me from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from al-Zuhrī, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, and some of the children of Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, who said: A remnant of the people of Khaybar remained, so they fortified themselves and asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to spare their lives and set them free, and he did so. When the people of Fadak heard of that, they came down upon the same terms, and so it belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), because he had not attacked it with horses or riders. [Weak]"
"12832","(12827) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, that Abū Dāwūd said: It was recited to al-Ḥārith ibn Miskīn while I was present, that Ibn Wahb informed you, who said: Mālik narrated to me from Ibn Shihāb that Khaybar was partly taken by force (ʿanwah) and partly by treaty (ṣulḥ), and the majority of the Kitābah was taken by force, though there was treaty in it as well. I said to Mālik: ""What is the Kitābah?"" He said: ""The land of Khaybar, which comprised forty thousand date-palm clusters."" [Authentic]"
"12833","(12828) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʤAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, that Ibn Wahb informed us, that Ibn Lahīʿah informed me from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from one who heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah ibn Abī Burdah say: I heard Sufyān ibn Wahb al-Khawlānī say: When we conquered Egypt without a treaty, al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām stood up and said: ""Divide it, O ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ!"" ʿAmr said: ""I will not divide it."" Al-Zubayr said: ""By Allah, you shall divide it just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) divided Khaybar!"" ʿAmr said: ""By Allah, I will not divide it until I write to the Commander of the Faithful."" So he wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), and ʿUmar wrote back: ""Leave it until the matter of warfare is fully settled."" [Weak]"
"12834","(12829) He said: And Ibn Lahīʿah informed me, who said: Khālid ibn Maymūn narrated to me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah, from Sufyān ibn Wahb, with this [narration], except that he said: ʿAmr said: ""I will not do anything regarding it until I write to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him)."" So he wrote to him, and ʿUmar wrote back to him with this [ruling]. [Weak]"
"12835","(12830) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, that Muḥammad informed us, that Ibn Wahb informed me that Mālik ibn Anas narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam that when ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) conquered Shām, Bilāl stood before him and said: ""You must divide it, or we shall draw swords over it!"" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Were it not that I leave the people with nothing to sustain them, I would not conquer any village without dividing it into shares, just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) divided Khaybar. But I leave it for those who come after them as a land tax (jizyah) which they may divide among themselves.""

And it was narrated by Nāfiʿ, the freedman of Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The people achieved a conquest in Shām, among whom was Bilām—and I think he mentioned Muʿādh ibn Jabal as well—so they wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding its division, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had done with Khaybar. But he refused, and they also refused, so he invoked [Allah] against them and said: ""O Allah, suffice me against Bilāl and the companions of Bilāl.""

In all of this there is evidence that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) considered it in the interest of public welfare to leave the lands intact, and he sought to win the hearts of the warriors. When they were not pleased with leaving them, the argument for dividing them stood established by what is confirmed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding the division of Khaybar. Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, Bilāl, his companions, and Muʿādh—with some doubt from the narrator—opposed ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) in his view, and Allah knows best. And we have narrated from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the conquest of al-Sawād and its division among the warriors, until he won their hearts by returning [what was due], which accords with the view of others, and that will appear in its place in the abridgment, if Allah the Most High wills. [Weak]"
"12836","(12831) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Yaʿlā al-Muhallabī both said: Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, that Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, that Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Any village that you come to and in which you settle—your share is in it""—or words to that effect—""And any village that disobeys Allah and His Messenger, its fifth belongs to Allah and His Messenger, and then it is for you.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and he said in its text: ""Any village that you come to and in which you settle, your share is in it.""

And Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and others narrated it from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and they said in its text: ""Your share is in it."" It is possible that what is meant by ""your share"" is the share of public interest from the fayʾ (war spoils), after which he mentioned what was taken by force. [Authentic. Muslim 1756]"
"12837","(12832) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, that Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us, that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, that ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us. Both said: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us from Thābit al-Bunānī, from Anas ibn Mālik, that eighty men from the people of Makkah descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and his companions from the mountain of al-Tanʿīm at the time of the Fajr prayer, intending to kill them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) captured them peacefully and then freed them. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: ""And it is He who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them"" to the end of the verse. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ḥammād ibn Salamah. [Authentic. Muslim 1808]"
"12838","(12833) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Sayyārī and Abū Aḥmad al-Ṣayrafī in Marw both said: Ibrāhīm ibn Hilāl narrated to us, that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq informed us, that al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid narrated to us, who said: Thābit al-Bunānī narrated to me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mughaffal al-Muzanī, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) at al-Ḥudaybiyyah, and he mentioned the narrative. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mughaffal said: While we were in that state, thirty armed young men came upon us and rushed at our faces. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) invoked [Allah] against them, so Allah took away their sight. We rose toward them and captured them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to them: ""Have you come under a covenant of anyone, or has anyone given you a guarantee of safety?"" They said: ""No, by Allah."" So he set them free, and Allah, the Blessed and Most High, revealed: ""And it is He who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them, in the valley of Makkah, after He had granted you victory over them, and Allah is ever Seeing of what you do."" [Authentic. Aḥmad 16923]"
"12839","(12834) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, that Abū al-Yamān informed me that Shuʿayb narrated to me from al-Zuhrī, who said: Sinān ibn Abī Sinān al-Duʾalī and Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me that Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī informed them that he went on a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) toward Najd. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) returned, he returned with him. They were overtaken by the midday rest one day in a valley abundant with thorny trees. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) dismounted, and the people dispersed among the thorny trees seeking shade under the trees. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) dismounted beneath a samurah tree and hung his sword upon it. Jābir said: We slept a sleep, and suddenly the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was calling us, so we responded to him. We found a Bedouin sitting beside him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""This one drew my sword against me while I was asleep. I awoke and it was in his hand, unsheathed. He said: 'Who will protect you from me?' I said: 'Allah.' He said a second time: 'Who will protect you from me?' I said: 'Allah.' So he sheathed the sword and sat down."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not punish him for what he had done.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr al-Ṣaghānī from Abū al-Yamān. [Authentic. Muslim 843]"
"12840","(12835) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, that Ibn Milḥān narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us, that al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us, that Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated to me that he heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent a cavalry force toward Najd, and they brought a man from Banī Ḥanīfah called Thumāmah ibn Uṣāl al-Ḥanafī, the chief of the people of al-Yamāmah. They tied him to one of the pillars of the mosque. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) went out to him and said: ""What do you have, O Thumāmah?"" He said: ""I have good, O Muḥammad. If you kill, you kill one who has [the right to] blood [retribution]; if you show kindness, you show kindness to one who is grateful; and if you want wealth, ask and you will be given as much as you wish."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) left him until the next day, then said to him: ""What do you have, O Thumāmah?"" He said: ""What I told you: if you show kindness, you show kindness to one who is grateful; if you kill, you kill one who has [the right to] blood [retribution]; and if you want wealth, ask and you will be given as much as you wish."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Release Thumāmah."" So he went to a palm grove near the mosque, took a bath, then entered the mosque and said: ""I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. O Muḥammad, by Allah, there was no face on earth more hateful to me than your face, but your face has become the most beloved of all faces to me. By Allah, there was no religion more hateful to me than your religion, but your religion has become the most beloved of all religions to me. By Allah, there was no land more hateful to me than your land, but your land has become the most beloved of all lands to me. And your cavalry captured me while I intended to perform ʿUmrah. What do you think?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) gave him glad tidings and commanded him to perform ʿUmrah. When he arrived in Makkah, someone said to him: ""Have you abandoned your religion, O Thumāmah?"" He said: ""No, rather I have submitted [in Islam] with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). By Allah, not a single grain of wheat will come to you until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) permits it."" [Bukhārī 4372]"
"12841","(12836) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: it was recited to Shu'ib ibn al-Layth — ""Your father informed you"" — and he mentioned it similarly, except that he added: ""until the following day,"" he said: ""What do you have, O Thumamah?"" And he mentioned his statement similarly. Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Sahih]"
"12842","(12837) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Bishran informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us; Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr, from his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said regarding the captives of Badr: ""Were Mut'im ibn 'Adiyy alive and then spoke to me concerning these foul-smelling ones, I would have set them free for him."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Mansur from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih. Bukhari 3139]"
"12843","(12838) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us; Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: ""concerning these, I would have set them free for him,"" meaning the captives of Badr. Sufyan said: ""He (Mut'im) had a favor owed to him by the Prophet (ﷺ), and the Prophet (ﷺ) was the most generous of people in repaying a favor."" [Sahih. Bukhari 313]"
"12844","(12839) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Muqri' narrated to us; Sa'id ibn 'Uthman al-Tanukhi narrated to us; 'Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Sami narrated to us; Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us; al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: Abu 'Azzah said on the day of Badr: ""O Messenger of Allah, you are the most aware of my poverty and my dependents, and I have daughters."" So the Prophet (ﷺ) showed compassion for him, granted him his freedom, and pardoned him, and he left for Makkah without any ransom. When he reached Makkah, he satirized the Prophet (ﷺ) and incited the polytheists against the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He was then captured on the day of Uhud and was brought to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Prophet (ﷺ) used to say: ""A believer is not bitten from the same hole twice."" This chain contains weakness, but it is well-known among the scholars of military expeditions. [Sahih]"
"12845","(12840) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Among those whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) released from the captives of Badr without ransom was al-Muttalib ibn Hantab al-Makhzumi, who was in need and did not pay a ransom, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted him his freedom. And Abu 'Azzah al-Jumahi said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have daughters,"" so he showed mercy to him and granted him his freedom. And Sayfi ibn 'A'idh al-Makhzumi — the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took a promise from him, but he did not fulfill it. [Da'if]"
"12846","(12841) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh al-Harawi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: Abu 'Azzah al-Jumahi was captured on the day of Badr. He said to the Prophet (ﷺ): ""O Muhammad, I have daughters and need, and there is no one in Makkah who can ransom me, and you know my need."" So the Prophet (ﷺ) spared his blood, freed him, and let him go. He made a covenant with him that he would not aid against him with hand or tongue, and he praised the Prophet (ﷺ) when he pardoned him. Then he mentioned the poetry, and then he mentioned his story with Safwan ibn Umayyah al-Jumahi and Safwan's indication to him to go out with him in the battle of Uhud, and his undertaking responsibility for his daughters, and that he kept at him until he obeyed him and went out among the Ahabish of Banu Kinanah. He said: Abu 'Azzah was captured on the day of Uhud, and when he was brought to the Prophet (ﷺ), he said: ""Bestow favor upon me and let me go."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: ""No, lest the people of Makkah say that you played with Muhammad twice."" So he ordered his execution. [Da'if. Previously mentioned]"
"12847","(12842) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and others narrated to us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; al-Shafi'i narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Abd al-Majid — and Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated to us; Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, who said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) captured a man from Banu 'Uqayl, bound him, and threw him into the Harrah. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by him while we were with him — or he said: he came upon him on a donkey with a velvet cloth beneath him — and he called out: ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" So he went to him and said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""Why was I taken?"" He said: ""You were taken for the crime of your allies, Thaqif."" Thaqif had captured two men from the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" He said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I am a Muslim."" He said: ""Had you said it while you had control over your affair, you would have achieved all success."" He said: ""He left him and moved on."" Then he called out: ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" So he turned back and said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I am hungry, so feed me"" — and I think he said — ""I am thirsty, so give me water."" He said: ""This is your need."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ransomed him with the two men whom Thaqif had captured. The wording is that of the hadith of Ishaq. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih. Muslim 1614]"
"12848","(12843) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Saffar narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yunus al-Dabbi narrated to us — and Isma'il ibn Ahmad al-Jurjani narrated to us; Abu Ya'la narrated to us; they said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us; 'Umar ibn Yunus al-Hanafi narrated to us; 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us; he said: Abu Zumayl — who was Simak al-Hanafi — narrated to me; he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas narrated to me; he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, he said: When the day of Badr came — and he mentioned the story. Abu Zumayl said: Ibn 'Abbas said: When they captured the captives, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O Abu Bakr, 'Ali, and 'Umar, what do you see regarding these captives?"" Abu Bakr said: ""O Prophet of Allah, they are your paternal relatives and your clan. I think you should take a ransom from them, so that it may be a strength for us against the disbelievers, and perhaps Allah will guide them to Islam."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What do you see, O Ibn al-Khattab?"" I said: ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I do not hold the view that Abu Bakr holds. Rather, I think you should give us leave so that we strike their throats. Give 'Ali leave over 'Aqil so that he may strike his throat, and give me leave over so-and-so, a relative of 'Umar, so that I may strike his throat. For these are the leaders of disbelief and its chieftains."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) inclined toward what Abu Bakr said and did not incline toward what I said. When the following day came, I found the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and Abu Bakr sitting and weeping. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, tell me about what you and your companion are weeping. If I find cause for weeping, I will weep, and if I do not find cause for weeping, I will feign weeping out of sympathy for your weeping."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I weep for what your companions suggested to me regarding taking the ransom from them. The punishment for them was shown to me closer than this tree"" — a tree near the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ). Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land} until His words: {So enjoy what you have taken of booty, lawful and good.} So Allah made booty lawful for them. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb. [Sahih. Muslim 1763]"
"12849","(12844) Abu Muhammad Janah ibn Nadhir al-Qadi in Kufah informed us; Abu Ja'far ibn Duhaym narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Hazim ibn Abi Ghazrah narrated to us; Bakr and 'Uthman, the two sons of Abi Shaybah, narrated to us; they said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu 'Ubaydah, from 'Abdullah, who said: When the day of Badr came, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What do you say about these captives?"" Abu Bakr said: ""O Messenger of Allah, they are your people and your lineage. Spare them and accept repentance from them; perhaps Allah will accept their repentance."" 'Umar said: ""O Messenger of Allah, they disbelieved in you and expelled you. Bring them forward and strike their throats."" 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you are in a valley abundant in firewood. Set the valley ablaze upon them with fire, then cast them into it."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was silent and did not respond to them with anything. Then he stood and entered. Some people said: ""He will follow the opinion of Abu Bakr."" Some said: ""He will follow the opinion of 'Umar."" Some said: ""He will follow the opinion of 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out to them and said: ""Indeed, Allah softens the hearts of certain men in this matter until they become softer than milk, and indeed, Allah hardens the hearts of certain men in this matter until they become harder than stones. Indeed, your example, O Abu Bakr, is like the example of Ibrahim, who said: {Whoever follows me is of me, and whoever disobeys me, then indeed You are Forgiving, Merciful.} And indeed, your example, O Abu Bakr, is like the example of 'Isa, who said: {If You punish them, then they are Your servants, and if You forgive them, then indeed You are the Almighty, the Wise.} And indeed, your example, O 'Umar, is like the example of Musa, who said: {Our Lord, obliterate their wealth and harden their hearts so that they will not believe until they see the painful punishment.} And indeed, your example, O 'Umar, is like the example of Nuh, who said: {My Lord, do not leave upon the earth any of the disbelievers.} You are in a state of hardship, so let none of them escape except by ransom or by striking the throat."" Ibn Mas'ud said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, except Suhayl ibn Bayda', for I heard him mentioning Islam."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was silent. I did not see myself on a day more fearful of having stones fall upon me from the sky than I was on that day, until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Except Suhayl ibn Bayda'."" Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {It is not for a prophet to have captives} to the end of the three verses. [Da'if]"
"12850","(12845) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf al-Shaybani narrated to us by dictation; Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Shahid narrated to us; Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ar'arah narrated to us; Azhar ibn Sa'd al-Samman narrated to us; Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad, from 'Ubaydah, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said regarding the captives on the day of Badr: ""If you wish, you may kill them, and if you wish, you may ransom them and benefit from the ransom, and a number of you equal to their count will be martyred."" The last of the seventy to be martyred was Thabit ibn Qays, who was martyred at al-Yamamah. [Sahih]"
"12851","(12846) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us, Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, Sufyān ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abī al-ʿAnbas, from Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set the ransom for the people of Jāhiliyyah on the day of Badr at four hundred. [Weak]"
"12852","(12847) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAttāb al-ʿAbdī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zibriqān narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn ʢĀṣim informed us, Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind informed us.

(Muḥammad said:) And Muḥammad informed me, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Shāhīn narrated to us, Khālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: There were some captives on the day of Badr who had no ransom, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made their ransom that they teach the children of the Anṣār writing. He said: Then a boy from among the children of the Anṣār came to his father and said: What is the matter with you? He said: My teacher struck me. He said: The wicked one seeks the vengeance of Badr! By Allah, do not ever go to him again. [Authentic]"
"12853","(12848) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī the Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdād informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad, meaning Ibn Ḥamdān al-Naysābūrī, narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah, client of the family of al-Zubayr, narrated from his paternal uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Ibn Shihāb, from Anas ibn Mālik: that some men from the Anṣār sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying: Permit us, O Messenger of Allah, to leave the ransom of our sister's son al-ʿAbbās. He said: No, by Allah, you shall not leave a single dirham.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Uways. [Authentic. Bukhārī 4018]"
"12854","(12849) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When the people of Makkah sent for the ransom of their captives, Zaynab bint Rasūl Allāh (ﷺ) sent for the ransom of Abū al-ʿĀṣ and sent with it a necklace that Khadījah had given her when she married her to Abū al-ʿĀṣ. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw it, he was deeply moved and said: If you wish, release her captive and return to her what is hers, so do so. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So they released him and returned to her what was hers. Al-ʿAbbās said: O Messenger of Allah, I was a Muslim. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: Allah knows best about your Islam. If it is as you say, then Allah will reward you. So ransom yourself and your two nephews, Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and your ally ʿUtbah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jaḥdam, brother of Banī al-Ḥārith ibn Fihr. He said: I do not have that, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then where is the money that you and Umm al-Faḍl buried, and you said to her: If I am killed, then this money is for my sons al-Faḍl, ʿAbd Allāh, and Qutham? He said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I know that you are His Messenger. This is a matter that no one knew of other than myself and Umm al-Faḍl. So take from me, O Messenger of Allah, twenty ounces of wealth that I had with me. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: I will do so. So al-ʿAbbās ransomed himself, his two nephews, and his ally. And Allah revealed concerning him: *""O Prophet, say to those in your custody among the captives: If Allah knows any good in your hearts, He will give you better than what was taken from you and will forgive you. And Allah is Forgiving, Merciful.""* So Allah gave me in place of the twenty ounces, in Islam, twenty slaves, all of whom had wealth at their disposal with which they traded, along with what I hope for of the forgiveness of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. This is how our shaykh Abū ʿAbd Allāh narrated it to us in the book *al-Mustadrak*. [Good]"
"12855","(12850) And he informed us with it in the *Maghāzī* of Ibn Isḥāq, mentioning the story of Zaynab with this chain of narration. Then after several pages, Yūnus says: Then Ibn Isḥāq returned to the first chain of narration and mentioned the dispatch of Quraysh to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for the ransom of their captives. Each people ransomed their captive with what they agreed upon. Then he mentioned this story of al-ʿAbbās. What Yūnus meant by the first chain of narration is his narration from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Yazīd ibn Rūmān narrated to me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, and al-Zuhrī, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, and others among our scholars narrated to me. Some of them narrated what others did not narrate, and their narrations have converged in what I have mentioned to you concerning the day of Badr. He mentioned the story, then inserted between its parts narrations from other than this chain, then returned to it. And Allah knows best. [Authentic]"
"12856","(12851) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Bāhilī narrated to us, Ḍabbah ibn Miḥṣan narrated to us, who said: I said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him): Abū Mūsā selected forty sons of the Asāwirah for himself. Abū Mūsā came to him, and he said: What is the matter with these forty you have selected for yourself from among the sons of the Asāwirah? He said: O Commander of the Faithful, I selected them and feared that the army might deceive them, so I ransomed them and strove in their ransom, then I divided the fifth and distributed them. Ḍabbah said: He spoke the truth, by Allah. The Commander of the Faithful did not lie, and I did not lie to him. [Good]"
"12857","(12852) Abū Naṣr informed us, Abū al-Faḍl informed us, Aḥmad narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from ʿAnbasah ibn Saʿīd, from al-Mughīrah ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Nakhaʿī, who said: Our elders narrated to us, saying: There was a man from among the sons of kings in the share of the Nakhaʿ on the day of al-Qādisiyyah. Saʿd wanted to take him from them, so they came to him in the morning with their whips. He sent to them: I have written to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. They said: We are pleased. So ʿUmar wrote to him: We do not take the fifth from the sons of kings. So Saʿd took him from them. Al-Mughīrah said: Because his ransom was greater than that. [Authentic]"
"12858","(12853) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Abū al-Azhar narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Shurḥbīl al-Abnāwī informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us.

(And Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad said: Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us. And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr and Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us,) from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: that the Jews of Banī al-Naḍīr and Qurayẓah fought against the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) expelled Banī al-Naḍīr and left Qurayẓah in place, showing favor to them, until Qurayẓah fought thereafter. He killed their men and distributed their women, children, and wealth among the Muslims, except for some of them who joined the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he granted them security and they accepted Islam. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) expelled all the Jews of Madīnah — Banī Qaynuqāʿ, who were the people of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, and the Jews of Banī Ḥārithah, and every Jew in Madīnah.

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr, all from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Authentic. Muslim 1766]"
"12859","(12854) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh and Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdūs said: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated — reciting it to Mālik. (And Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us, and the wording is his: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated. I said to Mālik ibn Anas: Ibn Shihāb narrated to you from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Prophet (ﷺ) entered Makkah on the day of the Conquest with a helmet on his head. When he removed it, a man came and said: Ibn Khaṭal is clinging to the curtains of the Kaʿbah. He said: Kill him. He said: Yes.)

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī and Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from a group from Mālik. [Authentic. Muslim 1355]"
"12860","(12855) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Among the captives were ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ and al-Naḍr ibn al-Ḥārith. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was at al-Ṣafrāʾ, al-Naḍr ibn al-Ḥārith was killed — ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) killed him, as I was informed. Then he moved on, and when he was at ʿAraq al-Ẓabyah, ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ was killed. ʿUqbah said when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered his killing: Who is for the girl? He said: The Fire. And ʿĀṣim ibn Thābit ibn Abī al-Aqlaḥ killed him. [Weak]"
"12861","(12856) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī — and others — informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me from his father, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) executed Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab by way of detention, after he had been bound."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12862","(12857) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding His statement — Exalted is He — {It is not for a prophet to take captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land}: ""That was on the day of Badr, when the Muslims were few in number. Then when they grew in number and their authority became strong, Allah — Exalted is He — revealed after this regarding the captives: {Either [showing] favour afterwards or [by] ransom}. Thus Allah gave the Prophet and the believers discretion in the matter of the captives: if they wished, they could kill them; if they wished, they could enslave them; and if they wished, they could ransom them."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12863","(12858) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Abū Salamah Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ghālib ibn Ḥajrah narrated to us, saying: Umm ʿAbdillāh narrated to me from her father, from his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever brings a slave captive, his spoils of war belong to him."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12864","(12859) And Hushaym narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿUmar ibn Kathīb ibn Aflaḥ, from Abū Muḥammad al-Anṣārī, from Abū Qatādah, who said: ""When the day of Ḥunayn came…"" — and he mentioned the hadith regarding his killing of a man. He said: ""I went to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and heard him say: 'Whoever establishes proof against a captive, his spoils of war belong to him.'"" Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it: Abū ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Hushaym informed us — and he mentioned it. Muslim extracted the chain of narration of this hadith in the Ṣaḥīḥ but did not cite its text. The ḥuffāẓ regard it as an error, for Mālik ibn Anas and al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated it from Yaḥyā, and al-Layth said in the hadith: ""Whoever establishes proof against a slain person, his spoils of war belong to him."" And Mālik said: ""Whoever kills a slain person against whom there is proof, his spoils of war belong to him."" And no one said ""against a captive"" in it other than Hushaym. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Mālik 940]"
"12865","(12860) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Asadī informed me: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: ʿAdiyy ibn Thābit narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Anṣārī — and he was his grandfather, his mother's father — say: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade looting (al-nuhbah) and mutilation (al-muthlah).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam. The remainder of this chapter will appear in the Kitāb al-Siyar, if Allah — Exalted is He — wills. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 5516]"
"12866","(12861) We have narrated previously from Ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obtained a war-gain, he would command Bilāl, who would then announce among the people. They would bring their war-gains, and he would extract the khums (one-fifth) from it and distribute it."" Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī informed me: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Maḥbūb ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shawdhab: ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to me from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr — and he mentioned it. [Ḥasan]"
"12867","(12862) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Budayl ibn Mayṣarah, and Khālid, and al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrīt, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq, from a man from Balqayn, who said: ""I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was in Wādī al-Qurā, and he was presenting a horse. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the war-gain?' He said: 'One-fifth belongs to Allah, and the remaining four-fifths belong to the army.' I said: 'Is anyone more entitled to it than anyone else?' He said: 'No — nor even an arrow that you extract from your side: you are not more entitled to it than your Muslim brother.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12868","(12863) Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmash al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allotted for the horse two shares and for its owner one share."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1762]"
"12869","(12864) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Sulaym ibn Akhḍar narrated to us from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: Nāfiʿ narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that the Prophet (ﷺ) distributed in the matter of the additional grant (al-nafl): for the horse two shares, and for the man one share.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd ibn Ismāʿīl from Abī Usāmah, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12870","(12865) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr narrated to us: My father narrated to me: ʿUbaydullāh mentioned it with its like, except that he did not say ""in the additional grant (fī al-nafl).""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr. Some of the narrators erred in it, for they narrated it from Abī Usāmah and Ibn Numayr from ʿUbaydullāh: ""and for the foot-soldier one share."" The correct narration is the narration of the group from both of them and from others from ʿUbaydullāh, as we have mentioned. And Sufyān al-Thawrī — who is an imām — and Abū Muʿāwiyah al-Ḍarīr — who is among the ḥuffāẓ — narrated it from ʿUbaydullāh with explanation. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12871","(12866) As for the hadith of al-Thawri, it was narrated to us by Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran in Baghdad, who informed us that Abu Ja'far 'Amr ibn Muhammad al-Razzaz narrated to us, that Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Qadi narrated to us, that Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, that Sufyan narrated from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) allocated shares for a man: three shares — one share for the man and two shares for the horse.

And similarly, 'Abdullah ibn al-Walid al-'Adani and others narrated it from al-Thawri. [Sahih]"
"12872","(12867) As for the hadith of Abu Mu'awiyah, it was narrated to us by Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari and Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran, who both said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us, that Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us, that Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) allocated for the man and his horse three shares: one share for him and two shares for his horse.

And similarly, Ahmad ibn Hanbal and a group narrated it from Abu Mu'awiyah. [Sahih]"
"12873","(12868) As for what was narrated to us by Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān, who informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us, that Abu Muslim narrated to us, that al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, that 'Abdullah — meaning Ibn 'Umar al-'Umari — narrated from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) distributed on the day of Khaybar: two shares for the horseman and one share for the foot soldier.

(c) 'Abdullah al-'Umari is prone to error (kathir al-wahm). And this was narrated through another chain from al-Qa'nabi, from 'Abdullah al-'Umari, with doubt regarding ""al-faris"" (horseman) or ""al-faras"" (horse). Al-Shafi'i said in al-Qadim, as though he heard Nafi' saying: ""For the horse, two shares, and for the man, one share."" So he said: ""For the horseman, two shares, and for the foot soldier, one share."" And no one among the people of knowledge doubts the precedence of 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar over his brother in memorization. [Da'if]"
"12874","(12869) As for what was narrated to us by Abu Tahir al-Faqih, who informed us that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us, that Abu al-Azhar narrated to us, that Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn al-Taba' narrated to us, that Mujami' ibn Ya'qub al-Ansari said: I heard my father narrate from his uncle 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Ansari, from his uncle Mujami' ibn Jariyah al-Ansari — who was one of the reciters who recited the Qur'an — who said: We witnessed al-Hudaybiyah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). When we departed from it, we found the people urging on their camels. Some of them said to others: ""What is the matter with the people?"" He said: ""Allah has revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."" So we went out at a brisk pace and found the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) standing on his mount at Kur' al-Ghimim. The people gathered around him, and he recited to them: {Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory} [al-Fatih: 1]. A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is it truly a victory?"" He said: ""Yes, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it is indeed a victory."" Then Khaybar was divided among the people of al-Hudaybiyah; no one was included with them except those who had witnessed al-Hudaybiyah. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) divided it into eighteen shares. The army numbered one thousand five hundred, of whom three hundred were horsemen. He gave the horseman two shares and the foot soldier one share.

Al-Shafi'i said in al-Qadim: Mujami' ibn Ya'qub is an unknown shaykh. We relied upon the hadith of 'Ubaydullah in this matter, and we did not find for him a comparable report that contradicts it, and it is not permissible to reject a report except with a comparable report.

The Shaykh said: The narrations regarding the division of Khaybar are contradictory, for it was divided among the people of al-Hudaybiyah, and the people of al-Hudaybiyah, in most of the narrations, numbered one thousand four hundred. [Da'if]"
"12875","(12870) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us, that Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, that al-Humaydi narrated to us, that Sufyan narrated to us, that 'Amr heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah say: We were at al-Hudaybiyah one thousand four hundred. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Today you are the best of those upon the earth."" Jabir said: ""Were it not for my eyesight, I would show you the place of the tree."" Both al-Bukhari and Muslim recorded it from the hadith of Sufyan. [Bukhari and Muslim]

And similarly, Abu al-Zubayr narrated it from Jabir, and similarly Ma'qil ibn Yasar narrated it, and he said: We were fourteen hundred, and this is the position of the scholars of military campaigns (ahl al-maghazi). And he (peace and blessings be upon him) distributed on the day of Khaybar for two hundred horses. [Sahih. Bukhari 4155]"
"12876","(12871) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, that Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, that Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq, who said: A son of Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to me from one of his family who had met them, and 'Abdullah ibn Abu Bakr ibn Hazm narrated it to me, and both said: The divisions of the wealth of Khaybar were into one thousand eight hundred shares, and that was the number of those among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) among whom Khaybar was divided — their horses and their foot soldiers. The foot soldiers were one thousand four hundred men, and the horses were two hundred horses. The horse received two shares, its rider one share, and each foot soldier one share. And he mentioned the hadith regarding the manner of the division. [Da'if]"
"12877","(12872) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, that Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us, that Ibn Wahb informed us, who said: Yahya ibn Ayyub told me that Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated from Kathir, the freedman of Banu Makhzum, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) allocated for two hundred horses on the day of Khaybar two shares each, two shares. And we have been narrated from Salih ibn Kaysan and Bishr ibn Yasar and others what indicates this. And it was narrated through another chain in which there is weakness. [Da'if]"
"12878","(12873) It was narrated to us by Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah and Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi, who both said: Abu 'Amr ibn Matar informed us, that Ibrahim ibn 'Ali narrated to us, that Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, that Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated from Ishaq ibn Abu Farwah that Abu Hazim, the freedman of Abu Rahm al-Ghifari, informed him from Abu Rahm and his brother, that they were both horsemen on the day of Khaybar — or he said, ""the day of Hunayn"" — ""I doubt,"" — and that they were given six shares: four for their two horses and two shares for themselves. They then sold the two shares for two young camels."" [Da'if]"
"12879","(12874) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us, that Abu al-Azhar narrated to us, that al-Muqri' narrated to us, that al-Mas'udi narrated from Ibn Abu 'Amrah, from his father, who said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), four of us, and we had a horse with us. He gave each one of us a share and gave the horse two shares. [Da'if]"
"12880","(12875) Umayyah ibn Khalid added therein from al-Mas'udi: ""So the horseman had three shares."" It was narrated to us by Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari, who informed us that Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, that Abu Dawud narrated to us, that Musaddad narrated to us, that Umayyah ibn Khalid narrated to us, that al-Mas'udi narrated from a man from the family of Abu 'Amrah, from Abu 'Amrah, and he mentioned its meaning with this addition. [Da'if]"
"12881","(12876) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn Rajāʾ al-Adīb informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Muḥāḍir ibn al-Muwarrʿ Abū al-Muwarrʿ narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, that the Prophet ﷺ allocated for al-Zubayr four shares: one share for his mother among the relatives, one share for himself, and two shares for his horse.

Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān similarly narrated it from Hishāh as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, and Ibn ʿUyaynah and Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated it from Hishām from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād as his own statement, without mentioning ʿAbdullāh in its chain of narration. [Ṣaḥīh through other chains]"
"12882","(12877) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us; Ibn Zanbar narrated to us; Mālik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abū al-Zinād, from Khārijah ibn Zayd ibn Thābit, from Zayd ibn Thābit, who said: The Prophet ﷺ gave al-Zubayr on the day of Khaybar four shares: two shares for the horse, one share for him, and one share for the relative.

This is among the rare narrations of al-Zanbarī from Mālik, and it is only known through the first chain of narration, and in it there is sufficiency. [Ṣaḥīh]"
"12883","(12878) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; al-Muʿallā ibn Asad narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥumrān narrated to us; Abū Saʿīd ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr narrated to us, from Abū Kabshah al-Anmārī, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah, al-Zubayr was on the left flank and al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad was on the right flank. He said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered, he wiped the dust from their faces with my garment and said: ""I have allocated for the horse two shares and for the rider one share. So whoever is given less, Allah has given him less.""

In the chapter, besides what we have mentioned, there are narrations from ʿUmar, Ṭalḥah, al-Zubayr, Jābir, al-Miqdād, Abū Hurayrah, and Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah from the Prophet ﷺ, and in some of what we have mentioned there is sufficiency. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12884","(12879) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm narrated to me, saying: The division and the shares of war spoils only took place in the expedition of Banū Qurayẓah. The horses on that day numbered thirty-six. In this regard, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established the shares of the horses and their shares according to his Sunnah. The divisions were carried out accordingly. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ allocated on that day for the rider and his horse three shares: one share for him and two shares for his horse, and for the foot soldier one share. As for the day of Badr, the shares were not established therein, and the war spoils were not made permissible for them therein, until what Allah decreed came to pass. Then He made them permissible for them after the people were nearly destroyed. He said: {Were it not for a decree from that had already preceded from Allah} (8:68) to the end of the two verses. Then came the day of Uḥud, which was a year of affliction. Then came the year of the Khandaq, which was a year of siege. Then came Banū Qurayẓah, and according to its Sunnah the divisions have been carried out to this day. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12885","(12880) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ, who said: There is no difference of opinion regarding it from the Prophet ﷺ. He said: ""For the horseman there are three shares, and for the foot soldier one share."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"12886","(12881) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Ḥaḍramī informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Kulthūm al-Wādiʿī, from Mundhir ibn ʿAmr al-Wādiʿī, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) had sent him in charge of horses in Syria, and among the horses there were non-Arab (Persian) horses. He said: The horses raced ahead, and the owners of the non-Arab horses came. Then Mundhir ibn ʿAmr allocated for the horse two shares and for its owner one share, and he wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: ""You have acted upon the Sunnah.""

In the older copy, in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from al-Shāfiʿī, there is the ḥadīth of Shādhān from Zuhayr, from Abū Isḥāq, who said: ""I went on a military expedition with Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān, and he allocated for my horse two shares and for me one share.""

Abū Isḥāq said: ""And thus Ḥānīʾ ibn Ḥānīʾ narrated to me from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), and likewise Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib narrated to me from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him)."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"12887","(12882) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us; Abū ʿAqīl Anas ibn Salm informed us; Asad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Ḥarrānī informed us; Ḥammād ibn Khālid narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith, from Makḥūl, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of Khaybar: ""Maintain the pure Arab breed and discard the mixed breed.""

This is the preserved narration as a mursal (disconnected) ḥadīth. Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Jurjānī, a resident of Ḥimṣ, narrated it from Ḥammād ibn Khālid, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith, from Makḥūl, from Ziyād ibn Jāriyah, from Ḥabīb ibn Maslamah, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12888","(12883) Abū Saʿd informed us; Abū Aḥmad informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Jarjānī narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Khālid narrated to us, and he mentioned it, adding in its text: ""For the horse two shares, and for the mixed-breed (hajīn) one share.""

Abū Aḥmad said: ""No one other than Aḥmad narrates this as connected, and his ḥadīths are not sound, as though he makes errors in them."""
"12889","(12884) Abū Dāwūd narrated in his Marāsīl from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, from Wakīʿ, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Shaʿbīthī, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ allocated for the pure Arab horses (ʿirāb) two shares and for the mixed-breed (hajīn) one share.""

Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, and he mentioned it. It is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected), and no proof can be established by it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12890","(12885) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Ibn al-Aqmār, who said: The horses raided in Syria. The horses caught up on the same day, and the mule-riders caught up at mid-morning. The commander of the horses was al-Mundhir ibn Abī Ḥamḍah al-Hamdānī. He gave preference to the horses over the mules and said: ""I do not equate what caught up with what did not catch up."" This reached ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: ""May the mother of al-Wādiʿī be bereaved! He has reminded us of something. Carry on according to what he said.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""If we were to establish a narration like this, we would not contradict it."" And he said in the older copy: ""These are two mursal narrations; neither of the two narrators witnessed what was reported."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12891","(12886) And in what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from him, from Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, he said: The ḥadīth of Makhūl from the Prophet ﷺ is mursal: that al-Zubayr attended Khaybar with two horses, and the Prophet ﷺ gave him five shares — one share for himself and four shares for his two horses. He said: And if it were as Makhūl narrated — that al-Zubayr attended Khaybar with two horses and took five shares — then his son would be more knowledgeable of his ḥadīth and more eager for what is in it of increase over others, if Allah, the Most High, wills. He said in the older version, in other than this narration — and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Khaffāf has mentioned from al-ʿUmarī from his brother — that al-Zubayr came to Khaybar with several horses, and he was not allotted a share except for one horse only. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Shāfiʿī, al-Umm 14614]"
"12892","(12887) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ and Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Faḍl al-Muzakkī narrated to us, they said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, Mālik narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, he said: I read to Mālik from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection."" And in the narration of al-Qaʿnabī, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, and he mentioned something similar.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12893","(12888) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr al-Mukharramī narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us — he said: Shabīb ibn Gharrādah heard ʿUrwah al-Bāriqī saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""Goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection."" Sufyān said: And Mujālid added in it from al-Shaʿbī from ʿUrwah al-Bāriqī: ""Reward and spoils of war.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī from Ibn ʿUyaynah, and Muslim narrated it from Ibn Rāhawayh and others from Ibn ʿUyaynah, without the addition of Mujālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3643]"
"12894","(12889) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of that addition, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Tamīm ibn Sayyār al-Ṭabarī narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us from ʿĀmir from ʿUrwah al-Bāriqī that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Horses have goodness tied to their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection — reward and spoils of war.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Zakariyyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12895","(12890) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh Abū Muslim and Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad said: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr — he is Ibn Saʿīd — from Abū Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarīr from Jarīr, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ stroking the forelock of his horse with his hand and saying: ""Goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1872]"
"12896","(12891) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated to us, Sufyān narrated it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said ""with his finger"" and he added: ""Reward and spoils of war.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12897","(12892) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, Abū al-Tayyāḥ narrated to us, he said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said —

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abū al-Tayyāḥ from Anas that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Blessing is in the forelocks of horses."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through various routes from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2851]"
"12898","(12893) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection. The likeness of the one who spends on horses is like the one who holds back in giving charity."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12899","(12894) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us, Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from Salm — meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from Abū Zurʿah from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked shikāl in horses. Shikāl is when a horse has a white marking on its right leg and on its left foreleg, or on its right foreleg and on its left leg. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 7402]"
"12900","(12895) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us, Abū al-Azhar informed us, Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrating from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from ʿAlī ibn Rabbāḥ from Abū Qatādah al-Anṣārī that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The best of horses is the black (adham) with a white blaze (aqraḥ) and a white upper lip (artham), with three white stockings (muḥajjal) and a loose (ṭalq) right foreleg. If it is not black, then dark bay (kumayt) with these same characteristics."" [Ḥasan. Aḥmad 5/300]"
"12901","(12896) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Sukkari informed me; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Mūsā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Masrūqī narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us; Mūsā ibn ʿUlayy ibn Rabāḥ narrated to me, from his father, from ʿUqbah ibn ʀmir who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you intend to go on a military expedition, purchase a black horse with a blaze and stockings, with the right foreleg unmarked, for you shall gain booty and be safe."" Thus he narrated it from ʿUqbah ibn ʀmir. [Ḥasan]"
"12902","(12897) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Hishām, meaning Ibn Saʿīd al-Ṭālaqānī, narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated to us; ʿUqayl ibn Shabīb narrated to me, from Abū Wahb al-Jushamī, who had been a Companion, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Hold fast to every dark horse with a blaze and stockings, or a sorrel with a blaze and stockings, or a horse with a white face and stockings."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12903","(12898) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf al-Ṭāʾī narrated to us; Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated to us; ʿUqayl ibn Shabīb narrated to me, from Abū Wahb who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Hold fast to every sorrel with a blaze and stockings, or a dark horse with a blaze."" Similar to it. Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Muhājir, said: ""I asked him, 'Why did you give preference to the sorrel?' He said: 'Because the Prophet (ﷺ) sent a raiding party, and the first one to bring the news of victory was the rider of a sorrel horse.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12904","(12899) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Shaybān narrated to us, from ʿĪsā ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, from his father, from his grandfather who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The blessing of horses lies in their sorrels."" [Ḥasan — Aḥmad 1/272]"
"12905","(12900) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Marwān al-Raqqī narrated to us; Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī narrated to us, from Abū Ḥayyān al-Taymī, from Abū Zurʿah, from Abū Hurayrah that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to call the female of the horses a faras (horse).

It was also narrated by ʿAbd Allāh ibn Marwān from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Abū Dāwūd 5246]"
"12906","(12901) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qāḍī in Marw narrated to us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us; Rūḥ ibn ʿUbadah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to me; Suwayd ibn Qays narrated to me; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥudayj narrated to me, from Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""There is no Arabian horse except that two supplications are made for it every day. It says: 'O Allah, You have entrusted me to the one to whom You have entrusted me, so make me among the most beloved of his wealth and his family to him.'"" [Munkar]"
"12907","(12902) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Hishām, who is Ibn Saʿīd, narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated to us; ʿUqayl ibn Shabīb narrated to me, from Abū Wahb al-Jushamī, who had been a Companion, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Keep horses, and wipe their forelocks and their rumps"" — or he said, ""and their haunches"" — ""and do not hang bowstrings around their necks."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12908","(12903) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Abū Tawbah narrated to us, from al-Haytham ibn Ḥumayd.

(Ḥ) And Khushaysh ibn Aṣram narrated to us; Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us — both of them — from Thawr ibn Yazīd, from Naṣr al-Kinānī, from a man, and Abū Tawbah said: from Thawr ibn Yazīd, from a shaykh of Banī Sulaym, from ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd al-Sulamī, and this is his wording: that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Do not cut the forelocks of horses, nor their manes, nor their tails. For their tails are their means of driving away flies, their manes are their warmth, and in their forelocks goodness is tied."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12909","(12904) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿAṭṭār in Baghdad narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to us, from his father, from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd who said: Saʿd saw that he had a virtue over those below him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, Allah supports this nation through their weak ones, by their prayers and their supplications.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, from Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 2896]"
"12910","(12905) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us; Ibn Jābir narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Arṭāh, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abū al-Dardāʾ who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Seek me among your weak ones, for indeed you are provided for and given victory through your weak ones."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Aḥmad 5/198]"
"12911","(12906) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Ya'qub ibn Ahmad ibn Mihran al-Thaqafi al-Zahid informed me; 'Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Junayd al-Maliki narrated to us in Rayy; Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us in Egypt; 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb al-Qurashi informed me; 'Asim ibn Hakim narrated to me from Yahya ibn Abi 'Amr al-Saybani from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Dilami that Ya'la ibn Munayyah said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) announced a military expedition, and I was an old man with no servant. So I sought a hired man, agreeing to give him his share [of the spoils]. I found a man, and when the time of departure drew near, he came to me and said: 'I do not know what the shares [of war booty] are, nor how much my share will amount to. So fix something for me—whether there ends up being a share or not.' So I fixed three dinars for him. When the war booty arrived, I intended to give him his share, but I remembered the dinars. So I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and mentioned his matter to him. He said: 'I find for him in this expedition—in this world, and I believe he said: and in the Hereafter—nothing except the dinars that were fixed for him.'"" [Hasan]"
"12912","(12907) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us; al-Qa'nabi narrated from Malik.

(Reference) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Saffar narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah narrated to us; Malik ibn Anas narrated from Yahya ibn Sa'id from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Actions are judged only by intentions, and a person shall have only that which he intended. So whoever's migration was for the sake of Allah and His Messenger, his migration is for the sake of Allah and His Messenger. And whoever's migration was for the sake of worldly gain he might obtain, or a woman he might marry, his migration is for that which he migrated."" [Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi.] [Sahih]"
"12913","(12908) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah al-Sa'di narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Jabalah ibn 'Ayyash from Yahya ibn al-Walid ibn 'Ubadah from his grandfather 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever goes out on a military expedition and does not intend by his expedition anything except a rope [for tying a camel], he shall have only what he intended."" [Da'if]"
"12914","(12909) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Nafi' narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn Salam narrated from Zayd ibn Salam that he heard Abu Salam say: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Salman narrated to me that a man from among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) narrated to him, saying: ""When we conquered Khaybar, they brought out their possessions from goods and captives. The people began purchasing their [shares of] war booty. Then a man came and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have made a profit today that no one in this valley has made!' He said: 'Woe to you! What profit have you made?' He said: 'I kept selling and buying until I made three hundred uqiyyahs.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Shall I not inform you of the best profit a man has made?' He said: 'What is it, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'Two rak'ahs performed after the prayer (Salah).'"" [Da'if]"
"12915","(12910) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyub from Muhammad from Abi al-'Ajfa' al-Sulami who said: ""'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) delivered a speech and said: 'And there is another thing you say about those who were killed in your military campaigns or who died: ""So-and-so was martyred""—when perhaps he loaded the two saddlebags of his mount with gold or silver, seeking worldly gain through it'—or he said: 'trade.' So do not say that. Rather, say as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever is killed in the cause of Allah, or dies, he is in Paradise.'"" [Hasan]"
"12916","(12911) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us; 'Ali ibn Ibrahim ibn Mu'awiyah al-Naysaburi narrated to us; Muslim ibn Muslim ibn Warah narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Sabiq narrated to us; 'Amr, meaning Ibn Abi Qays, narrated from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani from Ibn Sirin from Ibn Abi al-'Ajfa' al-Sulami from his father who said: ""'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'And another thing you say: a man goes out to fight and you say: ""So-and-so has been martyred""—when perhaps he went out having filled the rear of his mount with dinars or dirhams for trade. So do not say that. Rather, say as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever is killed in the cause of Allah, he is in Paradise.""'""

Hammad ibn Zayd also narrated it from Ayyub from Muhammad from Abi al-'Ajfa' al-Sulami, without mentioning his son in his chain of narration. [Hasan]

Chapter: The Slave and the Woman Are Given a Gift (Ridkh) but No Fixed Share"
"12917","(12912) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus al-'Anazi informed me; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us; Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us; Jarir ibn Hazim narrated. He said: I heard Qays narrating from Yazid ibn Hurmuz who said: ""Najdah wrote to Ibn 'Abbas asking him about certain matters. He mentioned the hadith, including his question and Ibn 'Abbas's reply. He said: 'And you asked about the woman and the slave—did they have a fixed share when they were present at the battle?' He said: 'They did not have a fixed share, except that they would be given a gift from the enemy's war booty.'""

[Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Jarir ibn Hazim. And in the narration of Muhammad ibn 'Ali from Yazid in this hadith, he said: 'As for the fixed share (sahm), no share was assigned for them.'] [Sahih]"
"12918","(12913) And Abu 'Abd Allah narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father from Yazid ibn Hurmuz in this story. He said: ""Ibn 'Abbas wrote to him: 'You wrote to me asking whether the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to take women on military expeditions. Indeed, he used to take them; they would treat the wounded and would be given gifts from the war booty. As for the fixed share (sahm), no share was assigned for them.'""

[Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from Hatim.] [Sahih]"
"12919","(12914) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated from Muhammad ibn Zayd who said: 'Umayr, the freed slave of Abu al-Lahm, narrated to me, saying: ""I was present at Khaybar as a slave. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, assign a share for me.' So he gave me a sword and said: 'Hang this sword [around your neck].' And he gave me worn-out goods, but he did not assign me a share.""

[Muslim narrated with this chain another hadith regarding Zakah, and this text is also Sahih according to his conditions.] [Sahih]"
"12920","(12915) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Sa'id and others said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab informed us; Rafi' ibn Salamah ibn Ziyad narrated to me. He said: Hushraj ibn Ziyad narrated to me from his grandmother, Umm Abiha, that she went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the expedition of Khaybar, being the sixth of six women. The news reached the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he sent for us. We came and saw anger in his face. He said: 'With whom did you go out, and with whose permission did you go out?' We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we went out to spin wool and to assist thereby in the cause of Allah. We have with us remedies for the wounded, and we hand over arrows and serve sawiq.' He said: 'Stand [back].' When Allah granted him the conquest of Khaybar, he assigned us a share as he assigned the men. He [the narrator] said: I said to her: 'O grandmother, what was that?' She said: 'Dates.'""

The Sheikh said: Her report of what was given to them indicates that it was a gift (ridkh), and in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas, ""no share was assigned for them"" clarifies that. And Allah knows best. It has been narrated from Makhul and others that shares were assigned for them at Khaybar, but it is disconnected (munqati') and no proof can be established by it. [Da'if]"
"12921","(12916) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu'l-Fadl ibn Ibrahim informed us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Abu Kurayb narrated to us; Abu Usamah narrated to me; Burayd narrated from Abu Burdah from Abu Musa — he mentioned their arrival to Ja'far ibn Abi Talib in Abyssinia. He said: ""We stayed with him until we all arrived together. We met the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he conquered Khaybar. He allotted a share for us — or he said: 'He gave us from it' — and he did not allot anything from it for anyone who was absent from the conquest of Khaybar except those who were present with him, except the companions of our ship with Ja'far and his companions; so he allotted for them along with them."" Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Kurayb.

And if they arrived before the cessation of warfare or before the securing of the booty, he included them in it — and this is in accordance with our position — and if they arrived after that, upon him is the proof. [Sahih. Bukhari 3136. Muslim 3502]"
"12922","(12917) Abu'l-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us; Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us; Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us; Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us; Burayd ibn 'Abdillah narrated from Abu Burdah from Abu Musa, who said: ""We arrived to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) after the conquest of Khaybar, and he allotted a share for us, and he did not allot a share for anyone other than us — meaning, those who had not witnessed the conquest."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Hafs.

Yusuf ibn Musa also narrated it from Hafs, and he said: ""After he had conquered it, by three.""

It is possible that he (ﷺ) only gave them from the share of public interests, or that he included them in the booty with the consent of those who had taken the spoils. It has also been narrated in the story of Ja'far and others through another chain: that he asked his Companions to include them in the shares of Khaybar, and they did so. There is a sound witness for this in the story of the arrival of Abu Hurayrah. [Sahih]"
"12923","(12918) Abu'l-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; 'Abdillah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh informed us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us; Abu Bakr al-Humaydi narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us; al-Zuhri narrated; 'Anbasah ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As narrated from Abu Hurayrah, who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and his Companions at Khaybar after they had conquered it. I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to allot me a share of the booty. One of the Banu Sa'id ibn al-'As said: 'Do not allot him a share, O Messenger of Allah!' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, this is the killer of Ibn Qawqal!' The son of Sa'id said: 'How astonishing — a hair of a goat dangling down to us from the peak of a mountain, reminding me of the killing of a Muslim man whom Allah honoured at my hands and did not humiliate me at his hands!'"" Sufyan said: ""I do not remember whether he said: 'He allotted him a share' or 'He did not allot him a share.'"" Sufyan said: ""I heard Isma'il ibn Umayyah ask al-Zuhri about it while I was present."" [Sahih. Bukhari 2827]"
"12924","(12919) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us; Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us; al-Humaydi narrated to us; Sufyan narrated — and he mentioned it with its chain, similar to it, except that he did not mention the statement of Sufyan. And he added: Sufyan said: ""Al-Sa'idi also narrated it to me from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ).""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from al-Humaydi. The name of al-Sa'idi is 'Amr ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As, and his grandfather is Sa'id ibn 'Amr. Al-Bukhari said: ""And it is mentioned from al-Zubaydi from al-Zuhri."" [Sahih]"
"12925","(12920) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us; Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated from Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi from al-Zuhri that 'Anbasah ibn Sa'id informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating to Sa'id ibn al-'As: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As on an expedition from Madinah towards Najd. Aban ibn Sa'id and his companions arrived to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at Khaybar after he had conquered it, and the girths of their horses were of palm fibre. Aban said: 'Allot for us, O Messenger of Allah.' Abu Hurayrah said: 'I said: Do not allot for them, O Messenger of Allah.' Aban said: 'Are you the one [to speak], O hair of a goat, who has come down to us from the head of a lost mountain?' The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Sit down, O Aban.' And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not allot for them.""

'Abdillah ibn Salim also narrated it from al-Zubaydi, and it is among what Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Dhuhali mentioned from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Zubaydi from 'Amr ibn al-Harith from 'Abdillah. Muhammad ibn Yahya said: ""Ibn 'Uyaynah did not establish its text — meaning — and the hadith is the hadith of al-Zubaydi."" [Sahih. Bukhari 4238]"
"12926","(12921) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu'l-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us; 'Ali ibn Bahr al-Qattan narrated to us; al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us; Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us; al-Zuhri narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab from Abu Hurayrah: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, granted conquest to His Messenger (ﷺ) at Khaybar. Then Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As came to him with his cavalry and asked him to allot a share for him and his companions, but the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not do that."" Abu Hurayrah said: ""The girths of their horses were of palm fibre."" This agrees with the narration of al-Zubaydi in its text but differs from it in its chain. And Allah knows best. Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Dhuhali said: ""The two hadiths are preserved — the hadith of 'Anbasah from the hadith of al-Zubaydi, and the hadith of Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab from the hadith of Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz."" [Sahih]"
"12927","(12922) Abu'l-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us; 'Abdillah ibn Ja'far informed us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us; al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us; Hammad narrated from 'Ali ibn Zayd from 'Ammar ibn Abi 'Ammar from Abu Hurayrah, who said: ""I did not witness any spoils with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) without him allotting a share for me, except Khaybar, for it was exclusively for the people of al-Hudaybiyyah. And Abu Musa and Abu Hurayrah came between al-Hudaybiyyah and Khaybar."" [Da'if]"
"12928","(12923) Abu'l-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated to us; Khuthaym ibn 'Irak narrated from his father from a group of Banu Ghifar, who said: ""Abu Hurayrah arrived in Madinah while the Prophet (ﷺ) had gone out to Khaybar and had appointed a man from Banu Ghifar over Madinah, whose name was Sibaq ibn 'Urfutah. Abu Hurayrah said: 'We found him in the morning prayer. When we finished our prayer, we came to Sibaq ibn 'Urfutah, and he provisioned us with dates until we arrived to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who had conquered Khaybar. He spoke to the Muslims, and they included us in their shares."" Ruh ibn al-Qasim also narrated it from Khuthaym ibn 'Irak with its chain and its meaning. He said: ""He asked the people for permission to allot us a share of the spoils, and they gave him permission, so he allotted for us."" [Da'if]"
"12929","(12924) Abu'l-Hasan ibn Abi al-Ma'ruf al-Isfarayini informed us; Abu 'Amr Isma'il ibn Najayd al-Sulami informed us; Abu 'Abdillah al-Bushanj narrated to us; Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us; Ruh ibn al-Qasim narrated — and he mentioned it.

The narrations regarding his arrival after the conquest of Khaybar are more sound. As for the narration of the one who narrated that he did not allot a share for him, he intended the distribution for those who witnessed it. And it is possible that he included them in their shares with their consent, as in this narration. And Allah knows best. [Da'if]"
"12930","(12925) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu'l-Fadl Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Khamirawayh informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us; 'Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated from Yunus from al-Zuhri, who said: ""It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not allot a share for any absent person in booty that he did not witness, except on the day of Khaybar, when he allotted a share for the absent people of al-Hudaybiyyah, because Allah, the Blessed and Most High, had granted Khaybar to the Muslims from among the people of al-Hudaybiyyah. He said: {'And Allah has promised you many spoils which you will take, and He has hastened this for you.'} So it was for the people of al-Hudaybiyyah — those of them who were present and those who were absent — and for those other people who were present.""

Yunus said: ""And Ibn Shihab said: 'It has reached us — and Allah knows best — that he allotted a share for 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of Badr, and 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) had stayed behind to care for his wife Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who had been afflicted with the measles. Zayd ibn Harithah came as a bearer of glad tidings of the battle of Badr, while 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was at the grave of Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), burying her.'""

Ibn Shihab said: ""And it has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allotted a share for Talhah ibn 'Ubaydillah and Sa'id ibn Zayd, and they were absent in Syria.""

The Shaykh said: ""We have narrated from Ibn Ishaq that none of the people of al-Hudaybiyyah were absent from Khaybar except Jabir ibn 'Abdillah al-Ansari. As for his allotment for 'Uthman ibn 'Affan and others from the spoils of Badr, the evidences have already passed in this book indicating that it was for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to place it wherever Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, willed. The booty became for those who witnessed the battle only after the distribution of Badr. And Allah knows best."" [Da'if]"
"12931","(12926) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated from Shuʿbah, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb al-Aḥmasī, who said: Banū ʿUṭārid raided the district of Baṣrah and were reinforced by ʿAmmār from Kūfah. He set out before the battle and arrived after the engagement. He said: ""We are your partners in the spoils."" A man from Banū ʿUṭārid stood up and said: ""O mutilated slave! Do you wish us to divide our war spoils with you?"" His ear had been severed in the cause of Allah. He said: ""You reproach me with the dearest of my ears—or the best of my ears."" He wrote concerning this matter to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he wrote back: ""The spoils are only for those who witnessed the battle.""

And it has been narrated to us from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, in another account, that he wrote: ""The spoils are only for those who witnessed the battle.""

[Sound. Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr 2791]"
"12932","(12927) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī; Abū Bakr al-Ghassānī informed us from ʬāṭiyyah ibn Qays and Rāshid ibn Saʿd, who both said: The Romans marched against Ḥabīb ibn Muslimah while he was in Armenia. He wrote to Muʿāwiyah requesting reinforcements, and Muʿāwiyah wrote to ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding this. ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to the governor of Iraq, commanding him to reinforce Ḥabīb. He dispatched the people of Iraq to his aid and appointed Salmān ibn Rabīʿah al-Bāhilī as their commander. They set out to relieve Ḥabīb, but did not reach him before he and his companions met the enemy. Allah granted them victory. When Salmān and his companions arrived to Ḥabīb, they asked him to include them in the spoils, saying: ""We have reinforced you."" The people of Shām said: ""You did not witness the fighting; you have no share with us."" Ḥabīb refused to include them, and he and his companions retained the spoils. The people of Shām and the people of Iraq disputed over this until a conflict nearly arose between them. Some of the people of Iraq said: ""If you kill Salmān, we will kill your Ḥabīb. And if you depart towards Ibn ʿAffān, we will depart as well."" Abū Bakr al-Ghassānī said: ""I heard that this was the first enmity that arose between the people of Shām and the people of Iraq."""
"12933","(12928) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; my father narrated to me, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Abū Kurayb narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated from Burayd, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, who said: When the Prophet ﷺ finished with Ḥunayn, he sent Abū ʿĀmir to the army of Awṭās. He encountered Durayd ibn al-Ṣimmah. Durayd was killed, and Allah put his companions to rout. And he mentioned the hadith. Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it from Abū Kurayb.

[Sound. al-Bukhārī 4323. Muslim 2498]"
"12934","(12929) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to me from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon on the year of the Conquest and said: ""O people, whatever alliance existed in the Age of Ignorance, Islam has only added it in strength. There is no alliance in Islam. The Muslims are one hand against others; the nearest among them bears the responsibility of their protection, and the furthest among them responds to their call. Their raiding parties defend those who remain behind."" And he mentioned the hadith.

[Ḥasan. Aḥmad 6681]"
"12935","(12930) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, who said: It reached us that Ḥabīb ibn Muslimah raided the Romans. They captured a man and accused him. He informed them that he was a spy. He said: ""This is the king of the Romans with the people behind this mountain."" He said to his companions: ""Advise me."" Some of them said: ""We think you should remain until the people join you,"" for they were cut off. Others said: ""We think you should return to your main body and not advance against these people, for we have no strength against them."" He said: ""As for me, I give Allah a covenant that I will not break it—I will surely join them."" When the day rose, he found that they had filled the land. He charged, and his companions charged, and the enemy was routed, and they obtained abundant spoils. Then those who had not been present at the fighting arrived and said: ""We are your partners in the spoils."" Those who had witnessed the fighting said: ""You have no share; you were not present at the fighting."" ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, who had been present with Ḥabīb, said: ""You have no share."" He wrote concerning this to Muʿāwiyah, and he wrote back: ""Divide it among all of them."" He said: ""I think Muʿāwiyah had written concerning this to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote accordingly."" And the poet said:

""Indeed, Ḥabīb is a poor provider;
They are neither brave men nor prudent.

And Ibn al-Zubayr is one who departs from fairness,
Nor is he one who is competent in the affairs of people."""
"12936","(12931) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Budayl ibn Mayṣarah, and Khālid, and al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrīt, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq, from a man from Balqayn, who said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in Wādī al-Qurā, and he was presenting a horse. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, with what have you been commanded?"" He said: ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: 'There is no deity but Allah, and I am the Messenger of Allah.' When they say it, they have protected from me their blood and their property, except by right, and their reckoning is with Allah."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, who are these people whom you fight?"" He said: ""These are the Jews, upon whom wrath has been brought, and these are the Christians, who are astray."" I said: ""What do you say about the spoils?"" He said: ""One-fifth is for Allah, and the remaining four-fifths are for the army."" I said: ""Is anyone more entitled to it than another?"" He said: ""No, nor is the arrow you extract from your side more entitled to you than your Muslim brother.""

[Weak]"
"12937","(12932) He said: And Yūsuf narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Budayl ibn Mayṣarah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq, from a man from Balqayn, who said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he mentioned something similar to it.

And Mūsā ibn Dāwūd narrated from Ḥammād ibn Zayd, and he said in the hadith: ""If you are shot with an arrow in your side and you extract it, you are not more entitled to it than your Muslim brother."" In this is an explanation of what has been narrated to us, and the hadith of ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit has already passed, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he took, on the day of Ḥunayn—or he said, on the day of Khaybar—a tuft of hair from the side of a camel and said: ""O people, it is not lawful for me from what Allah has restored to you anything the size of this, except the one-fifth, and the one-fifth is returned to you.""

[Weak]"
"12938","(12933) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to me from his father, from his grandfather, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Ḥunayn. When he took from the Hawāzin what he took of their wealth and their captives, the delegation of the Hawāzin caught up with him at al-Jiʿrānah, and they had embraced Islam. They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we have families and kinship ties, and affliction has befallen us that is not hidden from you. Bestow upon us; may Allah bestow upon you."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Are your women and children more beloved to you, or your wealth?"" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you have given us a choice between our honour and our wealth—our children and our women are more beloved to us."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""As for what belongs to me and to Banī al-Muṭṭalib, it is for you. When I have led the people in prayer, stand and say: 'We seek the intercession of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the Muslims, and of the Muslims with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, concerning our children and our women.' I will give you then and will ask on your behalf."" When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led the people in the Ẓuhr prayer, they stood and said what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""As for what belongs to me and to Banī al-Muṭṭalib, it is for you."" The Muhājirūn said: ""And what is ours belongs to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" And the Anṣār said: ""And what is ours belongs to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" Al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis said: ""As for me and Banū Tamīm, no."" And al-ʿAbbās ibn Mirdās said: ""As for me and Banū Sulaym, no."" Banū Sulaym said: ""Rather, what is ours belongs to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" And ʿUyaynah ibn Badr said: ""As for me and Banū Fazārah, no."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever among you withholds his right, for every person there are six camels from the first portion of the restored wealth. Return to the people their women and their children."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted, and the people followed him, saying: ""O Messenger of Allah, divide our restored wealth among us,"" until they pressed him against a tree, and his cloak was pulled from him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O people, return my cloak to me. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, if you had the number of the trees of Tihāmah in camels, I would divide them among you, and then you would not find me miserly, cowardly, or untruthful."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood beside a camel and took a tuft of hair from its hump, placed it between his two fingers, and said: ""O people, by Allah, I have nothing from your restored wealth, nor even this tuft, except the one-fifth, and the one-fifth is returned to you. Return the thread and the needle, for embezzlement is a disgrace, a fire, and a shame for its people on the Day of Resurrection."" A man from the Anṣār came with a ball of hair threads and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I took this to stitch the saddle-cloth of my camel that had been galled."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""As for my share from it, it is for you."" The man said: ""When the matter has reached this point, I have no need of it,"" and he threw it from his hand.

[Ḥasan. Aḥmad 2/184]"
"12939","(12934) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Bazzāz in Baghdād narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham al-Dayrāqūlī narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed me; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that some of the Anṣār said: ""O Messenger of Allah, concerning what Allah has bestowed upon His Messenger from the wealth of the Hawāzin."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began giving men of Quraysh a hundred camels each. They said: ""May Allah forgive the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—he gives Quraysh and leaves us, while our swords drip with their blood."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was told of their words, so he sent for the Anṣār and gathered them in a leather tent, not summoning anyone else with them. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them, he said: ""What is this report that has reached me about you?"" Their scholars said: ""As for our men of judgment, they said nothing, but some of us who are young in years said: 'May Allah forgive the Messenger of Allah—he gives Quraysh and leaves us, while our swords drip with their blood.'"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I give men who are newly come to disbelief—I seek to reconcile their hearts. Are you not pleased that the people go away with wealth and you return to your homes with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, what you return with is better than what they return with."" They said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah, we are pleased."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""You will find, after me, severe preference shown to others over you. Be patient until you meet Allah and His Messenger at the Ḥawḍ."" Anas said: ""Then we shall be patient.""

al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim recorded it through other chains from al-Zuhrī. And he said in the hadith: ""I am at the Ḥawḍ."" And likewise Bisr ibn Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated from his father: ""I am at the Ḥawḍ.""

[Sound. al-Bukhārī 4331. Muslim 1059]"
"12940","(12935) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Khālid ibn Khalī narrated to us; Bisr ibn Shuʿayb narrated it with its chain, similarly, except that he said in the hadith: ""By Allah, what you return with is better than what they return with."" And he said at its end: Anas ibn Mālik said: ""But we were not patient.""

[Sound. Preceded before it]"
"12941","(12936) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Abū Muslim informed us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Faqīh informed me: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us: Shuʿbah narrated, from Abū al-Tayyāḥ, who said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say: On the day of the Conquest, the Ansār said, ""By Allah, this is indeed astonishing — our swords are dripping with the blood of Quraysh, yet our war spoils are being divided among them."" This reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he sent for the Ansār specifically and said, ""What is this that has reached me about you?"" — and they were not people who lie. They said, ""It is what has reached you."" He said, ""Are you not pleased that the people go away with the livestock and you go away with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to your homes?"" Then he said, ""If the people were to take a valley or a ravine, I would take the valley of the Ansār."" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. In the narration of Abū al-Ḥasan: ""On the day of Ḥunayn,"" and the rest carries the same meaning.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]

Al-Shāfiʿī said: One may say regarding the khums of the war spoils, once it is distinguished from it, ""We acquired this as spoils,"" meaning that the cause of ownership of it rests with them, and this is found in the speech of the people. Upon this basis the Ansār spoke. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said regarding the khums: ""It is for me, then it is returned among you."" When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave it to those who were distant, the Ansār — who were his closest allies — objected to that.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: And some of our companions informed us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet ﷺ gave al-Aqraʿ and his companions from the khums of the khums. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3146]"
"12942","(12937) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū al-Ṭāhir informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim informed us, that Ayyūb informed him, that Nāfiʿ informed him, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar informed him: That ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāllah ﷺ, and he was at al-Jiʿrānah after returning from al-Jiʿrānah. He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I made a vow during the Jāhiliyyah to observe iʿtikāf for one day in the Sacred Mosque. What do you think?"" He said, ""Go and observe iʿtikāf for one day."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given him a slave-girl from the khums. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ freed the captives of the people, ʿUmar said, ""O ʿAbd Allāh, go to that slave-girl and set her free.""

It was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir, and al-Bukhārī used it as a supporting witness. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1656]"
"12943","(12938) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. He said: I heard Abū al-Ḥasan Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Shaʿrānī say: I heard my grandfather say: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Shaybah say: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah said: Allah opened the discourse regarding the fayʾ and the war spoils by mentioning His own Self, because it is the most noble of earnings, and everything that is honoured and exalted is attributed to Him. He did not attribute the ṣadaqah to His own Self, because it is the refuse of people. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Tadhkirat al-Ḥuffāẓ 654]"
"12944","(12939) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī informed us: Sufyān narrated, from Qays ibn Muslim, who said: I asked al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad about the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""And know that whatever you obtain as spoils, then indeed one-fifth is for Allah and for the Messenger"" (al-Anfāl 8:41). He said: ""This is the opening of the speech of Allah — whatever is in this world and the Hereafter."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12945","(12940) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us: Jarīr narrated, from Mūsā ibn Abī ʿĀʾishah, who said: I asked Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār. I said: ""How much of the khums was for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" He said: ""One-fifth of the khums."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12946","(12941) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Hushaym informed us: Mughīrah narrated, regarding the words of Allah: ""And know that whatever you obtain as spoils, then indeed one-fifth is for Allah and for the Messenger"" (al-Anfāl 8:41), from Ibrāhīm, who said: ""The khums is divided into five portions. The portion of Allah and the Messenger is one, and what remains besides that is divided among the others."" And it has been narrated to us from Mujāhid and Qatādah likewise. And from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: ""The portion of Allah and His Messenger is one."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12947","(12942) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr al-Mashshāṭ both informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated, from ʿAbd al-Malik, from ʿAṭāʾ, regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""And know that whatever you obtain as spoils, then indeed one-fifth is for Allah and for the Messenger"" (al-Anfāl 8:41). He said: ""The khums of Allah and His Messenger is one. The Prophet ﷺ would do with it as he willed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12948","(12943) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah informed us: al-Walīd informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ informed us that he heard Abū Salām al-Aswad say: I heard ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer beside a camel from the war spoils. When he gave the salām, he took a hair from the side of the camel and then said: ""It is not lawful for me from your war spoils except the like of this, except the khums, and the khums is returned among you.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has passed on — may my father and mother be his ransom, and may Allah and His angels send blessings upon him. The scholars among us differed regarding his share (sahm). Among them are those who said: It is returned to the shares that Allah mentioned along with it. Among them are those who said: The imām places it wherever he sees fit, by way of ijtihād for Islam and its people. Among them are those who said: He places it in weapons and armour. And what I prefer is that the imām place it in every matter by which Islam and its people are fortified — whether it be securing a frontier, preparing weapons and armour, or giving it to the people of trial in Islam as a grant (nafl), during war or otherwise, in preparation for the enhancement of the strength of Islam and its people, in accordance with what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did regarding it. For the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave to the muʾallafah qulūbuhum, granted nafl during war, and gave, in the year of Khaybar, a group of his companions from among the Muhājirūn and the Ansār — people of need and distinction — and most of them were people of poverty. We consider all of that, and Allah knows best, to be from his share.

The Sheikh said: As for his giving to the muʾallafah qulūbuhum — [Ḥasan li-ghayrih]"
"12949","(12944) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ informed us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah informed us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us: Wuhaib informed us: ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā narrated, from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd ibn ʿĀṣim, who said: When Allah bestowed upon His Messenger the spoils on the day of Ḥunayn, he distributed among the people among the muʾallafah qulūbuhum, and he did not distribute — or did not give — the Ansār anything. It was as though he felt displeasure that they did not receive what the others received, or as though they felt displeasure that they did not receive what the people received. So he addressed them and said: ""O company of the Ansār, did I not find you astray and Allah guided you through me, and you were divided and Allah united you through me, and you were poor and Allah enriched you through me?"" Every time he said something, they said, ""Allah and His Messenger are most bountiful."" He said, ""What prevents you from responding?"" They said, ""Allah and His Messenger are most bountiful."" He said, ""If you wished, you could have said, 'You came to us with such and such.' Are you not pleased that the people go away with sheep and camels, and you go away with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to your homes? Were it not for the Hijrah, I would be a man from the Ansār. If the people were to take a valley or a ravine, I would take the valley of the Ansār and its ravine. The Ansār are an inner garment and the people are an outer garment. You will meet after me a time of selfishness (atharah), so be patient until you meet me at the Ḥawḍ.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4336]"
"12950","(12945) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: al-Thawrī narrated, from his father, from Ibn Abī Nuʿm, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — sent to the Prophet ﷺ from Yemen a quantity of gold in its raw earth. The Prophet ﷺ divided it among Zayd al-Ṭāʾī, then one of the Banī Nabhān, and al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis al-Ḥanẓalī, then one of the Banī Mujāshiʿ, and ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn, and ʿAlqamah ibn ʿUlāthah al-ʿĀmirī, then one of the Banī Kalāb. Quraysh became angry and said, ""He gives to the chieftains of Najd and leaves us out."" He said, ""I am only reconciling their hearts (muʾallafah qulūbuhum)."" A man came — sunken-eyed, protruding-browed, thick-cheeked, with a thick beard and a shaved head — and said, ""Fear Allah, O Muḥammad!"" The Prophet ﷺ said, ""Who will obey Allah if I disobey Him? Does He entrust me over the people of the earth, yet you do not trust me?"" A man from the people asked permission to kill him. He thought it was Khālid ibn al-Walīd. He prevented him. When the man turned to leave, the Prophet ﷺ said: ""From the offspring of this man will be a people who recite the Qurʾān, but it will not go beyond their throats. They will pass through Islam as an arrow passes through its game. They will kill the people of Islam and leave the people of idols. If I encounter them, I will kill them as the people of ʿĀd were killed.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Saʿīd ibn Masrūq, the father of al-Thawrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3344]"
"12951","(12946) As for the nafal (additional grant), it is narrated in what Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Walīd informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Mushʿir and ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched a sariyyah (military expedition) towards Najd, and I went out with it. We obtained camels and sheep, and our share reached twelve camels. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted us an additional camel each.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shayyah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from another route, as has preceded.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1749]"
"12952","(12947) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥurr narrated to us, al-Ḥakam narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to grant nafal (additional shares) before the verse regarding the spoils of war was revealed. When it was revealed, he discontinued the nafal that he used to grant, and that (portion) became part of the khums al-khums (one-fifth of the one-fifth), which is the share of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and the share of the Prophet ﷺ.

And it has been narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab that he said: The people used to be given the nafal from the khums.

[Ḥasan li-ghayrih]"
"12953","(12948) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar, from Ayyūb, from Ibn Sīrīn: that Anas ibn Mālik was with ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Bakrah in a military expedition in which they went out and obtained captives. ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Bakrah intended to give Anas a captive before the division (of the spoils). Anas said: ""No, but divide (the spoils) first, then give me from the khums."" And he mentioned the hadith.

As for his (the Prophet's) granting on the day of Khaybar, it is in what follows.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12954","(12949) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Mahrī informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Usāmah ibn Zayd al-Laythī narrated from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: When Khaybar was conquered, the Jews asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to allow them to remain on the condition that they would work for half of what was produced from it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I shall allow you to remain on that for as long as we wish."" So they remained on that condition, and the fruits were divided according to the shares from half of Khaybar, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would take the khums. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give each of his wives from the khums one hundred wasq of dates and twenty wasq of barley. When ʿUmar intended to expel the Jews, he sent a message to the wives of the Prophet ﷺ and said to them: ""Whoever among you wishes, I shall give her palm trees estimated at one hundred wasq, so that she will have their origin, their land, and their water, and from the crops a cultivated area estimated at twenty wasq — we shall do so. And whoever among you wishes, we shall set aside for her what she has in the khums as it is — we shall do so.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 3008]"
"12955","(12950) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, from a son of Muḥammad ibn Salamah, from one of his family members who had met (the Companions), and from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Ḥazm. They both mentioned the division of Khaybar and said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided his khums among his relatives, his wives, and among men and women of the Muslims, giving them from it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ allocated for his daughter Fāṭimah, peace be upon her, two hundred wasqs; for ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, one hundred wasqs; for Usāmah ibn Zayd, two hundred wasqs, of which fifty wasqs were of pitless dates; for ʿĪsā ibn Nuqaym, two hundred wasqs; and for Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, two hundred wasqs. They both mentioned a group of men and women to whom he allocated from it.

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"12956","(12951) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, that he said: I and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, walked to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you gave Banū al-Muṭṭalib and left us, while we and they are of the same status."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Banū al-Muṭṭalib and Banū Hāshim are but one thing.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr and Ibn Yūsuf.

Al-Bukhārī said: And al-Layth said — Yūnus narrated to me — and he added, saying: ""And the Prophet ﷺ did not allocate anything to Banū ʿAbd Shams or to Banū Nawfal.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 3140]"
"12957","(12952) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed him, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from Yūnus, from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, that Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim informed him: that he and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to speak to him when he had divided the fayʾ (war gains) of Khaybar between Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you have divided for our brothers, Banū al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Manāf, and have not given us anything, while our kinship is the same as their kinship."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: ""Hāshim and al-Muṭṭalib are but one thing.""

And Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not allocate anything from that khums to Banū ʿAbd Shams or to Banū Nawfal, just as he allocated to Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in another place in the book from Ibn Bukayr. And likewise ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated it from Yūnus.

Al-Bukhārī said: And Ibn Isḥāq said, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it. Al-Bukhārī]"
"12958","(12953) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq. Al-Zuhrī informed me, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided the share of dhī al-qurbā (the near relatives) of Khaybar between Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib, I and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, went (to him). I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, these are your brothers, Banū Hāshim — we do not deny their excellence because of the position that Allah has given you among them. But what do you say about our brothers from Banū al-Muṭṭalib? You have given them and left us, while we and they are of the same status with you."" He said: ""They did not abandon us in the Jāhiliyyah nor in Islam. Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib are but one thing."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intertwined his fingers, one hand into the other.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it]"
"12959","(12954) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Muṭrif ibn Māzin narrated from Maʿmib ibn Rāshid, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Muḥammad ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim informed me from his father, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided the share of dhī al-qurbā between Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib, I came to him along with ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him. And he mentioned the hadith with the same meaning as the hadith of Ibn Isḥāq, except that he did not mention his statement: ""They did not abandon us in the Jāhiliyyah nor in Islam."" And he said: ""Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib are but one thing."" Thus he said, and he intertwined his fingers. Then al-Shāfiʿī mentioned the hadith of Yūnus and Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from Ibn Shihāb, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Jubayr. Al-Shāfiʿī said: I mentioned that to Muṭrif ibn Māzin, and he said: Maʿmar narrated it to us as you have described. Perhaps Ibn Shihāb narrated it from both of them together. The Shaykh said: And Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl narrated it from al-Zuhrī in a similar manner.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it]"
"12960","(12955) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mujammiʿ al-Anṣārī, from al-Zuhrī, from Muḥammad ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, from his father, who said: I walked, along with so-and-so, to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you have given Banū al-Muṭṭalib and left us, while we and they are of the same status with you."" He ﷺ said: ""Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib are but one thing.""

Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl and Muṭrif ibn Māzin are both weak (in narration). And in the narration of the group from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Jubayr, there is sufficiency.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12961","(12956) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān narrated to me.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥbūb ibn Faḍayl al-Tājir and Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī both said: Abū al-Muwajjih Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Jabalah narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed us, from al-Zuhrī, who said: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn informed me that Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī informed him that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said:

""I had a she-camel from my share of the war-booty on the day of Badr, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had given me a she-camel from the khums on that day. When I resolved to build a home with Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), I made an appointment with a goldsmith from Banī Qaynuqāʿ that he should accompany me so that we could bring some idhkhir grass, which I intended to sell to the goldsmiths and use the proceeds for my wedding feast. While I was gathering equipment for my two she-camel — saddles, sacks, ropes, and hobbles — and the two she-camels were resting beside the room of a man from the Anṣār, I returned after I had gathered what I had gathered, and behold, the two she-camels had had their humps cut open, their bellies slit, and their livers taken out. I could not hold back my tears when I saw that sight of them. I said: 'Who did this?' They said: 'Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib did it, and he is in this house, in a drinking gathering of the Anṣār. A singing girl was singing to him and his companions.' She sang in her song:

'O Ḥamzah, to the noble she-camels, tethered in the courtyard!'

So Ḥamzah rose to his sword, cut off their humps, slit their bellies, and took from their livers.""

He (ʿAlī) said: ""I set out until I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah was with him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recognized in my face what had befallen me. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'What is the matter?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have never seen the like of this day. Ḥamzah has seized my two she-camels, cut off their humps, and slit their bellies, and there he is, in a drinking gathering.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called for his cloak, put it on, and set out walking. I followed him, and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah followed him, until he came to the house in which Ḥamzah was. He sought permission, and they gave him permission. They were a drinking party. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) began to rebuke Ḥamzah for what he had done. Ḥamzah was intoxicated, his eyes red. Ḥamzah looked at the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then raised his gaze and looked at his knees, then raised his gaze and looked at his navel, then raised his gaze and looked at his face. Then Ḥamzah said: 'You are nothing but slaves to my father!' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) realized that he was intoxicated. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned back on his heels, retreating, and we went out with him.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān, and Muslim narrated it from Ibn Qahzād from ʿAbdān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2089]"
"12962","(12957) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qāḍī in Marw narrated to us: al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Suwayd ibn Manjūf narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) to Khālid ibn al-Walīd (may Allah be pleased with him) to collect the khums. He took from it a slave-girl. Khālid said to Buraydah: ""Do you not see what this one is doing?"" He (Buraydah) said: ""And I used to harbour hatred towards ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), so I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said: 'O Buraydah, do you hate ʿAlī?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then love him, for he has a greater right to the khums than that.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bindār, from Rūḥ ibn ʻUbādah. This is what has reached us from our master al-Muṣṭafā (ﷺ) regarding the share of dhī al-qurbā. As for the two Imams, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), the narrations differ concerning them in that regard. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4350]"
"12963","(12958) In what Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Mayṣarah narrated to us. Both said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak, from Yūnus ibn Yazīd, from al-Zuhrī, who said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib informed me, who said: Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim informed me:

""That he and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with them both) came to speak to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about what he had distributed from the khums among Banī Hāshim and Banī al-Muṭṭalib. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you have distributed to our brothers, Banī al-Muṭṭalib, and given us nothing, yet our kinship and theirs is the same.' The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Banī Hāshim and Banī al-Muṭṭalib are one and the same.'""

Jubayr said: ""He did not distribute anything from that khums to Banī ʿAbd Shams or Banī Nawfal, as he had distributed to Banī Hāshim and Banī al-Muṭṭalib."" He said: ""And Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to distribute the khums in a manner similar to the distribution of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), except that he did not give the relatives of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) what the Prophet (ﷺ) used to give them from it. And ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to give them, and ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) after him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12964","(12959) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah, who said:

""The people differed concerning these two shares after the death of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Some said: 'The share of dhī al-qurbā is for the kin of the Prophet (ﷺ).' Others said: 'It is for the kin of the Caliph.' And others said: 'The share of the Prophet (ﷺ) belongs to the Caliph after him.' Their opinion converged upon placing these two shares in horses and equipment in the cause of Allah. And so it remained during the caliphates of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12965","(12960) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Zurʿah narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Khālid narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us. He said: ""I said to Abū Jaʿfar —""

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: ʿĀrim ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said:

""I asked Abū Jaʿfar, meaning al-Bāqir: 'How did ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) deal with the share of dhī al-qurbā?' He said: 'He followed the way of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both).' I said: 'But how, when you say what you say?' He said: 'By Allah, his family did not act except according to his opinion, but he disliked that any disagreement with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) should be attributed to him.'""

In the narration of Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī, he said: ""By Allah, his household did not act except according to his opinion, but he used to dislike that disagreement with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) should be claimed against him.""

Sufyān al-Thawrī and Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah also narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) weakened this narration, noting that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) held views differing from those of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding not giving the slaves any share in the distribution, and differing from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding equality among people and the sale of the mothers of children (ummahāt al-awlād). He also opposed Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the grandfather. His statement ""He followed the way of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar"" is a general expression that may carry multiple meanings.

He (al-Shāfiʿī) said: ""And we have been informed from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, that al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, Ibn ʿAbbās, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar (may Allah be pleased with them) asked ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) for their share from the khums. He said: 'It is your right, but I am engaged in fighting Muʿāwiyah. If you wish, you may forgo your right from it.'""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""I mentioned this ḥadīth to ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad, and he said: 'He spoke the truth; thus Jaʿfar used to narrate it. What he narrated to you from his father from his grandfather —' I said: 'No.' He said: 'I do not think it is otherwise than from his grandfather.' And Jaʿfar is more reliable and more knowledgeable of his father's ḥadīth than Ibn Isḥāq.""

The Shaykh said: ""Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī's narration from Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them) is mursal, and likewise the narration of al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah is mursal. As for the narration of Yūnus from al-Zuhrī, I do not yet know whether the statement at the end of it belongs to Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, in which case it would be mawṣūl, or to Ibn al-Musayyib or al-Zuhrī, in which case it would be mursal.""

The Shaykh said: ""Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhalī narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Layth ibn Saʿd, from Yūnus, and he distinguished the action of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), making it the statement of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī, so it is then munqaṭiʿ. And a similar view to ours has been narrated from Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them)."" [Ḥasan]"
"12966","(12961) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dawrī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAẓīm narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Rāzī narrated to us, from Muṭarrif, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, who said:

""I heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed me over the khums of the khums, and I placed it in its proper places during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), during the lifetime of Abū Bakr, and during the lifetime of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both).'""

Al-Rūdhbārī added in his ḥadīth: ""Then wealth was brought, and he called for me and said: 'Take it.' I said: 'I do not want it.' He said: 'Take it, for you are more entitled to it.' I said: 'We have no need of it.' So he placed it in the bayt al-māl (public treasury).""

[Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 1/84]"
"12967","(12962) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad, from the original of his book, narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated to us: Hāshim ibn Burayd narrated to us: Ḥusayn ibn Maymūn narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, who said:

""I heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: 'I, al-ʿAbbās, Fāṭimah, and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (may Allah be pleased with him) gathered before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Al-ʿAbbās asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying: ""O Messenger of Allah, my age has advanced, my bones have become thin, and my knees are a burden. If you see fit to command for me such-and-such and such-and-sawāʾ of food, then do so."" So he commanded that for him. Then Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am from you in the position you know. If you see fit to command for me as you have commanded for your uncle, then do so."" So he did that for her. Then Zayd ibn Ḥārithah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you had given me land in which I lived, and then you took it back from me. If you see fit to return it to me, then do so."" So he did that.'""

He (ʿAlī) said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, if you see fit to entrust to me our right from the khums in the Book of Allah, so that I may distribute it during your lifetime, lest anyone dispute with me after you, then do so.' So he did that. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned to al-ʿAbbās and said: 'O Abū al-Faḍl, will you not ask me what your nephew asked me?' He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, my request has reached the same point as what you have given.'""

He (ʿAlī) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entrusted it to me, and I distributed it during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Then Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) entrusted it to me, and I distributed it during the lifetime of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) entrusted it to me, and I distributed it during the lifetime of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), until the last year of ʿUmar's life (may Allah be pleased with him). A great deal of wealth came to him, and he set aside our right. Then he sent to me and said: 'This is your wealth; take it and distribute it wherever you used to distribute it.' I said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, we are independent of it this year, and the Muslims are in need of it.' So he returned it to the Muslims that year. Then no one called us to it after ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) until I assumed this position of mine. I met al-ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) after I had left ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: 'O ʿAlī, you have deprived us this morning of something that will never be returned to us until the Day of Resurrection.' And he was a man of great intelligence.""

Abū ʿAbdillāh said: ""Its narrators are among the trustworthy Kūfans."" The Shaykh said: ""Abū Dāwūd extracted it in the Sunan with part of its meaning in an abbreviated form, from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12968","(12963) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm narrated to us, from Maṭar al-Warrāq and another man whom he did not name, both from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, who said:

""I met ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) at the stones of az-Zayt, and I said to him: 'May my father and mother be your ransom, what did Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) do regarding your right, the People of the Household, from the khums?' ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'As for Abū Bakr (may Allah have mercy on him), there were no fifths (akhmās) during his time, and what there was, he gave us in full. As for ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), he kept giving us until the wealth of Sūs and al-Ahwāz — or he said al-Ahwāz, or he said Fāris — came to him.' Al-Shāfiʿī said: 'I am doubtful.' He said: ""In the narration of Maṭar or the other, he said: 'He said: ""The Muslims are in need. If you wish, you may forgo your right, and we will place it in the need of the Muslims until wealth comes to us, and I will give you your right from it.""' Al-ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) said to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): 'Do not give it up regarding our right.' I said to him: 'O Abū al-Faḍl, are we not the most deserving of those who respond to the Commander of the Faithful and relieve the need of the Muslims?' Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) passed away before wealth came to him by which he could fulfil it for us.""

Al-Ḥakam said in the narration of Maṭar and the other: ""ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'You have a right, but my knowledge does not reach, when it is abundant, that all of it should be for you. If you wish, I will give you from it according to what I see as your due.' But we refused except all of it, and he refused to give us all of it.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said regarding what he did not hear from Abū Zakariyyā: ""Al-Zuhrī narrated it from Ibn Hurmuz, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), with a meaning close to this, and he mentioned it in the older version of the ḥadīth of Yūnus from al-Zuhrī."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"12969","(12964) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: ʿAnbasah narrated to us: Yūnus narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Yazīd ibn Hurmuz informed me:

""That Najdah al-Ḥarūrī, when he performed Ḥajj during the tribulation of Ibn al-Zubayr, sent to Ibn ʿAbbās asking him about the share of dhī al-qurbā, and saying: 'What is your view?' Ibn ʿAbbās said: 'It is for the kin of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) distributed it to them. And ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) presented to us an offer from it that we considered to be less than our due, so we returned it to him and refused to accept it.'""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Rūdhbārī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12970","(12965) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Ṭayyib Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Zāhid, from the original of his book, narrated to us: Sahl ibn ʿAmmār al-ʿAtakī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, Abū Jaʿfar — I think he said — and al-Zuhrī, from Yazīd, meaning Ibn Hurmuz, who said:

""Najdah, meaning al-Ḥarūrī, wrote to Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) asking him about the share of dhawī al-qurbā: 'For whom is it?' He said: 'You wrote to me asking me about the share of dhawī al-qurbā, for whom is it? It is for us. And ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to invite us to marry from it our unmarried women, to provide from it servants for our dependents, and to pay from it the debts of our debtors. But we refused except that he should hand it over to us entirely, and he refused to do that. So we left it.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"12971","(12966) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us; he informed us that Abū Ḥāmid — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl — narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī narrated to us. Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Yazīd ibn Hurmaz, who said:

Najdah wrote to Ibn ʿAbbās, asking him about the dhawī al-qurbā (kinsfolk) — who they are — and he asked him about the slave and the woman who are present at the battle: do they have any share in the spoils? And he wrote asking about the killing of children. He (Ibn ʿAbbās) said: ""Write, O Yazīd — were it not that I would fall into foolishness, I would not have written to him. You asked about the dhawī al-qurbā — who they are. We believed that we ourselves were they, but our people rejected that. And you asked about the slave and the woman who are present at the spoils — they have nothing, except that they may be given a gift. And you asked about the children — the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not kill them, and you shall not kill them, unless you know of them what the companion of Mūsā knew of the boy. And you asked about the orphan — when does his orphanhood end? His orphanhood ends when sound judgment (rushd) is discerned in him.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar and others, from Sufyān.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: It is possible that Ibn ʿAbbās, by his statement ""our people rejected that,"" meant other than the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) — namely Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah and his family.

---"
"12972","(12967) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us. Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us. Ibn Abī Masarrat narrated to us. Al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me. Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh ibn Naṣr narrated to us. Bishr ibn Mūsā al-Asadī narrated to us. Al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us. Sufyān narrated to us. ʿAmr ibn Dīnār and Maʿmar narrated from Ibn Shihāb, that he heard Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān say:

I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāẓ (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""The wealth of Banī al-Naḍīr was among that which Allah bestowed upon His Messenger, over which the Muslims had mobilized neither horses nor camels. Thus it belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to spend from it on his household for the maintenance of a year, and whatever remained he placed in weapons and mounts as provisions in the cause of Allah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, both from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2904]

---"
"12973","(12968) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us. Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us. Al-Shāfiʿī informed us, saying: I heard Ibn ʿUyaynah narrate from al-Zuhrī, mentioning it with its meaning. He added:

""Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, and Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) took charge of it, managing it as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had managed it. Then I took charge of it, managing it as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) had managed it.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: Sufyān said to me: ""I did not hear it from al-Zuhrī, but ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed me from al-Zuhrī."" The rest of the aḥādīth on this topic have already been mentioned in the first part. [Ṣaḥīḥ]

---"
"12974","(12969) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd ibn Ibrāhīm al-Asadī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Hamadhān narrated to us. Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dīzīl narrated to us. Abū al-Yamān al-Ḥakam ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us. Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, who said:

When fayʾ (spoils of war) came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he would distribute it on the same day. He would give the married man two shares and the unmarried man one share. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 6/25]

---"
"12975","(12970) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Ibn al-Muṣaffā narrated to us. Abū al-Mughīrah narrated from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr, mentioning something similar to it, except that he said:

""He gave the unmarried man one share."" He added: ""We were called, and I was called before ʿAmmār. I was called and given two shares, as I had a family. Then ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir was called after me and was given one share."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded it]

---"
"12976","(12971) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us. ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us. Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us. Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn ʿUfayr al-Miṣrī narrated to us. Ibn Lahīʿah narrated that Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to him that Abū al-Khayr narrated to him that ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Marwān said to Kurayb ibn Abrahah: ""Were you present with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāẓ (may Allah be pleased with him) at al-Jābiyah?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then who will inform us about it?"" Kurayb said: ""If you send for Sufyān ibn Wahb al-Khawlānī, he will narrate to you about it."" So he sent for him and said: ""Narrate to me about the sermon of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāẓ (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of al-Jābiyah."" Sufyān said:

""When the fayʾ had been gathered, the commanders of the military detachments sent word to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāẓ (may Allah be pleased with him) to come in person. So he came, praised Allah, and extolled Him. Then he said: 'To proceed: Indeed, we shall divide this wealth among those upon whom Allah has bestowed it with justice, except for these two tribes — Lahm and Judhām — for they have no right in it.' Abū Ḥudayrah al-Ajdaʿī stood up and said: 'We adjure you by Allah, O ʿUmar, regarding justice!' ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Justice is what I intend — that I should treat a people who spent their provisions, saddled their mounts, and traversed the lands the same as a people who remained settled in their own lands. And if emigration had been to Ṣanʿāʾ or ʿAdan, not one of Lahm or Judhām would have emigrated to it.' Abū Ḥudayrah stood up and said: 'Allah placed us in His land wherever He willed, and He directed emigration toward our land. We accepted it and supported it. Does that, O ʿUmar, nullify our right?' Then he said: 'You shall have your right along with the Muslims.' Then he divided it. A man received half a dīnār; if his wife was with him, he was given a dīnār. Then he called Ibn Qāṭūrā, the owner of the land, and said: 'Tell me: what suffices a man for sustenance in a month and in a day?' He brought a mudd and a qisṭ (measure). He said: 'These two mudd suffice for a month, and a qisṭ of oil and a qisṭ of vinegar suffice for a day.' ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered that two mudd of wheat be ground, then kneaded, then baked, then accompanied by two qisṭ of oil. He seated thirty men upon it, and it was sufficient for their nourishment. Then ʿUmar took the mudd in his right hand and the qisṭ in his left hand and said: 'O Allah, I do not permit anyone after me to reduce these two. O Allah, whoever reduces them, then reduce his lifespan.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]

---"
"12977","(12972) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Al-Nufaylī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAṭāʾ, from Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān, who said:

ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāẓ (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned the fayʾ one day and said: ""I am not more entitled to this fayʾ than any of you, and none of us is more entitled to it than another, except that we are according to our stations from the Book of Allah and the division of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — a man and his precedence, a man and his contribution, a man and his dependents, and a man and his need."" [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 1/42]

---"
"12978","(12973) Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Iṣbahānī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us. Al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us. Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us. Wakīʿ narrated to us. Sufyān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAmr ibn Salamah al-Hamdānī, from his father, from ʿUbaydah al-Salmānī, who said:

ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""How much do you think suffices a man from his stipend?"" I said: ""Such and such."" He said: ""If I remain alive, I shall make a man's stipend four thousand: one thousand for his weapons, one thousand for his expenses, one thousand to leave behind for his family, and one thousand for — I think he said — his horse."" [Ḥasan. Ibn Abī Shaybah 32872]

---"
"12979","(12974) Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī informed us. Abū ʿAmr informed us. Al-Ḥasan narrated to us. Abū Bakr narrated to us. Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from Ḥasan, from Simāk, from ʿIyāḍ al-Ashʿarī, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to provide stipends for male slaves, female slaves, and horses. [Ḍaʿīf]

---"
"12980","(12975) He said: And Abū Bakr narrated to us. Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to allocate a stipend for the infant when it cried (i.e., at birth). [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
"12981","(12976) He said: And Abu Bakr narrated to us — Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us from Abdullah ibn Sharik from Bishr ibn Ghalib, who said: Ibn al-Zubayr asked Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, about the newborn child. He said: ""When it cries out (upon birth), its stipend and provision become obligatory."" [Da'if]"
"12982","(12977) He said: And Abu Bakr narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Shu'ayb, or he said Ibn Abi Shu'ayb, narrated to us from Umm al-'Ala': that her father took her to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and he assigned her a stipend from the allowances while she was still young. And Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Is the child who eats food and bites upon a piece of bread more deserving of this stipend than the newborn who merely suckles the breast?""

These narrations, along with all that has been reported on this subject, are understood to mean that he (Ali) used to assign to each man a stipend sufficient for his own maintenance and that of his family, his children, his slave, and his mount. And Allah knows best. [Da'if; Ibn Abi Shaybah 32894]"
"12983","(12978) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Sufyan narrated to us from 'Amr ibn Dinar from al-Zuhri from Malik ibn Aws, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""There is no one who does not have a right in this wealth — whether I give it to him or withhold it from him — except what your right hands possess (i.e., slaves).""

This is the well-known position reported from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. [Sahih; narrated by al-Shafi'i in al-Umm 15614]"
"12984","(12979) And Ahmad ibn Ali al-Isfahani informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn Hamdan informed us; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us from 'Amr from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad from Makhlad al-Ghifari: that three slaves witnessed the Battle of Badr, and 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to give each one of them every year three thousand, three thousand.

This may be understood to mean that he singled them out for this on account of their nobility in having witnessed Badr. It is also possible that he gave them this after they had been manumitted. And Allah knows best.

Ibn al-Mubarak narrated it from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah with its chain of narration, adding therein: ""from Ghifar, together with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him."" [Da'if]"
"12985","(12980) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us; Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, and he mentioned it. Sufyan ibn Uyaynah said: ""I think it was after they had been manumitted."" [Da'if; see the preceding narration]"
"12986","(12981) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us; Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us from al-Qasim ibn 'Abbas from Abdullah ibn Niyar from 'Urwah from 'Aishah: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was brought a gazelle-hide (stuffed with) beads, and he divided it between the free woman and the slave woman.

This was likewise narrated by a group from Ibn Abi Dhi'b. [Sahih; Ahmad 6/156]"
"12987","(12982) And Abu Tahir al-Faqih, Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hakam narrated to us; Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us. He said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me from al-Qasim ibn 'Abbas ibn Muhammad from Abdullah ibn Niyar al-Aslami from 'Urwah from 'Aishah, the wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, brought me a gazelle-hide (stuffed with) beads, and I divided it between the free woman and the slave woman."" She said: ""And my father used to divide between the free person and the slave."" [Sahih; see the preceding narration]"
"12988","(12983) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from Abu Ma'shar from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father, who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, assumed authority in the first year and divided among the people equally, so that each person received ten dirhams. Then he divided in the second year, and they received twenty dirhams each. There remained with him a few small dirhams, so he addressed the people and said: ""O people, there remain from this wealth a few dirhams, and you have servants who tend to you and perform your work. If you wish, we shall grant them something as a gratuity."" They said: ""Do so."" So he gave them five dirhams for each person.

In this narration, if it is authentic, there is clarification of the basis upon which he divided for the slaves, and that this was a gratuity given with the permission of their masters — as though he gave it to their masters. And Allah knows best. [Da'if]"
"12989","(12984) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ali al-Isfahani informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn Hamdan informed us; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Mu'tamir narrated to us from Dawud from Yusuf ibn Sa'd from Wahayb: that Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, was in charge of the Bayt al-Mal during the caliphate of 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. 'Uthman entered and saw Wahayb assisting them, so he said: ""Who is this?"" He said: ""A slave of mine."" He said: ""I see he is assisting them — assign him two thousand."" So he assigned him one thousand, or he said two thousand. [Da'if]"
"12990","(12985) He said: And Abu Bakr narrated to us — 'Abbad ibn al-'Awwam narrated to us from Harun ibn 'Anarah from his father, who said: I witnessed 'Ali and 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, providing stipends for the slaves of the people.

This may be understood to mean that they were giving their masters sufficient provision for themselves and for their slaves, without whom they could not manage. And Allah knows best. And the slave of Zayd was given (the stipend) on account of the assistance he rendered. And in Allah is success. [Hasan]"
"12991","(12986) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿAlqamah ibn Murthad from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said regarding the Bedouin Muslims: ""They have no share in the fayʾ (spoils acquired without fighting) and the ghanīmah (war booty) unless they strive in jihād alongside the Muslims.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam, within a longer hadith.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1731]"
"12992","(12987) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Abī Maʿshar from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father, who said: Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was appointed as governor, and he distributed among the people with equity. It was said to Abū Bakr: ""O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, if only you would give preference to the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār."" He said: ""I would purchase their merit from them. As for this livelihood, equity in it is better than favouritism.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"12993","(12988) He said: And Yūnus narrated to us from Hishām ibn Saʿd al-Qurashī from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbdillāh, the freed servant of Ghufrah, who said: Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) made the first distribution, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""Give preference to the early Muhājirūn and the people of precedence."" He said: ""I would purchase their precedence from them."" So he distributed and made it equal.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: And ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) made the distribution among the people with equality, and this is what I choose, and I ask Allah for tawfīq (divine facilitation).

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"12994","(12989) Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī, the jurist, narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Firās narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Ṣabīḥ narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb from his father, who heard from him that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) received wealth from Aṣbahān. He divided it into seven portions, and one loaf of bread remained over. He broke it into seven pieces and placed one piece on each portion. Then he drew lots among the people as to who would take first.

[Ḥasan]"
"12995","(12990) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyāṭī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Daghshī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Qurayr narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Hāshimī narrated from his father from his grandfather, who said: Two women came to ʿAlī — an Arab woman and her freed slave woman — and they asked him for provision. He ordered that each of them be given a kurʿ (measure) of food and forty dirhams, forty dirhams. The freed slave woman took what she was given and departed. The Arab woman said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, you give me the same as you gave her, while I am an Arab woman and she is a freed slave?"" ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said to her: ""I have looked into the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and I have not found in it any preference for the children of Ismāʿīl over the children of Isḥāq.""

[Ḍaʿīf Jiddan]"
"12996","(12991) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us: Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father, that when Muʿāwiyah came to Madīnah for Ḥajj, ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUmar came to him. Muʿāwiyah said to him: ""What is your need, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān?"" He said: ""My need is the stipend of the freed slaves (al-muḥarrarīn), for I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when something came to him, he would begin with them first.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"12997","(12992) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad, meaning al-Ḥāfiẓ al-Naysābūrī, narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Ibn Fuḍayl narrated from Ismāʿīl from Qays, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) fixed the stipend of the people of Badr at five thousand and said: ""I shall give them preference over anyone else.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4022]"
"12998","(12993) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Shākir narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Hishām narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: He fixed the stipend for the early Muhājirūn at four thousand each, and he fixed the stipend for Ibn ʿUmar at three thousand five hundred. It was said to him: ""He is from the Muhājirūn, so why do you reduce him from four thousand?"" He said: ""It was his parents who emigrated with him. He is not like the one who emigrated by himself.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ in this manner.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3912]"
"12999","(12994) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bukayr al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) fixed the stipend for ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) at three thousand, and he fixed the stipend for Usāmah at three thousand five hundred. He was asked about that, and he said: ""I place the beloved of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the same position as my own beloved.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13000","(12995) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh, meaning Ibn al-Mubārak, narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Yazīd narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Ḥārith ibn Yazīd al-Ḥaḍramī narrating from ʿUlayy ibn Rabbāḥ from Nāshirah ibn Sammāʾ al-Yazanī, who said: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) saying on the day of al-Jābiyah, while he was addressing the people: ""Indeed, Allah has made me a treasurer of this wealth and a distributor of it."" Then he said: ""Rather, Allah is the One who distributes it, and I begin with the family of the Prophet (ﷺ), then those among them of higher standing."" So he fixed the stipend for the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ), except for Juwayriyah, Ṣafiyyah, and Maymūnah (may Allah be pleased with them). ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to be just among us, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was just among them."" Then he said: ""I begin with myself and my companions, the early Muhājirūn, for we were driven out of our homes unjustly and aggressively."" Then he considered those among them of higher standing, and he fixed the stipend for the companions of Badr among them at five thousand, and for those of the Anṣār who witnessed Badr at four thousand. He fixed the stipend for those who witnessed al-Ḥudaybiyah at three thousand. And he said: ""Whoever was swift in emigration, the stipend shall be swift for him, and whoever was slow in emigration, the stipend shall be slow for him. Let no man blame except the resting place of his mount.""

[Ḥasan]"
"13001","(12996) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiṣ al-Aṣbahānī — informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān — narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah — narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn — informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr — from Abū Salamah — from Abū Hurayrah, who said:

He came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) from al-Baḥrayn. He said: ""I prayed ʿIshāʾ with him. When he saw me, I greeted him with salām, and he said: 'What have you brought?' I said: 'I have brought five hundred thousand.' He said: 'Do you know what you are saying?' So I said: 'I have brought five hundred thousand.' He said: 'You are drowsy. Go to your house and sleep, then come to me in the morning.'""

He said: ""So I went to him in the morning, and he said: 'What have you brought?' I said: 'Five hundred thousand.' He said: 'Is it genuine?' I said: 'Yes, I know of nothing other than that.'""

He said: ""Then ʿUmar said to the people: 'A great amount of wealth has come to me. If you wish, we shall count it out for you, and if you wish, we shall measure it out for you by volume.' A man said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, I have seen these non-Arabs (aʿājim) maintaining registers (dawāwīn) by which they distribute to the people.' So ʿUmar established the registers and assigned for the Muhājirūn five thousand each, and for the Anṣār four thousand each, and he assigned for the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) twelve thousand each."" [Ḥasan]"
"13002","(12997) And Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī — informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān — narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān — narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah — narrated to us; Zayd ibn Ḥubāb — narrated to me; Abū Maʿshar — narrated to me; ʿUmar, the mawlā of Ghufrah, and others, who said:

When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, wealth came from al-Baḥrayn. Abū Bakr said: ""Whoever has a claim upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) or a promise from him, let him stand and take it."" So Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) stood and said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'If wealth comes to me from al-Baḥrayn, I shall give you such and such'"" — three times, and he gestured with his hand. Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""Stand and take with your hand."" So he took, and it amounted to five hundred. He said: ""Count for him one thousand."" And he distributed among the people ten dirhams each, and said: ""These are promises that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made to the people.""

Then when the following year came, wealth arrived that was greater than that, so he distributed among the people twenty dirhams each. Some dirhams remained over, so he gave the servants five dirhams each and said: ""You have servants who serve you and attend to your needs, so we have given them a gratuity."" They said: ""If only you would give preference to the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār on account of their precedence and their standing with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" He said: ""Their reward is with Allah. Indeed, this livelihood — the example (uswah) in it is better than preference (ithrah).""

He acted according to this throughout his caliphate, until when it was the year thirteen, in Jumādā al-Ākhirah, with some nights remaining in it, he passed away. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed authority. He opened the conquests, and wealth came to him. He said: ""Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) held an opinion regarding this wealth, and I hold another opinion. I shall not make the one who fought against the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) equal to the one who fought alongside him.""

So he assigned for the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār who witnessed Badr five thousand each, and for those whose Islam was equal to the Islam of the people of Badr but who did not witness Badr, four thousand each. He assigned for the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) twelve thousand each, except Ṣafiyyah and Juwayriyyah, for whom he assigned six thousand each. They refused to accept it. He said to them: ""I assigned this for them on account of their emigration (hijrah)."" They said: ""You assigned it for them on account of their standing with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and we have the same standing."" So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) recognized that and assigned for them twelve thousand each.

He assigned for al-ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) twelve thousand. He assigned for Usāmah ibn Zayd four thousand, and for ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar three thousand. He (ʿAbd Allāh) said: ""O Father, why did you increase him over me by a thousand, when his father had no virtue that my father did not also have, and he had no standing that I did not also have?"" He said: ""The father of Usāmah was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) than your father, and Usāmah was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) than you.""

He assigned for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) five thousand each, attaching them to their father on account of their standing with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He assigned for the sons of the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār two thousand each. Then ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah passed by, and he said: ""Increase him by a thousand."" Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh said to him: ""His father had no standing that our fathers did not have, and he has no standing that we do not have."" He said: ""I assigned for him two thousand on account of his father, Abū Salamah, and I increased him one thousand on account of his mother, Umm Salamah. If you had a mother like his mother, I would increase you a thousand.""

He assigned for the people of Makkah and the people eight hundred each. Then Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh came with his brother ʿUthmān, and he assigned for him eight hundred. Then al-Naḍr ibn Anas passed by, and ʿUmar said: ""Assign for him two thousand."" Ṭalḥah said: ""I came to you with the like of him, and you assigned for him eight hundred, and for this one you assigned two thousand?"" He said: ""The father of this one met me on the day of Uḥud and said to me: 'What has happened to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?' I said: 'I think he has been killed.' So he drew his sword and broke its scabbard and said: 'If the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) has been killed, then Allah is Ever-Living and does not die.' And he fought until he was killed. And this one tends sheep in such and such a place."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13003","(12998) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān — in Baghdād — informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk — narrated to us; Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Taymī, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — narrated to us; Ḥammād — from ʿAlī ibn Zayd — from Anas ibn Mālik and Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote for the Muhājirūn five thousand each, and for the Anṣār four thousand each, and for those who did not witness Badr among the sons of the Muhājirūn four thousand each. Among them were ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad al-Makhzūmī, Usāmah ibn Zayd, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh al-Asadī, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar.

ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf said: ""Ibn ʿUmar is not among these — he is such and such, and such and such, and such and such."" Ibn ʿUmar said: ""If I have a right, then give it to me; otherwise, do not give it to me."" ʿUmar said to Ibn ʿAwf: ""Write him down for five thousand, and write me down for four thousand."" ʿAbd Allāh said: ""I do not want this."" ʿUmar said: ""By Allah, I shall not be gathered with you at five thousand.""

And likewise it was narrated by ʿAffān from Ḥammād ibn Salamah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13004","(12999) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār — narrated to us; al-Asfāṭī — narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd — narrated to us; Shuʿbah — from ʿAdī ibn Thābit — from Abū Ḥāzim — from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

""Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs. And whoever leaves dependents (kallā) in need, then their responsibility falls upon us.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1619]"
"13005","(13000) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr — narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr — narrated to us; Sufyān — from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad — from his father — from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say: ""I am closer to the believers than their own selves. Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his family. And whoever leaves a debt or dependents in need, then they are my responsibility and upon me.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ within a long ḥadīth from the sermon of the Prophet (ﷺ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 867]"
"13006","(13001) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiṣ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī — narrated to us; Abū Ṣāliḥ — narrated to me; al-Layth — narrated to me; Hishām ibn Saʿd — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from his father Aslam, who said:

We were one day with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) when a Bedouin woman came to him and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, I am the daughter of Khufāf ibn Imāʾ. My father witnessed al-Ḥudaybiyah with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" ʿUmar said: ""A close lineage."" She said: ""I have young children, and the eldest among them cannot even cook a sheep's trotter."" So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered for her a camel laden with food and clothing. A man said: ""You have been excessive for her, O Commander of the Faithful."" He said: ""Her father witnessed al-Ḥudaybiyah with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and perhaps he witnessed the conquest of such and such a city and the conquest of such and such a city, and he had a share in that, and we collect it from him. Shall I not give her from that?""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Mālik from Zayd ibn Aslam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4161]"
"13007","(13002) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq — informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn — informed us; Hishām ibn Saʿd — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from his father, who said:

I heard ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""Gather this wealth together and see whom you consider it to be for."" Then he said to you: ""I commanded you to gather this wealth and see whom you consider it to be for. Indeed, I have read verses from the Book of Allah. I heard Allah say: {What Allah has bestowed upon His Messenger from the people of the towns is for Allah and for the Messenger, and for the near relatives, and the orphans, and the needy, and the wayfarer, so that it may not be a perpetual distribution among the rich among you. And whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, abstain from it. And fear Allah; indeed, Allah is severe in punishment.} — for the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties, seeking bounty from Allah and His pleasure, and supporting Allah and His Messenger — those are the truthful ones."" By Allah, it is not exclusively for these alone.

{And those who had settled in the home and in faith before them love those who emigrated to them and find not in their breasts any need of what they were given, and prefer others over themselves.} By Allah, it is not exclusively for these alone either.

{And those who came after them.} By Allah, there is not a single Muslim except that he has a right in this wealth — he is given from it or withheld from it — even a shepherd in ʿAdan. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13008","(13003) And Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd — narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī — narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb — narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd — from Ayyūb — from ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid — from Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān — from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), in a story he mentioned. He then recited: {Indeed, charitable offerings are only for the poor and the needy} to the end of the verse. He said: ""This is for these."" Then he recited: {And know that anything you obtain of war gains, then indeed, one-fifth of it is for Allah and for the Messenger} to the end of the verse. Then he said: ""This is for these."" Then he recited: {What Allah has bestowed upon His Messenger from the people of the towns} to the end of the verse. Then he recited: {For the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties} to the end of the verse. Then he said: ""These are the Muhājirūn."" Then he recited: {And those who had settled in the home and in faith before them} to the end of the verse. He said: ""These are the Anṣār."" And he said: {And those who came after them, saying: 'Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith'} to the end of the verse. He said: ""This has encompassed all the people. There remains not a single Muslim except that he has a right in this wealth — except for the slaves you own. So if I live, Allah willing, there will not remain a single Muslim except that his right will reach him — even a shepherd in Ṣur and Ḥimyar — his right will reach him, and his brow will not have sweated for it.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""This ḥadīth carries several meanings, among them that we may say: No one is given on the basis of need from the people of ṣadaqah, or on the basis of being among the people of fayʾ who wage war, except that he has a right in this wealth — fayʾ or ṣadaqah. And this is as though it is the most appropriate of its meanings. For the Prophet (ﷺ) said regarding ṣadaqah: 'There is no share in it for a wealthy person nor for one of robust earning capacity.' And what I have preserved from the people of knowledge is that the Bedouins (aʿrāb) are not given from the fayʾ.""

Al-Shaykh said: ""This has preceded in the ḥadīth of Buraydah from the Prophet (ﷺ). And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Shāfiʿī narrated from al-Shāfiʿī that he said in the Kitāb al-Sīr al-Qadīm something to this effect, then he made an exception and said: 'Unless one of the two categories of wealth is obtained and the other is not, and there is a need for the two categories among the people, in which case they are shared between them in it.' And Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) assisted Khālid ibn al-Walīd (may Allah be pleased with him) in his expedition against the people of apostasy with wealth that ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim had brought from the ṣadaqah of his people, and this was not objected to, since the people had a need for it. And the fayʾ is similar to that."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13009","(13004) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiṣ — informed me; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiṣ — informed us; Abū Jaʿfar — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Khathʿamī — narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn Ismāʿīl — narrated to us; Abū Usāmah — from ʿUbayd Allāh, who said: Nāfiʿ narrated to me, who said: Ibn ʿUmar narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) presented him for battle on the day of Uḥud. He said: ""I was fourteen years of age, and he did not permit me."" He said: ""Then he presented me on the day of the Trench, and I was fifteen years of age, and he permitted me.""

Nāfiʿ said: ""I came to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz while he was at that time the caliph, and I narrated this ḥadīth to him. He said: 'Indeed, this is the boundary between the minor and the adult.' Then he wrote to his governors: 'Assign a share for whoever has reached fifteen years of age, and whoever is below that, count him among the dependents (ʿiyāl).'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd, and Muslim narrated it from other chains from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"
"13010","(13005) Abū al-Ḥusayn — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl — in Baghdād — informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār — narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr — narrated to us; Wakīʿ — from Hishām ibn Saʿd — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from his father, who said:

ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Gather this fayʾ together so that we may assess it."" Then he said to them afterwards: ""Indeed, I had commanded you to gather it so that we may assess it. And indeed, I have read verses from the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and I have no need of your assessment."" Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: {What Allah has bestowed upon His Messenger from the people of the towns is for Allah and for the Messenger} until His words: {severe in punishment.} By Allah, it is not exclusively for these alone.

Then he recited: {For the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties.} Then he recited: {And those who came after them, saying: 'Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith.'} By Allah, it is not exclusively for these alone either. And if I remain until next year, I shall surely attach the last of the people to the first of them, and I shall make them a single category (bāban wāḥidan) — meaning a single class.""

He said: ""Then a son of his came to him while he was distributing — he is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the son of Lahīʿah, a woman who had been married to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). He said to him: 'Clothe me with a ring.' He said to him: 'Go to your mother and let her give you a drink of sawīq.' By Allah, he gave him nothing."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13011","(13006) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Yūnus informed me from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: ""When Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — was appointed as caliph, he said: 'My people know that my trade was never insufficient for the maintenance of my family, but I have been occupied with the affairs of the Muslims. So the family of Abū Bakr will eat from this wealth, and I shall work for the Muslims therein.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2070]"
"13012","(13007) Ibn Shihāb said: ʿUrwah informed me from ʿľishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — was appointed as caliph, he and his family ate, and he engaged in trade with his own wealth.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13013","(13008) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash from Shaqīq from Masrūq from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — said when death approached him: ""Look at everything that has increased in my wealth since I entered this office of governance, and return it to the caliph after me."" She said: ""When he died, we looked and found that nothing had increased in his wealth except a camel that used to water his garden and a Nubī boy who used to carry his child."" She said: ""So I sent them to ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him."" She said: ""I was informed that ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — wept and said: 'May Allah have mercy on Abū Bakr; he has placed those who come after him in severe hardship.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13014","(13009) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ṭāhir ibn Yaḥyā informed us: My father informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Khālid al-Farrāʾ narrated to us: My father narrated to us: al-Mubārak ibn Faḍālah narrated from al-Ḥasan that Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — addressed the people and praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: ""Indeed, the wisest of wisdom is taqwā (God-consciousness), and the most foolish of folly is immorality. Beware! Truthfulness, in my view, is trustworthiness, and lying is treachery. Beware! The strong one, in my view, is weak until I take the right from him, and the weak one, in my view, is strong until I secure his right. Beware! I have been appointed over you, and I am not the best of you.""

Al-Ḥasan said: ""By Allah, he was the best of them, without dispute, but a believer diminishes himself."" Then he said: ""I wish one of you would relieve me of this matter.""

Al-Ḥasan said: ""He spoke the truth, by Allah. And if you desire from me that which Allah established His Prophet ﷺ upon through revelation, that is not with me; I am only a human being. So watch over me."" When morning came, he went to the market. ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — said to him: ""Where are you headed?"" He said: ""To the market."" He said: ""Something has come to you that occupies you from the market."" He said: ""Glory be to Allah! It has occupied me even from my family."" He said: ""Take a known provision."" He said: ""Woe to you, ʿUmar! I fear that I may not be permitted to eat anything from this wealth."" He said: ""He spent eight thousand dirhams in two years and part of another year. When death approached him, he said: 'I had said to ʿUmar that I feared I might not be permitted to eat from this wealth, but he overpowered me. So when I die, take eight thousand dirhams from my wealth and return it to the Bayt al-Māl.'"" He said: ""When they were brought to ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — he said: 'May Allah have mercy on Abū Bakr; he has placed those who come after him in severe hardship.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13015","(13010) Abū Ḥāzim ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAbdawī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Khamīruwayh narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: Ayyūb narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays, who said: ""We were at the door of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — waiting for him to grant us permission to enter, when a slave girl came out. We said: 'She is the slave girl of the Commander of the Faithful.' She heard and said: 'I am not the slave girl of the Commander of the Faithful, and I am not lawful for him; I am from the wealth of Allah, the Exalted.'"" He said: ""This was mentioned to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, so we entered upon him and he informed us of what we had said and what she had said. He said: 'She spoke the truth. It is not lawful for me, nor is she my slave girl; she is from the wealth of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. And I shall inform you of what I deem lawful for myself from this wealth: I deem lawful for myself two garments — one for winter and one for summer — and what suffices me for my Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and my provision and the provision of my household, and my share among the Muslims like the share of a man — I am neither the highest nor the lowest among them.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13016","(13011) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Naḍrawī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Abī Isḥāq from al-Yarfāʾ, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — said to me: ""I have placed myself, with regard to the wealth of Allah, in the position of the guardian of an orphan. If I am in need, I take from it, and when I am in ease, I return it, and when I am free of need, I abstain."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13017","(13012) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Narsī narrated to us: Rūḥ narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated from Qatādah from Lāḥiq ibn Ḥumayd, who said: ""When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — sent ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, and ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf to Kūfah, he appointed ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir over the prayer and the armies, and he appointed Ibn Masʿūd over the judiciary and the Bayt al-Māl, and he appointed ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf over the surveying of the land. He assigned among them each day one sheep — half of it, along with its offal and extremities, for ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, and the half shared between those two."" Saʿīd said: ""I do not remember the food."" Then he said: ""I have placed you and myself, with regard to this wealth, in the position of the guardian of an orphan's wealth: {Whoever is rich, let him abstain, and whoever is poor, let him consume what is reasonable.} And I do not think a village from which a sheep is taken every day will be spared from rapid ruin."""
"13018","(13013) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: ʿĀmir ibn Shaqīq narrated that he heard Abū Wāʾil say: ""Ibn Ziyād appointed me over the Bayt al-Māl. A man came to me with a document ordering that eight hundred dirhams be given to the head of the kitchen. I said to him: 'Stay where you are.' I went to Ibn Ziyād and informed him. I said: 'ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — appointed ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd over the judiciary and the Bayt al-Māl, and ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf over the land irrigated by the Euphrates, and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir over the prayer and the troops, and he granted them each day one sheep. He made half of it, along with its offal and extremities, for ʿAmmār, because he was in charge of the prayer and the troops, and he made a quarter for ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd and a quarter for ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf. Then he said: ""A wealth from which a sheep is taken every day — that is indeed swift in its depletion.""'"" Ibn Ziyād said: ""Put down the keys and go wherever you wish."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13019","(13014) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Layth narrated from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ashajj from Busr ibn Saʿīd from Ibn al-Sāʿidī, who said: ""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — appointed me over the ṣadaqah (charitable alms). When I had finished, he ordered a stipend for me. I said: 'I only worked for Allah.' He said: 'Take what you have been given, for I worked during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he appointed me (and gave me a stipend).'""

It was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from al-Layth, and he said: ""From Ibn al-Saʿdī."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13020","(13015) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad al-Harawī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Jakkānī narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān informed me: Shuʿayb informed me from al-Zuhrī, who informed me: al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd informed me that Ḥuwayṭib ibn al-ʿUzzā informed him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Saʿdī informed him: ""He came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — during his caliphate. ʿUmar said to him: 'Shall I not inform you that you oversee some of the affairs of the people, and when you are given a stipend, you dislike it?' He said: 'Yes.' ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — said: 'What do you intend by that?' He said: 'I have horses and slaves, and I am in good circumstances, and I wish my service to be charity upon the Muslims.' ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — said: 'Do not do that, for I had intended the same. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give me a stipend, and I would say: ""Give it to one more in need than me."" Until one time he gave me wealth, and I said: ""Give it to one more in need than me."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Take it and keep it as your own, or give it in charity. And whatever comes to you of this wealth while you are neither desirous nor asking, take it; and whatever does not, do not let your soul pursue it.""'""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith from Ibn Shihāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 7164]"
"13021","(13016) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me, saying: al-Layth narrated to me, saying: Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated to me from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father Aslam, that he said: ""When the Year of al-Ramādāt came and the lands of the Arabs were afflicted with drought, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — wrote to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ: 'From ʿUmar, the servant of Allah and Commander of the Faithful, to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ — by my life, you care not if you grow fat while those around you grow emaciated, nor do I and those around me care.' Then he said: 'Woe unto you!' — and he mentioned the hadith, and said therein: 'Then he called Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāh, and he set out for that purpose. When he returned, he sent him a thousand dinars. Abū ʿUbaydah said: ""I did not do this work for you, O son of al-Khaṭṭāb; I did it only for Allah, and I will not take anything for it."" ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give us things when he sent us on missions, and we disliked that, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ refused to accept them back from us. So accept it, O man, and use it to pay off your debts and for your worldly needs."" So Abū ʿUbaydah accepted it."" [Weak]"
"13022","(13017) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Firās al-Faqīh in Makkah narrated to us, saying: Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyāṭī narrated to us, saying: Shuʿayb ibn Yaḥyā al-Tujībī narrated to us, saying: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us from Hishām ibn Saʿd — and he mentioned it with its chain, similar to it, and mentioned what the first narration omitted. He said: ""So ʿAmr wrote: 'Peace be upon you, and thereafter — here I am, here I am! A caravan is coming to you, the first of it with you and the last of it with me, and I hope that I may find a route by sea.' When the first caravan arrived, he called al-Zubayr — may Allah be pleased with him — and said: 'Go out with the first of this caravan and direct it toward Najd, and bring to me the people of every household whom you are able to transport to me. And for those you cannot transport, command the people of each household to take a camel along with what is upon it, and instruct them to wear two garments, slaughter the camel, render its fat, dry its meat into strips, and tan its hide. Then let them take a bundle of dried meat, a bundle of fat, and a bowl of flour, and let them cook and eat until Allah provides them with sustenance.' But al-Zubayr refused to go out and said: 'By Allah, you will not find the like of this until you depart from this world.' Then he called another — I think he was Ṭalḥah — and he also refused. Then he called Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāh, and he set out for that purpose."" And he mentioned the remainder of the hadith in a similar manner. [Weak]"
"13023","(13018) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: Mūsā ibn Marwān al-Raqqī narrated to us, saying: al-Muʿāfā narrated to us, saying: al-Awzāʿī narrated to us from al-Ḥārith ibn Yazīd, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from al-Mustawrid ibn Shaddād, who said: ""I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: 'Whoever is appointed as a worker for us, let him acquire a wife; if he does not have a servant, let him acquire a servant; and if he does not have a dwelling, let him acquire a dwelling.'"" He said: ""Then Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — said: 'I was informed that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Whoever takes anything beyond that is a usurper or a thief.""'"" [Authentic]"
"13024","(13019) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to me, saying: al-Ḥusayn ibn Idrīs al-Anṣārī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmmār al-Mawṣilī narrated to us, saying: al-Muʿāfā ibn ʿImrān narrated — except that he said: ""from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from al-Mustawrid,"" and at the end of it he said: ""I was informed"" — he did not say: ""Abū Bakr said."" [Authentic. Abū Dāwūd 2560]"
"13025","(13020) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad al-Sammāk in Baghdad narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Ḥayyān ibn Mulāʿib narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd narrated to us from Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Whomsoever we appoint to a task and we have assigned him a provision, then whatever he takes beyond that is misappropriation (ghulūl)."" [Authentic. Abū Dāwūd]"
"13026","(13021) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Bālawayh narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Sabrah narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah narrated to us from al-Zuhrī, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ assigned ʿAttāb ibn Asīd, when he appointed him over Makkah, forty uqiyyahs per year."" This is a disconnected (munqaṭiʿ) narration. It has been narrated through another chain as a connected (musnad) narration. [Weak]"
"13027","(13022) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, saying: Jaʿfar al-Khuldī narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd ibn Bashīr al-Rāzī narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥuṣayn al-Raqqī, the grandson of Muʿammar ibn Sulaymān — (H) and Abū Isḥāq Sahl ibn Abī Sahl al-Mihrānī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣibghī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān al-Nasawī narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥuṣayn narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Maslamah narrated to us from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed ʿAttāb ibn Asīd over Makkah and assigned him a stipend of forty uqiyyahs of silver. [Weak]"
"13028","(13023) Abū Bakr al-Fārisī narrated to us, saying: Abū Isḥāq al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: Abū Aḥmad ibn Fāris narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, saying: Ḥarmī ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, saying: Khālid ibn Abī ʿUthmān al-Qurashī narrated to us, saying: Ayyūb ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yasār narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAqrab, who said: ""I heard ʿAttāb ibn Asīd, with his back leaning against the House of Allah, say: 'By Allah, I have not obtained from this task to which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed me except two tied garments, which I gave to my freed slave Kayyisān to wear.'"" [Weak]"
"13029","(13024) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn Musāfir al-Tinnīsī narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us, saying: al-Zamʿī narrated to us from al-Zubayr ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Surāqah, that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān informed him that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī — may Allah be pleased with him — informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Beware of al-qusāmah."" He said: ""So we said: 'What is al-qusāmah?' He said: 'A thing that is shared among people, and then it is diminished from.'"" [Weak]"
"13030","(13025) Abū ʿAlī narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to us from Sharīk ibn Abī Namir — (H) and Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us, saying: Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad narrated to us from Sharīk, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Beware of al-qusāmah."" They said: ""What is al-qusāmah, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""A man who is among a group of people, and he takes from the share of this one and the share of that one."" [Weak]"
"13031","(13026) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us; Ibn Abī Masarraḥ narrated to us; al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, who said: When the war spoils (fayʾ) came, the Prophet ﷺ would distribute them on the very same day.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr (2356)]"
"13032","(13027) He said, and Abū Saʿīd informed us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ibn al-Muṣaffā narrated to us; Abū al-Mughīra narrated from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr with his chain of narration, the same (hadith), adding in it: He gave the unmarried man one share and the married man two shares. Then he called me and gave me two shares, while I had a family. Then he called ʿAmmār and gave him one share.
[Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd (2953)]"
"13033","(13028) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Ṭayyib Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Shaʿīrī narrated to us; Maḥmish ibn ʿIṣām narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought wealth from al-Baḥrayn, so he said: ""Scatter it in the mosque."" He said: ""And it was the most wealth that had ever been brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the prayer and did not turn his attention toward it. When he finished the prayer, he came and sat by it. He did not see anyone but that he gave him (from it). When al-ʿAbbās came, he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, give me, for I ransomed myself and I ransomed ʿAqīl."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""Take."" So he scooped into his garment, then tried to carry it but could not. He said: ""Order someone to lift it for me."" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then you lift it for me."" He said: ""No."" He said: ""So he scattered some of it, then carried it and placed it upon his shoulder, then departed."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept following him with his gaze until he disappeared from him, out of astonishment at his greed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not stand while there remained a single dirham of it.""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: ""And Ibrāhīm said…""
[Ḥasan]"
"13034","(13029) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Abū Salamah Manṣūr narrated to us; Bakr ibn Muḍar narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Jubayr narrated from Abī Umāmah, who said: ""One day ʿUrwah and I visited ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. She said: 'If only you two could have seen the Prophet of Allah ﷺ during the illness in which he fell ill. I had six dinars with me' — Mūsā ibn Jubayr said: 'or seven' — 'and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ commanded me to distribute them. But the illness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ occupied me, until Allah restored him to health. Then he asked me about them and said: ""Did you distribute the six or the seven?"" She said: ""No, by Allah, your illness occupied me."" She said: ""So he called for them, then distributed them and said: 'What does the Prophet of Allah think, if he were to meet Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, while they are still with him?'""
[Ḥasan. Aḥmad (24212)]"
"13035","(13030) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Saqqāʾ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ṭarāʾifī narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿAbd al-Malik Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Abū ʿAwanah narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh, from Umm Salamah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me while his face was drawn and pale."" She said: ""I thought it was from pain, so I said: 'What is the matter with you, your face being so drawn?' He said: 'Because of the seven dinars that came to us yesterday and we did not distribute them, and they are in the fold of the bedding.'""
[Ḥasan]"
"13036","(13031) ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; al-Dabarī narrated from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, from Ibn Jurayj.
(Ḥ) He said, and Abū Saʿīd informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿUbayd narrated to me: Ḥajjāj narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed me from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not let wealth remain overnight, nor would he let it stay until the afternoon."" Abū ʿUbayd said: ""If it came to him in the morning, he would not let the day reach its midpoint before distributing it; and if it came to him in the evening, he would not let the night pass before distributing it.""
This is a mursal hadith.
[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13037","(13032) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Daʿlaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijzī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us; al-Ḥurr ibn Mālik al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us; Mālik ibn Mighwal narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from his father, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said to ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam: ""Distribute the treasury of the Muslims every month once. Distribute the wealth of the Muslims every Friday once."" Then he said: ""Distribute the treasury of the Muslims every day once."" A man from the people said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, if you were to leave a remainder in the treasury of the Muslims that you could set aside for an unexpected calamity or an expedition"" — meaning a military campaign. ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the man who had spoken to him: ""Satan has run across his tongue. Allah has given me the proof against it and has protected me from its evil. I shall return it to the place where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned it — in obedience to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger ﷺ.""
[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13038","(13033) And in what Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from him: that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to them; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; more than one of the people of knowledge informed us that when the spoils from Iraq were brought to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, the custodian of the treasury said: ""Shall I deposit it in the treasury?"" He said: ""No, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah! It shall not be sheltered under the roof of any house until I have distributed it."" So he ordered that it be placed in the mosque, and leather mats were laid over it, and it was guarded by men from the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār. When morning came, ʿUmar went to it accompanied by al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, one of them holding the other's hand, or one of them holding his hand. When they saw it, they removed the leather mats from over the wealth, and they beheld a sight the like of which they had never seen — gold, rubies, emeralds, and pearls gleaming and sparkling. ʿUmar wept, and one of them said to him: ""By Allah, this is not a day of weeping, but rather a day of gratitude and joy."" He said: ""By Allah, I did not think what you thought. But by Allah, this never increased among any people without their enmity and hatred falling among them."" Then he turned toward the qiblah, raised his hands toward the heaven, and said: ""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from being gradually drawn to destruction, for I hear You saying: 'We shall draw them to destruction from whence they know not.'"" Then he said: ""Where is Surāqah ibn Juʿshum?"" He was brought — lean-armed, thin of the forearms — and he gave him the two bracelets of Kisrā. He said: ""Put them on."" So he did. He said: ""Say: 'Allāhu Akbar.'"" He said: ""Allāhu Akbar."" He said: ""Say: 'Al-ḥamdu lillāh, Who stripped them from Kisrā ibn Hurmuz and clothed Surāqah ibn Juʿshum, a Bedouin of Banī Mudlij, with them.'"" And he began to turn some of it over with a stick. He said: ""Indeed, the one who brought this is trustworthy."" A man said to him: ""Shall I inform you? You are the trustee of Allah, and they deliver to you what you deliver to Allah. When you prosper, they prosper."" He said: ""You have spoken the truth."" Then he distributed it.
Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""He clothed Surāqah with them only because the Prophet ﷺ had said to Surāqah, looking at his forearms: 'It is as though I can see you wearing the bracelets of Kisrā.' And he did not assign for him anything other than the two bracelets.""
Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""A trustworthy person from the people of Madīnah informed us that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, spent upon the people of al-Ramādah until rain fell and they departed. ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, went out to them on horseback, watching them as they broke camp with their womenfolk. His eyes shed tears, and a man from Banī Muḥārib ibn Khaṣafah said: 'I bear witness that it has been stripped from you, and you are not the son of a slave woman.' ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: 'Woe to you! Had I spent upon them from my own wealth or the wealth of al-Khaṭṭāb — I only spent upon them from the wealth of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.'""
[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13039","(13034) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdbārī, Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad, and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated from Hishām ibn Saʿd, from al-Zuhrī, and Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought livestock from the spoils of al-Qādisiyyah. He began examining them and looking at them while weeping, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf was with him. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said to him: ""O Commander of the Faithful, this is a day of joy, and this is a day of happiness."" He said: ""Indeed, yes. But this was never given to any people without it bequeathing enmity and hatred among them.""
[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13040","(13035) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who said: When the treasures of Kisrā were brought to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam al-Zuhrī said to him: ""Will you not place them in the treasury?"" — meaning, ""Will you not put them in the bayt al-māl?"" ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Do not place them in the treasury until we have distributed them."" And ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, wept. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: ""What makes you weep, O Commander of the Faithful? By Allah, this is truly a day of gratitude, a day of happiness, and a day of joy."" ʿUmar said: ""Allah never gave this to any people without casting enmity and hatred among them.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13041","(13036) And Abū Muḥammad informed us — Abū Saʿīd informed us, he said: I found in my book, in my own handwriting, from Abū Dāwūd, he said: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated to us, Yūnus narrated from al-Ḥasan: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought the spoils of Chosroes, which were placed before him. Among the people present was Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum. He cast upon him the two bracelets of Chosroes ibn Hurmuz and placed them in his hand, and they reached up to his shoulders. When he saw them in the hands of Surāqah, he said: ""All praise is due to Allah — the bracelets of Chosroes ibn Hurmuz are in the hands of Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum, a Bedouin from Banī Mudlij."" Then he said: ""O Allah, I know that Your Messenger ﷺ used to love to receive wealth and spend it in Your way and upon Your servants, and You turned that away from him as a favour and a choice of Your own. O Allah, I know that Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, used to love to receive wealth and spend it in Your way and upon Your servants, and You turned that away from him as a favour and a choice of Your own. O Allah, I seek refuge in You lest this be a plot from You against ʿUmar."" Then he recited: {Do they think that what We extend to them of wealth and children is hastening for them in good deeds? Nay, they are unaware.} [Weak]"
"13042","(13037) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us — Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us — Jaʿfar ibn Awn informed us — Misʿar narrated from Mūsā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Saʿīd, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, distributed wealth one day, and the people began praising him. He said: ""How foolish you are! If this were mine, I would not have given you a single dirham."" [Weak]"
"13043","(13038) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān informed us — Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us — ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham informed us — ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād informed us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us — ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb informed us from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, when he would perform a prayer, he would sit, and whoever had a need would speak to him, and whoever had no need would leave. One day he prayed and did not sit. I came and said: ""O Yarfā', is the Commander of the Faithful unwell?"" He said: ""Nay, all praise is due to Allah."" Then ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, came and sat. It was not long before Yarfā' came and said: ""Rise, O Ibn ʿAffān; rise, O Ibn ʿAbbās."" So we entered upon ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and with him was a heap of wealth, upon each heap a shoulder (of burden). He said: ""I have looked into the people of Madinah and found you two to have the largest households among them. Take this wealth and divide it, and if anything remains, return it."" As for ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, he was ashamed (to take it). I said: ""If anything falls short, will you make it up for us?"" He said: ""A habit from the worst of characters. Do you think this is from Allah, and Muḥammad ﷺ and his Companions used to eat leather?"" I said: ""Yes, by Allah, this was from Allah, and Muḥammad ﷺ and his Companions used to eat leather, and if this had been opened upon Muḥammad ﷺ, he would not have done as you are doing."" It was as though he was startled by this, and he said: ""What would he have done?"" I said: ""He would have eaten and fed us."" He began to weep until his ribs were gripped (with sobs), and he said: ""I wish I could come out of it with a clean slate — neither against me nor for me."" [Ḥasan]"
"13044","(13039) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān informed us — Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Yazīd informed us — Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us — ʿAwf informed us from Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah — Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā al-Ashʿarī informed me, he said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Do you know what my father said to your father?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""My father said to your father: 'O Abū Mūsā, would it please you that our Islam be with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and our jihād be with him, and our deeds be with him — all of it is settled for us — and that every deed we perform after him, we come out of it squarely, head for head?'"" He said: ""Your father said to my father: 'By Allah, we have striven after the Messenger of Allah ￺, and we have prayed, and we have fasted, and we have done much good, and many people have accepted Islam at our hands, and we hope for that.' My father said: 'But I — by Him in whose hand is the soul of ʿUmar — I wish that were settled for us, and that everything we have done after him, we come out of it squarely, head for head.'"" I said: ""By Allah, your father was better than my father."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bursh from Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3915]"
"13045","(13040) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us — Shaybān ibn Farrūkh informed us — Abū al-Ashhab informed us — Khulayd al-ʿAṣarī informed us — from al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays, who said: I was among a group of Quraysh when Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by, saying: ""Give glad tidings to the hoarders of wealth of a branding upon their backs that will emerge from their sides, and a branding upon their heels that will emerge from their foreheads."" He then withdrew and sat by a pillar. I said: ""Who is this?"" They said: ""This is Abū Dharr."" I stood up to him and said: ""What was that I heard you saying just now?"" He said: ""I said nothing except what I heard from their Prophet ﷺ."" I said: ""What do you say about this stipend (ʿaṭā')?"" He said: ""Take it, for in it is assistance today. But when it becomes the price of your religion, then leave it."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh, and it is in the context of the stipend (ʿaṭā') as a mawqūf narration, though it has also been narrated from another chain as a marfūʿ narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 992]"
"13046","(13041) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Ḥawārī informed us — Sulaym ibn Muṭayr, a man from the people of Wādī al-Qurá, informed us — my father Muṭayr informed me: that he went out on pilgrimage until, when they were at al-Suwaydā', a man came as though he were seeking a remedy or a gift. He said: ""I was informed by one who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage, while he was admonishing the people, commanding them and forbidding them, and he said: 'O people, take the stipend (ʿaṭā') as long as it is a stipend. But when Quraysh contend among themselves over sovereignty, and the stipend becomes bribery (rushwah), then leave it.'"" [Very Weak. Abū Dāwūd 2958]"
"13047","(13042) And Abū ʿAlī informed us — Muḥammad informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Hishām ibn ʿAmmār informed us — Sulaym ibn Muṭayr from the people of Wādī al-Qurá, from his father, that he informed them, he said: I heard a man saying, ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage commanding the people and forbidding them, then he said: 'O Allah, have I conveyed?' They said: 'O Allah, yes.' Then he said: 'When Quraysh contend among themselves over sovereignty, and the stipend becomes bribery (rushwah), then leave it.'"" It was said: ""Who is this?"" They said: ""This is Dhū al-Zawā'id, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" [Very Weak]"
"13048","(13043) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us — Ibn al-Mubārak informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid informed us — Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim informed us: that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, gave Bajīlah a quarter of the military stipend (al-sawād). They held it for several years. Then Jarīr, may Allah be pleased with him, came as an emissary to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: ""Were it not that I am a distributor who is held accountable, you would have remained upon what was distributed to you. But I think you should return it."" So he returned it and gave him eighty dinars in compensation. [Ḥasan]"
"13049","(13044) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs al-ʿAnazī informed us — ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr and ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ, the two Egyptians, informed us — that Layth ibn Saʿd informed them, he said: ʿUqayl informed us from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿUrwah claimed that Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah informed him: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood when the delegation of Hawāzin came to him as Muslims, and they asked him to return to them their wealth and their women. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: ""I have with me those whom you see, and the truest speech to me is the most sincere. So choose one of the two options — either the captives or the wealth. And I had been granting them respite."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had waited for them for ten nights after returning from Ṭā'if. When it became clear to them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not return to them their wealth except one of the two options, they said: ""Then we choose our captives."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among the Muslims and praised Allah as He deserves, then said: ""To proceed: Your brothers have come as repentant, and I have seen that I should return their captives to them. Whoever wishes to make that gratuitous (ṭayyib) for them, let him do so. And whoever of you wishes to hold his share until we give it to him from the first booty that Allah grants us, let him do so."" The people said: ""We have made it gratuitous for them, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""We do not know who among you has given permission and who has not. Go back until your chiefs (ʿurafā') bring us your decision."" The people went back and their chiefs spoke to them, then they returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him that they had made it gratuitous and given permission. This is what has reached us regarding the captives of Hawāzin. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2308]"
"13050","(13045) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAttāb al-ʿAbdī informed us — al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mughīrah informed us — Ibn Abī Uways informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah informed me — Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah said: Ibn Shihāb informed me — ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed him: that Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah informed him: that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked the people regarding the freeing of the captives of Hawāzin, he said: ""I do not know who among you has given permission and who has not. Go back until your chiefs (ʿurafā') bring us your decision."" The people went back and their chiefs spoke to them, then they returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him that the people had made it gratuitous and given permission. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways. [Weak]"
"13051","(13046) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us: Ghassān ibn Muḍar narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Yazīd narrated from Abū Naḍrah, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: When ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed the caliphate, he fixed the shares (of inheritance), established the registers, appointed the chiefs (ʿurafāʾ), and appointed me over my companions. [Ḥasan]"
"13052","(13047) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb narrated from Abū Salamah Sulaymān ibn Sulaym, from Yaḥyā ibn Jābir, from Ṣāliḥ ibn Yaḥyā ibn al-Miqdām, from his grandfather al-Miqdām, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) struck him on his shoulder and then said: ""You have attained success, O Qudaym, if you die without having been an amīr, a scribe, or an ʿarīf."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13053","(13048) ʿAlī informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Aḥmad ibn Bishr al-Marthadī narrated to us: Ḥājib ibn al-Walīd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb narrated, mentioning something similar to it, except that he said: ""from his father, from his grandfather,"" and he said: ""without having been an amīr, a collector (jābiyy), or an ʿarrāf."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13054","(13049) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal narrated to us: Ghālib al-Qaṭṭān narrated from a man, from his father, from his grandfather, that they were at one of the watering places. When the news of Islam reached them, the owner of the water gave his people a hundred camels on the condition that that they embrace Islam. They accepted Islam, and he distributed the camels among them. Then it occurred to him to take them back from them, so he sent his son to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said to him: ""Go to the Prophet (ﷺ) and say to him: 'My father sends you greetings of peace, and he gave his people a hundred camels on the condition that they embrace Islam. They accepted Islam and he distributed the camels among them. Now he wishes to take them back from them. Is he more entitled to them, or are they?' If he says yes or no, then say to him: 'My father is an old man and he is the ʿarīf of the water source, and he asks you to appoint me as ʿarīf after him.'"" So he came to him and said: ""My father sends you greetings of peace."" He (ﷺ) said: ""Peace be upon you and upon your father."" He said: ""My father gave his people a hundred camels on the condition that they embrace Islam, and they accepted Islam and their Islam was good. Now it has occurred to him to take them back from them. Is he more entitled to them, or are they?"" He (ﷺ) said: ""If it occurs to him to give them to them, let him give them to them, and if it occurs to him to take them back, he is more entitled to them than they are. If they have accepted Islam, then their Islam is for them, and if they do not accept Islam, they will be fought until they accept Islam."" He said: ""My father is an old man and he is the ʿarīf of the water source, and he asks you to appoint me as ʿarīf after him."" He (ﷺ) said: ""The office of ʿarīf is a right, and people must have ʿurafāʾ, but the ʿurafāʾ will be in the Fire."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13055","(13050) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿUmar ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUrwah narrated to me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made the war cry (shiʿār) of the Muhājirūn on the day of Badr: ""O Banī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān,"" and the war cry of the Khazraj: ""O Banī ʿAbdullāh,"" and the war cry of the Aws: ""O Banī ʿUbaydullāh,"" and he named his cavalry: ""O cavalry of Allah."" This is a mursal narration, though it has also been narrated as mawṣūl. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13056","(13051) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā al-Muqriʾ informed us: ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿImrān narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Ḥabībah narrated from Yazīd ibn Rūmān, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made the war cry of the Muhājirūn on the day of Badr: ""Banī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān,"" and that of the Aws: ""Banī ʿAbdullāh,"" and that of the Khazraj: ""Banī ʿUbaydullāh."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13057","(13052) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated from al-Ḥajjāj, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: The war cry of the Muhājirūn was ""O ʿAbdullāh"" and the war cry of the Anṣār was ""O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13058","(13053) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm informed us: Abū al-Muwajjah informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbdullāh informed us: ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated from Iyās ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, from his father, who said: I went on a campaign with Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and our war cry was ""Amīt, amīt"" (Kill, kill). [Ḥasan]"
"13059","(13054) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Sayyār informed us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Muhallab ibn Abī Ṣufrah, who said: One who heard the Prophet (ﷺ) informed me that he said: ""If you launch a night attack, let your war cry be: Ḥam lā yunṣarūn (They shall not be victorious)."" It has also been said that al-Muhallab ibn Abī Ṣufrah mentioned it from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13060","(13055) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥakīm narrated to us: Sharīk narrated from Abū Isḥāq, who said: I heard al-Muhallab ibn Abī Ṣufrah mentioning from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""You will meet your enemy tomorrow, so let your war cry be: Ḥam lā yunṣarūn (They shall not be victorious)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13061","(13056) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī informed me: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from a man from Muzaynah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard a man calling out in his battle-cry, ""Yā Ḥarām! Yā Ḥarām!"" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Yā Ḥalāl! Yā Ḥalāl!"" It has also been related from him, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mughaffal al-Muzanī. [Weak]"
"13062","(13057) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Ḥammād al-Miṣrī narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl ibn Khālid, from Muḥammad ibn Muslim, that Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik al-Quraẓī informed him that Qays ibn Saʿd al-Anṣārī — who was the bearer of the standard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — intended to perform Hajj and combed one side of his head. His servant stood up and garlanded his sacrificial animal. Qays looked and saw that one side of his head had been combed while his sacrificial animal had already been garlanded, so he entered the state of iḥrām for Hajj and did not comb the other side of his head.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam from al-Layth, in abridged form, up to his words ""and he combed."" The purpose of the hadith was the mention of the standard. [Authentic. Bukhārī]"
"13063","(13058) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Ḥātim narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, who said: ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) had stayed behind from the Prophet ﷺ at Khaybar, and he had sore eyes. He said, ""Shall I stay behind from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" So he went out and caught up with the Prophet ﷺ. When it was the evening of the night in which Allah granted victory the following morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Tomorrow I shall give the flag — or the flag shall be taken — by a man whom Allah and His Messenger love,"" or he said, ""who loves Allah and His Messenger, and Allah will grant him victory."" And there was ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), though we had no hope of that. They said, ""This is ʿAlī."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him the flag, and Allah granted him victory.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Authentic. Bukhārī, Muslim]"
"13064","(13059) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qabbānī narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, who said: Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim narrated to me, saying: I heard al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib (may Allah be pleased with him) say to al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām (may Allah be pleased with him): ""O Abū ʿAbd Allāh, was it here that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded you to plant the standard?"" Abū Kurayb added: ""On the day of the Conquest of Makkah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb. [Good]"
"13065","(13060) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Sharīk narrated from ʿAmmār al-Duhnī, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who attributed it to the Prophet ﷺ: that his banner was white on the day he entered Makkah. [Weak]"
"13066","(13061) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: It was recited to al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram while I was listening: Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq al-Sālaḥānī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, saying: I heard Abā Mujliz narrate from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The banners of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — or the banner of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — were black, and his standard was white."""
"13067","(13062) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā al-Rāzī informed us: Ibn Abī Zāʾidah informed us: Abū Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī informed us: Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, a man from Thaqīf, a client of Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim, narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim sent me to al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib to ask him about the banner of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — what was it like? He said: ""It was black, square, made of a striped woolen garment (namirah)."" [Weak]"
"13068","(13063) And Abū ʿAlī informed us: Abū Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us: Salm ibn Qutaybah narrated from Shuʿbah, from Simāk, from a man from his people, from another among them, who said: ""I saw the banner of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; it was yellow."" [Weak]"
"13069","(13064) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us: Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from ʿĀṣim, from Zirr, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""The first banner that was raised in Islam was that of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh."" [Good]"
"13070","(13065) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī narrated from his father that a man came to Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was in the mosque at Minā and asked him about letting the end of the turban hang down. Ibn ʿUmar said to him: ""I shall inform you about it, if Allah wills, with knowledge. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched a military expedition and appointed ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) over it, and he tied a standard for him and said: 'Take it in the name of Allah and with His blessing.'"" And he mentioned the hadith, saying: ""ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) was wearing a turban of coarse cloth dyed black. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called him and untied his turban, then tied a turban for him with his own hand, leaving the end of his turban approximately four fingers' length, or thereabouts, and said: 'Turban yourself in this manner, for it is better and more beautiful.'""

ʿUthmān ibn ʿAṭāʾ is not strong. [Weak]"
"13071","(13066) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr — narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus — narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Aṭāʾ — from his father — from Ibn ʿUmar that the Prophet (ﷺ) sent ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) on a military expedition and tied the banner with his own hand. [Weak]"
"13072","(13067) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Baṭṭah — narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Jahm — narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faraj — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar al-Wāqidī — who said: al-Nuʿmān ibn Muqrin was among those who carried one of the banners of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and was the bearer of the banner of Muzaynah, which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had tied for them on the day of the Conquest of Makkah. [Very Weak]"
"13073","(13068) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Shawdhab al-Wāsiṭī — informed us; Aḥmad ibn Rashīd ibn Khuthaym al-Kūfī — informed us; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh — from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Nujūd — who said: al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥassān al-Bakrī said: I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was upon the pulpit and Bilāl was standing, girded with a sword, and there were black banners, and the people were saying: ""This is ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ; he has arrived.""

Thus did Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrate it from ʿĀṣim. And Salām ibn al-Mundhir narrated it from ʿĀṣim — from Abū Wāʾil — from al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥassān, and he said in its text: ""And there was a black banner fluttering, so I said: 'What is the matter with the people today?' They said: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) wishes to send ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ on an expedition.'"" [Good]"
"13074","(13069) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād — informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Miṣrī — narrated to us; Ibn Abī Maryam — narrated to us; al-Firyābī — narrated to us; Sufyān — from al-Aʿmash — from Abū Wāʾil — from Ḥudhayfah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Write down for me the names of those among the people who have uttered the declaration of Islam."" So one thousand five hundred men were written down for him. I said: ""Shall we fear, when we are one thousand five hundred men?"" He said: ""We were tested to such an extent that a man among us would pray alone in a state of fear.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firyābī. He said: And Abū Ḥamzah narrated from al-Aʿmash: ""We found them to be five hundred."" And Abū Muʿāwiyah said: ""Between six hundred and seven hundred."" [Authentic. Bukhārī 3060. Muslim 149]"
"13075","(13070) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād — informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh — narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān — narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān — informed us; ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn al-Mubārak — informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Wahb — who said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me — who said: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: I came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī with eight hundred thousand dirhams. He said to me: ""What have you brought?"" I said: ""I have brought eight hundred thousand dirhams."" He said: ""You have brought eighty thousand dirhams!"" I said: ""Rather, I have brought eight hundred thousand dirhams."" He said: ""Did I not tell you that you are a foolish Yemenī? You have brought eighty thousand dirhams."" So I said: ""How much is eight hundred thousand?"" And I counted a hundred thousand and a hundred thousand until I counted eight hundred thousand. He said: ""Is it sound?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then ʿUmar spent his night in great restlessness until, when the call for the Fajr prayer was given, his wife said to him: 'O Commander of the Faithful, you have not slept tonight!' He said: 'How can ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb sleep when the people have come with the like of which has not come to them since Islam began? And ʿUmar would not be safe were he to perish and that wealth be with him without having placed it in its rightful place.'""

""When he prayed Fajr, a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gathered around him, and he said to them: 'The people have come this night with the like of which has not come to them since Islam began, and I have formed an opinion — advise me. I think that I should distribute to the people by measure.' They said: 'Do not do so, O Commander of the Faithful. The people are entering Islam and the wealth is abundant. Rather, give them according to a register, and whenever the people increase and the wealth increases, give them accordingly.' He said: 'Then advise me — with whom should I begin?' They said: 'With yourself, O Commander of the Faithful, for you are the guardian of this matter.' And some of them said: 'The Commander of the Faithful knows best.' He said: 'No, rather I shall begin with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then the nearest of kin to him, then the next nearest.' So he established the register upon that.""

ʿUbayd Allāh said: ""He began with Hāshim and al-Muṭṭalib and gave to all of them. Then he gave to Banū ʿAbd Shams, then to Banū Nawfal ibn ʿAbd Manāf. He began with Banū ʿAbd Shams because they were the brothers of Hāshim through their mother."" ʿUbayd Allāh said: ""The first to differentiate between Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib in the register was ʿAbd al-Malik, and he mentioned a story regarding that."" [Weak]"
"13076","(13071) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan — narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us; al-Shāfiʿī — narrated to us; Sufyān — from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār — from Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī — that when ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) established the registers, he said: ""With whom do you think I should begin?"" It was said to him: ""Begin with the nearest of kin to you."" He said: ""Rather, I shall begin with the nearest of kin to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then the next nearest."" [Weak]"
"13077","(13072) And in what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from him — from Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī — informed me; more than one person of the people of knowledge and truth from the people of Madīnah and Makkah, from the tribes of Quraysh and from others, some of them being more precise in their narration of the hadith than others, and some of them adding to others in the hadith: that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), when he established the registers, said: ""I shall begin with Banū Hāshim."" Then he said: ""I was present with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he would give to them and to Banū al-Muṭṭalib. If the age was the same in a Hāshimī, he would give him precedence over a Muṭṭalibī, and if it was the same in a Muṭṭalibī, he would give him precedence over a Hāshimī."" So he established the register upon that and gave them the stipend of a single tribe.

Then ʿAbd Shams and Nawfal were equal in the root of lineage, so he said: ""ʿAbd Shams are the siblings of the Prophet (ﷺ) through his father and mother, to the exclusion of Nawfal."" So he gave them precedence. Then he called Banū Nawfal to follow them.

Then ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā and ʿAbd al-Dār were equal, so he said regarding Banū Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā: ""They are the in-laws of the Prophet (ﷺ), and among them are al-Muṭayyabūn."" And some of them said: ""They are allies of the Pact of al-Fuḍūl."" And among both of them was the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). And it was said: ""He mentioned precedence."" So he gave them precedence over Banū ʿAbd al-Dār. Then he called Banū ʿAbd al-Dār to follow them.

Then Zuhrah stood alone, so he called them to follow ʿAbd al-Dār.

Then Taym and Makhzūm were equal, so he said regarding Banū Taym: ""They are of the Pact of al-Fuḍūl and al-Muṭayyabūn, and among both of them was the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" And it was said: ""He mentioned precedence."" And it was said: ""He mentioned a marital connection."" So he gave them precedence over Makhzūm. Then he called Makhzūm to follow them.

Then Sahm, Jumah, and ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb were equal, and it was said to him: ""Begin with ʿAdī."" He said: ""Rather, I shall keep myself where I was, for when Islam entered, our affair and the affair of Banū Sahm were one. But look at Banū Jumah and Sahm."" It was said: ""Give precedence to Banū Jumah."" Then he called Banū Sahm. And the register of ʿAdī and Sahm was mixed as though they were a single clan. When his own clan was distinguished, he raised a loud takbīr and then said: ""All praise is due to Allah, who has delivered to me my portion from His Messenger."" Then he called Banū ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: Some of them said that when Abū ʿUbaydah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Jarrāh al-Fihrī saw those who were being given precedence over him, he said: ""Are all of these being called before me?"" He said: ""O Abū ʿUbaydah, be patient as I was patient, or speak to your people. Whoever among them gives you precedence over himself, I shall not prevent him. As for me and Banū ʿAdī, we shall give you precedence over ourselves if you wish."" He said: ""So he gave precedence to Muʿāwiyah after Banū al-Ḥārih ibn Fihr, and he separated between them and Banū ʿAbd Manāf and Banū Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā. And there was a dispute between Banū Sahm and ʿAdī during the time of al-Mahdī, so they separated. Al-Mahdī commanded that Banū ʿAdī be given precedence over Sahm and Jumah on account of their precedence in Islam."" [Weak]"
"13078","(13073) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and others — said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — narrated to us; Bishr ibn Bakr — narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān — informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī — narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Shuʿayb al-Kaysānī — narrated to us; Bishr ibn Bakr — who said: I heard al-Awzāʿī say: Abū ʿAmmār narrated to me — from Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose Banū Kinānah from among Banī Ismāʿīl, and He chose Quraysh from among Banū Kinānah, and He chose Banū Hāshim from among Quraysh, and He chose me from among Banū Hāshim.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of al-Awzāʿī.

The Shaykh said: The beginning in the distribution of stipends was made with Banū Hāshim on account of their closeness to the Prophet (ﷺ), for he is Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Quṣayy ibn Kilāb ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr ibn Mālik ibn al-Naḍr ibn Kinānah ibn Khuzaymudah ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyās ibn Muḍar ibn Nizār ibn Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān ibn Add ibn al-Muqawwam ibn Nāḥūr ibn Tāriḥ ibn Yaʿrub ibn Yashjub ibn Nābit ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Āzar — and he is in the Torah Tāraḥ ibn Nāḥūr ibn Arʿuwā ibn Shāriḥ ibn Fāliḥ ibn Ghābir ibn Shāliḥ ibn Arfakhshad ibn Sām ibn Nūḥ ibn Lamek ibn Mutūshaliḥ ibn Akhnūkh ibn Burd ibn Mahlāʾīl ibn Qamʿān ibn Qūsh ibn Shīth ibn Ādam, the father of mankind (ﷺ), and upon all the prophets of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. [Authentic. Muslim 2276]"
"13079","(13074) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us of it; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī — narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr — from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — who mentioned this lineage.

The Shaykh said: ""Fihr ibn Mālik is the root of Quraysh according to the majority of their statements. Banū Hāshim are united by the third father of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), while the rest of Quraysh are united by some of them at the fourth father, ʿAbd Manāf, and some of them at the fifth father, Quṣayy, and so on up to Fihr ibn Mālik. For this reason, the beginning was made with Banū Hāshim.""

He only joined Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib, the two sons of ʿAbd Manāf, in the stipend because of what we narrated earlier from Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) distributed the share of the near relatives from Khaybar to Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib, I and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) walked to him and I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, these are your brothers, Banū Hāshim — we do not deny their excellence due to the position Allah has given you among them. But what about our brothers from Banū al-Muṭṭalib? You have given them and left us, while we and they are of equal standing with you.' He said: 'They did not separate from us in the Jāhiliyyah nor in Islam. Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib are one thing.'"" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) intertwined his fingers, one hand within the other. [Authentic]"
"13080","(13075) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us of it; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār — narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr — from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed me; al-Zuhrī — from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib — from Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim — and he mentioned it."
"13081","(13076) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: my grandfather Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī narrated to me, from Zayd ibn ʿAlī, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Hāshim and al-Muṭṭalib are like these two"" — and he joined his fingers and intertwined them — ""May Allah curse the one who separates between the two of them. They nurtured us in our childhood, and we carried them in their old age.""

The Sheikh said: ʿUthmān and Jubayr (may Allah be pleased with them both) spoke concerning this matter because ʿUthmān is ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf, and Jubayr is Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdī ibn Nawfal ibn ʿAbd Manāf. Hāshim, al-Muṭṭalib, ʿAbd Shams, and Nawfal were brothers. He gave the share of dhī al-qurbā (the near kinship) to Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib, excluding Banū ʿAbd Shams and Banū Nawfal, and said: ""They never separated from me in the Jāhiliyyah nor in Islam. Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib are one and the same."" In the mursal narration it states: ""They nurtured us in our childhood, and we carried them"" — or he said: ""and they carried us in our old age."" He said that — and Allah knows best — because Hāshim ibn ʿAbd Manāf married Salmā bint ʿAmr ibn Labīd ibn Ḥarām from Banū al-Najjār in Madīnah, and she bore him Shaybah al-Ḥamd. Then Hāshim passed away while he was with her. When the boy grew up and came of age, his uncle al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Manāf came to him, took him from his mother, and brought him to Makkah, with him riding behind on his mount. It was said: ""A slave whom al-Muṭṭalib owned,"" and that name prevailed over him, so he was called ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was sent with the Message, his people harmed him and plotted against him, so Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib — their Muslims and their disbelievers alike — stood by him and refused to hand him over. When Quraysh realized that there was no way to reach Muḥammad (ﷺ) while they were with him, they agreed among themselves to write a pact against Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib that they would not marry them, nor give them in marriage, nor buy from them, nor sell to them. Abū Ṭālib took them into the Shiʿb — the Shiʿb of Abū Ṭālib — in a corner of Makkah. Quraysh persisted in this matter concerning Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib for two or three years, until they endured severe hardship. Then Allah, the Most High, by His mercy, sent the earth-creatures (al-arḍah) upon the scroll of Quraysh, and it consumed every name of Allah upon it, leaving only the words of injustice, severance, and slander. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was informed of this, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) informed Abū Ṭālib of it, and Abū Ṭālib used it to seek help against his people. Hishām ibn ʿAmr ibn Rabīʿah stood up, along with a group whom Ibn Isḥāq mentioned in the Maghāzī, to annul what was in the scroll and to tear it apart. For this reason, the Commander of the Faithful ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) combined Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib in all grants and gave them precedence over Banū ʿAbd Shams and Banū Nawfal. The initial precedence was given to Banū ʿAbd Shams before Banū Nawfal because Hāshim, al-Muṭṭalib, and ʿAbd Shams were full brothers — their mother being ʿĀtikah bint Murrah — while Nawfal was their half-brother on their father's side, his mother being Wāqidah bint Ḥarmal. ʿAbd Manāf, ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, and ʿAbd al-Dār, the sons of Quṣayy, were brothers. The precedence thereafter was given to Banū ʿAbd Manāf, and it was given to Banū ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā because they were the tribe of Khadījah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ) — she being Khadījah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā. It has been said that they were among the Muṭayyabīn."
"13082","(13077) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Naysābūrī informed us: Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal informed us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Isḥāq, from al-Zuhrī, from Muḥammad ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, from his father, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I witnessed, as a young boy, the Ḥilf al-Muṭayyabīn, and I would not love to break it even if I were given red camels."""
"13083","(13078) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā al-Adīb narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Qabbānī narrated to us: Abū Hishām al-Muʾammal ibn Hishām al-Yashkurī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl — that is, Ibn ʿUlayyah — narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Isḥāq, and he mentioned it with its chain and its meaning, except that he said: ""I witnessed it together with my paternal uncles."""
"13084","(13079) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Saʿīd al-Mawṣilī narrated to us: al-Muʿallā ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Abū ʿAwānah narrated to us, from ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I did not witness any pact except the pact of Quraysh — the Ḥilf al-Muṭayyabīn — and I would not love to have red camels in exchange for it, even though I broke it."" The Muṭayyabīn are Hāshim, Umayyah, Zuhrah, and Makhzūm.

The Sheikh said: I do not know whether this explanation is from Abū Hurayrah or from someone below him. The Sheikh said: It has been conveyed to me that it was called the Ḥilf al-Muṭayyabīn because they dipped their hands in perfume on the day they made their pact and shook hands with their oaths. This was at the time when a dispute arose between Banū ʿAbd Manāf and Banū ʿAbd al-Dār over what was in their custody of the siqāyah, the ḥijābah, the rifādah, the liwāʾ, and the nadwah. Banū Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, along with a group of the tribes of Quraysh, were in alliance with Banū ʿAbd Manāf, and thus they had honor, virtue, and a bond of favor with Banū ʿAbd Manāf. Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yāsār named them and said: ""The Muṭayyabīn among the tribes of Quraysh are Banū ʿAbd Manāf — Hāshim, al-Muṭṭalib, ʿAbd Shams, and Nawfal — and Banū Zuhrah, Banū Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, Banū Taym, and Banū al-Ḥārith ibn Fihr — five tribes."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: Some of them said it was a pact of the Fuḍūl."
"13085","(13080) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Muḥammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhājir ibn Qunfudh narrated to me, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwf, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I witnessed in the house of ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān a pact for which I would not love to have red camels in exchange, and if I were called to it in Islam, I would respond."" Al-Qutaybī said, as it has been conveyed to me from him: The cause of the pact was that Quraysh used to wrong one another within the Ḥaram, so ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān and al-Zubayr ibn al-Muṭṭalib stood and called them to a pact of mutual support and of taking up the cause of the oppressed against the oppressor. Banū Hāshim and some of the tribes of Quraysh responded to them. The Sheikh said: Ibn Isḥāq named them. He said: Banū Hāshim ibn ʿAbd Manāf, Banū al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Manāf, Banū Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy, Banū Zuhrah ibn Kilāb, and Banū Taym ibn Murrah. Al-Qutaybī said: They made their pact in the house of ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān, and they called that pact the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl, likening it to a pact that had been made in Makkah in the days of Jurhum, based on mutual fairness and taking up the cause of the weak against the strong and the stranger against the resident. Men from Jurhum stood for it whose names were al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥārith, al-Faḍl ibn Wadāʿah, and al-Fuḍayl ibn Faḍālah, so it was called the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl, a collection of these names. Others besides al-Qutaybī said regarding their names: Faḍl, Faḍḍāl, Fuḍayl, and Faḍālah. Al-Qutaybī said: Al-Fuḍūl is the plural of faḍl, just as one says saʿd and suʿūd, and zayd and zuyūd. As for what is in the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf — the Ḥilf al-Muṭayyabīn — al-Qutaybī said: I believe he intended the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl, based on the other ḥadīth, and because the Muṭayyabīn were those who concluded the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl. He said: What virtue could there be in such a first pact that the Prophet (ﷺ) would say, ""I would not love to break it even if I were given red camels""? Rather, he intended the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl that the Muṭayyabīn concluded. Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Marwazī said: Some of the people of knowledge regarding the sīrah and the days of the people said that his statement in this ḥadīth — ""the Ḥilf al-Muṭayyabīn"" — is an error; rather, it is the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl. This is because the Prophet (ﷺ) did not witness the Ḥilf al-Muṭayyabīn, as that was a long time before he was born by a considerable period. As for the sābiqah (precedence) that is mentioned, it may be that he intended by it the precedence of Khadījah (may Allah be pleased with her) in embracing Islam, for she was the first woman to become Muslim."
"13086","(13081) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us by dictation: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Usāmah al-Ḥalabī narrated to us: Ḥajjaj ibn Abī Manīʿ narrated to me: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Ziyād narrated to me, from al-Zuhrī, who said: Khadījah (may Allah be pleased with her) was the first to believe in the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."
"13087","(13082) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim al-Sayyārī in Marw informed us: Abū al-Muwajjah informed us: Ṣadaqah narrated to us: ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, who said: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar saying: I heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) saying: ""The best woman of her time is Maryam ibn ʿImrān, and the best woman of her time is Khadījah bint Khuwaylid.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq al-Ḥanẓalī from ʿAbdah. It is likely that by the sābiqah (precedence) he intended the precedence of al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, for al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy was among those who embraced Islam early."
"13088","(13083) With this chain of narration: Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Abū ʿUlāthah Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Khālid al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah narrated to us: Abū al-Aswad narrated to us, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr."
"13089","(13084) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us: Abū al-Aswad narrated to me, from ʿUrwah, who said: Al-Zubayr embraced Islam when he was eight years old. ʿUrwah said: A rumor spread from Shayṭān that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had been seized at the upper part of Makkah. So al-Zubayr went out as a boy of twelve years, with a sword. Whoever saw him and did not recognize him would say: ""The boy has a sword,"" until he came to the Prophet (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: ""What is the matter with you, O Zubayr?"" He said: ""I was told that you had been seized."" He said: ""Then what would you have done?"" He said: ""I would have struck the neck of the one who seized you."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) invoked blessings for him and for his sword, and it was the first sword drawn in the cause of Allah."
"13090","(13085) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Qutaybah al-Ghanawī narrated to us in Kūfah: Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn Mūsā al-Ḥammār narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: al-Firyābī narrated to us. Sufyān mentioned, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on the day of al-Aḥzāb: ""Who will bring me news of the people?"" Al-Zubayr said: ""I will."" Then he said again: ""Who will bring me news of the people?"" Al-Zubayr said: ""I will."" Then he said again: ""Who will bring me news of the people?"" Al-Zubayr said: ""I will."" So the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Every prophet has a ḥawārī (disciple), and my ḥawārī is al-Zubayr.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it from another route from al-Thawrī. Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated it from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Al-Zubayr is the son of my paternal aunt and my ḥawārī from my family."""
"13091","(13086) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiya narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwa, and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it from Abū Kurayb from Abū Usāma from Hishām.

And it is likely that by this ""precedence"" (sābiqa) is meant the steadfastness of al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) alongside a group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him) with the Prophet (peace be upon him) on the day of Uḥud, and their pledge of allegiance to him unto death. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13092","(13087) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb al-ʿAdl informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-ʿAbdī informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid informed us, from al-Bahī, from ʿUrwa, who said: ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said to me: ""O my son, verily your father is among those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after the wound had befallen them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"13093","(13088) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiya narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwa from his father, who said: ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O son of my sister, verily your two fathers — meaning al-Zubayr and Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) — are among those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after the wound had befallen them."" She said: ""When the polytheists withdrew from Uḥud and the Prophet (peace be upon him) and his Companions had suffered what they suffered, he feared that they might return. So he said: 'Who will volunteer to follow their tracks so that they may know that we have strength?' So Abū Bakr and al-Zubayr volunteered, along with seventy men, and they went out in pursuit of the people. They [the enemy] heard of them and turned back, by the grace and bounty of Allah."" He said: ""They encountered no enemy.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad from Abū Muʿāwiya.

As for Zurara, he was a brother of Quṣayy ibn Kilāb, and among his children from the [ten] were ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ. [Ṣaḥīḥ — previously mentioned]"
"13094","(13089) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Abū ʿUlātha narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿa narrated from Abū al-Aswad from ʿUrwa, regarding those who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) from Banū Zuhra ibn Kilāb ibn Murra: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAwf ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Zuhra, and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ ibn Wahb ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Zuhra. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13095","(13090) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ibn Judʿān from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: ""Saʿd — meaning Ibn Abī Waqqāṣ — came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: 'Who am I, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'Saʿd ibn Mālik ibn Wahb ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Zuhra. Whoever says other than this, upon him be the curse of Allah.'""

As for Taym, they were brothers of Kilāb. As for Makhzūm, he was not their brother; rather, he was Makhzūm ibn Yaqẓa ibn Murra, except that the tribe became well-known by Makhzūm, so it was attributed to him. Banū Taym were given precedence over Banū Makhzūm because they were among the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl and the Muṭayyibīn. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13096","(13091) And it was said that the mention of ""precedence"" (sābiqa) refers to the precedence of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), for Abū Bakr was ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿĀmir ibn ʿAmr ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murra ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13097","(13092) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Usāma al-Ḥalabī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Abī Manīʿ narrated from his grandfather from al-Zuhrī, and he mentioned this lineage. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13098","(13093) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Ḥajjāj ibn Abī Manīʿ narrated from his grandfather from al-Zuhrī, and he mentioned it, except that he said: ""ʿAtīq"" instead of ""ʿAbdullāh."" Then he said: ""ʿAtīq is a title (laqab), and his name is ʿAbdullāh.""

The Shaykh said: ""And he was the first to accept Islam among free men."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13099","(13094) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Mujālad narrated from Bayān from Wabara ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Hammām ibn al-Ḥārith, who said: ""I heard ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir say: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and there were with him no more than five slaves, two women, and Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him).'""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh from Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 3660]"
"13100","(13095) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shīrūya narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAẓīm narrated to us: al-Naḍr ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: ʿIkrama ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us: Shaddād ibn ʿAbdullāh — Abū ʿAmmār — and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from Abū Umāma, who said: ʿAmr ibn ʿAbsa al-Sulamī said — and he mentioned his coming to the Prophet (peace be upon him) in Makka — he said: ""I said to him: 'What are you?' He said: 'I am called a Prophet.' I said: 'What is a Prophet?' He said: 'Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has sent me.' I said: 'With what has He sent you?' He said: 'He has sent me with the joining of kinship ties, the breaking of idols, and that Allah alone be worshipped and nothing be associated with Him.' I said to him: 'Who is with you?' He said: 'A free man and a slave.' He said: 'And with him on that day were Abū Bakr and Bilāl, among those who had believed in him.' And he mentioned the ḥadīth.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar from al-Naḍr ibn Muḥammad. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 832]"
"13101","(13096) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Mālik ibn Miʿghwal from a man who said: Ibn ʿAbbās was asked, ""Who was the first to believe?"" He said: ""Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Have you not heard the saying of Ḥassān:

'When you remember the grief of a brother in whom you have confidence, then remember your brother Abū Bakr for what he did—

The best of creation in faithfulness and justice, after the Prophet, and the foremost in bearing what he bore—

The second in rank, whose station is praised, and the first of the people to confirm the Messengers.'

He lived praiseworthy, following the command of Allah, upon the guidance of his departed companion and what he conveyed.""

The Sheikh said: It is as though by ""the precedence"" among Banū Taym he intends the steadfastness of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, among a group of the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, alongside the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the day of Uḥud, among them being Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, who was also from Banū Taym, for he is Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿAmr ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murrah. [Weak]"
"13102","(13097) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us with this chain: Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Abū ʿUlāthah narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah narrated to us: Abū al-Aswad narrated from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, and he mentioned it. Likewise, al-Zuhrī and others mentioned the steadfastness of Ṭalḥah with the Prophet (ﷺ) on the day of Uḥud, and that Mālik ibn Zuhayr threw a stone at the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on that day, so Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh shielded the face of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) with his hand, and it struck his finger, rendering it paralyzed.

Al-Wāqidī mentioned it with his chain. [Weak]"
"13103","(13098) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Khālid ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid narrated from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim who said: I saw the hand of Ṭalḥah with which he had shielded the Prophet (ﷺ), and it had become paralyzed.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Authentic. Bukhārī 3724]"
"13104","(13099) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me from his father, from his grandfather, from al-Zubayr, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he went to ascend the rock. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had worn two coats of mail that day, and he was unable to ascend it. So Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh sat beneath him, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ascended upon him until he settled upon it. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then said: ""Ṭalḥah has earned [his reward].""

Ibn Isḥāq mentioned that Ṭalḥah was among the eight who preceded the people in Islam. As for the kinship tie he mentioned among Banū Taym, it is on account of ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with them both, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), the beloved of the beloved of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. [Good]"
"13105","(13100) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ and Abū Manṣūr Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-ʿAtakī both said: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: al-Muʿallā ibn Asad narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ narrated to us: Abū ʿUthmān narrated to me: ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) sent him to the army of Dhāt al-Salāsil. He said: ""I came to him and said: 'Who among the people is most beloved to you?' He said: 'ʿĀʾishah.' I said: 'Among the men?' He said: 'Her father.' I said: 'Then who?' He said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.' He said: Then he enumerated several men.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Muʿallā ibn Asad, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ.

And it has been narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said to Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her: ""Do you not love whom I love?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then love this one""—meaning ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. And he said to Umm Salamah: ""Do not hurt me regarding ʿĀʾishah, for by Allah, revelation has never come to me while I was in the bed-clothes of any of you other than her.""

As for ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb, he was the brother of Murrah ibn Kaʿb.

As for Sahm and Jamah, they are the sons of ʿAmr ibn Ḥuṣayṣ ibn Kaʿb, except that the tribe became renowned by them, so it was attributed to them. The precedence of Banū Jamah was given on account of Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf ibn Wahb ibn Ḥudhāfah ibn Jamah, and what he did on the day of Ḥunayn of lending weapons, and his statement when Abū Sufyān and Kaladah said what they said: ""May Allah shatter your mouth! By Allah, that a man from Quraysh should raise me is more beloved to me than that a man from Hawāzin should raise me""—and he was at that time a polytheist. Then he later emigrated and embraced Islam. It is also said that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, did this deliberately to delay his right, for he is ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb ibn Nufayl ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Rabāḥ ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Qurṭ ibn Rāzāḥ ibn ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr. [Authentic]"
"13106","(13101) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us with this chain: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us: Ḥajjāj narrated from his grandfather, from al-Zuhrī, and he mentioned it, stating that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, gave them precedence over their tribe. When the time of al-Mahdī came, al-Mahdī gave the command regarding Banū ʿAdī, so they were given precedence over Sahm and Jamah on account of their early precedence, which is the precedence of ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him. It has been narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said: ""O Allah, honor Islam through ʿUmar."" [Authentic]"
"13107","(13102) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Fārisī narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Uwaysī narrated to us: al-Mājishūn ibn Abī Salamah narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O Allah, honor Islam especially through ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb."" [Authentic]"
"13108","(13103) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādī narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Rāzī narrated to us: Abū ʿAlqamah al-Farwī al-Madanī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Mājishūn narrated from Muslim ibn Khālid from Hishām, and he mentioned it in similar terms. [Authentic]"
"13109","(13104) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us. And Abū Zakariyyā informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid narrated from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim. And in the narration of Jaʿfar and Muḥammad, they both said: [Authentic. Bukhārī 3863]"
"13110","(13105) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad al-Sammāk narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl narrated from Qays who said: ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, said: ""We have not ceased to be honored since ʿUmar embraced Islam.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid.

As for the statement of ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him: Islam came and our affair and the affair of Banū Sahm were one, because Banū Sahm had been allies of Banū ʿAdī in the Jāhiliyyah. Banū Jamah gathered against Banū ʿAdī for a blood-feud between them, so Sahm—the brothers of Jamah—rose up on their behalf and said: ""ʿAdī are fewer in number than you. If you wish, send out against them a number from among yourselves equal to their number, and we will leave between you and them [to fight]. Or if you wish, we will count them as part of us until they are equal to you, and then you may desist from one another."" This was stated by al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār.

As for Abū ʿUbaydah, he is ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Jarrāḥ ibn Hilāl ibn Uhaybah ibn Ḍabbah ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Fihr ibn Mālik. This was stated by Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq and others."
"13111","(13106) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū 'Amr informed me; Abū Ya'lā narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Abū Khaythamah both said: Ismā'īl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, from Khālid, from Abū Qilābah, who said: Anas — may Allah be pleased with him — said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Verily, every nation has its trustworthy one, and the trustworthy one of this nation is Abū 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr and Abū Khaythamah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another route from Khālid.

The Sheikh said: Abū 'Ubaydah was delayed in receiving stipends due to the distance of his lineage, not due to any deficiency in his honour. He is superior to some of those who preceded him, despite being among the Quraysh from among the broader relatives.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim]"
"13112","(13107) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣabīḥ informed me; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; 'Umar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us; my father narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated to me; 'Amr ibn Murrah narrated to us; Sa'īd ibn Jubayr narrated to us; from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: When the verse {And warn your closest kinsfolk} [al-Shu'arā': 214] was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended al-Ṣafā and began calling out: ""O Banū Fihr! O Banū 'Adī! O Banū so-and-so!"" — addressing the clans of Quraysh — until they gathered, and he mentioned the hadith.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Umar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth. And in it is evidence that Banū Fihr are from Quraysh.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13113","(13108) Abū 'Amr al-Adīb informed me; Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed me; al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us; Rajā' ibn Murajjā' al-Marwazī narrated to us; Shādhān ibn 'Uthmān ibn Jabalah ibn Abī Rawād — the brother of 'Abdān — narrated to us; 'Uthmān, meaning his father, narrated to us; Shu'bah ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us; Hishām ibn Zayd narrated to us. He said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say: Abū Bakr and al-'Abbās — may Allah be pleased with them both — passed by a gathering of the Anṣār while they were weeping. He said: ""What makes you weep?"" They said: ""We remember our gatherings with the Prophet ﷺ."" So Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — went to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. He came out with his head wrapped with the edge of a garment. He ascended the pulpit — and he did not ascend it again after that — praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: ""I enjoin you regarding the Anṣār, for they are my inner circle and my trusted ones. They have fulfilled what was upon them, and what remains is what is due to them. So accept from those among them who do good, and overlook the faults of those who do wrong.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz from Shādhān.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13114","(13109) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; Abū Muḥammad 'Abdillāh ibn Ja'far ibn Aḥmad ibn Fāris narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from Qatādah. He said: I heard Anas narrating from Abū Usayd al-Anṣārī that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""The best households of the Anṣār are Banū al-Najjār, then Banū 'Abd al-Ashhal, then Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, and Banū Sā'idah; and in every household of the Anṣār there is good."" He said: It was said: ""He has given us preference."" It was said: ""He has given you preference over many.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from Abū Dāwūd, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another route from Shu'bah, and he said: ""Then Banū Sā'idah."" And in it he said: Sa'd, meaning Ibn 'Ubaydah, said: ""I do not think the Prophet ﷺ except that he has given us preference."" It was said: ""He has given you preference over many.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2511]"
"13115","(13110) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn 'Amr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us; al-Qa'nabī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; 'Abbās ibn Sahl narrated to us; from Abū Ḥumayd, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the expedition of Tabūk, and he mentioned the hadith regarding his departure and return. He said: Until we overlooked al-Madīnah, and he said: ""This is Ṭābah, and this is Uḥud — and it is a mountain that loves us and we love it."" Then he said: ""Verily, the best households of the Anṣār are the household of Banū al-Najjār, then the household of Banū 'Abd al-Ashhal, then the household of Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, then the household of Banū Sā'idah; and in every household of the Anṣār there is good."" Then Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah met us, and Abū Usayd said: ""Did you not see that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the households of the Anṣār to be the best and placed our household last?"" Sa'd caught up with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you declared the households of the Anṣār to be the best and placed us last."" He said: ""Is it not sufficient for you that you be among the best?""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qa'nabī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1492]"
"13116","(13111) Ma'n ibn 'Īsā said: I heard Mālik ibn Anas say: Whoever curses the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has no right in the fay' (war spoils). Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: {For the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties, seeking bounty from Allah and His pleasure} [al-Hashr: 8] — these are the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who emigrated with him. Then He says: {And those who established themselves in the home and in faith} [al-Hashr: 9] — these are the Anṣār. Then He says: {And those who came after them} [al-Hashr: 10]. Mālik said: So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, made an exception, saying: {They say: Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith} [al-Hashr: 10]. So the fay' belongs to these three groups. Whoever curses the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is not among these three, and he has no right in the fay'.

[Ḥasan]"
"13117","(13112) Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Alī al-Muqri' informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Isfarāyīnī narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn al-'Alā' narrated to us; Ismā'īl ibn Umayyah narrated to us; from Yaḥyā ibn 'Abdillāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Ṣayfī, who heard Abū Ma'bad say: I heard Ibn 'Abbās — may Allah be pleased with them both — say: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Mu'ādh ibn Jabal towards Yemen, he said: ""You are going to a people from the People of the Book. Let the first thing you call them to be the Tawḥīd (Oneness) of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. When they have acknowledged that, inform them that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has obligated upon them five prayers in their day and night. When they have prayed, inform them that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has obligated upon them a zakāh on their wealth, taken from their wealthy and returned to their poor. When they have accepted that, then take from them, and beware of the most precious of their wealth.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdillāh ibn Abī al-Aswad from al-Faḍl ibn al-'Alā', and Muslim narrated it through another route from Ismā'īl.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1395, 1496. Muslim 19]"
"13118","(13113) Abū 'Amr Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed us; 'Abdillāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Nāji'ah narrated to us; Ibn Abī al-Naḍr said: Abū al-Naḍr narrated to me. He said: Abū Bakr informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; 'Alī ibn Sa'īd narrated to us; Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us; 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Abdillāh narrated to us; from his father; from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Sammān; from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever is given wealth by Allah and does not pay its zakāh, it will be made for him on the Day of Resurrection in the form of a bald serpent with two black spots. It will be placed around his neck on the Day of Resurrection. Then it will take hold by his jowls — meaning his cheeks — and say: 'I am your wealth! I am your treasure!'"" Then he recited this verse: {Let not those who withhold what Allah has given them of His bounty think that it is good for them} [Āl 'Imrān: 180] to the end of the verse.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Alī ibn al-Madīnī from Abū al-Naḍr.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1402, 1403. Muslim 987]"
"13119","(13114) Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Juwaynī narrated to us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Rajā' al-Sindī narrated to us. (Ḥ) He said: And 'Alī ibn 'Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me; 'Abdillāh ibn Muḥammad al-Baghawī narrated to us. Both said: Suwayd ibn Sa'īd narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah narrated to us; from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Abū Ṣāliḥ Dhakwān informed him that he heard Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no owner of gold or silver who does not pay its due right except that, when the Day of Resurrection comes, plates of fire will be prepared for him. They will be heated in the Fire of Jahannam, and his side, his forehead, and his back will be branded with them. Whenever they cool down, they will be reheated for him, in a day the extent of which is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the servants, and he will see his path — either to Paradise or to Fire."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about camels?"" He said: ""And there is no owner of camels who does not pay their due right — and among their rights is that they be milked on the day they come to water — except that, when the Day of Resurrection comes, a vast, level plain will be spread out for them, more abundant than they ever were, not missing a single young one. They will trample him with their hooves and bite him with their mouths. Whenever the first of them passes over him, the last of them will be returned to him, in a day the extent of which is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the servants, and he will see his path — either to Paradise or to Fire."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about cattle and sheep?"" He said: ""And there is no owner of sheep or cattle who does not pay their due right except that, when the Day of Resurrection comes, a vast, level plain will be spread out for them, not missing anything — among them there will be no twisted-horned, nor broken-horned, nor defective ones. They will gore him with their horns and trample him with their hooves. Whenever the first of them passes over him, the last of them will be returned to him, in a day the extent of which is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the servants, and he will see his path — either to Paradise or to Fire."" Then he mentioned the remainder of the hadith, which I have cited in the Book of Zakāh. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Suwayd ibn Sa'īd. His statement: ""And among their rights is that they be milked on the day they come to water"" — it is likely that this is from the words of Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him.

And we have narrated in the Book of Zakāh from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, in this hadith: ""And there is no owner of camels who does not pay their zakāh except that a vast, level plain will be spread out for them.""

[Al-Bukhārī 140. Muslim 20]"
"13120","(13115) Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; 'Ubayd ibn al-Wāḥid narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from 'Uqayl; from Ibn Shihāb, who said: 'Ubaydullāh ibn 'Abdillāh ibn 'Utbah ibn Mas'ūd informed me that Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — informed him, and he said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away and Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — was appointed as khalīfah after him, and those among the Arabs who disbelieved disbelieved, 'Umar said: ""O Abū Bakr, how can you fight the people when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. Whoever says: There is no god but Allah has protected from me his wealth and his person, except by its right, and his reckoning is with Allah'?"" Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — said: ""By Allah, I will surely fight whoever separates between the prayer and the zakāh, for the zakāh is the right of wealth. By Allah, if they withhold from me a young she-goat that they used to give to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I will fight them for withholding it."" 'Umar — may Allah be pleased with him — said: ""By Allah, it was only that I saw Allah had opened the chest of Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — to fighting, and I knew that it was the truth.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr with this wording: ""a young she-goat.""

[Al-Bukhārī 1400, 1457]"
"13121","(13116) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Layth narrated it with the same wording, except that he said ""ʿiqālan"" instead. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd, and he also said ""ʿiqālan.""

It was also narrated by Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah from al-Zuhrī, and he said: ""ʿanāqan."" Maʿmar and al-Zubaydī likewise reported it from al-Zuhrī. And Rabbāḥ ibn Zayd narrated it from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī, and he said ""ʿanāqan,"" while in another narration from him he said: ""ʿiqālan."" Ibn Wahb likewise reported it from Yūnus from al-Zuhrī. And ʿAnbasah narrated it from Yūnus from al-Zuhrī, and he said: ""ʿanāqan."" There was a difference of opinion regarding Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī's report from al-Zuhrī: in one instance it was reported from him as ""ʿanāqan"" and in another as ""ʿiqālan."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13122","(13117) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, from Abī ʿUbayd, from al-Kisāʾī, who said: ""Al-ʿiqāl is the ṣadaqah of the year."" And from al-Aṣmaʿī, who said: It is said, 'So-and-so was sent to collect the ʿiqāl of the Banū so-and-so,' meaning he was sent to collect their ṣadaqāt (zakāh). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13123","(13118) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Manṣūr ibn Salamah narrated us: Ḥizām ibn Hishām ibn Ḥubaysh al-Khuzāʿī narrated, saying: I heard my father say: I saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) tying his waistband with a rope (ʿiqāl), while he was engaged in handling some of the camels of ṣadaqah. Manṣūr said: My recollection is that he used to sell them to the highest bidder; whenever he sold a camel from among them, he would tie his waistband with its rope, and then give that rope in charity—meaning those ropes.

The Shaykh said: ʿImrān ibn Dāwar al-Qaṭṭān narrated from Maʿmar ibn Rāshid from al-Zuhrī from Anas, in the story of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) only said, ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah, and they establish the ṣalāh and pay the zakāh. By Allah, if they withhold from me a young she-goat (ʿanāq) that they used to give to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), I will surely fight them over it.""

We have narrated this addition regarding the establishment of ṣalāh and the payment of zakāh through two other chains from Abū Hurayrah. [Ḥasan]"
"13124","(13119) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah both informed us, saying: Abū Muḥammad Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAnbas narrated, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they say 'Lā ilāha illā Allāh,' establish the ṣalāh, and pay the zakāh. When they do that, their blood and their wealth are inviolable from me, and their reckoning is with Allah."" The Shaykh said: This Abū al-ʿAnbas is Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn ʿUbayd, as stated by al-Bukhārī and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13125","(13120) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us. And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us. Both said: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated, from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they say 'Lā ilāha illā Allāh,' establish the ṣalāh, and pay the zakāh. When they do that, they have protected from me their blood and their wealth, except by its right, and their reckoning is with Allah."" The wording of both their narrations is the same. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13126","(13121) Abū Manṣūr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Dāmighānī informed us in Bihaq from the original of his hearing: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Qazzāz narrated to us in Baṣrah: Abū Hishām al-Rifāʿī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated, from ʾAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: ""Peace be upon you, O young man of the Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib."" He (ﷺ) said: ""And upon you."" He said: ""I am a man from your maternal uncles, from the children of Saʿd ibn Bakr, and I am the messenger of my people to you and their envoy."" Then he mentioned the hadith until he said: ""We have found in Your Book, and Your messengers have commanded us, that we take from the surplus of our wealth and place it among our poor. I adjure you by Allah—did He command you to do that?"" He (ﷺ) said: ""Yes."" [Ḍaʿīf]

The Shaykh said: This wording, if it is preserved, indicates the permissibility of the owner of the wealth distributing the zakāh of his wealth himself. And the hadith of Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) in this story—""Did Allah command you to take this ṣadaqah from your wealthy and distribute it among your poor?""—its chain is more authentic. And Allah knows best."
"13127","(13122) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī in Kūfah narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥuḍaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym and ʿAlī ibn Qādim narrated to us, from Shuʿbah.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated, from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfá (may Allah be pleased with him)—and he was among the Companions of the Tree—say: Whenever a people brought ṣadaqah to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he would say: ""O Allah, send blessings upon them."" Then my father brought his ṣadaqah, and he said: ""O Allah, send blessings upon the family of Abī Awfá."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim reported it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī, Muslim 1078]"
"13128","(13123) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Saʿdī informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Layth narrated, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah ibn Abī Ḥasan al-Anṣārī, from his father Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on fewer than five camels (dhawd), nor on fewer than five awāqī, nor on fewer than five awsuq."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ from Layth, and al-Bukhārī reported it through another chain from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [al-Bukhārī, Muslim 979]"
"13129","(13124) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Munādī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated, from Maʿrūr ibn Suwayd, from Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)… and he mentioned the hadith, in which he said: ""There is no man who dies leaving behind sheep, camels, or cattle without having paid their zakāh, except that they will come to him on the Day of Resurrection at their largest and fattest, trampling him with their hooves and goring him with their horns, until judgment is passed among the people, and then the first of them will return upon the last of them."" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim reported it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of al-Aʿmash, and he termed the obligation due on livestock as ""zakāh."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1460, Muslim 990]"
"13130","(13125) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated, from Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Tammār, from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from ʿAttāb ibn Asīd (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Regarding the zakāh of grapevines, they are estimated just as date palms are estimated, and then their zakāh is paid as dried raisins (zabīb), just as the zakāh of date palms is paid as dried dates (tamr)."" Thus he (ﷺ) termed the ʿushr (tenth) on grapevines and date palms as ""zakāh."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13131","(13126) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān —
(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Muhammad ibn Naṣr al-Asadābādhī informed us therein; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥamdān al-Qaṭīʿī informed us; Abū ʿAlī Bishr ibn Mūsā al-Asadī informed us; Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān al-Muqriʾ, meaning ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd, informed us; ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn Ziyād ibn Anʿum informed us; Ziyād ibn Nuʿaym al-Ḥaḍramī informed me; he said: I heard Ziyād ibn al-Ḥārith al-Ṣudāʾī, may Allah be pleased with him, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrate, saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and gave him my pledge of allegiance upon Islam. He mentioned the hadith and said: Then another man came to him and said: Give me from the charity. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, was not pleased with the ruling of any prophet or anyone else regarding the charities until He Himself decreed regarding them. So He divided them into eight categories. If you are among those categories, I shall give you — or he said: We shall give you your right. [Weak]"
"13132","(13127) Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿUlūsā al-Asadābādhī informed us therein; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥamdān al-Qaṭīʿī informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Sulaymān, meaning al-Qaṭīʿī, informed us; al-Masrūqī, meaning Mūsā ibn ʿAbd al-Rahmān, informed us; Ḥafṣ ibn Rāshid informed us from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī from his father from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ obligated charity upon eight categories, then it is placed into eight shares. He obligated it upon gold, silver, camels, cattle, sheep, crops, grapes, and date palms. And it is placed into eight shares among the people of this verse: {Indeed, charities are only for the poor and the needy…} [al-Tawbah: 60] to the end of the verse. The chain of this hadith is weak, and the text of the book is sufficient. [Weak]"
"13133","(13128) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Ḥibbān informed us; ʿAbdullāh informed us from Zakariyyā ibn Isḥāq from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣayfī from Abū Maʿbad, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Muʿādh ibn Jabal when he sent him to Yemen: You are going to a people of the Book. When you come to them, call them to bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. If they obey you in that, then inform them that Allah has obligated upon them five prayers every day and night. If they obey you in that, then inform them that Allah has obligated upon them a charity that is taken from their wealthy and returned to their poor. If they obey you in that, then beware of the precious possessions of their wealth, and fear the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no barrier between it and Allah. al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥibbān ibn Mūsā. [Authentic — Agreed Upon]"
"13134","(13129) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us —
(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us; they both said: Abū Nuʿaym informed us; Sufyān informed us from Ibrāhīm ibn Mayssarah from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Aswad from ʿAbdullāh ibn Hilāl al-Thaqafī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I was nearly killed after you over a young goat or a sheep from the charity. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Were it not that it is given to the poor among the Muhājirūn, I would not have taken it. [Weak]"
"13135","(13130) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us; Abū Muʿāwiyah informed us from al-Ḥajjāj from al-Minhāl ibn ʿAmr from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh from Ḥudhayfah, who said: When a man gives charity from one category of the eight categories, it suffices him. And from al-Ḥajjāj from ʿAṭāʾ with something similar. al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāʾ is not used as a proof. [Weak]"
"13136","(13131) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Khālid al-Ḥimṣī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Khālid informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah informed us from Wāṣil ibn Ḥayyān and Ḥakīm ibn Jubayr from Shaqīq ibn Salamah, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought the charity of zakāh, and he gave it to a household as it was. [Very Weak]"
"13137","(13132) With this chain of narration, al-Ḥasan informed us from al-Ḥakam from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿAbbās, and from al-Minhāl from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh from Ḥudhayfah, that the two of them did not see any harm in this. And it was narrated by Layth ibn Abī Sulaym from ʿAṭāʾ from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and it is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). And al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah is matrūk (abandoned). [Very Weak]"
"13138","(13133) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Muzanī informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr regarding the verse {Indeed, charities are only for the poor…} [al-Tawbah: 60], he said: It suffices you to place it in one category of these categories. And similarly it was narrated by al-Thawrī, Ḥammād ibn Salamah, and Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān from ʿAṭāʾ from Saʿīd, as his own statement. And it was narrated by Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb from Wuhayb from ʿAṭāʾ from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with both of them. And Allah knows best. [Authentic]"
"13139","(13134) The noble Abū al-Fatḥ informed us; ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn Abī Shurayḥ informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Baghawī informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us; Shuʿbah informed us from al-Ḥakam, who said: I said to Ibrāhīm: Shall I place the zakāh of my wealth in one category of those categories that Allah mentioned in His Book: {Indeed, charities are only for the poor and the needy…} [al-Tawbah: 60] to the end of the verse? He said: Yes. And it was narrated from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī and from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ. [Authentic]"
"13140","(13135) Abū Saʿd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-ʿAbbās informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Hudhayl informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Mukharramī informed us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us. He said: Shuʿbah said: Do you not marvel at this madman Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim? He and Ḥammād ibn Zayd came to me and spoke to me to refrain from mentioning al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah. I will not refrain from mentioning him — no, by Allah, I will not refrain from mentioning him. By Allah, I asked al-Ḥakam about charity being placed in one category of those that Allah named, and he said: There is no harm in it. I said: From whom did you hear it? He said: Ibrāhīm used to say it. And this al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah narrates from al-Ḥakam from Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and from al-Ḥakam from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with both of them, and from al-Ḥakam from Ḥudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: There is no harm in a man placing the charity in one category. [Authentic — al-Kāmil of Ibn ʿAdī 2/284]"
"13141","(13136) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Anbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us; Zakariyyā ibn Isḥāq al-Makkī narrated from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣayfī, from Abū Maʿbad, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent Muʿādh to Yemen, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: ""You are going to a people of the People of the Book. So first invite them to the testimony that there is no deity but Allah. If they respond to that, then inform them that Allah has made obligatory upon them five prayers every day and night. If they respond to that, then inform them that Allah has made obligatory upon them a charity taken from their wealthy and distributed to their poor. If they respond to that, then beware of the choicest of their wealth, and beware of the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no veil between it and Allah."" Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Mūsā from Wakīʿ, and narrated by Muslim from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"13142","(13137) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to me; Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated; Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated that he heard Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the mosque, a man came on a camel. He made it kneel in the mosque, then hobbled it, and said to them: 'Which of you is Muhammad?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was reclining among them. We said to him: 'This white man who is reclining.' The man said to him: 'O son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'I have answered you.' The man said: 'O Muhammad, I am going to ask you, and I shall be emphatic in my question, so do not take offence.' He said: 'Ask whatever you wish.' The man said: 'I adjure you by your Lord and the Lord of those before you: has Allah sent you to all the people?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'O Allah, yes.' He said: 'I adjure you by Allah: has Allah commanded you to pray the five prayers every day and night?' He said: 'O Allah, yes.' He said: 'I adjure you by Allah: has Allah commanded you to fast this month of the year?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'O Allah, yes.' He said: 'I adjure you by Allah: has Allah commanded you to take this charity from our wealthy and distribute it among our poor?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'O Allah, yes.' The man said: 'I believe in what you have brought, and I am the messenger of my people behind me, and I am Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah, brother of Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr.'"" Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 63]"
"13143","(13138) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us; ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʬAṭāʾ ibn Abī Maymūnah narrated to me; my father narrated that ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him) was sent to collect the charity. When he returned, they said to him: ""Where is the wealth?"" He said: ""Did you send me for the wealth? I took it from where we used to take it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and I placed it where we used to place it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13144","(13139) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Ashʿath ibn Sawwār narrated from ʿAwn ibn Abī Juhayfah, from his father, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) sent among us a collector of charity. He took the charity from our wealthy and distributed it among our poor, and he commanded that a young she-camel be given to me."" This hadith is known through Ashʿath ibn Sawwār, and he is not strong. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13145","(13140) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū Umayyah Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Khālid ibn Yazīd narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Zakariyyā narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibn Abī Juhayfah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent a collector of charity and commanded him to take the charity from our wealthy and distribute it among our poor. I was an orphan boy with no wealth, and he gave me a young she-camel from it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13146","(13141) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ granted me permission to narrate it from him, from Abū al-ʿAbbās; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Muṭrif ibn Māzin narrated from Maʿmar, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṭāwūs, from his father, that Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) ruled: ""Any man who transfers from the district of his clan to other than the district of his clan — his ʿushr (tithe) and his charity go to the district of his clan."" [From al-Umm of al-Shāfiʿī, 2/72]"
"13147","(13142) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Marwazī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿImrān al-Hamadhānī narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us; Sawwār ibn Muṣʿab narrated from Ḥammād ibn Abī Sulaymān, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""Zakāh is not to be transferred from one town to another except for a relative."" It is mawqūf, and its chain of narration contains weakness. [Very weak]"
"13148","(13143) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqrīʾ, Ibn al-Ḥammāmī (may Allah have mercy on him), in Baghdād informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Abū ʿAwanah narrated from al-Mughīrah, from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, who said: ""I came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) among a group of my people. He began assigning stipends to men of Ṭayyʾ at two thousand each, while he turned away from me. I said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, do you know me?' He laughed until he fell on his back and said: 'Yes, by Allah, I know you. You believed when they disbelieved, you came forward when they turned back, you fulfilled your pledge when they betrayed. And the first charity that brightened the face of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and the faces of his Companions was the charity of Ṭayyʾ that you brought to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' Then he began to apologize and said: 'I only assigned stipends to a people whom poverty had overwhelmed, and they are the poor among their clans, on account of the obligations that befall them.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ in abridged form from the hadith of Abī ʿAwanah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Aḥmad 1/45, hadith no. 316]"
"13149","(13144) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us; Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Nuʿaym al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us; Hushaym narrated from Mujāld, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim al-Ṭāʾī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him)…"" and he mentioned the hadith with part of its meaning, except that he said: ""And the first charity that brightened the faces of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was the charity of Ṭayyʾ that you brought to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and you said: 'Indeed, I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) in the first year, just as I came to you with it.'"" Similar to the previous hadith."
"13150","(13145) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq regarding the story of ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: ""When ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim accepted Islam, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed him over the charity of his people. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, a great number of camels from their charity had accumulated with him. When those who apostatized among the people did so, and it reached them that they had reclaimed their charities, and Banū Asad — who were their neighbors — also apostatized, the people of Ṭayyʾ gathered to ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, and he mentioned the story. When they saw his firmness, they desisted and submitted to him. When the Muslims rallied to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), he went out with them. The first camels of charity that arrived to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) were his and the camels of al-Zibriqān ibn Badr.""

Ibn Isḥāq said: ""And from the hadith of al-Zibriqān ibn Badr al-Saʿdī is that Banū Saʿd gathered to him and asked him to return their wealth to them and to treat them as Mālik ibn Nuwayrah had treated his people. He refused, held fast to what was in his hand, remained firm upon his Islam, and said: 'Do not be hasty, O people, for by Allah, a successor to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) will surely arise to manage this affair.' He mentioned a story and said: 'He kept them away from himself until the people's rallying to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) reached him. He went out with them, and the people had dispersed from him by night, with men driving them. They did not know of it until word reached them that he had delivered it to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). These were the camels that al-Zibriqān and ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim brought — the first camels of charity to reach Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) after the passing of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""

Ibn Isḥāq said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had sent ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him) over the charity of Ṭayyʾ, al-Zibriqān ibn Badr over the charity of Banū Saʿd, Ṭulayḥah ibn Khuwaylid over the charity of Banū Asad, ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn over the charity of Banū Fazārah, Mālik ibn Nuwayrah over the charity of Banū Yarbūʿ, and al-Fujāʿah over the charity of Banū Sulaym. When news of the Prophet's (ﷺ) death reached them, and they had considerable wealth with them, they returned it to their people — except ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim and al-Zibriqān ibn Badr, for they held fast to it and kept the people away from it until they delivered it to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him)."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13151","(13146) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Abū Yaʿlī informed us, Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf narrated to us, Jarīr, meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, narrated to us from ʿUmārah ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ, from Abū Zurʿah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I will continue to love Banī Tamīm after three things I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: 'They are the fiercest of my Ummah against the Dajjāl.' And there was a slave-girl from among them in the possession of ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon her) said to her: 'Free her, for she is from the progeny of Ismāʿīl (peace be upon him).' And their charitable gifts came, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'These are the charitable gifts of our people.'"" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr. [Bukhārī 2543, Muslim 2525]"
"13152","(13147) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh — he is Ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī — narrated to us from Mālik.
(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī informed us in Baghdād, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad, meaning Ibn Ḥamdān, informed us, Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Shādhān informed us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us, al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us — both of them from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The miskeen is not this beggar who goes around among the people, to whom a morsel or two morsels, or a date or two dates are offered."" They said: ""Then who is the miskeen, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""The one who does not find enough to suffice him, and he is not recognized so that charity is given to him, and he does not ask the people for anything."" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Mughīrah. And in the narration of Mālik: ""and he does not stand up to ask the people."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Uways from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah. And likewise Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated it from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him).
And in it is an indication that the miskeen is the one who does not have enough to suffice him, but he has some means of sufficiency, with which he makes do and refrains from asking. [Bukhārī 1476, Muslim 1038]"
"13153","(13148) And the Master Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us by dictation. Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated to us, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: ""This is what Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The miskeen is not this beggar who goes around among the people, to whom a morsel or two morsels, or a date or two dates are offered. Rather, the miskeen is the one who does not find enough to suffice him, and he feels shy of asking the people, and he is not recognized so that charity is given to him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13154","(13149) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Yaʿqūb al-Iyādī informed us in Baghdād, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf ibn Khallād al-Naṣībī informed us.
(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us. Both said: ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us, Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us, Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Nimr narrated to us, from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmrah, that they both heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The miskeen is not the one to whom a date or two dates, or a morsel or two morsels are offered. Rather, the miskeen is the one who refrains (from asking). Recite, if you wish: {They do not ask the people persistently}."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī from Ibn Abī Maryam. And whoever refrains and does not have some measure of sufficiency is a killer of himself. This indicates that the miskeen is the one who has some means of sufficiency but does not have enough to make him independent, and the faqīr is the one who has no wealth and no trade that meets his need. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13155","(13150) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn Yasār, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say in his supplication: ""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from poverty, scarcity, and humiliation, and I seek refuge in You from oppressing or being oppressed.""
And it has also been narrated to us in the ḥadīth of Abū Bakrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from disbelief and poverty.""
And in the ḥadīth of Abū Ṣāliḥ from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): ""Settle our debt and enrich us from poverty."" [Aḥmad 8039]"
"13156","(13151) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥulwānī informed us, Mūsā ibn Muḥammad, the client of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him), narrated to us. He said: Ḥiql ibn Ziyād narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ziyād narrated to us, Junādah ibn Abī Umayyah narrated to us. He said: I heard ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say: ""O Allah, grant me life as a miskeen, grant me death as a miskeen, and resurrect me among the group of the miskeen."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13157","(13152) And Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓufar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mātī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim al-Ghifārī informed us, Thābit ibn Muḥammad al-Kinānī informed us, al-Ḥārith ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Laythī narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Allah, grant me life as a miskeen, grant me death as a miskeen, and resurrect me among the group of the miskeen on the Day of Resurrection."" ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Why is that, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Because they will enter Paradise forty years before the wealthy. O ʿĀʾishah, do not turn away the miskeen, even if it be with half a date. O ʿĀʾishah, love the miskeen and draw them near, for Allah will draw you near on the Day of Resurrection.""
Our companions said: He sought refuge from poverty and asked for miskeen-ness, and he had some measure of sufficiency. This indicates that the miskeen is the one who has some measure of sufficiency.
The Shaykh said: It has been narrated in the ḥadīth of Shaybān from Qatādah from Anas from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he sought refuge from miskeen-ness and poverty. It is not permissible that his seeking refuge be from the state which he honored in numerous reports, nor from the state in which he asked to live and die, and it is not permissible that his request contradict the state upon which he (peace and blessings be upon him) passed away. He passed away in a state of sufficiency, enriched by what Allah, the Most High, bestowed upon him. The meaning of these aḥādīth, according to me — and Allah knows best — is that he sought refuge from the trial of poverty and miskeen-ness, the meaning of which returns to scarcity, just as he sought refuge from the trial of wealth. [Ḍaʿīf Jiddan]"
"13158","(13153) That is made clear by what Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd Ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to seek refuge, saying: ""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the trial of the Fire, the trial of the grave, the punishment of the grave, the evil of the trial of wealth, and the evil of the trial of poverty. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of the trial of the Dajjāl."" And he mentioned the ḥadīth. This is a firmly established ḥadīth, which they both narrated in the Ṣaḥīḥ.
And in it is an indication that he sought refuge from the trial of poverty, not from the state of poverty, and from the trial of wealth, not from the state of wealth.
As for his statement — if he said it — ""Grant me life as a miskeen and grant me death as a miskeen,"" then if its chain is sound, there is room for consideration. And what his state at the time of his death indicates is that he did not ask for the miskeen-ness that refers to scarcity. Rather, he asked for the miskeen-ness that refers to humility and submissiveness. It is as if he (peace and blessings be upon him) asked Allah, the Most High, not to make him among the arrogant tyrants, and not to resurrect him among the wealthy who live in luxury.
Al-Qutaybī said: Al-maskanah is a word derived from stillness (sukūn). It is said: ""The man tamaskanah,"" meaning he became soft, humble, and submissive. And from that is the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) to the one praying: ""Tabbaʿsū wa tamaskanū,"" meaning: ""Be humble and submissive to Allah."" [Bukhārī 833, Muslim 589]"
"13159","(13154) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Tirmidhī narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Sharḥbīl narrated to us, Khālid ibn Yazīd ibn Abī Mālik narrated to us, from his father, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabaḥ, who said: I heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""O people, fear Allah, and do not let recklessness carry you to seek sustenance from what is unlawful. For I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'O Allah, resurrect me among the group of the miskeen, and do not resurrect me among the group of the wealthy. For the most wretched of the wretched is the one upon whom the poverty of this world and the punishment of the Hereafter are combined.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13160","(13155) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us.
(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us. Both said: al-Thawrī narrated to us, from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, from Riyāḥ ibn Yazīd al-ʿĀmirī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Charity is not lawful for a wealthy person, nor for one who is strong and able-bodied.""
Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī and Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated it from al-Thawrī, and they both said in the ḥadīth: ""nor for one who is strong and robust."" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi]"
"13161","(13156) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us that Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us. (In another chain:) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-'Abbas al-Mahbubi narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Sayyar narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us. Both said: Sufyan narrated it. And Shu'bah narrated it from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, thus in a marfu' (elevated) form. There was also disagreement regarding its wording. [Sahih through other chains]"
"13162","(13157) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us that Abu Adam ibn Iyas narrated to us that Shu'bah narrated from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, and he mentioned it with similar wording, adding: ""wa la lirri mirratin qawiyy"" (nor for one possessing strong vigour). [Sahih through other chains]"
"13163","(13158) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Muqri' in Baghdad informed us that Ahmad ibn Salman narrated to us that 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad narrated to us that 'Abd al-Samad, i.e. Ibn 'Abd al-Warith, narrated to us that Shu'bah narrated from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, and he mentioned it, saying: ""wa la lirri mirratin sawiyy"" (nor for one possessing sound vigour). Ibrahim ibn Sa'd ibn Ibrahim also narrated it from his father, and there was disagreement regarding both its elevation (raf') and its wording. In the narration of the one who elevated it, there is sufficiency (kifayah). The meaning of al-mirrah is strength, and its origin lies in the tight twisting of a rope. [Sahih through other chains]"
"13164","(13159) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Musa al-Sunni in Marw narrated that Abu al-Mawjih informed us that 'Abd ibn 'Uthman informed us that 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Shumayt narrated to us that my father and al-Akhdar ibn 'Ajlan narrated from 'Ata' ibn Zuhayri al-'Amiri from his father, who said: I said to 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'AS, may Allah be pleased with both of them: ""Inform me about charity (sadaqah) — what kind of wealth is it?"" He said: ""It is the worst of wealth; it is only the wealth of the blind, the lame, the crippled, the orphans, and every person cut off (from means)."" I said: ""But those who work upon it (the collectors) and the mujahidin have a right to it."" He said: ""For those who work upon it, according to their labour, and for the mujahidin in the cause of Allah, according to their need"" — or he said ""their condition."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Indeed, charity is not permissible for a wealthy person, nor for one possessing sound strength (dhī mirratin sawiyy)."" [Sahih through other chains]"
"13165","(13160) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us that Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us that Sufyan narrated from Mansur from Abu Hazim from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. It was said to Sufyan: ""Is it from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him?"" He said: ""Perhaps he said: 'Charity is not permissible for a wealthy person, nor for one possessing sound strength (dhī mirratin sawiyy).'"" Al-Humaydi also narrated it from Sufyan with his chain, and he said: ""From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reaching him (the Prophet)."" [Sahih through other chains]"
"13166","(13161) Abu al-Fath Hilal ibn Muhammad al-Haffar in Baghdad informed us that al-Husayn ibn Yahya ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn Mushajjar narrated to us that Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated from Abu Hasin from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Indeed, charity is not permissible for a wealthy person, nor for one possessing sound strength (dhī mirratin sawiyy)."" Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash also narrated it on another occasion from Abu Hasin from Abu Salih from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"13167","(13162) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari and Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran both informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us. (In another chain:) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani narrated to us that Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us. Both said: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us that Sufyan narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwah from his father from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Adi ibn al-Khayyar from two men, who said: ""We came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, while he was distributing the camels of charity, and we asked him. He raised his gaze upon us and lowered it, and said: 'Whatever you wish, but there is no right in it for a wealthy person, nor for a strong, earning one (qawiyy muktasib).'"" In the narration of al-Saffar, it reads: ""He raised his gaze upon us and lowered it."" [Ahmad 4/224, Hadith 18135]"
"13168","(13163) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us that Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated from his father from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Adi ibn al-Khayyar, who said: ""Two men informed me that they came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, during the Farewell Pilgrimage while he was distributing charity, and they asked him for some of it. He raised his gaze upon us and lowered it, and when he saw that we were strong (jalidayn), he said: 'If you wish, I will give you, but there is no share in it for a wealthy person, nor for a strong, earning one (qawiyy muktasib).'"" [Sahih]"
"13169","(13164) Abu Ahmad 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-'Adl informed us that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Malik narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam that he said: ""'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, drank some milk and was pleased by it. He asked the one who gave him the drink: 'Where did you get this milk?' He informed him that he had come to a watering place — which he named — and there were camels from the charity camels being watered. They milked their milk for me, and I placed it in my water-skin."" So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, inserted his finger and vomited it up. [Da'if. Al-Muwatta' 606]"
"13170","(13165) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us that Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Isbahani informed us that Muhammad ibn Sulayman informed us that 'Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiki informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us from 'Amr ibn al-Harith that Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Ibn Abi Rabi'ah came with charities upon which he had served as a collector. When they arrived at al-Harrah, 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, came out to meet him. He brought close to him dates, milk, and butter, and they ate, but 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, refused to eat. Ibn Abi Rabi'ah said: ""By Allah, may Allah set you right — we do drink their milk and benefit from them."" He said: ""O Ibn Abi Rabi'ah, I am not like your condition. By Allah, you follow their tails (i.e., you tend to them and care for them)."" [Sahih]"
"13171","(13166) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated to us from Mālik from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʬāʾ ibn Yasār that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Ṣadaqah is not permissible for a wealthy person except for five: a ghāzī (warrior) fī sabīlillāh (in the cause of Allah), or one appointed to collect it (ʿāmil ʿalayhā), or a debtor (ghārim), or a man who purchased it with his own wealth, or a man who had a poor neighbour and gave ṣadaqah to the poor man, and the poor man then gifted it to the wealthy man."" Mālik sent it as a mursal narration, and Ibn ʿUyaynah also sent it, while Maʿmar narrated it with a complete isnād from Zayd ibn Aslam."
"13172","(13167) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī in Baghdad informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us — Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Maʿmar informed us from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʬāʾ ibn Yasār from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Ṣadaqah is not permissible for a wealthy person except for five: a man appointed to collect it (rajul ʿalayhā), or a man who purchased it with his own wealth, or a poor person to whom ṣadaqah is given and then gifts it to a wealthy person, or a debtor (ghārim), or a warrior in the cause of Allah (ghāzī fī sabīlillāh), ʿAzza wa Jall."" Al-Thawrī also narrated it from Zayd, saying: ""Al-Thabit narrated to me from the Prophet ﷺ,"" and at other times from a man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. And Abū al-Azhar al-Sulayṭī narrated it from ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar and al-Thawrī from Zayd ibn Aslam, just as Maʿmar narrated it alone."
"13173","(13168) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us — Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us — Abū al-Azhar informed us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Maʿmar and al-Thawrī informed us from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʬāʾ ibn Yasār from Abī Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned it with its meaning."
"13174","(13169) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-ʿIyādī informed us — Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf ibn Khallād informed us — al-Ḥārith ibn Muḥammad informed us — Abū al-Naḍr informed us — al-Layth narrated to us — Bukayr narrated to us from Busr ibn Saʿīd from Ibn al-Sāʿidī al-Mālikī.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Muḥammad ibn Shādhān and Aḥmad ibn Salamah said: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us — al-Layth narrated from Bukayr from Busr ibn Saʿīd from Ibn al-Saʿdī al-Mālikī that he said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) appointed me over the ṣadaqah. When I had completed it and delivered it to him, he ordered that I be given a wage (ʿumālah). I said: ""I only worked for Allah, and my reward is upon Allah."" He said: ""Take what you have been given, for I worked during the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he appointed me, and I said the same as you say, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: 'When you are given something without having asked for it, then consume it and give ṣadaqah from it.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd."
"13175","(13170) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā informed us — Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah informed us — my grandfather informed us — Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us — Abū ʿĀṣim informed us — al-Akhḍar ibn ʿAjlān informed us from ʬāʾ ibn Zuhayr from his father from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: I asked: ""Do the collectors (ʿāmilīn) have a right to it?"" He said: ""Yes, in proportion to their labour (ʿumālah)."""
"13176","(13171) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us — Ibn Numayr informed us from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) spoke to a man who had been appointed over the ṣadaqah, and he relieved me of going out with him and gave me my provision while I remained resident."
"13177","(13172) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadhābī informed us — al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us — ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid informed us from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim from ʿAdī ibn ʿAmīrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O people! Whoever among you is appointed to a task for us and then conceals from us even a needle (mikhyāṭ) or anything smaller than that, it is ghulūl (embezzlement), and he will come with it on the Day of Resurrection."" A black man stood up — it was as though I were looking at him — from among the Anṣār, and said: ""Accept your task from me, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""And what is that?"" He said: ""I heard you say what you said."" He said: ""And I say it now: Whoever we appoint to a task, let him bring to us the small and the large of it. Whatever is given to him, he may take, and whatever he is forbidden from, he must abstain."""
"13178","(13173) And Abū Ṭāhir informed us — Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb informed us — al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid informed us from Qays from ʿAdī ibn ʿAmīrah al-Kindī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying — he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: ""A black man from among the Anṣār stood up, as though I could see him, and said: 'Here is your task, O Messenger of Allah.'"" And at the end he said: ""Whatever is given to him, he may take, and whatever he is forbidden from, he must abstain."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Rāhawayh from al-Faḍl, and he extracted it through other chains from Ismāʿīl."
"13179","(13174) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī informed us — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us — Abū al-Yamān informed us — Shuʿayb informed me from al-Zuhrī — ʿUrwah informed me from Abī Ḥumayd al-Anṣārī then al-Sāʿidī that he informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed a collector over the ṣadaqah. When the collector arrived from his work, he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is what is yours, and this is what was given to me as a gift."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Why did you not sit in the house of your father and mother and see whether gifts would be given to you or not?"" Then the Prophet ﷺ stood up one evening on the minbar after the prayer, pronounced the tashahhud, and extolled Allah as is His due, and then said: ""To proceed: What is the matter with a collector whom we appoint, and he comes to us saying, 'This is from your work, and this is what was given to me as a gift'? Why did he not sit in the house of his father and mother and see whether gifts would be given to him or not? By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, none of you will take anything from it except that he will come with it on the Day of Resurrection, carrying it upon his neck — if it is a camel, he will come with it grumbling; if it is a cow, he will come with it mooing; and if it is a sheep, he will come with it bleating. I have conveyed."" Abū Ḥumayd said: Zayd ibn Thābit heard that from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so ask him. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān."
"13180","(13175) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qāḍī in Marw informed us — al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah informed us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shumāsah from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""The collector of maks shall not enter Paradise."" Yazīd ibn Hārūn said: He means the ʿashshār (tax collector). Abū Dāwūd extracted it in al-Sunan through another chain from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq. And al-maks refers to deficiency and shortfall — so when the collector of the ṣadaqāt diminishes the rights of the poor and does not give them in full, he is then a ṣāḥib maks (one who collects unlawful tax), upon whom sin and punishment are feared. And Allah knows best."
"13181","(13176) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu Zur'ah 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated from 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah from Mahmud ibn Labid from Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The one who administers the Zakat with justice is like the warrior in the cause of Allah until he returns to his home."" Abu Dawud narrated it through another chain from Muhammad ibn Ishaq. [Hasan. Ahmad 4/172, 173, 174]"
"13182","(13177) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawi, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated to us by dictation: Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadabadi narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated, having heard 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrating from his father from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, returned from the Battle of Hunayn, the people's concern was to ask him. They surrounded his she-camel, and a tree snatched his cloak. He said: ""Return my cloak to me. Do you fear that I would be miserly? If Allah bestowed upon me livestock as numerous as the samur trees of Tihamah, I would distribute them among you, and you would not find me miserly, nor cowardly, nor a liar."" Then he took a tuft of hair from the hump of his camel and said: ""I have no right to anything that Allah has bestowed upon you, not even the like of this, except the Khums, and it is to be returned to you. Return the thread and the needle, for dishonesty (ghulul) is a disgrace, a fire, and a scandal."" [Hasan]"
"13183","(13178) And Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'ig narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur and al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and Ibn 'Ajlan narrated from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father from his grandfather — one of them narrating additionally upon the other — that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Nothing is lawful to me of what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has bestowed upon you except the like of this, except the Khums, and it is to be returned to you."" [Hasan]"
"13184","(13179) 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghayyath narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""I have no right to this fay' except the Khums, and the Khums is to be returned among you."" Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""By 'the Khums' he means his share from the Khums, and his statement 'it is to be returned among you' means for your welfare."" [Sahih li-ghayrihi]"
"13185","(13180) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih narrated to us: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us: Al-Humaydi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us.

(And) 'Ali ibn 'Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: Ibn Abi 'Umar narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us.

(And) Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Jarudi and Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Isma'ili said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdah al-Dabbi narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us — and this is the wording of the hadith of al-Humaydi: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from 'Umar ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq from his father from 'Ubaydah ibn Rifa'ah from Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Safwan ibn Umayyah, 'Uaynah ibn Hisn, and al-Aqra' ibn Habis one hundred camels each, and he gave 'Abbas ibn Mirdas less than that. Then Sufyan said — and 'Umar or someone else said in this hadith — 'Abbas ibn Mirdas said:

""You place my share and the share of al-'Ubayd between 'Uaynah and al-Aqra' —

Yet neither Badr nor Habis surpass Mirdas in the assembly —

And I was not lesser than either of them; and whoever is humbled today will not be raised.""

So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, completed one hundred for him. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Abi 'Umar and Ahmad ibn 'Abdah. [Sahih. Muslim 1060]"
"13186","(13181) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-Walid narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Jabalah narrated to us: al-Hasan al-Hulwani narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us: My father narrated from Salih ibn Kaysan. Ibn Shihab said: Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me that he said: When Allah bestowed upon His Messenger the wealth of the Banu Hawazin on the Day of Hunayn, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, began giving men of his tribe one hundred camels each. A man from the Ansar said: ""May Allah forgive the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him — he gives Quraysh and leaves us, while our swords are still dripping with their blood."" Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""This reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, so he sent for the Ansar and gathered them in a leather tent, and he did not leave anyone with them. When they had assembled, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'What is this report that has reached me about you?' The learned men of the Ansar said: 'As for the men of sound judgment among us, O Messenger of Allah, they have said nothing. But as for some among us who are young in years, they said: ""May Allah forgive His Messenger — he gives Quraysh and leaves us, while our swords are still dripping with their blood.""' The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'I give to men who are newly removed from disbelief, that I may win their hearts. Are you not pleased that the people go away with the wealth and you return to your homes with the Messenger of Allah? By Allah, what you return with is better than what they return with.' They said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah.' He said to them: 'You will, after me, encounter severe selfishness, so be patient until you meet Allah and His Messenger, for I shall be at the Pond (al-Hawd).'"" Anas ibn Malik said: ""But we were not patient."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Hulwani. [Sahih. Bukhari 1059]"
"13187","(13182) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal al-Qattan in Baghdad narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustuyah narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated from al-Hasan from 'Amr ibn Taghlib, who said: Wealth came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he gave to some people and withheld from others. It reached him that they were displeased, so he said: ""I give to a man and withhold from a man. The one from whom I withhold is more beloved to me than the one to whom I give. I give to certain people because of what is in their hearts of anxiety and distress, and I withhold from certain people because of what Allah has placed in their hearts of contentment and goodness — among them is 'Amr ibn Taghlib."" 'Amr said: ""I would not love that I had in exchange for the word of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, the red camels."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Nu'man. [Sahih. Bukhari 923, 3145]"
"13188","(13183) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Saydalani and Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Jarudi and Ahmad ibn Salamah said: Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated to us: Abu al-Ahwas narrated from Sa'id ibn Masruq from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was in Yemen, sent a piece of gold in its ore to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, divided it among four men: al-Aqra' ibn Habis al-Hanzhali, 'Uaynah ibn Hisn al-Fazari, 'Alqamah ibn 'Ulathah al-'Amiri, one of the Banu Kilab, and Zayd al-Khayl al-Ta'i, one of the Banu Nabhan. The leaders of Quraysh became angry and said: ""He gives the leaders of Najd and leaves us!"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""I only did this to win their hearts."" Then a man came — thick-bearded, with prominent cheeks, sunken eyes, a protruding forehead, and a shaved head — and said: ""Fear Allah, O Muhammad!"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Who would obey Allah if I disobeyed Him? He has entrusted me to the people of the earth, yet you do not trust me."" Then the man turned away. A man from the people — they thought it was Khalid ibn al-Walid — sought permission to kill him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""From the offspring of this man will be a people who recite the Quran, but it will not go beyond their throats. They will kill the people of Islam and leave the people of idols. They will pass through the religion as an arrow passes through its quarry. If I reach them, I will kill them as the people of 'Ad were killed."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Hannad ibn al-Sari, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from Sa'id ibn Masruq. [Sahih. Bukhari, Muslim 90]"
"13189","(13184) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Jarir narrated from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufi' from Unas from the family of 'Abd Allah ibn Safwan that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""O Safwan, do you have any weapons?"" He said: ""As a loan or by force?"" He said: ""Rather, as a loan."" He said: ""So he lent him between thirty and forty coats of mail."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, fought at Hunayn, and when he defeated the polytheists, Safwan's coats of mail were gathered, and some of them were missing. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to Safwan: ""We have lost some of your coats of mail. Shall we compensate you?"" He said: ""No, O Messenger of Allah, for in my heart today is what was not there that day."" [Da'if]"
"13190","(13185) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Ja'far al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated from Abu al-Aswad from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr.

(And) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl in Baghdad narrated to us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn 'Itath al-'Abdi narrated to us: al-Qasim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us: Ibn Abi Uways narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn 'Uqbah narrated from his uncle Musa ibn 'Uqbah — I think it was from al-Zuhri, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent to Safwan ibn Umayyah regarding some equipment that was mentioned as being with him, and he asked him for it. Safwan said: ""Where is the guarantee? Will you take it by force?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""If you wish, you may keep your equipment, and if you lend it to me, it is guaranteed by me until it is returned to you."" Safwan said: ""There is no harm in that,"" and he lent it to him. They say he gave him that day one hundred coats of mail and their equipment. Safwan had an abundance of weapons. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to him: ""Carry them for us."" So Safwan carried them. Then he mentioned the story of the Battle of Hunayn, and in it he said: ""A man from Quraysh passed by Safwan ibn Umayyah and said: 'Glad tidings of the defeat of Muhammad and his companions!' Safwan said to him: 'You give me glad tidings of the victory of the Bedouins? By Allah, a lord from Quraysh is more beloved to me than a lord from the Bedouins.' Safwan ibn Umayyah sent his slave and said: 'Listen — whose is the war cry?' The slave came and said: 'I heard them saying: O Banu 'Abd al-Rahman! O Banu 'Abd Allah! O Banu 'Ubayd Allah!' He said: 'Muhammad has prevailed.' That was their war cry in battle."" The wording is that of the hadith of Musa ibn 'Uqbah, and the hadith of 'Urwah has the same meaning. [Da'if]"
"13191","(13186) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Walīd informed me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Akfānī narrated to us, Yūnus ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated from Yūnus from Ibn Shihāb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) set out for the campaign of the Conquest, the Conquest of Makkah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) departed from Madīnah in the month of Ramaḍān. On that day, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) gave Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah one hundred camels, then another hundred, then another hundred. Ibn Shihāb said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me that Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah said: ""By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) gave me what he gave me, and he was the most hated of people to me. But he kept giving me until he became the most beloved of people to me."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2313]"
"13192","(13187) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb al-Kirmānī informed us from Muḥammad ibn Abī Yaʿqūb, Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us, Ḥumayd narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, al-Qāḍī Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil ibn Khalaf informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī narrated to us, Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from Mūsā ibn Anas from Anas, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was never asked for anything for the sake of Islam except that he gave it. A man came and asked him, and he ordered that he be given a flock of sheep between two mountains. The man returned to his people and said: ""O my people, embrace Islam, for Muhammad gives a gift without fearing poverty."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿĀṣim ibn al-Naḍr from Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2312]"
"13193","(13188) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Naḍr informed me, al-Dārimī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us. (Ḥ) He said: And Abū ʿAmr informed me — and the wording is his — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated from Ḥammād ibn Salamah from Thābit from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) for a flock of sheep between two mountains, and he gave it to him. He then went to his people and said: ""O my people, embrace Islam, for by Allah, Muhammad gives a gift without fearing poverty."" Anas said: ""A man may embrace Islam desiring nothing but the worldly life, but he does not truly become a Muslim until Islam becomes more beloved to him than the worldly life and all that it contains."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah."
"13194","In what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from him, from Abū al-ʿAbbās, who said: al-Rabīʿ informed us, who said: al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""The Mu'allafah Qulūbi-hum (those whose hearts are to be reconciled) have a share in the distribution of the ṣadaqāt (charities). And what I preserve from the earlier reports is that ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim came to Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) — I think he said — with three hundred camels from the ṣadaqāt of his people. Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) gave him thirty camels from them and instructed him to join Khālid ibn al-Walīd with those of his people who obeyed him. He then brought nearly a thousand men and acquitted himself admirably. The report does not specify from where Abū Bakr gave them to him. However, what the heart can almost discern through deduction from the reports — and Allah knows best — is that he gave them to him from the share of the Mu'allafah Qulūbi-hum. He may have given him more to encourage him in what he had done, or he may have given to him in order to reconcile through him others of his people from whom one could not expect the same trust as from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim. Thus, it is my view that the share of the Mu'allafah Qulūbi-hum may be given in such a circumstance, should a calamity befall the Muslims — and no calamity shall befall them, if Allah wills."""
"13195","(13189) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us, Abū Yūsuf Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Hārūn ibn Isḥāq al-Hamdānī narrated to us, al-Muḥāribī narrated from Ḥajjāj ibn Dīnār al-Wāsiṭī from Ibn Sīrīn from ʿUbaydah, who said: ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn and al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis came to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), we have a tract of saline land with no pasture and no benefit. If you see fit to grant it to us, perhaps we may cultivate it and plant crops."" He then mentioned the report concerning the land grant, the testimony of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding it, and its erasure. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to reconcile your hearts, and Islam at that time was in a state of weakness. But Allah has now made Islam mighty. Go forth and strive with all your effort; I will not show you any consideration if you tend to your flocks."" It is narrated from al-Shaʿbī that he said: ""There remains no one from the Mu'allafah Qulūbi-hum; they existed only during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). When Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was appointed as Caliph, bribery ceased."" And from al-Ḥasan it was said: ""As for the Mu'allafah, there is no such category today."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13196","(13190) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍarawī al-Harawī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Muhājir Abī al-Ḥasan, who said: ""I came to Abū Wāʾil and Abū Burdah with the zakāh, and they were in charge of the Bayt al-Māl (public treasury), and they took it. Then I came another time and found only Abū Wāʾil alone. He said: 'Take it back and place it in its proper places.' I said: 'What shall I do with the share of the Mu'allafah Qulūbi-hum?' He said: 'Return it to the others (i.e., distribute it among the other eligible recipients).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13197","(13191) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Walīd informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb that Abū Muʾammal was the first mukātab (slave who had contracted to purchase his freedom) to be contracted in Islam during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Help Abū Muʾammal."" So he was helped, and he gave what was required for his kitābah (manumission contract), and a surplus remained. He sought the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him) ruling regarding it, and he instructed him to place it in the cause of Allah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13198","(13192) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Walīd informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated from Ḥibbān ibn Mūsā from Ibn al-Mubārak from Saʿīd from ʿAbd Allāh al-Dānāj that a certain man from the Ḥanīfah narrated to him, saying: ""I was present on a Friday when a mukātab came to Abū Mūsā (may Allah be pleased with him). He was the first beggar I ever saw. He said: 'I am a burdened mukātab.' He urged the people to help him, and garments and dirhams were thrown at him until he said, 'That is enough for me.' He went to his family and found that they had given him his kitābah (manumission payment), and three hundred dirhams remained in excess. He came to Abū Mūsā and asked him, and he instructed him to spend it on people of his kind."" We have also narrated from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) a similar story, in which he said: ""He came to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him about the surplus, and he said: 'Place it among the mukātabūn (slaves under manumission contracts).'"" This is also recorded in the Kitāb al-Mukātab."
"13199","(13193) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr al-Mukharramī narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us, Saʿdān informed us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Riʾāb narrated from Kinānah ibn Nuʿaym from Qabīṣah ibn al-Mukhāriq, who said: ""I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) to ask him about a debt (ḥamālah) I had taken on. He said: 'Asking (for charity) has been made impermissible except in three cases: a man who has taken on a debt — for him asking is permissible until he fulfills it, then he must refrain; a man who has been struck by a calamity that has destroyed his wealth — for him asking is permissible until he attains a means of livelihood or sufficiency in living, then he must refrain; and a man who has been afflicted by need or poverty until three men of sound judgment from his people testify that such-and-such a person has indeed been afflicted by poverty — for him asking is permissible until he attains a means of livelihood or sufficiency in living. Beyond these cases, all other forms of asking are unlawful (suḥt).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13200","(13194) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Ḥurfī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, ʿĀrim ibn al-Faḍl informed us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us. (Ḥ) He said: And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed me — and the wording is his — Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd and Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah al-Dabbī both said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Riʾāb narrated to us, Kinānah ibn Nuʿaym al-ʿAdawī narrated from Qabīṣah ibn al-Mukhāriq al-Hilālī, who said: ""I took on a debt (ḥamālah) and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) to ask him about it. He said: 'Stay, O Qabīṣah, until the ṣadaqah comes to us, so that we may command that it be given to you.' Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'O Qabīṣah, asking (for charity) is not permissible except for one of three: a man who has taken on a debt — for him asking is permissible until he obtains it, then he must refrain; a man who has been struck by a calamity that has destroyed his wealth — for him asking is permissible until he attains a means of livelihood, or he said, sufficiency in living; and a man who has been afflicted by poverty until three men of sound judgment from his people say: ""Such-and-such a person has indeed been afflicted by poverty"" — for him the ṣadaqah is permissible until he attains a means of livelihood or sufficiency in living. Beyond these cases, O Qabīṣah, all other forms of asking are unlawful (suḥt), and the one who consumes it consumes what is unlawful.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13201","(13195) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Sulayman al-Faqih informed us — it was recited to Muhammad ibn Maslamah al-Wasiti while I was listening — Yazid ibn Harun informed us — Bahz ibn Hakim ibn Mu'awiyah al-Qushayri informed us.

(And in another chain:) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Ahmad ibn Sulayman informed us — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Qadi informed us — Abu Ma'mar al-Minqari informed us — 'Abd al-Warith informed us — Bahz ibn Hakim ibn Mu'awiyah informed us — from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we are a people whose wealth is sought after."" He ﷺ said: ""Let one of you ask only in case of need or of breach (al-fatq), so that he may set right the affairs of his people. When it is fulfilled or nearly fulfilled, let him refrain (ista'ffa).""

Abu 'Ubayd said: ""Al-fatq refers to war that breaks out between two factions, in which blood is shed and wounds are inflicted. A man undertakes the responsibility (hamalah) to reconcile between them by means of this wealth, and so he asks for it until he discharges it to them."" And his ﷺ statement ""aw kara"" (or he is near) means: he approaches that and draws near to it. And his ﷺ statement ""siddan min 'aysh"" (a sufficiency of livelihood) — with a kasra on the sin — means: anything by which you mend a deficiency is called siddad.

[Hasan]"
"13202","(13196) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us orally — Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Hayri informed us — Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn 'Abbad al-San'ani informed us — 'Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar from Zayd ibn Aslam from 'Ata' ibn Yasar from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Sadaqah is not lawful for a wealthy person except for five: a worker ('amil) employed to collect it, or a man who purchases it with his own wealth, or a debtor (gharim), or a warrior (ghazi) in the cause of Allah, or a poor person (miskin) upon whom sadaqah is given from it and then gives it as a gift to a wealthy person.""

[Munkar]"
"13203","(13197) 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — Abu Yahya ibn Abi Masarrat informed us — al-Muqr'i, Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, informed us — Sa'id informed us — 'Uqayl and Yunus from Ibn Shihab from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever among my Ummah carries a debt and strives to repay it, then dies before repaying it, then I am his guardian (waliyyuh).""

[Sahih. Ahmad 6/74, no. 154]"
"13204","(13198) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Ziyad informed us — Ibrahim ibn Musa informed us — 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us — Ma'mar from Zayd ibn Aslam from 'Ata' ibn Yasar from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Sadaqah is not lawful for a wealthy person except for five: a warrior in the cause of Allah, or a worker employed to collect it, or a debtor, or a man who purchases it with his own wealth, or a man who has a poor neighbor upon whom sadaqah is given and then the poor person gives it as a gift to the wealthy person."""
"13205","(13199) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Maryam informed us — Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us — Sufyan al-Thawri from 'Imran al-Bariq from 'Ata' al-'Awfi from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Sadaqah is not lawful for a wealthy person except in the cause of Allah, or for the wayfarer (ibn al-sabil), or for a poor neighbor who gives you a gift (from it).""

[Da'if]"
"13206","(13200) And Abu Bakr ibn Fwarak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Yunus ibn Habib informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Shayban from Firas al-Muktatib from 'Ata' al-'Awfi from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Sadaqah is lawful for a wealthy person when he is in the cause of Allah.""

[Da'if]"
"13207","(13201) Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Asbahani informed us — Abu 'Amr ibn Hamdan informed us — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us — Abu Usamah informed me — Ishaq ibn Sulayman informed me — from his father, who said: 'Amr ibn Abi Qurrah informed me, who said: ""There came to us a letter from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, stating: 'Some people take from this wealth in order to strive in the cause of Allah, then they oppose (this purpose) and do not perform jihad. Whoever among them does that, then we have a greater right to his wealth, so that we may take from him what he took.'""

Abu Ishaq said: ""I stood up before Usayd ibn 'Amr and said, 'Do you not see what 'Amr ibn Abi Qurrah has narrated to me?' And I narrated it to him. He said, 'He has spoken the truth. A letter came to us from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, to that effect.'""

[Hasan. Ibn Abi Shaybah 32826]"
"13208","(13202) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Warraq informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us — Ibn Abi Layla from 'Ata' from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Sadaqah is not lawful for a wealthy person except in the cause of Allah, and for the wayfarer (ibn al-sabil), or when you have a poor neighbor upon whom sadaqah is given and then he gives you a gift.""

And if this hadith is sound, then — and Allah knows best — what is meant is a wealthy wayfarer in his land who is in need during his journey. And the hadith of 'Ata' ibn Yasar from Abu Sa'id is more authentic in its chain, and it does not contain any mention of ibn al-sabil. And Allah knows best.

[Da'if]"
"13209","(13203) Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hurfi in Baghdad informed us — Ahmad ibn Sulayman informed us — Ibrahim ibn Ishaq al-Harbi informed us — Musaddad informed us — Hammad ibn Zayd informed us.

(And in another chain:) Abu Bakr ibn Fwarak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Yunus ibn Habib informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd from Harun ibn Ri'ab al-Asadi from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym al-'Adawi from Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq al-Hilali, who said: ""I undertook a debt (hamalah) and came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning it. He ﷺ said: 'Stay, O Qabisah, until the sadaqah comes to us, so that we may command that it be given to you.' Then he ﷺ said: 'O Qabisah, begging is not lawful except for one of three: a man who undertakes a debt and asks concerning it until he receives it, then withholds it; or a man struck by a calamitous loss (jā'ihah) that destroys his wealth, and asks until he attains a sufficiency (siddād) of livelihood, then withholds it; or a man afflicted by severe need, and three men of sound judgment from his people stand up and say, ""So-and-so has been afflicted by poverty or severe need,"" and he asks until he attains a sufficiency or a means of livelihood, then withholds it. And whatever other requests besides these are unlawful (suḥt), which its consumer consumes as unlawful.' He ﷺ said this two or three times."" Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Hammad ibn Zayd as mentioned above.

[Sahih]"
"13210","(13204) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us — Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami informed us — Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us — Sufyan from Mus'ab ibn Muhammad from Ya'la, a mawla of Fatimah.

(And in another chain:) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Muhammad ibn Kathir informed us — Sufyan informed us — Mus'ab ibn Muhammad ibn Sharahbil informed me — Ya'la ibn Abi Yahya from Fatimah bint al-Husayn from al-Husayn ibn 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The beggar has a right, even if he comes on horseback."" And in the narration of al-Firyabi: ""even if he comes on his horse.""

[Da'if]"
"13211","(13205) Abū ʿAlīyy informed us that Abū Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us that Zuhayr narrated from a shaykh — I saw Sufyān with him — from Fāṭimah bint Ḥusayn, from her father, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar narration. [Weak]"
"13212","(13206) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawīyy informed us that Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us that Abū Shihāb narrated from Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Thaqafīyy from Abū Jaʿfar, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlīyy, that he heard ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""Indeed, Allah has obligated upon the wealthy, from their wealth, that which suffices their poor. So if they go hungry, are naked, and are afflicted, it is because of the withholding of the wealthy. And it is a right upon Allah that He will hold them accountable on the Day of Resurrection and punish them for it."" This Muḥammad ibn ʿAlīyy is Ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah, and Abū Jaʿfar is Muḥammad ibn ʿAlīyy ibn al-Ḥusayn.

And Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl likewise narrated it from Abū Shihāb, and ʿAlīyy ibn Muslim narrated it from Abū Shihāb, from Abyaḍ ibn Abān, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlīyy, meaning Abū Jaʿfar. [Weak]"
"13213","(13207) As for the hadith that Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlīyy ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirīyy narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us that Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from Ḥakīm ibn Jubayr, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, from his father, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever asks while he has that which suffices him, there will come on the Day of Resurrection scratches, marks, or wounds upon his face."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, and what is self-sufficiency (al-ghinā)?"" He said: ""Fifty dirhams or its equivalent in gold."" Yaḥyā ibn Ādam said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān said to Sufyān: ""My recollection is that Shuʿbah used not to narrate from Ḥakīm ibn Jubayr."" Sufyān said: ""But Zubayd has narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd."" [Weak]"
"13214","(13208) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated, mentioning the meaning of this report by way of transmission from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam, from Sufyān. Then Yaʿqūb said: ""This is a far-fetched report, even though the hadith of Ḥakīm ibn Jubayr from Zubayd is not hidden from the people of knowledge."" [Sound]"
"13215","(13209) Abū ʿAlīyy al-Rūdhabārīyy informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from a man of Banī Asad, who said: ""I and my family descended upon Baqīʿ al-Gharqad. My family said to me: 'Go to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and ask him for something for us to eat.' They kept mentioning their need, so I went to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and found a man asking him, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was saying: 'I do not find anything to give you.' The man turned away from him, angry, and he was saying: 'By my life, you do indeed give whomever you wish.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'He is angry at me because I do not find anything to give him. Whoever among you asks while he has an uqiyyah or its equivalent, he has asked with importunity (ilḥāf).'"" The Asadī said: ""I said: 'A milch-camel for us is better than an uqiyyah,' and an uqiyyah is forty dirhams."" So I returned and did not ask him. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought barley and raisins, and he distributed to us from it — or as he said — until Allah enriched us. Abū Dāwūd said: Thus narrated al-Thawrīyy, just as Mālik narrated it. [Sound. Mālik narrated it as 2111]"
"13216","(13210) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlīyy ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us that Abū al-Jumāhir narrated to us that Ibn Abī al-Rijāl — meaning ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — narrated from ʿUmārah ibn Ghaziyyah, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrīyy, who said: Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrīyy said: ""My father, Mālik ibn Sinān, was martyred on the day of Uḥud, and he left us with no wealth. A severe need befell us, so my mother said to me: 'O my son, go to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and ask him for something for us.' So I came to him and greeted him and sat down while he was sitting among his Companions. When he turned toward me, he said: 'Whoever seeks self-sufficiency (istaghnā), Allah will make him self-sufficient, and whoever seeks chasteness (istaʿaffaf), Allah will make him chaste, and whoever stretches out his hand, it will be attached to it.' I said: 'He does not mean anyone other than me.' So I departed and did not speak to him about anything. My mother said to me: 'What did you do?' I told her the news. She said: 'Be patient.' So Allah provided for us, and we lived on it until a need more severe than that befell us. My mother said to me: 'Go to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and ask him for something for us.' So I came to him while he was sitting among his Companions. I greeted him and sat down. He turned toward me and said the first statement, adding to it: 'And whoever asks while he has an uqiyyah, he is importunate (mulḥif).' I said to myself: 'We have a yāqūtah, and it is better than an uqiyyah.' An uqiyyah is forty dirhams. So I returned and did not ask him."" ʿUbayd said: ""The yāqūtah is a she-camel."" [Good. Aḥmad 11059]"
"13217","(13211) Abū Saʿd al-Zāhid informed us that Abū al-Ṭayyib Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdūn al-Dhuhlīyy informed us that Abū ʿAmr Aḥmad ibn Naṣr narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from Dāwūd, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever asks while he has forty dirhams is importunate (mulḥif)."" [Nasāʾī 3/98]"
"13218","(13212) Abū ʿAlīyy al-Rūdhabārīyy informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādīyy informed me that ʿAlīyy ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Madīnīyy informed me that al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed me that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir informed me that Rabiʿah ibn Yazīd informed me that Abū Kabshah al-Salūlīyy informed me that he heard Ibn al-Ḥanẓalīyy al-Anṣārīyy, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), say:

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlīyy ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — and the wording is his — that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us that Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānīyy informed us that al-Nufaylīyy informed us that Miskīn ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated from Rabiʿah ibn Yazīd, from Abū Kabshah al-Salūlīyy, who narrated to us that Sahl ibn al-Ḥanẓalīyy said: ""ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn and al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and asked him. He commanded that they be given what they asked for, and he commanded Muʿāwiyah to write for them what they asked for. As for al-Aqraʿ, he wrapped his letter in his turban and departed. As for ʿUyaynah, he took his letter and came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'O Muḥammad, do you see that I am carrying to my people a letter the contents of which I do not know, like the scroll of al-Mutalammis?' The Prophet (ﷺ) took it and looked at it and said: 'That which I commanded for you has been written for you.' Then he mentioned the hadith.

Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever asks for something while he is free of need of it, he is merely accumulating more of the Fire.' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is the self-sufficiency (al-ghinā) after which asking is not appropriate?' He said: 'That he have enough to suffice him for a day and a night, or a night and a day.'

There is nothing differing among these hadiths, as though the Prophet (ﷺ) knew what would suffice each one of them and made their sufficiency accordingly. This is because people differ in the measure of their needs. Among them are those for whom fifty dirhams suffice, and less than that does not suffice them; among them are those for whom forty dirhams suffice, and less than that does not suffice them; and among them are those who have earnings that provide them daily with what suffices them for their morning and evening meals, and they have no dependents, so they are self-sufficient by that."" [Sound]"
"13219","(13213) That is clarified by what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us that ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us that al-Akḍar ibn ʿAjlān informed me that Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafīyy informed me from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and complained to him of poverty. Then he returned and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have come from a household — I do not think I will return to them before some of them die.' He said to him: 'Go until you find something.' So he went and came back with a ḥils (a type of garment/mat) and a bowl. He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, this ḥils — they used to spread some of it and wear some of it — and this bowl — they used to drink from it.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Who will take these two from me for a dirham?' A man said: 'I will, O Messenger of Allah.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Who will increase over a dirham?' A man said: 'I will take them for two.' He said: 'They are yours.' He called the man and said to him: 'Buy with one dirham an axe, and with one dirham food for your family.' He did so, then returned to the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: 'Go to this valley and do not leave a thorn, a piece of firewood, or a branch, and do not come to me for fifteen days.' So he went and earned ten. He said: 'Go, and buy with five food for your family, and with five clothing for your family.' He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, Allah has blessed me in what You commanded me.' He said: 'This is better for you than that you should come on the Day of Resurrection with a mark of begging on your face. Indeed, begging is not appropriate except for three: one with a painful debt, or a burdensome liability, or a crushing poverty.'""

The Shaykh said: ""If his sufficiency is not achieved except by two hundreds or by thousands, he should be given the minimum amount of sufficiency, as evidenced by what we have narrated in the hadith of Qabīṣah ibn al-Mukhāriq from the Prophet (ﷺ): 'Until you obtain a means of living or a sufficiency of livelihood.' And with Allah is the success."" [Weak]"
"13220","(13214) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānīyy informed us orally, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānīyy narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Qurashīyy in Harāt narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimīyy narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥakam ibn Abī Maryam informed us that Sulaymān ibn Bilāl informed me that Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī al-Muzarrid narrated from Yazīd ibn Rūmān, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""The womb (al-raḥim) is a branch from Allah. Whoever maintains it, Allah will maintain him, and whoever severs it, Allah will sever him."" This is the wording of the hadith of al-Ṣaghānīyy. In the narration of al-Dārimīyy: ""The womb is a branch from al-Raḥmān."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam. And Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated it from Muʿāwiyah, and he said in the hadith: ""The womb is a branch from al-Raḥmān."" And Wakīʿ narrated it from Muʿāwiyah, and he said in the hadith: ""The womb is suspended from the Throne."" From this angle, Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ. And it is narrated in the hadith of Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): ""The womb is a branch from al-Raḥmān."" [Bukhārī 5989. Muslim 2555]"
"13221","(13215) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated — and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar reported, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, who said: Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated to me that Abū al-Raddād al-Laythī informed him, on the authority of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him), that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, said: ""I am al-Raḥmān. I created the raḥim (ties of kinship) and derived for it a name from My name. Whoever maintains it, I shall maintain him, and whoever severs it, I shall sever him."" [Ḥasan in virtue of corroborating chains]"
"13222","(13216) And Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) visited Abū al-Raddād and said: ""The best of them and the most dutiful in maintaining ties is Abū Muḥammad, as far as I know."" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, said: 'I am Allah, and I am al-Raḥmān. I created the raḥim and derived for it a name from My name. Whoever maintains it, I shall maintain him, and whoever severs it, I shall sever him.'"" [Ḥasan in virtue of corroborating chains]"
"13223","(13217) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us: Abū al-Muwajjih narrated to us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Muzarrid narrated to us, saying: I heard my uncle Saʿīd ibn Yasār, Abū al-Ḥubāb, narrate from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Indeed, Allah created creation, and when He finished creating it, the raḥim (ties of kinship) said: 'This is the station of one seeking refuge from being severed.' He said: 'Yes. Are you not pleased that I shall maintain whoever maintains you and sever whoever severs you?' She said: 'Yes, my Lord.' He said: 'Then that is for you.'"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Recite, if you wish: *'Then perhaps you would, if you turn away, cause corruption on earth and sever your ties of kinship. Those are the ones whom Allah has cursed, so He made them deaf and blinded their sight*' (Muḥammad: 22–23)."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bishr ibn Muḥammad, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 5987]"
"13224","(13218) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated to us in Makkah: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated, on the authority of al-Zuhrī — and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar reported, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Muḥammad ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, on the authority of his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). And in the narration of Ibn ʿUyaynah, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ), he said: ""No one who severs ties of kinship shall enter Paradise."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar and others, on the authority of Ibn ʿUyaynah, and from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and ʿAbd, on the authority of ʿAbd al-Razzāq. al-Bukhārī also narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 2556; al-Bukhārī 5984]"
"13225","(13219) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Maymūn al-Raqqī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated, on the authority of al-Aʿmash, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAmr, and Fiṭr ibn Khalīfah, on the authority of Mujāhid, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both). Sufyān said: al-Aʿmash did not raise it (to the Prophet), but al-Ḥasan and Fiṭr raised it. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The one who maintains ties of kinship is not merely the one who reciprocates; rather, the one who truly maintains ties is the one who, when his ties of kinship are severed, restores them."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. [al-Bukhārī 5991]"
"13226","(13220) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Maymūn narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Fiṭr narrated, on the authority of Mujāhid, who said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, the raḥim (ties of kinship) is suspended from the Throne. The one who maintains ties is not merely the one who reciprocates; rather, the one who truly maintains ties is the one who, when his ties of kinship are severed, restores them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13227","(13221) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī and Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīb ibn Milḥān narrated, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated, on the authority of ʿUqayl, on the authority of Ibn Shihāb, who said: Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever loves for his provision to be expanded and for his lifespan to be extended, let him maintain his ties of kinship."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Layth. [al-Bukhārī 2067; Muslim 2557]"
"13228","(13222) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated orally, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Ibn ʿAwn reported, on the authority of Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, on the authority of Umm al-Rāʾiḥ ibn Ḍulayʿ, on the authority of Salmān ibn ʿĀmir al-Dabbī, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, your charity given to a poor person is charity, but when given to a kinsman, it is twofold: charity and maintenance of ties of kinship."" Abū al-ʿAbbās said ""Ḍulayʿ,"" though it is in fact ""Ṣulayʿ"" with a ṣād. [Ṣaḥīḥ in virtue of corroborating chains]"
"13229","(13223) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ṣanʿānī narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ṣanʿānī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar reported, on the authority of al-Zuhrī — and Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, on the authority of his mother, Umm Kulthūm bint ʿUqbah. Sufyān said: She had prayed with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) facing both qiblahs. She said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The best charity is that given to a kinsman who harbours enmity."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Ḥumaydī 3330]"
"13230","(13224) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd reported that Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm informed him, on the authority of ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that she heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Jibrīl continued to enjoin upon me the rights of the neighbour so much that I thought he would make the neighbour an heir."" al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī. [al-Bukhārī 6014; Muslim 2624]"
"13231","(13225) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us — ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawarīrī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār informed us — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Bartī informed us — Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl informed us — both saying: Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ narrated to us from ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad from his father from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Jibrīl did not cease exhorting me regarding the neighbour until I thought — or supposed — that he would bequeath him [a share of inheritance]."" This is the wording of al-Qawarīrī's ḥadīth. And in the narration of Ibn al-Minhāl: ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Zayd ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, and he did not say ""or I supposed."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawarīrī, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl. [Bukhārī 6015, Muslim 2625]"
"13232","(13226) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us — Shuʿbah informed me — Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī informed me, saying: I heard Ṭalḥah relate that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have two neighbours; to which of them should I begin [with kindness]?"" He ﷺ said: ""To the one whose door is nearest to yours."" [Bukhārī 2595]"
"13233","(13227) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq informed us — Wahb ibn Jarīr informed us — Shuʿbah informed us from Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī from Ṭalḥah from a man from Quraysh from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have two neighbours; to which of them should I direct [my gift]?"" He ﷺ said: ""To the one whose door is nearest to yours."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl from Shuʿbah. And they differed in its transmission from Shuʿbah; among them was one who transmitted it with precision, and among them was one who sent it [without full chain]. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13234","(13228) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us — Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān informed us from Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwf from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have two neighbours; to which of them should I direct [my gift]?"" He ﷺ said: ""To the one whose door is nearest to yours."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13235","(13229) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us — ʿAffān informed us — al-Sakan ibn Abī al-Sakan informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mukhtār informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""No child has a right to a prescribed charity, nor does a parent; and whoever has a child or a parent but does not maintain ties with them is disobedient.""

And it has been narrated to us from Ibn ʿAbbās that he said: ""Do not direct it [charity] to those whom you are responsible for."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13236","(13230) Abū Ḥāzim al-ʿAbdawī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Jahm Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qurashī in Damascus informed us — Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Ḥawārī informed us — Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth informed us — al-Aʿmash informed us from Shaqīq ibn Salamah from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith from Zaynab, the wife of Ibn Masʿūd, that she said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will it suffice us if we give the charity to a poor husband and an orphan nephew in our care?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""For you is the reward of charity and the reward of maintaining kinship ties."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ, and Muslim narrated it from Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf from ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ in a lengthy ḥadīth. [Bukhārī 446, Muslim 1000]"
"13237","(13231) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī informed us — Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī informed us — Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us — Shuʿbah informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ziyād informed me, saying: I heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) took a date from the dates of charity and placed it in his mouth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Kikh, kikh!"" — so that he would spit it out. Then he ﷺ said: ""Did you not know that we do not eat charity?"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim transmitted it through various routes from Shuʿbah. And in the narration of Wakīʿ from Shuʿbah: ""Charity is not permissible for us."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1466, Muslim 1069]"
"13238","(13232) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally, and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālūyah al-Muzakkī informed us by recitation — both saying: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qaṭṭān informed us — Qaṭan ibn Ibrāhīm al-Qushayrī informed us — Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān informed us from Muḥammad ibn Ziyād from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be brought dates as charity at the time of the people's harvest. This one would bring his dates and that one would bring his dates, until he ﷺ had a heap of dates before him. al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) began playing with those dates. He took a date and placed it in his mouth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at him, removed it from his mouth, and said: ""Did you not know that the family of Muḥammad do not eat charity?"" Al-Bukhārī extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan from his father from Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1466, Muslim 1069]"
"13239","(13233) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both informed us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Ḥusayn ibn Ḥasan ibn Muhājir informed us — Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed him that Abū Yūnus narrated to him from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he ﷺ said: ""I return to my household and find a date fallen upon my bed. I raise it to eat it, then I fear that it may be charity, so I cast it away."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2433, Muslim 1070]"
"13240","(13234) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr and Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us — Muʿādh ibn Hishām informed me — my father informed me — from Qatādah from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ found a date and said: ""Were it not that I fear it might be charity, I would eat it."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Bukhārī 2000, Muslim 1071]"
"13241","(13235) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb related to us: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī related to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī related to us, from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from Manṣūr, from Ṭalḥah ibn Muṣarrif, from Anas ibn Mālik (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), that the Prophet (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) used to see a date, and were it not that he feared it might be from the ṣadaqah, he would have eaten it. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ collections from the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13242","(13236) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq related to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī related to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr and Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb ibn ʿArabī said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd related to us, from Abī Jaḥḍam Mūsā ibn Sālim, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn al-ʿAbbās related to me, saying: We were sitting with Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā) in a gathering of the young men of Banī Hāshim, and he said: ""By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) did not single us out with anything exclusive over the people except three things: He commanded us to perform the wuḍūʾ thoroughly, and He commanded us not to eat from the ṣadaqah, and not to breed donkeys with horses."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭayālisī 2723]"
"13243","(13237) As for the ḥadīth which Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah related to us: Abū Dāwūd related to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Muḥāribī related to us: Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl related to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Kurayb, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā), who said: My father sent me to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) with camels that he had been given from the ṣadaqah.

(Ḥ) And Abū Dāwūd related to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah and Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ said: Muḥammad — that is, Ibn Abī ʿUbaydah — related to us, from his father, from al-Aʿmash, from Sālim, from Kurayb, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā), something similar, adding: ""i.e., in exchange for them."" This ḥadīth admits of only two interpretations. The first is that it occurred before the prohibition of ṣadaqah for Banī Hāshim, and was then abrogated by what came after. The second is that he (al-ʿAbbās) borrowed camels from the poor for the poor, and then repaid him from the camels of ṣadaqah. We have already narrated in the Book of Zakāh what indicates this. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13244","(13238) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb related to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Abū Ḥayyān — that is, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — related to us, from Yazīd ibn Ḥayyān, who said: I heard Zayd ibn Arqam (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) saying: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) stood among us one day delivering a sermon. He praised Allah and glorified Him, then said: ""To proceed: O people, I am but a human being, and it is near that the messenger of my Lord will come to me and I will respond to him. I am leaving among you two weighty matters: the first of them is the Book of Allah — in it is guidance and light — so hold fast to the Book of Allah and take hold of it."" He exhorted concerning it and encouraged it, then said: ""And my Ahl al-Bayt — I remind you of Allah regarding my Ahl al-Bayt."" Ḥuṣayn said to Zayd: ""And who are his Ahl al-Bayt — are his wives from his Ahl al-Bayt?"" He said: ""Indeed, his wives are from his Ahl al-Bayt, but his Ahl al-Bayt are those for whom ṣadaqah is forbidden after him."" He asked: ""And who are they?"" He said: ""The family of ʿAlī, the family of ʿAqīl, the family of Jaʿfar, and the family of al-ʿAbbās."" He said: ""Is ṣadaqah forbidden for all of these?"" He said: ""Yes."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Abī Ḥayyān.

And likewise their cousins from Banī Hāshim, by virtue of what we will mention in the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Rabiʿah ibn al-Ḥārith from his father. And likewise Banī al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Manāf, by virtue of what we have narrated in the established ḥadīth from Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) that he said: ""Indeed, Banī al-Muṭṭalib and Banī Hāshim are one and the same."" And he gave them from the share of dhī al-qurbā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13245","(13239) With what Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb related to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā and Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī related to us —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Abū al-Muthannā informed us — they said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ related to us: Juwayriyyah ibn Asmāʾ related to us, from Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib related to him that ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Rabiʿah ibn al-Ḥārith related to him, saying: Rabiʿah ibn al-Ḥārith and al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib gathered together and said: ""If only we were to send these two young men"" — meaning myself and al-Faḍl — ""to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) so that he might speak to him and appoint them over these ṣadaqāt, so that they would deliver what the people deliver and receive what the people receive."" While they were in that, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) came upon them and stood before them. They mentioned it to him, and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: ""Do not do that, for by Allah, he will not do it."" Rabiʿah ibn al-Ḥārith turned away from him and said: ""By Allah, you only do this out of envy toward us, for by Allah, you have attained the bond of marriage with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam), and we do not begrudge you that."" He (ʿAlī) said: ""I am Abū Ḥasan al-Qarm — send them."" So they departed and went. He (ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib) lay down, and when the Prophet (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) had prayed, we went ahead of him to the chamber and stood by it until he came. He took hold of our ears and said: ""Bring forth what you are concealing."" Then he entered, and we entered with him, while that day he was with Zaynab bint Jaḥsh. We hesitated regarding speaking, then one of us spoke and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you are the most trustworthy of people and the most connected of kin. We have reached the age of marriage, and we have come to you so that you may appoint us over some of these ṣadaqāt, so that we may deliver to you what the people deliver and receive what the people receive."" He remained silent for a long time. We wanted to speak to him, and Zaynab (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) gestured to us from behind the curtain not to speak to him. Then he said: ""Indeed, ṣadaqah is not permissible for the family of Muḥammad — it is merely the impurities of people. Call to me Maḥmiyyah."" And he was in charge of the khums. And he called Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. He said to Maḥmiyyah: ""Marry this young man, the son of your daughter, to al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās."" So he married her to him. And he said to Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith: ""Marry this young man, the son of your daughter, to me."" So he married her to me. And he said to Maḥmiyyah: ""Give them such and such from the khums."" Al-Zuhrī said: ""And he did not name it for me."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ. [Muslim 1072]"
"13246","(13240) And he narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yūnus from Ibn Shihāb, who said in the ḥadīth: He said to us: ""Indeed, this ṣadaqah is merely the impurities of people, and it is not lawful for Muḥammad nor for the family of Muḥammad."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13247","(13241) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq related to us: ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī related to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus informed me, and he mentioned it with its meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13248","(13242) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Aṣbahānī related to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb related to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī related to us: Shuʿbah related to us, from al-Ḥakam, from Ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, from Abī Rāfiʿ, that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) sent a man from Banī Makhzūm to collect the ṣadaqah. He said to Abī Rāfiʿ: ""Accompany me so that we may receive something from it."" He said: ""No, until I come to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) and ask him."" So he went to the Prophet (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) and asked him. He said: ""Indeed, ṣadaqah is not lawful for us, and the mawlā of a people is from among themselves."" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi]"
"13249","(13243) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us: al-Faḍl ibn Ḥubāb al-Jumaḥī related to us: Ibn Kathīr and al-Ḥawḍī and Abū al-Walīd and ʿAmr ibn Marzūq said: Shuʿbah informed us, and he mentioned it with its like. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi]"
"13250","(13244) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb related to us: Abū Qilābah related to us: Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ related to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī related to us —

(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār related to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq related to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd related to us, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā), who said: Arqam al-Zuhrī was appointed over the ṣadaqāt, and he took Abī Rāfiʿ with him. He came to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallAllāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) and asked him. He said: ""O Abā Rāfiʿ, indeed ṣadaqah is forbidden for the family of Muḥammad, and the mawlā of a people is from among themselves."" The narration of Shuʿbah from al-Ḥakam is more deserving than the narration of Ibn Abī Laylā. And Ibn Abī Laylā was known for poor memory and frequent error. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi]"
"13251","(13245) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓufar ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us: Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib from Umm Kulthūm bint ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with both of them) who said: I brought her something from charity, and she said: ""Guard our young ones and our freedmen against it, for Maimūn or Mihrān, the freedman of the Prophet (peace be upon him), informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Indeed, we, the People of the Household, are prohibited from charity, and our freedmen are of our own selves, so do not eat charity.'"""
"13252","(13246) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib who said: A man entrusted me with a bequest from zakāh or charity, so I came to Umm Kulthūm bint ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with both of them), and she said: ""Guard against our young ones taking from it."" Then he mentioned the hadith with the same meaning."
"13253","(13247) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥmad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated: al-Ḥakam narrated from Ibrāhīm from al-Aswad from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was brought some meat and it was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is from what was given in charity to Barīrah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""It is charity for her and a gift for us."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah."
"13254","(13248) Abū Bakr Muḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated: Qatādah informed us from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) was brought some meat and he said: ""What is this?"" He said: ""This was given in charity to Barīrah."" He said: ""It is a gift for us and charity upon her."" Al-Bukhārī said: Abū Dāwūd mentioned it, and they both narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah."
"13255","(13249) Abū Ḥāzim al-ʿAbdawī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Sharīk al-Asadī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūnus al-Yarbūʿī narrated to us: Abū Shiḥāb narrated from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ from Ḥafṣah from Umm ʿAṭiyyah al-Anṣāriyyah who said: Nusaybah al-Anṣāriyyah was sent a sheep, and she sent some of it to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Do you have anything?"" She said: ""Nothing except what Nusaybah sent from that sheep."" He said: ""Bring it near, for it has reached its [proper] place."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ."
"13256","(13250) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Bahz ibn Ḥakīm narrated from his father from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When the Prophet (peace be upon him) was brought something, he would ask about it: ""Is it a gift or charity?"" If they said it was a gift, he would extend his hand, and if they said it was charity, he would say to his Companions: ""Take it."" And we have narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ziyād from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) to the same effect."
"13257","(13251) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Quṭn ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ziyād from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), when food was brought to him, would ask about it: ""Is it a gift or charity?"" If it was said to be charity, he would say to his Companions: ""Eat,"" and he would not eat. And if it was said to be a gift, he would reach out his hand and eat with them."
"13258","(13252) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muʾaddib narrated to us: Suwayd — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr al-Qushayrī and ʿImrān ibn Mūsā — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Sarrāj — they said: Suwayd ibn Saʿīd narrated to me: Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Abū al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: A man said, ""I shall certainly give charity tonight with a charity."" So he went out with his charity and placed it in the hand of a prostitute. In the morning, the people began talking, saying, ""Charity was given to a prostitute!"" He said, ""O Allah, to You be the praise — a prostitute! I shall certainly give charity tonight with a charity."" So he went out with his charity and placed it in the hand of a wealthy man. In the morning, they began talking, saying, ""Charity was given to a wealthy man!"" He said, ""O Allah, to You be the praise — a wealthy man! I shall certainly give charity tonight with a charity."" So he went out with his charity and placed it in the hand of a thief. In the morning, they began talking, saying, ""Charity was given to a thief!"" He said, ""O Allah, to You be the praise — a prostitute, a wealthy man, and a thief!"" Then he was approached and told: ""As for your charity, it has been accepted. As for the prostitute, perhaps she will restrain herself from her unlawful intercourse by means of it; perhaps the wealthy man will take heed and spend from what Allah the Exalted has given him; and perhaps the thief will restrain himself from his theft by means of it."" The wording is from the hadith of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Suwayd ibn Saʿīd, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah from Abū al-Zinād. And in this is an indication that it pertains to voluntary charity."
"13259","(13253) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us: Abū al-Muwajjah informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Isrāʾīl narrated to us: Abū al-Juwayriyyah al-Jarmī narrated that Maʿn ibn Yazīd al-Sulamī narrated to him, saying: I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) — I, my father, and my grandfather — and he proposed on my behalf and married me. I brought a dispute to him. My father Yazīd had gone out with some dinars to give in charity and placed them with a man in the mosque. I came and took them and brought them to him. He said: ""By Allah, it was not you I intended with them."" So I brought the dispute to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and he said: ""For you is what you intended, O Yazīd, and for you, O Maʿn, is what you took."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf from Isrāʾīl. And this may have been pertaining to voluntary charity. As for the obligatory, we have narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he said: ""Charity is not permissible for a wealthy person nor for one of sound strength."" And we have narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""Neither a child nor a parent has a right to obligatory charity."" And we have narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) that which indicates the same."
"13260","(13254) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Tamīm in Baghdād narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAlqamah al-Marwazī narrated to us: Abū Ḥamzah al-Sukkārī narrated from Abū al-Juwayriyyah al-Jarmī who said: I heard Maʿn ibn Yazīd say: I brought a dispute to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and he decided in my favor. He proposed on my behalf and married me. I pledged allegiance to him — I and my grandfather. I said to him: ""What was your dispute?"" He said: ""A man used to frequent the mosque and give charity to men he knew. He came one night with a purse, and he supposed that I was one of those he recognized. When morning came, it became clear to him, so he came to me and said: 'Return it.' I refused, so we brought the dispute to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and he permitted the charity for me and said: 'For you is the reward of what you intended.'"" The Sheikh said: And it is apparent from this that the one who gave charity was an unrelated man, and Allah knows best."
"13261","(13255) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Duḥaym narrated to us: al-Walīd narrated from al-Awzāʿī, from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I went to the Prophet (ﷺ) in the morning with ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah so that he might chew something and soften it for him (taḥnīk). I came upon him and in his hand was a branding iron with which he was branding the camels of ṣadaqah."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir, and Muslim narrated it from Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf, both of them from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. [Bukhārī 1502, Muslim 2119]"
"13262","(13256) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Haytham ibn Khalaf, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān, and Ibn Yāsīn informed us, saying: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿAdī narrated from Ibn ʿAwn, from Muḥammad, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Umm Sulaym gave birth and said to me: 'O Anas, look after this boy and let nothing afflict him until you take him to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the morning.' So I took him in the morning and found him in a garden wearing a Ḥawtakī cloak, and he was branding the backs of the camels that had arrived at the time of the Conquest (of Makkah)."" al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Mūsā Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. [Bukhārī 5824, Muslim 2119]"
"13263","(13257) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought many camels from the camels of jizyah. He said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb: ""Among the herd there is a blind she-camel."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Hand it over to the people of the household (Ahl al-Bayt) so that they may benefit from it."" He said: ""But it is blind."" He said: ""They will attach it to the line of camels."" He said: ""How will it eat from the ground?"" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Is it from the camels of jizyah or from the camels of ṣadaqah?"" He said: ""From the camels of jizyah."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""By Allah, you intended to eat it."" He said: ""It bears the brand of jizyah."" So ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered that it be slaughtered. He said: ""There were nine plates with him, and there was no fruit or delicacy except that some of it was placed in those plates and sent to the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ). What was sent to Ḥafṣah (may Allah be pleased with her) was from the last of that, and if there was any shortfall, it was in Ḥafṣah's portion."" He said: ""He placed some of the meat of that camel in those plates and sent it to the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ), and he ordered that the remainder of the meat be prepared, and he invited the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār to it.""

al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""This indicates that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to apply two brands: the brand of jizyah and the brand of ṣadaqah, and this is our position."" [Muwaṭṭāʾ 619]"
"13264","(13258) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us: Salamah ibn Shabīb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan, meaning Ibn Muḥammad ibn Aʿyan, narrated to us: Maʿqil narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ﷺ) passed by a donkey that had been branded on its face and said: ""May Allah curse the one who branded it."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Salamah ibn Shabīb. [Muslim 2117]"
"13265","(13259) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf said: Sufyān mentioned from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw a donkey that had been branded on its face, its nostrils being cauterized, and he said: 'May Allah curse the one who did this. Did I not forbid that anyone brand the face or strike the face?'"" [Muslim 2116]"
"13266","(13260) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʪsá narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb that Nāʿim, Abū ʿAbd Allāh, the freed slave of Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), narrated to him that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw a donkey branded on the face and he disapproved of that. He said: 'By Allah, I shall not brand it except on the furthest part of the face.' He ordered that his donkey be cauterized on its haunches, and he was the first one to be cauterized on the haunches."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʪsá, and in it there is no mention of who the speaker was. [Muslim 2118]"
"13267","(13261) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn al-Faḍl narrated to me: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-ʿAllāf, the companion of Ibn Sāwāʾ, narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sāwāʾ narrated from Saʿīd, from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Prophet (ﷺ) saw a donkey that had been branded on its face and said: ""Was I not forbidding this?"" al-ʿAbbās said: ""Indeed, I shall not brand except on the furthest place from the face,"" so he branded it on the haunches. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13268","(13262) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: ʿAbd al-Bāqī ibn Qāniʿ narrated to us in Baghdād: Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us: ʿĀrim narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that al-ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) used to brand on the face. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade branding on the face, he said: ""I shall not brand except on the lowest place of the face,"" so he branded it on the haunches. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13269","(13263) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Abū Zurʿah ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Rāzī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: ʿAwn ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to me: Ziyād ibn Qurayʿ narrated to me: Ghaylān ibn Junādah narrated from his father, Junādah ibn Jarād, one of Banī Ghaylān ibn Jāwah, who said: ""I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) with camels that I had branded on their noses. He said: 'O Junādah, did you not find a limb on which to brand them other than the face? Indeed, before you is retaliation (qiṣāṣ).' He said: 'The matter is yours.' He said: 'Bring me something on which there is no brand.' So I brought him a two-year-old she-camel, a one-year-old she-camel, and a three-year-old she-camel. He said: 'Will you sell me this fine-colored she-camel? I shall buy it with its ṣadaqah.' He said: 'The matter is yours.' So I took hold of the branding iron, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Move it back.' He kept saying: 'Move it back, move it back,' until I reached the thigh. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Brand it, with blessing.' So I branded them on their thighs. Its ṣadaqah consisted of two three-year-old she-camels, and they numbered ninety."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13270","(13264) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Bāghandī narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Hishām ibn Zayd, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I entered with my brother to the Prophet (ﷺ) so that he might perform taḥnīk on him, and I saw him in a stable branding sheep — I think he said — on their ears."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from Shuʿbah. [Bukhārī, Muslim 2119]"
"13271","(13265) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us that Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us that Miskīn ibn Bukayr al-Ḥarrānī narrated from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr, who said: I was at the gate of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz — may Allah, the Most High, have mercy upon him — when horses appeared before us upon whose thighs was written: ""ʿuddatun lillāh"" (a provision for the sake of Allah).

The Sheikh said: Discussion has already taken place in the Book of Zakāh regarding what has been narrated from ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — concerning rikāz and other matters related to this book. And with Allah lies success. [Ḥasan]"
"13272","(13266) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us that ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us that Yūnus narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was commanded to give his wives a choice, he began with me and said: ""O ʿĀʾishah, I am about to tell you something, so it is not incumbent upon you to hasten until you consult your parents."" She said: ""And he knew that my parents would never command me to separate from him."" Then he said: ""O Prophet, say to your wives: If you desire the life of this world and its adornment, then come, I shall provide for you and release you with a gracious release. But if you desire Allah and His Messenger and the Abode of the Hereafter, then indeed Allah has prepared for the doers of good among you a great reward."" I said: ""Concerning this shall I consult my parents? Indeed I desire Allah and His Messenger and the Abode of the Hereafter."" Then his wives did the same as I had done.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yūnus ibn Yazīd. [Bukhārī 4786, Muslim 1475]"
"13273","(13267) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Thawr, from Ibn ʿAbbās — may Allah be pleased with them both — who said: I remained persistent in asking ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — about the two wives of the Prophet ﷺ concerning whom Allah, the Most High, said: ""If you two repent to Allah, then indeed your hearts have inclined"" [al-Taḥrīm: 4], until ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — performed Ḥajj and I performed Ḥajj with him.

When we were along part of the road, ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — turned aside for his need, and I turned aside with him carrying the vessel. He relieved himself, then came, and I poured water over his hands, and he performed wuḍūʾ. I said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, who are the two wives of the Prophet ﷺ concerning whom Allah, the Most High, said: 'If you two repent to Allah, then indeed your hearts have inclined'?"" ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — said: ""How astonishing of you, O Ibn ʿAbbās!"" Al-Zuhrī — may Allah, the Most High, have mercy upon him — said: ""By Allah, he disliked that he was asked about it, but he did not conceal it."" He said: ""They are Ḥafṣah and ʿĀʾishah."" Then he began to drive the narrative forward and said:

""We, the people of Quraysh, were a people who dominated their women. When we came to Madīnah, we found a people whose women dominated them. So our women began to learn from their women."" He said: ""My dwelling was in Banī Umayyah ibn Zayd in al-ʿĀwālī. One day I became angry with my wife, and she answered me back. I found it astonishing that she should answer me back. She said: 'What makes you astonished that I should answer you back? By Allah, the wives of the Prophet ﷺ answer him back, and one of them shuns him for the entire day until nightfall.'"" He said: ""So I set out and entered upon Ḥafṣah and said: 'Do you answer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ back?' She said: 'Yes.' I said: 'And does one of you shun him for the entire day until nightfall?' I said: 'She who does that among you has failed and lost. Is any one of you secure that Allah may become angry with her due to the anger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? She is ruined. Do not answer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ back and do not ask him for anything. Ask me whatever you wish, and let not your co-wife deceive you, for she is more beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than you' — meaning ʿĀʾishah.""

He said: ""I had a neighbor from the Anṣār, and we used to take turns descending to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — he would go down one day and I would go down the next. He would bring me news of the revelation and other matters, and I would bring him the like of that."" He said: ""We were saying that Ghassān were equipping the horses for our expedition. Then my companion came down one day and came to me at ʿIshāʾ, knocked on my door, and called out to me. I went out to him and he said: 'A great matter has occurred!' I said: 'What? Have the Ghassān come out?' He said: 'No, but greater than that and longer — the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has divorced his wives!' I said: 'Ḥafṣah has indeed failed and lost!' I had been thinking that this would happen. When I prayed Fajr, I tightened my garments, then went down and entered upon Ḥafṣah while she was weeping. I said: 'Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divorced you?' She said: 'I do not know. He is in seclusion in this upper chamber.' I went to a black slave of his and said: 'Seek permission for ʿUmar.' The slave entered, then came out to me and said: 'I mentioned you to him, and he remained silent.' I set out until I came to the mosque, and there were people sitting around the minbar, some of them weeping. I sat for a while, then what I felt overcame me, so I went to the slave and said: 'Seek permission for ʿUmar.' He entered, then came out to me and said: 'I mentioned you to him, and he remained silent.' I went out and sat by the minbar. Then what I felt overcame me, so I went to the slave and said: 'Seek permission for ʿUmar.' He entered, then came out to me and said: 'I mentioned you to him, and he remained silent.' I turned to leave, and the slave was calling me, saying: 'Enter, permission has been given to you.' I entered and greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he was reclining upon a mat of palm leaves that had left marks on his side. I said: 'Have you divorced your wives, O Messenger of Allah?' He raised his head toward me and said: 'No.' I said: 'Allāhu Akbar! If only you could see us, O Messenger of Allah! We were a people who dominated their women. When we came to Madīnah, we found a people whose women dominated them. So our women began to learn from their women. One day I became angry with my wife, and she answered me back — I found it astonishing. She said: ""What makes you astonished that I should answer you back? By Allah, the wives of the Prophet ﷺ answer him back, and one of them shuns him for the entire day until nightfall.""' I said: 'She who does that among them has indeed failed and lost. Is any one of them secure that Allah may become angry with her due to the anger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? She is ruined.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled. I said: 'I entered upon Ḥafṣah and said: ""Let not your co-wife deceive you, for she is more beautiful than you and more beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than you.""' He smiled again. I said: 'May I be at ease, O Messenger of Allah ﷺ?' He said: 'Yes.' So I sat and raised my house in the room, and by Allah, I saw nothing in it that would attract the eye except three hides. I said: 'Supplicate to Allah, O Messenger of Allah, to expand provision for His ummah, for provision has been expanded for Persia and Rome, and they do not worship Allah.' He sat up straight and said: 'Are you in doubt, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb? Those are a people whose good things were hastened for them in the life of this world.' I said: 'I seek forgiveness from Allah, O Messenger of Allah.' And he had sworn that he would not enter upon them for a month, out of the intensity of his displeasure with them, until Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, reproached him.""

Al-Zuhrī said: ʿUrwah informed me from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: ""When twenty-nine nights had passed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me, beginning with me. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you swore that you would not enter upon us — meaning for a month — yet you have entered upon me after twenty-nine — I counted them.' He said: 'Indeed, the month is twenty-nine.' Then he said: 'O ʿĀʾishah, I am about to mention a matter to you, so do not hasten in it until you consult your parents.' Then he recited to me: ""O Prophet, say to your wives: If you desire the life of this world and its adornment..."" [al-Aḥzāb: 28]. She said: 'By Allah, my parents would never command me to separate from him.' She said: I said: 'Concerning this shall I consult my parents? Indeed I desire Allah and His Messenger and the Abode of the Hereafter.'""

Maʿmar said, and Ayyūb informed me, that ʿĀʾishah said to him: ""Do not say that I have chosen you."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I have only been sent as a conveyor, and I have not been sent as one who imposes hardship.""

Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿUmar, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, in its full length."
"13274","(13268) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us.

(Ḥ) He said, and Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us in Baghdād that al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us; both said: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us that Zakariyyā ibn Isḥāq informed us that Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Jābir, who said: Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — came seeking permission to enter upon the Prophet ﷺ. He found people sitting at his door; permission had not been given to any of them. Permission was given to Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — and he entered. Then ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — approached and sought permission, and permission was given to him. He found the Prophet ﷺ sitting with his wives around him, silent and downcast. ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — said: ""I shall say something that will make the Prophet ﷺ laugh."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, if only you could see the daughter of Khārijah — she asked me for expenses, so I went to her and struck her neck."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and said: ""They are around me, as you see, asking me for expenses."" Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — stood up toward ʿĀʾishah and struck her neck, and ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — stood up toward Ḥafṣah and struck her neck, both of them saying: ""You ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for what he does not have."" They said: ""By Allah, we will not ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for what he does not have.""

Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ withdrew from them for a month, or for twenty-nine days. Then this verse was revealed: ""O Prophet, say to your wives..."" until His words: ""...for the doers of good among you a great reward"" [al-Aḥzāb: 28–29]. He said: He began with ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — and said: ""O ʿĀʾishah, I wish to present a matter to you, and I would love that you not hasten in it until you consult your parents."" She said: ""What is it, O Messenger of Allah?"" He recited the verse to her. She said: ""Concerning you, O Messenger of Allah, shall I consult my parents? Rather I choose Allah and His Messenger."" I ask you not to inform any of your wives of what I have said. He said: ""No woman among them will ask me but that I will inform her. Indeed Allah has not sent me as one who imposes hardship, but He has sent me as a teacher and one who facilitates.""

Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah. [Muslim 1478]"
"13275","(13269) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Shīrāzī al-Faqīh informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Akram informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb and ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī said: ""He informed us,"" and Muḥammad said: ""He informed us"" — Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd said: Ismāʿīl informed us.

Abū ʿAbd Allāh said: And Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us that ʿAbthar narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khāld, from al-Shaʿbī, from Masrūq, who said: ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave us a choice, and we did not regard it as a divorce.""

Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī recorded it from another chain from Ismāʿīl. [Bukhārī 1477]"
"13276","(13270) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Sūsī all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Asad informed us that al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from al-Awzāʿī, who said: I asked al-Zuhrī, ""Which of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ sought refuge from him?"" He said: ʿUrwah informed me from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — that when the daughter of al-Jawn al-Kilābiyyah was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, she said: ""I seek refuge in Allah from you."" He said: ""You have indeed sought refuge with a mighty one. Return to your family.""

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī, from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. [Bukhārī 4254]"
"13277","(13271) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Saʿd ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAṭiyyah al-ʿAwfī informed us that my father informed me, my paternal uncle informed me, my father informed me from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās — may Allah be pleased with them both — regarding the words of Allah, the Most High: ""And from the night, pray with it as an additional prayer for you"" [al-Isrāʾ: 79]. He said: By ""additional prayer"" (nāfilah) it means that it was made specific for the Prophet ﷺ — he was commanded to perform the night prayer and it was made obligatory upon him. [Very weak. Al-Ṭabarī 17/525]"
"13278","(13272) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — it was read over him — and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally; both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Bakr ibn Sahl informed us that ʿAbd al-Ghanī ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī informed us that Mūsā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ṣanʿānī narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Three things are obligatory upon me and are a Sunnah for you: the Witr prayer, the miswāk, and the night prayer (qiyām al-layl).""

Mūsā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān is very weak, and this chain is not established. And Allah knows best. [Very weak]"
"13279","(13273) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ṭabarānī informed us through it that Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī informed us that Ibn Abī Masarrat informed us that Khallād informed us that Miʿsar informed us that Ziyād ibn ʿAlāqah said: I heard al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah — may Allah be pleased with him — say: Indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray until his feet would swell or become inflamed. It was said to him, and he said: ""Shall I not be a grateful servant?""

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Khallād ibn Yaḥyā, and Muslim recorded it from two other chains from Ziyād ibn ʿAlāqah. [Bukhārī 1130, Muslim 2819]"
"13280","(13274) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed me that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muhannā al-Azdī informed me that Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf informed me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me that Abū Ṣakhr narrated from Ibn Qusayṭ, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, he would stand until his feet would crack. ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do this, when Allah has forgiven you what has preceded of your sin and what is to come?"" He said: ""O ʿĀʾishah, shall I not be a grateful servant?""

Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf. [Bukhārī 3837, Muslim 2820]"
"13281","(13275) The jurist Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb ibn Aḥmad ibn Ya'qūb — the nephew of my father — informed us: the jurist Abū al-Naḍr informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: al-Rabī' ibn Muslim al-Qurashī, from Muḥammad ibn Ziyād, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace be upon him) would accept gifts (hadiyyah) but would not consume charitable alms (ṣadaqah). Muslim recorded this in his Ṣaḥīḥ, as was previously mentioned at the end of the Kitāb al-Hibāt. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13282","(13276) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us: 'Abdullāh ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh informed us: Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān informed us: al-Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm said: Bahz mentioned it from his father, from his grandfather, who said: When food was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), he would ask about it: ""Is it a gift (hadiyyah) or charitable alms (ṣadaqah)?"" If they said it was a gift, he would extend his hand [to eat it]; and if they said it was charitable alms, he would say to his Companions: ""Eat."" Bahz is Ḥakīm ibn Mu'āwiyah ibn Ḥaydah al-Qushayrī, one of the Banū 'Āmir ibn Ṣa'ṣa'ah ibn Hawāzin — this is what Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān stated. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi]"
"13283","(13277) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Mufaḍḍal informed us: Asbāṭ ibn Naṣr al-Hamdānī said: al-Suddī claimed, from Muṣ'ab ibn Sa'd, from his father, who said: On the day of the Conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) granted safety to the people except for four men and two women among them. 'Abdullāh ibn Sa'd ibn Abī Sarḥ was among them. He [the narrator] mentioned the hadith until he said: As for 'Abdullāh ibn Sa'd ibn Abī Sarḥ, he had taken refuge with 'Uthmān ibn 'Affān (may Allah be pleased with him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) called the people to pledge allegiance, he was brought and made to stand before the Prophet (peace be upon him). ['Uthmān] said: ""O Messenger of Allah, accept the pledge of 'Abdullāh."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) raised his head and looked at him three times — each time refusing. Then he accepted his pledge after the third time. Then he turned to his Companions and said: ""Is there not among you a sensible man who will stand up to this one, seeing that I withheld my hand from his pledge, and kill him?"" They said: ""We do not know, O Messenger of Allah, what is in your own mind. Why did you not signal to us with your eye?"" He said: ""It is not befitting for a prophet to have a treacherous eye."" [Similar to the previous hadith]"
"13284","(13278) Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmish al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us: 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr informed us: Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn 'Abdullāh (may Allah be pleased with both of them), from the Prophet (peace be upon him), that he said: ""War is deception (khud'ah)."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl, and Muslim narrated it from 'Alī ibn Ḥujr and Zuhayr — all of them from Ibn 'Uyaynah. [Ḥasan]"
"13285","(13279) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: 'Ubayd ibn Sharīk informed us: Yaḥyī ibn Bukayr informed us: al-Layth, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Abdullāh ibn Ka'b ibn Mālik, that 'Abdullāh ibn Ka'b said: I heard Ka'b ibn Mālik narrating when he had stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). He mentioned the hadith and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would never intend to set out on a military expedition except that he would direct attention toward another [to conceal the true objective]. al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Bukayr, and Muslim recorded it from another chain from al-Layth. [al-Bukhārī, Muslim 2769]"
"13286","(13280) And Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsá informed us: al-Ḥumaydī informed us: Sufyān, from 'Amr, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Who will deal with Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf? He has indeed harmed Allah and His Messenger."" Muḥammad ibn Maslamah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, would you like me to kill him?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then permit me to speak [to him with deception]."" He said: ""I have given you permission."" He [the narrator] then mentioned the story of his stratagem in killing Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf. He said: When they overpowered him, they killed him and came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and informed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""War is deception (khud'ah)."" Both [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] recorded it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Ibn 'Uyaynah."
"13287","(13281) Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Ja'far Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Baghdādī informed us: Abū 'Ulāthah Muḥammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khālid informed us: My father informed us: Ibn Luhī'ah, from Abū al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, who mentioned the story of Uḥud and the indication of the Prophet (peace be upon him) to the Muslims to remain in Madīnah, and that many of the people refused except to go out [to the enemy]. He said: Had they heeded the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and his command, it would have been better for them. But divine decree and fate overpowered them. He said: The majority of those who advised him to go out were men who had not witnessed Badr, though they knew the precedence in virtue that the people of Badr had attained.

When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) had prayed the Friday prayer, he admonished the people, reminded them, and commanded them to strive and exert effort. Then he departed from his sermon and prayer, called for his armor, and put it on. Then he announced to the people to set out. When certain men of insight saw this, they said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) has commanded us to remain in Madīnah. If the enemy comes upon us, we will fight them in the alleys."" Yet Allah knows best what He intends and what revelation comes from the heavens. Then they made him set out and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, shall we remain as you have commanded us?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""It is not befitting for a prophet, once he has taken up the implements of war and announced to the people to set out against the enemy, to turn back until he fights. I called you to this matter, but you refused except to go out. So observe taqwā of Allah and patience when you meet the enemy, and see what I have commanded you to do — then do it."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) then set out, and the Muslims went with him, and he mentioned the hadith. And thus Mūsā ibn 'Uqbah narrated it from al-Zuhrī. And likewise Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yār mentioned it from his shaykhs among the people of the maghāzī [biographical accounts of military campaigns], and it is general among the people of the maghāzī, even though its chain is munqaṭi' (disconnected). [Ḍa'īf]"
"13288","(13282) And we have recorded it with a connected chain that is ḥasan. Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh ibn 'Abd al-Ḥakam informed us: 'Abdullāh ibn Wahb informed me: Ibn Abī al-Zinād, from his father, from 'Ubaydullāh ibn 'Abdillāh ibn 'Utban, from Ibn 'Abbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) acquired his sword Dhū al-Faqār on the day of Badr. Ibn 'Abbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) said: It is the one regarding which he saw a dream on the day of Uḥud. That is: when the polytheists came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) on the day of Uḥud, his opinion was to remain in Madīnah and fight them there. But people who had not witnessed Badr said: ""O Messenger of Allah, take us out to them at Uḥud, that we may fight them there,"" hoping to attain the virtue that the people of Badr had attained. They persisted with him until he put on his armor. Then they regretted it and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, stay — the opinion is your opinion."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""It is not befitting for a prophet to remove his armor after having put it on until Allah judges between him and his enemy."" He said: And among what the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to them on that day, before he put on his armor, was: ""I have seen in a dream that I am in a fortified stronghold — and I interpreted it as Madīnah — and that I am behind a large rock — and I interpreted it as the ram of the army detachment. I saw that my sword Dhū al-Faqār had broken — and I interpreted that as a defeat among you. And I saw cattle being slaughtered"" — and [he said] ""a cow, and by Allah, goodness"" — ""a cow, and by Allah, goodness."" [al-Bukhārī 3860, Muslim 2327]"
"13289","(13283) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us: 'Abdullāh ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh informed us: Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān informed us: 'Abdullāh ibn Maslamah, from Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from 'Urwah, from 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was never given a choice between two matters except that he would take the easier of the two, so long as it was not a sin. If it was a sin, he would be the farthest of people from it. And the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) never took revenge for himself, except when a sacred prohibition of Allah had been violated — then he would take revenge for Allah's sake. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 3560, Muslim 2327]"
"13290","(13284) And Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: 'Alī ibn 'Īsá informed us: Mūsá ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī informed us: Yaḥyá ibn Yaḥyá said: I read [the hadith] to Mālik, and he mentioned it, except that he did not mention his statement ""then he would take revenge for Allah's sake."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdullāh ibn Maslamah, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyá ibn Yaḥyá. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13291","(13285) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Ḥammād al-Anṣārī al-Miṣrī and Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl al-Nahdī both said: Jumayʿ ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-ʿIjlī narrated to me: a man in Makkah narrated to me, from a son of Abū Hālah al-Tamīmī, from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, who said: ""I asked my maternal uncle Hind ibn Abī Hālah al-Tamīmī—""

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥusaynī al-ʿAqīqī, the author of the book on genealogy, narrated to us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to me: ʿAlī ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to me, from his brother Mūsā ibn Jaʿfar, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn, who said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī said: ""I asked my maternal uncle Hind ibn Abī Hālah about the appearance of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he was one who described at length."" He mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: ""He would check on his Companions and ask the people about what was among the people. He would praise the good and affirm it, and he would disparage the ugly and weaken it."" In the first narration, instead of *yuṣawwibuhu* (affirm it), it is *yuqawwīhi* (strengthen it)."
"13292","(13286) And Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Sirāj al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the brother of Ḥusayn al-Juʿfī, narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from Idrīs al-Awdī, from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah, from Mujāhid, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbāṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: *""And you did not recite before it any scripture, nor did you inscribe one with your right hand""* (al-ʿAnkabūt, 48): He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) neither read nor wrote."" [Ḥasan]"
"13293","(13287) And Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭabarānī informed us in Ḥamāh: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbadah narrated to us: Shuʿbah said: I heard al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""We are an unlettered nation (*ummiyyah*); we do not write and we do not calculate. The month is thus and thus and thus""—and he folded one of his fingers—""and thus and thus and thus,"" meaning thirty. Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1913. Muslim 1080]"
"13294","(13288) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Khathʿamī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ḥakīm al-Awdī narrated to us: Sharīḥ ibn Muslimah narrated to us: Ibrāhīm—meaning Ibn Yūsuf ibn Abī Isḥāq—narrated to me: my father narrated to me, from Abū Isḥāq, who narrated to me: al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""When the Prophet (ﷺ) intended to perform ʿUmrah, he sent to the people of Makkah seeking their permission to enter Makkah. They stipulated upon him that he would not stay in it except three nights, that he would not enter it except with the sheaths of weapons, and that he would not call any of them to Islam.""

He said: ""ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) took up writing the terms between them. He wrote: 'This is what Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, has agreed upon.' They said: 'If we knew that you were the Messenger of Allah, we would not have prevented you and we would have pledged allegiance to you. But write: This is what Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh has agreed upon.' He said: 'By Allah, I am Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh, and by Allah, I am the Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'And he did not write.' He said: ""He said to ʿAlī: 'Erase ""Messenger of Allah.""' ʿAlī said: 'By Allah, I will never erase it.' He said: 'Then show it to me.' So he showed it to him, and the Prophet (ﷺ) erased it with his own hand.""

He said: ""When he had entered and the appointed time had passed, they came to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: 'Tell your companion to depart.' So ʿAlī mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said: 'Yes, we shall depart.'"" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān al-Awdī, and Muslim recorded it from the hadith of Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah, from Abū Isḥāq, with the same meaning. Al-Bukhārī also recorded it from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā, from Isrāʾīl, from Abū Isḥāq, and he said in the hadith: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took the document, and he did not write well."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3184. Muslim 783]"
"13295","(13289) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned the hadith of the incident, and he mentioned in it that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""O ʿAlī, erase 'Messenger of Allah.'"" He said: ""By Allah, I will never erase you."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took the document, and he did not write well. In the narration of Yūsuf ibn Abī Isḥāq, from his father, from al-Barāʾ, in this story, he said: ""He said: 'Show it to me.' So he showed it to him, and he erased it with his own hand."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13296","(13290) As for the hadith which Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of, among others, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyāṭī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn Manṣūr al-Qushayrī al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Abū al-Naḍr Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us: Abū ʿAqīl Yaḥyā ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us: Mujālid ibn Saʿīd narrated to me: ʿAwn ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to me, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not die until he had learned to write and read."" Mujālid said: ""I mentioned that to al-Shaʿbī, and he said: 'He has spoken the truth. I have heard from our Companions who mention that.'"" This is a disconnected hadith, and among its narrators are a number of weak and unknown narrators. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13297","(13291) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Nuʿaym, the agent of al-Muttaqī, narrated to us in Baghdād: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Hilāl al-Naḥwī al-Ḍarīr narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAmr al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never combined two verses of poetry together except for one single verse:

*Tafaʾal bimā tahwā yakun, fa-qallamā yuqālu li-shayʾin kāna illā taḥaqqaqa.*

('Be optimistic about what you desire, for rarely is anything said to be, except that it comes to pass.')""

ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""And he did not say *taḥaqqaqā* (dual), lest it become metrical and thus turn into poetry.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""I have not recorded it except through this chain of narration, and among them are those whose status is unknown. As for the rajaz verse, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13298","(13292) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us from the original of his hearing: Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdūs ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Simsār al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Rāzī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī narrated to me: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to me, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out on a cold morning while the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār were digging the trench.

He said: 'O Allah, the good is the good of the Hereafter, so forgive the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn.'

They responded:

*Naḥnu alladhīn bāyāʿū Muḥammadā ʿalā al-jihādi mā baqīnā abadā.*

('We are those who pledged allegiance to Muḥammad upon jihad, for as long as we remain.')

Al-Bukhārī recorded it through various routes from Ḥumayd. [Bukhārī 2834. Muslim 1805]"
"13299","(13293) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq narrated to us, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the day of the trench carrying earth until the earth covered the hair of his chest, and he was a man with much hair. He was reciting the rajaz verses of ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah (may Allah be pleased with him):

*Allāhumma lawlā anta mā hadaynā wa lā taṣaddaqnā wa lā ṣallaynā.*

*Fa-anzilnā sakīnah ʿalaynā wa thabbit al-aqdām in laqaynā.*

*Inna al-aʿdāʾa qad baghaw ʿalaynā wa in arāḍū fitnah abaynā.*

He raised his voice with them."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad.

And he recorded it from the hadith of Shuʿbah, from Abū Isḥāq, and Shuʿbah said in his narration: ""The earth had covered the whiteness of his belly, and he was saying,"" and he said: *""Inna al-ulā qad baghaw ʿalaynā""* (""Indeed, those people have transgressed against us"").

Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed me: al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Shuʿbah informed us: Abū Isḥāq informed us: I heard al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was carrying earth with us on the day of al-Aḥzāb,"" then he mentioned it. Al-Bukhārī recorded it from Abū al-Walīd."
"13300","(13294) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us in Baghdād: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdān al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr al-ʿAbdī informed us: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd informed us, from Abū Isḥāq, who said: I heard al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""A man came to him and said: 'O Abū ʿUmarah, did you flee on the day of Ḥunayn?' He said: 'As for me, I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not flee. But the hastiest of the people rushed forward, and Hawāzin shot at them with arrows, and Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith was holding the head of his white mule, and he was saying:

*Anā al-nabī lā kadhib, anā ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.*

(I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.)""

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, and Muslim recorded it through another route from Sufyān."
"13301","(13295) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays from Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ in a cave when his finger was injured, and he recited:

""You are nothing but a finger that has bled,
And what you have met is in the cause of Allah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Sufyān, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from al-Aswad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2802, Muslim 1796]"
"13302","(13296) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us; al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū Sufyān from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what are the two decisive matters?"" He said: ""Whoever dies without associating anything with Allah will enter Paradise, and whoever dies associating anything with Allah will enter the Fire.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Abū Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 93]"
"13303","(13297) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Yūnus informed me from Ibn Shihāb from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be brought a man upon whom he suspected there was a debt, and he would ask: ""Has he left anything to settle his debt?"" If he was told that he had left sufficient means, he would pray over him; otherwise he would say: ""Pray over your companion."" When Allah opened the conquests for him, he said: ""I am nearer to the believers than their own selves. Whoever dies owing a debt, I shall be responsible for its repayment, and whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Yūnus. [Bukhārī, Muslim 1619]"
"13304","(13298) Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Imām informed us; ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Thābit narrated to me; my father informed me from al-Hudhayl from Muqātil ibn Sulaymān regarding the words of Allah, the Most High: {And not equal are the good deed and the bad deed. Repel by that which is better} — and that was because Abū Jahl (may Allah curse him) used to harm the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ detested him and disliked seeing him. So Allah, the Most High, commanded him to pardon and to overlook, saying: {And the one between whom and you was enmity} — meaning Abū Jahl — {will be as though he were a close friend} in this world, {an intimate one} to you in lineage, compassionate toward you.

And he said regarding the words of Allah, the Most High: {Repel by that which is better the evil deed} — it was revealed concerning the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Jahl when Abū Jahl acted ignorantly toward the Prophet ﷺ. [Very Weak]"
"13305","(13299) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding the words of Allah, the Most High: {Repel by that which is better} — he said: Allah, Glorified and Exalted, commanded the believers to be patient at the time of anger, to show forbearance at the time of ignorance, and to pardon at the time of wrongdoing. When they do that, Allah protects them from Satan and subdues their enemy for them, so that he becomes as though he were a close and intimate friend. Al-Bukhārī mentioned its text in the translation [of the Qurʾān]. And it is as though Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) held the view that although the address was directed to the Prophet ﷺ, the intended meaning includes him and others. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"13306","(13300) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; al-Qāsim ibn Naṣr al-Bazzāz Dūst narrated to us; Surayj ibn al-Nuʿmān narrated to us; Fulayḥ narrated from Hilāl ibn ʿAlī from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, who said: I met ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said to me: ""Tell me about the description of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Torah."" He said: ""Yes, by Allah, he is indeed described in the Torah with some of his attributes mentioned in the Qurʾān: 'O Prophet, We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings, and a warner, and a protector for the unlettered. You are My servant and My Messenger. I have named you al-Mutawakkil (the one who places his trust in Allah). You are not harsh, nor hard-hearted, nor one who raises his voice in the marketplaces. You do not repel evil with evil, but you pardon and forgive. And I will not take you back until I straighten through you the crooked religion, so that they say: ""There is no deity but Allah,"" and I open through you blind eyes, deaf ears, and sealed hearts.'"" ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Then I met Kaʿb al-Ḥabr and asked him, and they did not differ regarding a single letter, except that Kaʿb would say: ʿUyūnan ʿumūman, qulūban gulūfan, ādhānan ṣumūman.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Sinān from Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2125]"
"13307","(13301) Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū ʿUmar Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Shuʿbah informed me; Abū Isḥāq informed me from Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Jadalī from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not obscene, nor did he seek obscenity, nor did he raise his voice in the marketplaces, and he did not repay evil with its like, but he would pardon and overlook. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭayālisī 1623]"
"13308","(13302) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān informed us; ʿAbdullāh — that is, Ibn al-Mubārak — informed us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never struck any of his wives, nor did he ever strike a servant, nor did he ever strike anything with his right hand, except when striving in the cause of Allah. And he was never wronged in any matter that he took vengeance for himself, unless the sacred limits of Allah were violated, in which case he would take vengeance on His behalf. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was never given a choice between two matters, one of which was easier than the other, except that he chose the easier of the two, unless it involved sin; and if it involved sin, he was the furthest of all people from it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī, Muslim 2327]"
"13309","(13303) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) informed us; Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I have not seen anyone who consulted his companions more than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: And Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {And consult them in the matter} [Āl ʿImrān 159]. [Weak]"
"13310","(13304) In what Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from him, from Abū al-ʿAbbās, from al-Rabīʿ, from al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī said: ""Indeed, the Prophet ﷺ had no need of consultation, but he intended thereby that the rulers after him should establish it as a practice."" And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"13311","(13305) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad al-Tājir informed us: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Yūnus narrated to us: ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated to me: Abū Zumayl — Simāk al-Ḥanafī — narrated to me: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, and he mentioned the hadith regarding the Prophet's (ﷺ) withdrawal from his wives. He said: ""I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was lying on a mat. I sat down. He had on him only his lower garment and nothing else, and the mat had left marks on his side. I looked into the storehouse of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and lo, I found a handful of barley, approximately a ṣāʿ, and a similar quantity of qaraẓ (acacia pods) in the corner of the room, and there was a suspended hide. My eyes welled up with tears."" He said: ""What makes you weep, O son of al-Khaṭṭāb?"" I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, and why should I not weep, when this mat has left marks on your side, and this storehouse of yours contains nothing that I see — and that is while Caesar and Chosroes enjoy fruits and rivers, and you are the Messenger of Allah and His chosen one, and this is your storehouse."" He said: ""O son of al-Khaṭṭāb, are you not satisfied that the Hereafter be for us and the worldly life for them?"" I said: ""Yes."" And he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it from the ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Ibn ʿAbbās from ʿUmar from the Prophet (ﷺ) in this story: ""Those are a people whose good things were hastened for them in the life of this world."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1479]"
"13312","(13306) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""If I had gold equal to Mount Uḥud, it would not please me that three nights pass upon me while I still have anything of it, except something I set aside for the repayment of a debt."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yūnus. [Bukhārī 2389. Muslim 991]"
"13313","(13307) Abū Muḥammad — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī — informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from ʿUmārah ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ, from Abū Zurʿah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O Allah, make the provision of the family of Muḥammad merely sufficient sustenance (qūt)."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Saʿīd al-Ashajj from Abū Usāmah, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it from the ḥadīth of Fuḍayl ibn Ghazwān from ʿUmārah. [Bukhārī 4260. Muslim 1055]"
"13314","(13308) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Isḥāq al-Khurāsānī al-ʿAdl — in Baghdād, narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn Kaysān, who said: Abū Ḥāzim narrated to me, who said: ""I saw Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) repeatedly gesturing with his fingers, saying: 'By the One in whose Hand is my soul, the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) and his family never ate their fill of wheat bread for three consecutive days until he departed from this world.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5374. Muslim 2976]"
"13315","(13309) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd ibn Ghālib narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never ate his fill for three consecutive days until he passed on to his destiny."" [Bukhārī 5416. Muslim 2970]"
"13316","(13310) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, mentioning something similar to it, adding therein: ""Since he arrived in Madīnah,"" and he said: ""From wheat bread."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13317","(13311) And both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it from the ḥadīth of Manṣūr from Ibrāhīm, except that he said in the hadith: ""The family of Muḥammad (ﷺ) never ate their fill of wheat food for three consecutive nights since the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived in Madīnah until he was taken in death."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Jarīr, from Manṣūr, with that. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq. And with the same meaning, ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr and ʿĀbis ibn Rabīʿah narrated it from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13318","(13312) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ informed us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""We, the family of Muḥammad (ﷺ), would see one new moon pass, then another, then another, and we would not light a fire for food — nothing but dates and water — except that around us were the people of the dwellings among the Anṣār, and the people of each dwelling would send the milk-skin of their sheep to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and from that the Prophet (ﷺ) would have milk."" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām and others from ʿUrwah. [Bukhārī 2567. Muslim 2972]"
"13319","(13313) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb and Tamīm ibn Muḥammad said: Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated to us: Hammād narrated to us, from Qatādah, who said: ""We used to visit Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) while his baker was standing. He would say: 'Eat, for I do not know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ever saw a thin loaf of bread until he met Allah, nor did he ever see a roasted sheep with his own eyes.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hudbah ibn Khālid. [Bukhārī 5386. Muslim]"
"13320","(13314) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq al-Ṭālaqānī narrated to us: Muʿādh ibn Hishām al-Dastawāʾī narrated to us, from his father, from Yūnus ibn Abī al-Furāt, from Qatādah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never ate at a table spread, nor did he ever eat thin bread, nor did he ever take condiments in a small bowl."" It was said: ""O Abū Ḥamzah, then on what did they eat?"" He said: ""On leather dining mats (sufr)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh and others from Muʿādh ibn Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13321","(13315) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis ibn Rabīʿah narrated on the authority of his father ʿĀbis ibn Rabīʿah that ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""We used to take out the shank bone after fifteen days and eat it."" I said: ""Why did you do that?"" She laughed and said: ""The family of Muhammad ﷺ never ate bread with condiments for three consecutive days until he met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Sufyān.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5570. Muslim 1971]"
"13322","(13316) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — both said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us, Abū Usāmah narrated to us, on the authority of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away and there was nothing in my house that a living being could eat except a small quantity of barley in a shelf of mine. I ate from it for a long time, and when I measured it, it was exhausted."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Shaybah, and Muslim narrated it on the authority of Abū Kurayb, both of them on the authority of Abū Usāmah. [Bukhārī 3097. Muslim 3973]"
"13323","(13317) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Marwazī narrated to us, al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us, my father informed me, on the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The bedding of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was made of leather, and its stuffing was palm fibre."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of Aḥmad ibn Abī Rajāʾ, on the authority of al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 6456. Muslim 2082]"
"13324","(13318) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr, i.e., al-Ramādī, narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab and Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I was aided by terror, and I was given the comprehensive words. While I was asleep, the keys of the treasures of the earth were brought and placed in my hand."" Abū Hurayrah said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has departed, and you are extracting them."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd, on the authority of ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Saʿīd alone. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2971. Muslim 523]"
"13325","(13319) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us, on the authority of Ibn Ṭāwūs, on the authority of his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I was aided by terror, and I was given the treasures, and I was given the choice between remaining until I see what will be opened for my Ummah and being hastened, and I chose hastening."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13326","(13320) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, Shabābah ibn Sawwār narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Sālim al-Asadī narrated to us, and he said: I heard al-Shaʿbī narrating on the authority of Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: ""Indeed Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to the Prophet ﷺ and gave him the choice between this world and the Hereafter, and he chose the Hereafter and did not desire this world."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13327","(13321) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us, on the authority of Ibn Ṭāwūs, on the authority of his father, who said: ""An angel was sent to the Prophet ﷺ whom he did not recognize, and he said: 'Indeed your Lord, the Most High, has given you the choice between being a Prophet-slave or a Prophet-king.' Jibrīl, peace be upon him, gestured to him to be humble, and he said: 'A Prophet-slave.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13328","(13322) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Saʿīd informed us, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, Ḥumayd al-Aʿraj informed me, on the authority of Mujāhid, that he said: ""The Prophet ﷺ used to pronounce the talbiyah: 'Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-ḥamda wa al-niʿmata laka wa al-mulka lā sharīka lak.' Then one day, when the people were dispersing from him, it pleased him what he was in, so he added to it: 'Labbayka inna al-ʿaysha ʿaysh al-ākhirah.'"" Ibn Jurayj said: ""And I think that was the day of ʿArafah."" This is a mursal narration. It has also been narrated in a connected, abbreviated form from ʿIkrimah on the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās. This is a word that was uttered by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the most blessed state during the Ḥajj at ʿArafah, and in his most difficult state on the day of the Khandaq. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13329","(13323) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Jawharī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah informed us, Aḥmad ibn al-Miqdām al-ʿIjlī narrated to us, al-Fuḍayl, i.e., ibn Sulaymān, narrated to us, Abū Ḥāzim narrated to us, Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us and said: ""We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the Khandaq; he was digging and we were carrying the soil. He looked at us and said: 'O Allah, there is no life except the life of the Hereafter, so forgive the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of Aḥmad ibn al-Miqdām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3797. Muslim 1804]"
"13330","(13324) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me, on the authority of Ibn Shihāb, on the authority of Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, on the authority of his father, on the authority of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""While I was asleep, I was brought a vessel containing milk, and I drank from it until I could see the moisture running through my fingernails. Then I gave my remaining share to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb."" They said: ""How did you interpret it, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Knowledge."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of Ḥarmalah, on the authority of Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 82. Muslim 2391]"
"13331","(13325) Abū Isḥāq, the Imām Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Isfarāyīnī, informed us: Abū Bakr al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Abū Qilābah narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥaḍramī and Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir both said: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from ʿAlī ibn al-Aqmar, from Abū Juḥayfah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""As for me, I do not eat while reclining.""

[Bukhārī 5398]"
"13332","(13326) Abū al-Qāsim Ghaylān ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz informed us in Baghdād: Abū Muḥammad Daʿlaj ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Muʿādh ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated to us, from Manṣūr and Ruqbah, from ʿAlī ibn al-Aqmar, with the same chain identically. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Jarīr, from Manṣūr, with the same meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13333","(13327) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ informed us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated to us, from al-Zubaydī, from al-Zuhrī, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās, who said: Ibn ʿAbbās used to relate that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent an angel from among the angels to His Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), with Jibrīl (peace be upon him) accompanying him. The angel said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): ""Indeed, Allah gives you the choice between being a servant-prophet and being a king-prophet."" The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) turned to Jibrīl (peace be upon him) as one seeking his counsel. Jibrīl gestured to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to choose humility. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Rather, I shall be a servant-prophet."" He said: ""After that word, he never ate any food while reclining until he met his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13334","(13328) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Marwazī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ḥātim narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū Tumaylah narrated to us: Khālid ibn ʿUbayd narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from his mother, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Jibrīl continued to counsel me regarding the siwāk (tooth-stick) until I feared for my teeth."" Others also narrated this from Abū Tumaylah: Yaḥyā ibn Wāḍiḥ. Al-Bukhārī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This is a ḥasan ḥadīth.

[Mawḍūʿ]

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: We have narrated in the book of Purification from ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī ʿĀmir that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was commanded to perform wuḍūʾ for every prayer, whether in a state of purity or not. When that became difficult for him, he was commanded to use the siwāk for every prayer."
"13335","(13329) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me by way of ijāzah: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Yūsuf ibn Khālid narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Sālim narrated to us, from ʿAmr, the freed slave of al-Muṭṭalib, from al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbdullāh, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""I have adhered to the siwāk so much that I feared it would strip my gums."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13336","(13330) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihāb: ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ informed me that Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever eats garlic or onion, let him keep away from us, or let him keep away from our mosque, and let him sit in his house."" He said: A vessel containing green vegetables was brought to him, and he noticed a foul odor from it. He asked and was informed of what it contained among the vegetables. He said: ""Bring it near,"" to some of his Companions who were with him. When he saw that he (the Prophet) disliked eating it, he (the Companion) said: ""Eat, for I converse with one whom you do not converse with."" Aḥmad said: ""With a vessel"" — Ibn Wahb explained it as a ""platter."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ, and Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir and others, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 854, Muslim 564]"
"13337","(13331) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us: Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, and said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿAṭāʾ informed me: Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah informed me that Yaʿlā used to say to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Would that I could see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) when revelation would come upon him.""

When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was at al-Jiʿrānah, wearing a garment that had been draped over him, with some of his Companions present, a man came to him anointed with perfume. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you think of a man who enters iḥrām in a jubbah (long garment) after having applied perfume?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) looked at him for a while, then revelation came to him. ʿUmar gestured to Yaʿlā (may Allah be pleased with them both) with his hand to come. So Yaʿlā came and inserted his head, and behold, his face was flushed red, panting thus for a while. Then the revelation lifted from him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Where is the one who asked me about ʿUmrah just now?"" The man was searched for and was brought to him. He said: ""As for the perfume that is on you, wash it three times. As for the jubbah, remove it. Then do in your ʿUmrah as you do in your Ḥajj.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj. In the ḥadīth of Hammām, from his father: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) while he was at al-Jiʿrānah, wearing a jubbah upon which was the trace of perfume, then he mentioned its meaning. In it, Hammām said: ""I think he said: 'Like the panting of a young camel.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym and others, and Muslim narrated it from Shaybān, all of them from Hammām. Al-Bukhārī said: Musaddad said: Yaḥyā narrated to us, and he mentioned it. And Muslim narrated it from various routes from Ibn Jurayj.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī, Muslim 1180]"
"13338","(13332) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Abū Khalīfah narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Firās informed us in Makkah: Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad al-Jumaḥī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭālaqānī narrated to us: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Muḥārib ibn Dithār, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, which of the lands is the best?"" He said: ""I do not know."" He said: ""Which of the lands is the worst?"" He said: ""I do not know."" He said: Then Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came to him and said: ""O Jibrīl, which of the lands is the best?"" He said: ""I do not know."" He said: ""Which of the lands is the worst?"" He said: ""I do not know. Ask your Lord."" He said: Jibrīl (peace be upon him) trembled a tremor that nearly caused Muḥammad (peace and blessings be upon him) to faint. He said: ""I will not ask Him about anything."" Allah, the Exalted and Most High, said to Jibrīl: ""Muhammad asked you which of the lands is the best and you said, 'I do not know,' and he asked you which of the lands is the worst and you said, 'I do not know.' So inform him that the best of the lands are the mosques, and the worst of the lands are the marketplaces."" There are many narrations on this theme."
"13339","(13333) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Zakariyyā ibn ʿAdī narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from al-Awzāʿī, from Ibn ʿAṭāʾ — Zakariyyā said: I think he meant ʿUmar — from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding the words of the Most High: {And whatever you give of ribā that it may increase among the people's wealth, it does not increase with Allah} — he said: It is the permissible form of ribā, meaning that one gives a gift intending to receive more in return. There is no reward in it and no sin. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) specifically prohibited it: {And do not confer favor to acquire more} [al-Muddaththir: 6].

And Abū ʿAbdullāh and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Salamah ibn Sābūr narrated to us, from ʿAṭiyyah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding {And do not confer favor to acquire more} — he said: Do not give a man (anything) so that he may give you more in return."
"13340","(13334) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Layth narrated to us, from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb: Muḥammad ibn al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr al-Anṣārī, who resided in Damascus, informed him that the angel came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""Read."" He said: ""I am not one who reads."" Then he returned to the same, then he sent me and said: ""Read."" I said: ""I am not one who reads."" Then he returned to the same, then he sent me and said: ""Read in the name of your Lord who created — created man from a clinging clot."" Muḥammad ibn al-Nuʿmān said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) returned with that.

Ibn Shihāb said: I heard ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr say: ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), said: He returned to Khadījah (may Allah be pleased with him), his heart trembling, and said: ""Cover me, cover me."" So he was covered. When the fear left him, he said to Khadījah: ""I have feared for myself, I have feared for myself."" Khadījah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Rejoice, for by Allah, Allah will never disgrace you. Indeed, you speak the truth and maintain the ties of kinship."" She said: ""Come with us."" So Khadījah (may Allah be pleased with her) went to Waraqah ibn Nawfal, who was a man who had become a Christian, an elderly blind man who could read the Gospel in Arabic. Khadījah (may Allah be pleased with her) said to him: ""O son of my uncle, listen to your nephew."" Waraqah said to him: ""What do you see?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) informed him of what he had seen. Waraqah said to him: ""This is the Nāmūs (the Trustworthy Spirit) whom Allah sent down to Moses (peace be upon him). Would that I were alive when your people drive you out."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Will they drive me out?"" He said: ""Yes. No man has ever come with the like of what you have come with but he was met with hostility. If your day reaches me, I will support you with a mighty support."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī, Muslim 160]"
"13341","(13335) And with this isnad, from Ibn Shahab, who said: I heard Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman say: Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: ""Then the revelation ceased from me. While I was walking, I heard a voice from the heaven. I raised my gaze toward the heaven, and lo, the angel who used to come to me was seated upon a chair between the heaven and the earth. I was seized by terror on account of him until I fell to the earth. I came to my family and said to them: 'Cover me!' So they covered me. Then Allah, the Most High, revealed: 'O you who are wrapped in garments, arise and warn, and your Lord magnify, and your garments purify, and uncleanliness abandon.' (Al-Muddaththir: 1–5)."" Abu Salamah said: ""Al-Rujz means the idols."" Then he said: ""After that, the revelation came again and continued in succession."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another route from al-Layth, through both isnads together, without the words of Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man."
"13342","(13336) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-Alawi narrated to us. Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Sharqi informed us. Abdullah ibn Hashim narrated to us. Waki' narrated to us. Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""If you knew what I knew, you would laugh little and weep much."""
"13343","(13337) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us. Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Duhaym al-Shaybani narrated to us in Kufah. Ahmad ibn Hazim al-Ghifari narrated to us. Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us. Isra'il narrated to us from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir from Mujahid from Muwarriq al-Ijli from Abu Dharr, may Allah the Most High be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, recited: ""Has there come upon man a period of time when he was not a thing mentioned?"" (Al-Insan: 1) until he completed it. Then he said: ""Indeed, I see what you do not see, and I hear what you do not hear. The heaven groans, and it is its right to groan. There is not in it the space of a finger's width except that there is an angel placing his forehead in prostration to Allah. By Allah, if you knew what I knew, you would laugh little and weep much, and you would not take pleasure with women on beds, but you would go out to the open roads, crying out to Allah the Most High. By Allah, I wish I were a tree that is cut down."" He said: His statement, ""By Allah, I wish I were a tree that is cut down,"" is from the words of Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him. [Da'if]"
"13344","(13338) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us. Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us. Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us. Yahya ibn Yahya informed us. Abu Khaythamah narrated to us from Simak ibn Harb, who said: I said to Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him: ""Did you used to sit with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him?"" He said: ""Yes, frequently. He would not rise from his place of prayer in which he prayed until the sun rose. When it rose, they would converse and speak of matters of the Days of Ignorance, and they would laugh, and he would smile."" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Muslim 2322]"
"13345","(13339) Abu Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us. Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us. Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us. Abu Dawud narrated to us. Shariq and Qays narrated to us from Simak ibn Harb, who said: I said to Jabir ibn Samurah: ""Did you used to sit with the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""He was long in silence and seldom laughed. His Companions would sometimes recite poetry before him and speak of some of their affairs, and they would laugh, and he would sometimes smile."" [Da'if]"
"13346","(13340) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad. Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustuyah narrated to us. Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us. Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqr'i narrated to us. al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us from al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid that Sulayman ibn Kharijah informed him from Kharijah ibn Zayd that Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A group of people came to his father and said: ""Narrate to us about some of the character of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him."" He said: ""I was his neighbor. When revelation descended, he would send for me, and I would come to him and write down the revelation. When we mentioned the world, he would mention it with us. When we mentioned the Hereafter, he would mention it with us. When we mentioned food, he would mention it with us. Or: Is it of this that we narrate to you about him?"" [Da'if]"
"13347","(13342) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us. Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us. Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us. Yahya ibn Yahya informed us. Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us.

(And) Abu Abdullah informed me. Ahmad ibn Ya'qub al-Thaqafi narrated to us. Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us. Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us. Hammad narrated to us. Thabit narrated to us from Abu Burdah from al-Agharr al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, who had been a Companion. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Indeed, my heart becomes overcome, and I seek forgiveness from Allah a hundred times in a day."" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani. [Muslim 2702]"
"13348","(13342) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us. Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ubdus al-Anazi narrated to us. Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us. Al-Qa'nabi narrated to us from what he recited to Malik.

And Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us. Malik narrated to us from Hisham ibn Urwah from his father from A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, that al-Harith ibn Hisham, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: ""O Messenger of Allah, how does the revelation come to you?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Sometimes it comes to me like the ringing of a bell, and it is the most severe upon me. Then it departs from me, and I have understood what the angel said. And sometimes the angel appears to me in the form of a man and teaches me, and I retain what he says."" A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""I saw the revelation descend upon him on an intensely cold day, and when it departed, his forehead was dripping with sweat."" Al-Qa'nabi said: ""And he speaks to me."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it through various routes from Hisham. [Bukhari 2. Muslim 2333]"
"13349","(13343) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us. Ali ibn Hamshadh al-Adl narrated to us. al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah narrated to us. Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' informed us. Sa'id narrated to us from Qatadah from al-Hasan from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, who was an Aqabi, a Badri, and one of the chiefs of the Ansar, that when revelation descended upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, he would be distressed by it, and his face would become overcast. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah. [Sahih. Muslim 1690]"
"13350","(13344) Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan informed us. Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us. Isma'il al-Qadi narrated to us. Hajjaj ibn Minhal and Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us. Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. Ammar ibn Abi Ammar narrated to us from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""I was with my father in the presence of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and there was a man with the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who was speaking to him privately. He seemed to be turning away from my father. We left his presence, and he said to me: 'Did you not see that my nephew was turning away from me?' I said to him: 'O father, there was a man with him who was speaking to him privately.' He said: 'Was there anyone?' I said: 'Yes.' So we returned, and he said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I said to Abdullah such and such, and he said to me such and such. Was there anyone with you?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Did you see him, O Abdullah?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'That was Jibril, peace be upon him. He was the one who occupied me from attending to you.'"" [Sahih. Musnad Ahmad 2679]"
"13351","(13345) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be brought a deceased man upon whom there was a debt, and he would ask: ""Has he left anything to settle his debt?"" If it was reported that he had left sufficient means, he would perform the funeral prayer over him; otherwise, he would say to the Muslims: ""Pray over your companion."" Then when Allah opened the conquests upon him, he stood and said: ""I am more entitled to the believers than their own selves. So whoever among the Muslims dies leaving a debt, its repayment is upon me, and whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Layth.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī, Muslim 1419]"
"13352","(13346) Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us that Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Abū Salamah al-Hamdānī, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: The following was revealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ""O Prophet, say to your wives: If you should desire the worldly life and its adornment…"" [al-Aḥzāb 33:28] to the end of the two verses. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave them the choice, and they chose Allah, His Messenger, and the Hereafter. So Allah thanked them for that and revealed upon him: ""Not lawful to you are [any additional] women after this, nor [is it] for you to exchange them for [other] wives, even if their beauty were to please you, except what your right hand possesses."" [al-Aḥzāb 33:52]

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13353","(13347) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us that Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us that Ḥājib ibn Abī Bakr informed us that Isḥāq ibn Sayyār informed us that ʿĀrim ibn al-Faḍl narrated from Abū Hilāl, from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: When he gave them the choice, they chose Allah and His Messenger, so He restricted that upon them. Then Allah, the Most High, revealed: ""Not lawful to you are [any additional] women after this…"" [al-Aḥzāb 33:52]

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13354","(13348) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Sulamī informed us by dictation that Abū al-Walīd Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh al-Qurashī informed us that Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not die until marriage to women was made permissible for him.

al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As though she meant those who were forbidden to him in His statement: ""Not lawful to you are [any additional] women after this, nor [is it] for you to exchange them for [other] wives…"" [al-Aḥzāb 33:52]

And I reckon that ʿĀʾishah's statement, may Allah be pleased with her, ""marriage to women was made permissible for him,"" refers to the statement of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""O Prophet, indeed We have made lawful to you your wives…"" [al-Aḥzāb 33:50] to His statement: ""an exclusive gift for you, not for the [rest of] the believers."" [al-Aḥzāb 33:50]

[Ḥasan]"
"13355","(13349) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿIṣmah ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muʿaddal informed me that al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah informed us that Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us that Wuhayb narrated to me that Ibn Jurayj informed me regarding the statement of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""Not lawful to you are [any additional] women after this, nor [is it] for you to exchange them for [other] wives…"" [al-Aḥzāb 33:52] — Ibn Jurayj said: ʿAṭāʾ narrated to me from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not die until Allah made it permissible for him to marry. And what was made permissible for him were only those who had migrated with him, and that is clear in the verse.

[Ḥasan]"
"13356","(13350) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us that Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us that Isrāʾīl narrated from al-Suddī, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Umm Hānīʾ, who said: The Prophet ﷺ proposed to me, and I excused myself to him, and he accepted my excuse. Then Allah, the Blessed and Most High, revealed: ""O Prophet, indeed We have made lawful to you your wives…"" [al-Aḥzāb 33:50] to His statement: ""those who migrated with you."" [al-Aḥzāb 33:50] She said: So I was not lawful for him, for I had not migrated with him; I was among the divorced women."
"13357","(13351) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿUrubah al-Sulamī informed us that Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār —

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr — they all said: Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us; my father narrated to me from Qatādah; Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go around to his wives during the night and the day in a single hour, and they were eleven [in number]. I said to Anas: Did he have the strength for that? He said: We used to discuss that he had been given the strength of thirty. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr, and the ḥadīth of Ibn Bashshār carries the same meaning. And in the narration of Ibn al-Muthannā, it says ""the strength of forty."" And he narrated from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: ""in a single hour during the night and the day.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār, and he said: ""the strength of thirty."" al-Bukhārī said: And Saʿīd said, from Qatādah, that Anas narrated to them: nine wives.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 368, Muslim 309]"
"13358","(13352) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us that Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr and Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī both informed us that ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād informed us that Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us that Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah that Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to them that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ used to go around to his wives in a single night, and he had nine wives at that time.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in another place in his book from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13359","(13353) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz informed us that Manṣūr ibn Abī Muzāḥim narrated to us —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb al-Bisṭāmī informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us that Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī informed us that Manṣūr ibn Abī Muzāḥim informed us that Abū Saʿīd al-Muʾaddib narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The woman who offered herself in marriage to the Prophet ﷺ was Khawlah bint Ḥakīm.

al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him, pointed to this narration and narrated it from the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl, from Hishām, from his father, who said: Khawlah, may Allah be pleased with her, was among those who offered themselves in marriage to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he mentioned this wording from ʿUrwah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13360","(13354) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us that Abū Usāmah narrated to us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I used to feel jealous of those women who offered themselves in marriage to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I would say: ""Does a woman offer herself in marriage?"" Then when Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: ""You may put aside whom you wish of them and take to yourself whom you wish"" [al-Aḥzāb 33:51], I said: ""By Allah, I do not see your Lord except that He hastens to fulfill your desire.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zakariyyā, and Muslim narrated it from Abī Kurayb, both from Abū Usāmah.

[al-Bukhārī 4788, Muslim 1467]"
"13361","(13355) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, saying: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah from al-Shaʿbī, who said: Women offered themselves in marriage to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He consummated the marriage with some of them and deferred [the consummation with] others, and did not approach them until he passed away. After him, none of them [who had been deferred] remarried, among them being Umm Sharīk. That is the meaning of His Most High's statement: *""You may defer any of them you wish, and you may take to yourself any you wish; and if you desire any of those you have set aside, there is no blame upon you.""* Thus said al-Shaʿbī. [Weak]"
"13362","(13356) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad narrated to us, saying: Yūnus narrated to us from ʿAnbasah ibn al-Azhar from Simāk ibn Ḥarb from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: There was no woman with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who had offered herself in marriage to him. Based on this [narration], if its chain of transmission is authentic, it is as though he ﷺ deferred them and did not accept them, even though they were lawful for him. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"13363","(13357) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us, saying: Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā from Ibn Qusaīṭ, who said: A man was given glad tidings of [receiving] a young slave girl. A man said: ""Give her to me as a gift."" He said: ""She is yours."" Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib was asked about it, and he said: ""Gifting [a woman in marriage] is not permissible after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Had he given her [a dowry of] even a whip, it would have been permissible for him."" [Weak]"
"13364","(13358) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us, saying: Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib, and Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Baṭḥā narrated to us, saying: ʿAffān narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: Thābit narrated to us from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A slave girl fell into the share of Ḥuṣayd [i.e., Dihyah]. It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a beautiful slave girl has fallen into the share of Dihyah."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ purchased her for seven head [of livestock], then gave her to Umm Sulaym to prepare and ready her. He said: ""And I think he said: 'She shall observe her waiting period in her house,' and she was Ṣafiyyah ibn Ḥuyayy."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made her wedding feast of dates, dried yogurt, and fat. The ground was leveled [by sweeping], and leather mats were brought and spread out. Then the dried yogurt and fat were brought, and the people ate their fill. The people said: ""We do not know whether he married her or took her as a concubine."" They said: ""If he veils her, then she is his wife; and if he does not veil her, then she is a concubine."" When he ﷺ intended to depart, he veiled her, so that she sat upon the rear of the camel, and they knew that he had married her. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from ʿAffān. [Authentic. Bukhārī, Muslim 1365]"
"13365","(13359) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Ibn al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: Sharīk narrated to us from Abī Hārūn from Abī Saʿīd, who said: ""There is no marriage except with a guardian, witnesses, and a dowry, except that which was [permitted] for the Prophet ﷺ."" [Very Weak]"
"13366","(13360) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, saying: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, saying: Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us, saying: Thābit narrated to us from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the waiting period of Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, had ended, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Zayd: ""Go to her and mention her to me [in marriage]."" Zayd said: ""So I set out, and when I saw her, I found her covering her face with her dough. I could not bring myself to look at her due to the greatness of her status in my heart when I realized that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had mentioned her. I said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has mentioned you.' She said: 'I shall not do anything until I consult my Lord.' So she rose to her place of prayer, and the Qurʾān was revealed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and entered upon her without permission.""

Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I saw us feeding the people bread and meat on her account until the day had lengthened. The people departed, but a group of men remained conversing in the house after the meal."" Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out, and I followed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began going around to the chambers of his wives, greeting them, and they would say: 'O Messenger of Allah, how did you find your family?' I do not know whether I told him that the people had departed, or whether I was informed. So he went until he came to the house and entered. I went to enter with him, but he cast a curtain between me and him, and the [command of] Ḥijāb was revealed. The people were admonished with what they were admonished, [i.e., the verses]: *'O you who believe, do not enter the houses of the Prophet unless permission is given to you…'* until His statement: *'Indeed, that was [a matter] grave in the sight of Allah.'*"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through two other routes from Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah. [Authentic. Bukhārī, Muslim 1428]"
"13367","(13361) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to us, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Saʿd al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us.

(And) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Thābit from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining about Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept saying: ""Fear Allah and keep your wife."" Anas said: ""Had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ been one to conceal anything, he would have concealed this."" She used to boast over the other wives of the Prophet ﷺ, saying: ""Your families married you off, but Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, married me from above the seven heavens."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr. [Authentic]"
"13368","(13362) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, saying: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, saying: ʿĪsā ibn Ṭahmān narrated to us. He said: I heard Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Zaynab ibn Jaḥsh, may Allah be pleased with her, used to boast over the other wives of the Prophet ﷺ, saying: ""Allah married me from the heavens."" And concerning her the verse of Ḥijāb was revealed. He said: ""The people were sitting in the house of the Prophet ﷺ. Then he came out, then returned, then came out while the people were as they were. That was seen in his face, and the verse of Ḥijāb was revealed: *'O you who believe, do not enter the houses of the Prophet unless permission is given to you…'*"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Khallād ibn Yaḥyā from ʿĪsā ibn Ṭahmān. [Authentic]"
"13369","(13363) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: ʿĀrim narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Abī Ḥāzim from Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and offered herself in marriage to him. He said: ""I have no need of women."" A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, marry her to me."" He said: ""What do you have?"" He said: ""I have nothing."" He said: ""What do you have of the Qurʾān?"" He said: ""Such and such [surahs]."" He said: ""I have given her in marriage to you with what you have of the Qurʾān."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿĀrim, and he narrated it from ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn from Ḥammād, who said: ""I have married her to you with what you have of the Qurʾān."" And likewise said Musaddad and others from Ḥammād. Muslim narrated it from Khalaf ibn Hishām from Ḥammād. [Authentic. Bukhārī 5141, Muslim 1425]"
"13370","(13364) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Marwazī narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī narrated to us, saying: Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us from Hilāl ibn ʿAlī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmrah from Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""There is no believer except that I have the greatest right over him in this world and the Hereafter. Recite, if you wish: *'The Prophet has a greater claim over the believers than they have over themselves.'* So whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs; and whoever leaves a burden or dependents, then I am his guardian."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad from Abī ʿĀmir. [Authentic. Bukhārī 6745, Muslim 1619]"
"13371","(13365) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sahl narrated to us: Ibn Abi 'Umar narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated from Abu al-Sha'tha' from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with both of them, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contracted a marriage while he was in the state of ihram. [Sahih. Bukhari 1837. Muslim 1410]

'Amr said: I narrated the hadith of Abu al-Sha'tha' to Ibn Shihab, and he said: Yazid ibn al-Asamm narrated to me that the Prophet ﷺ contracted a marriage while he was not in the state of ihram. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Ghassan from Sufyan, without the hadith of Ibn Shihab. And Muslim narrated it from Ibn Numayr from Sufyan, and he mentioned the hadith, i.e., the hadith of Ibn Shihab. And Yazid ibn al-Asamm narrated it from Maymunah bint al-Harith that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her while he was in the state of halal (not in ihram). Thus, the narrations differ regarding his ﷺ marriage while in the state of ihram. If it is confirmed that he contracted a marriage while in ihram, and he ﷺ said: ""The one in ihram shall neither contract a marriage nor have a marriage contracted on his behalf,"" then in that case, specification (takhsis) is conceivable."
"13372","(13366) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Husayn ibn Mansur narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from Thabit and Shu'ayb, meaning ibn al-Habbab, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ manumitted Safiyyah and made her manumission her dowry (sadaq). Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in the Sahih from Qutaybah. [Sahih. Bukhari, Muslim 1365]"
"13373","(13367) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us: Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah narrated from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ manumitted Safiyyah and married her. I asked Thabit: ""What was her dowry?"" He said: ""Her own self (i.e., her freedom)."" [Sahih]"
"13374","(13368) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Qurrah narrated: I heard Yazid ibn 'Abdullah, meaning ibn al-Shikh-khir, say: We were at al-Mirbad when a man with dishevelled hair came to us, holding a piece of red leather in his hand. We said: ""It is as though you are from the people of the desert."" He said: ""Yes."" We said: ""Give us this piece of leather."" He handed it to us, and we read what was in it. In it was: ""From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Banu Zuhayr ibn Uqaysh: If you bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and you establish the prayer, and you give the zakah, and you pay the khums from the war booty, and the share of the Prophet ﷺ and the share of al-Safiyy, then you are secure under the protection of Allah and His Messenger."" We said: ""Who wrote this for you?"" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" [Sahih. Ahmad 5/77]"
"13375","(13369) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Shayban narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar from al-Zuhri from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, who said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sent for me, so I was called, and I entered upon him while he was sitting on sand. He said: ""O Malik, delegations from your people have come to us. Take this wealth and distribute it among them."" I said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, appoint someone else for this."" He said: ""Take it, O man."" So I sat down, and Yarfa' came and said: ""Do you have any need for 'Abd al-Rahman, Talhah, al-Zubayr, and Sa'd?"" He said: ""Tell them to enter."" So they entered. He said: ""Do you have any need for 'Ali and al-'Abbas?"" He said: ""Tell them both to enter."" So they both entered, and each of them was speaking to his companion. When they sat down, they said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, judge between them and relieve them."" He said: ""I adjure you both by Allah, by whose command the heavens and the earth stand: Did you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'We do not leave inheritance; whatever we leave is charity (sadaqah)'?"" He said: ""Yes."" Then he said the same to the others, and the people said: ""Yes."" He also said: ""The wealth of Banu al-Nadir was among that which Allah bestowed upon His Messenger, over which the Muslims did not mobilize horses or camels. It belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, from which he spent on his family for a year's provision, and whatever remained he allocated to weapons and mounts in preparation for the cause of Allah. Then it remained exclusively for the Prophet ﷺ."" He narrated it from the hadith of Sufyan in abridged form. [Muslim 1757]"
"13376","(13370) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Nasr ibn 'Ali narrated to us: Safwan ibn 'Isa narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd from al-Zuhri from Malik ibn Aws, who said: Among the arguments that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used was that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had three properties (safaya): Banu al-Nadir, Khaybar, and Fadak. As for Banu al-Nadir, it was reserved for his emergencies. As for Fadak, it was reserved for the wayfarer (ibn al-sabil). As for Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided it into three portions: two portions for the Muslims, and one portion for the provision of his family. Whatever exceeded the provision of his family, he distributed among the poor of the Muslims.

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: As for the khums, the verse speaks of it, along with what we have narrated in the book on the division of the fay'. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Weak. Narrated by al-Sijistani 2967]"
"13377","(13371) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated from Ibn Shihab from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with both of them, that al-Sa'bi ibn Jaththamah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no reserved pasture (himya) except for Allah and His Messenger."" He said: ""And it has reached us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ designated al-Naqi' as a reserved pasture, and that 'Umar designated al-Sharaf and al-Rabadhah as reserved pastures."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Bukayr. [Bukhari 2379. Muslim 1745]"
"13378","(13372) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us: Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Ammar al-Duhni narrated to us.

(Additionally,) Abu Bakr ibn Abi Darim al-Hafiz in Kufa informed me: Musa ibn Harun narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Ammar al-Duhni narrated from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered on the day of the Conquest of Makkah wearing a black turban, without being in the state of ihram. This is the wording of the hadith of Qutaybah. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and Qutaybah. [Sahih. Muslim 1358]"
"13379","(13373) Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Faqih al-Shayrazi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi both said: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Nasr and Ja'far ibn Muhammad both said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us. He said: I said to Malik: Did Ibn Shihab narrate to you from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Makkah on the year of the Conquest and on his head was a helmet (mighfar)? When he removed it, a man came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Ibn Khatal is clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Kill him."" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it through other chains from Malik. [Sahih. Bukhari 1357]"
"13380","(13374) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Milhan narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi from Abu Shurayh al-'Adawi, who said: He said to 'Amr ibn Sa'id, while he was dispatching troops to Makkah: ""Permit me, O Commander, to relate a statement that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered on the day of the Conquest. My ears heard it, my heart retained it, and my eyes witnessed it when he spoke. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'Indeed, Makkah was sanctified by Allah, and it was not sanctified by the people. It is not permissible for anyone who believes in Allah and the Last Day to shed blood therein or to cut down a tree therein. If anyone seeks an exemption on account of the fighting of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ therein, then say to him: Indeed, Allah permitted His Messenger, and He did not permit you. He only permitted me for an hour of a day, and its sanctity has returned today as it was yesterday. Let the one who is present convey to the one who is absent.'"" It was said to Abu Shurayh: ""What did 'Amr say to you?"" He said: ""I know that better than you, O Abu Shurayh. Indeed, the sanctuary does not grant refuge to a sinner, nor to one fleeing from bloodshed, nor to one fleeing on account of theft."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Qutaybah and others, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from al-Layth. And Allah knows best. [Sahih. Bukhari 104. Muslim 1354]"
"13381","(13375) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: al-Ḥārith ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated from ʿUthmān al-Shaḥḥām from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbāṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said:

A slave woman (umm walad) of a man, during the time of the Prophet (ﷺ), used to frequently revile the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and insult him. He would forbid her, but she would not desist, and he would rebuke her, but she would not heed.

One night, she mentioned the Prophet (ﷺ) and reviled him. He said: ""I could not bear it. I got up, took a pickaxe, placed it on her belly, and then leaned upon it until I killed her."" He said: ""Her child fell between her legs, smeared with blood. In the morning, I mentioned that to the Prophet (ﷺ).""

He said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) gathered the people and then said: 'I adjure by Allah a man who saw a right due to the Prophet (ﷺ) and did what he did — let him stand.'"" He said: ""The blind man — meaning the killer — came forward, trembling. He mentioned a word"" — Abū al-Ḥusayn said: ""It escaped me"" — ""and said: 'She was indeed gentle and kind to me, but she used to frequently revile you and insult you. I would forbid her, but she would not desist, and I would rebuke her, but she would not heed. Last night she mentioned you and reviled you. I could not bear it, so I got up, took the pickaxe, and placed it on her belly.' The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Bear witness that her blood is forfeit (hadar).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 4361]"
"13382","(13376) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah and ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated from Jarīr from Mughīrah from al-Shaʿbī from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) that:

A Jewish woman used to revile the Prophet (ﷺ) and insult him. A man strangled her until she died. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) declared her blood forfeit. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13383","(13377) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Tawbah al-ʿAnbarī from Abī al-Sawwār from Abī Barzah that:

A man reviled Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). I said: ""Shall I not strike his neck, O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?"" He said: ""No. This is not for anyone after the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13384","(13378) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ al-ʿUkbari narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ismāʿīl al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated from al-Zuhrī from Abī Salamah from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""No one is to be killed for reviling another, except for reviling the Prophet (ﷺ).""

Abū Aḥmad (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""This ḥadīth is known through Yaḥyā ibn Ismāʿīl."" [Ḥasan]"
"13385","(13379) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Ḥarmalah informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yūnus informed me from Ibn Shihāb: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab narrated to me from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:

""O Allah, any believing servant whom I have reviled, make that for him a means of nearness to You on the Day of Resurrection.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ from Ibn Wahb, and Muslim narrated it from Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā. [Bukhārī 6361, Muslim 2601]"
"13386","(13380) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī narrated to us: Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: ""This is what Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us."" And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""O Allah, I have taken a covenant with You that You will not break. I am only a human being. Any believer whom I have harmed, or reviled, or flogged, or cursed, make that for him a ṣalāh (blessing), a zakāh (purification), and a means of nearness by which You draw him near on the Day of Resurrection.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Sulamī. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ in some of its versions from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Musayyab from Abī Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13387","(13381) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Abī Ṣāliḥ from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""O Allah, any believer whom I have reviled, or flogged, or cursed, make that for him a zakāh (purification) and a mercy.""

And from al-Aʿmash from Abī Sufyān from Jābir, a similar narration, with the addition: ""a zakāh (purification) and a reward (ajr).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Abī Kurayb from Abī Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13388","(13382) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj al-Aʿwar narrated. Ibn Jurayj said: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh say: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say:

""I am only a human being, and I have stipulated with my Lord that any servant among the Muslims whom I strike or revile, that should be for him a zakāh (purification) and a reward (ajr).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh and others from Ḥajjāj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2602]"
"13389","(13383) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Abī Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Muslim from Masrūq from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

Two men entered upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he spoke harshly to them. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, whoever has received good from you, these two have received no good from you."" He said: ""Do you not know what covenant I have made with my Lord?"" I said: ""What covenant have you made with your Lord?"" He said: ""I said: 'O Allah, any believer whom I revile or curse, make that for him forgiveness and well-being, and such and such.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kurayb from Abī Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2600]"
"13390","(13384) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated. It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Mālik ibn Anas, Usāmah ibn Zayd al-Laythī, and others informed you that Nāfiʿ narrated to them from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that:

The Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited continuous fasting (wiṣāl). It was said to him: ""But you fast continuously."" He said: ""I am not like you. I am fed and given drink.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Mālik. Its meaning is confirmed from the ḥadīth of Abī Hurayrah, Anas ibn Mālik, and ʿĀʾishah bint al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with them all) from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Bukhārī 1922, Muslim 1102]"
"13391","(13385) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Husayn ibn Hasan ibn Muhajir narrated to us: Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me from 'Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id from Makhramah ibn Sulayman from Kurayb, the mawla of 'Abdillah ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them) that he said: I spent the night at Maymunah's, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and Allah be pleased with her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was with her that night. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) performed wudu, then stood to pray. I stood to his left, so he took me and placed me to his right. He prayed thirteen rak'ahs that night. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) slept until he breathed heavily (in sleep), for when he slept, he would breathe heavily. Then the mu'adhdhin came to him, so he went out and prayed without performing wudu. 'Amr said: I narrated this to Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, and he said: Kurayb narrated it to me thus. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Ahmad from Ibn Wahb, and Muslim narrated it from Harun ibn Sa'id. [Bukhari, Muslim 763]"
"13392","(13386) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: al-Qa'nabi narrated from Malik. (H) And Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated, he said: I recited to Malik from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, who said: I asked 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) during Ramadan? She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not exceed eleven rak'ahs in Ramadan or in any other month. He would pray four — do not ask about their beauty and length — then he would pray four — do not ask about their beauty and length — then he would pray three. 'A'ishah said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you sleep before you pray Witr? He said: O 'A'ishah, my two eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep. This is the wording of the hadith of al-Qa'nabi. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Bukhari 1147, Muslim 738]"
"13393","(13387) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: al-'Abbas Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us: 'Abdillah ibn Wahb ibn Muslim al-Qurashi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us: Sharik ibn 'Abdillah ibn Abi Namir narrated, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik narrating to us about the night in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was taken on the Night Journey (al-Isra') from the Mosque of the Ka'bah: Three men came to him before revelation was sent down to him, while he was sleeping in the Masjid al-Haram. The first of them said: Is he the one? The middle one said: He is the best of them. The last one said: Take the best of them. That was that night, and he did not see them again until they came to him another night, in accordance with what his heart saw. The Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him) eye sleeps but his heart does not sleep, and so it is with the Prophets (peace and blessings be upon them): their eyes sleep but their hearts do not sleep. And he mentioned the hadith in its entirety. Al-Bukhari narrated it from 'Abd al-'Aziz from Sulayman, and Muslim narrated it from Harun from Ibn Wahb. [Bukhari 3570, Muslim 3570]"
"13394","(13388) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Qudamah ibn A'yan narrated to us: Jarir narrated from Mansur from Hilal, that is, ibn Yasaf, from Abu Yahya from 'Abdillah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The prayer of a man seated is half of the prayer.

So I came to him and found him praying sitting, so I placed my hand upon my head (in surprise). He said: What is the matter with you, O 'Abdillah ibn 'Amr? I said: I was told, O Messenger of Allah, that you said: The prayer of a man seated is half of the prayer, and yet you are praying seated. He said: Yes, but I am not like any one of you. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb from Jarir, and Allah knows best. [Weak. Ahmad 6512]"
"13395","(13389) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us: Abu 'Abdillah al-Sufi narrated to us: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us: Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from Abu Musa from al-Hasan from Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: This son of mine is a master — meaning al-Hasan ibn 'Ali (peace be upon them both) — and perhaps Allah will bring about reconciliation through him between two groups of the Muslims. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from a group from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) had named his son when he was born, and he named his two brothers likewise when they were born. He said to 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): What have you named my son? [Bukhari 2704]"
"13396","(13390) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us: Ibn Raja' narrated to us: Isra'il narrated from Abi Ishaq. (H) And Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Shaybani in Kufah narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Ishaq al-Zuhri narrated to us: Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated to us: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated from his father from Hani' ibn Hani' from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When al-Hasan was born, I named him Harb. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me: What have you named my son? I said: Harb. He said: He is al-Hasan.

When al-Husayn was born, I named him Harb. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: What have you named my son? I said: Harb. He said: He is al-Husayn.

When Muhsin was born, he said: What have you named my son? I said: Harb. He said: He is Muhsin. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: I have named my sons with the naming of Harun's sons: Shabbar, Shabbir, and Mushabbir. This is the wording of the hadith of Yunus. In the narration of Isra'il it is: Show me my son; what have you named him? And the rest is similar in meaning. [Weak. Ahmad 1/98, hadith 769]"
"13397","(13391) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari, Abu 'Abdillah al-Husayn ibn 'Umar ibn Burhan al-Ghazzal, and Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan, and others, said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Thabit al-Jazari narrated to us: Bukayr ibn Mismar, the mawla of 'Amr ibn Sa'd, narrated, he said: I heard 'Amir ibn Sa'd say — and Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Revelation came down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), so he placed 'Ali, Fatimah, and her two sons under his garment and said: O Allah, these are my family and the people of my household. [Muslim 2404]"
"13398","(13392) And Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated from Bukayr ibn Mismar from 'Amir ibn Sa'd from his father, who said: When this verse was revealed — {We call our sons and your sons, and our women and your women} — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) called 'Ali, Fatimah, al-Hasan, and al-Husayn and said: O Allah, these are my family. Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated it to us: Ja'far al-Khuldi and Abu Bakr ibn Baluwayh said: Musa ibn Harun narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Hatim ibn Isma'il, and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Qutaybah. [Weak. Al-Daraqutni]"
"13399","(13393) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: al-Hasan ibn Ya'qub and Ibrahim ibn 'Ismah said: al-Sari ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us: Wuhib ibn Khalid narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father from 'Ali ibn al-Husayn. (H) And Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq, who informed me: Abu Ja'far from his father 'Ali ibn al-Husayn, who said: When 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) married Umm Kulthum bint 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with them), he came to a gathering in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) between the grave and the pulpit, which was for the Muhajirun, and no one else would sit in it. They supplicated for blessings for him. He said: By Allah, nothing prompted me to marry her except that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: Every tie of kinship and lineage will be severed on the Day of Resurrection except that which is from my lineage and my ties of kinship. This is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Ishaq, and it is a good mursal hadith. It has been narrated through other routes both as mawsul and mursal. [Weak]"
"13400","(13394) And Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us: Dalaj ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Musa ibn Harun narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Wak'i ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us: Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me: Ibn Abi Mulaikah informed me: al-Hasan ibn al-Hasan informed me from his father: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) proposed to 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) for Umm Kulthum. 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: She is too young for that. 'Umar said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: Every tie of kinship and lineage will be severed on the Day of Resurrection except my lineage and my ties of kinship. So I would like to have a lineage and tie of kinship with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said to al-Hasan and al-Husayn: Marry your uncle (in marriage). They said: She is a woman among women who chooses for herself. So 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up angry. Al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) held onto his garment and said: I cannot bear your estrangement, O father. So they married her to him. [Weak]"
"13401","(13395) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to us, Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far said: Umm Bakr bint al-Miswar ibn Makhramah narrated to us from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi' from al-Miswar (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: ""Fatimah is a piece of me; whatever grieves her grieves me, and whatever pleases her pleases me. Verily, all lineages shall be severed on the Day of Resurrection except my lineage, my ties of relationship, and my marital connections."" [Da'if]"
"13402","(13396) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us, Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ishaq informed us, Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Zuhri narrated to us from Umm Bakr bint al-Miswar ibn Makhramah from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Every lineage shall be severed except my lineage, my ties of relationship, and my marital connections."" Thus did a group narrate it from 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far without Ibn Abi Rafi' in its chain of narration. [Da'if]"
"13403","(13397) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us from Khubayb ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from Hafs ibn 'Asim from Abu Sa'id ibn al-Mu'alla al-Ansari: that the Prophet (ﷺ) called him while he was praying, so he completed his prayer and then came to him. He said: ""What prevented you from answering me when I called you?"" He said: ""I was praying."" He said: ""Has not Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: 'O you who believe, respond to Allah and to the Messenger when he calls you'?"" [al-Anfal: 24]. Then he said: ""Shall I not teach you the greatest Surah in the Qur'an?"" He said: ""It was as though he had forgotten it, or it was made to be forgotten."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what did you say to me?"" He said: ""Al-hamdu lillahi Rabb al-'alamin [All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the Worlds] — it is the seven oft-repeated verses and the great Qur'an that I have been given."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Shu'bah. [Bukhari 4474]"
"13404","(13398) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Milhan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us from 'Uqayl from Ibn Shihab from 'Urwah from 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), who informed him that Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), sent a message to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) asking him about her inheritance from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from what Allah had granted him in the way of fay' in Madinah, Fadak, and what remained of the khums of Khaybar. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'We do not leave inheritance; whatever we leave is charity. The family of Muhammad may eat from this wealth.' By Allah, I shall not change anything from the charity of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from the state it was in during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and I shall deal with it as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) dealt with it."" And he mentioned the hadith. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from another route from al-Layth. [Bukhari, Muslim 1757]"
"13405","(13399) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan all said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us, al-Shafi'i informed us, Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us.

(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me, Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi 'Umar al-Makki narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us from Abu al-Zinad from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""My heirs shall not divide any dinars. What I leave after the maintenance of my wives and the expenses of my workers is charity."" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Ibn Abi 'Umar, and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of Malik from Abu al-Zinad. [Bukhari 2776, Muslim 1760]"
"13406","(13400) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Ibn Abi Ghaniyyah narrated to us from Abu al-Khattab al-Hajari from Mahdudh al-Dhuhli from Jasrah from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) came out and turned toward this mosque and said: ""Beware! This mosque is not permissible for any person in a state of major ritual impurity or any menstruating woman, except for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), 'Ali, Fatimah, al-Hasan, and al-Husayn. Beware! I have made the names clear to you so that you may not go astray."" [Da'if]"
"13407","(13401) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us, Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Hammad say: Al-Bukhari (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Mahdudh al-Dhuhli narrated from Jasrah — Ibn Abi Ghaniyyah narrated it from Abu al-Khattab — and there is a matter concerning it.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This has also been narrated from another route from Jasrah, and it contains weakness. [Sahih]"
"13408","(13402) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed it to us, Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Isma'il al-Sarraj narrated to us, Mutayyin narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hamzah al-Tammar narrated to us, I heard 'Ata' ibn Muslim mention from Isma'il ibn Umayyah from Jasrah from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Beware! My mosque is forbidden to every menstruating woman and every man in a state of major ritual impurity, except for Muhammad and his family — 'Ali, Fatimah, al-Hasan, and al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them)."" Abu Bakr al-Farisi informed us, Abu Ishaq al-Isbahani informed us, Abu Ahmad ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Al-Bukhari said: He mentioned the narration of Mahdudh from Jasrah, then al-Bukhari said: And Aflat narrated from Jasrah from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) from the Prophet (ﷺ), and this is not authentic from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Da'if]"
"13409","(13403) Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated from Salim ibn Abi Hafsah from 'Atiyyah from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): ""O 'Ali, it is not permissible for anyone in a state of major ritual impurity to be in this mosque other than me and you."" Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed me that Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Ziyad informed them, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, Salim ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, and he mentioned it. That was also narrated from another route from 'Atiyyah. 'Atiyyah is Ibn Sa'd al-'Awfi, and he is not used as a proof. Allah, the Exalted, knows best. [Sahih]"
"13410","(13404) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Abu Usamah 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Usamah al-Halabi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Abi Mani' al-Rusafi narrated to us, my grandfather narrated to us from al-Zuhri, who said: 'Umarah ibn Khuzaymah narrated to me that his uncle — who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) purchased a horse from a Bedouin man. He asked him to accompany him so that he might pay him the price of his horse. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) walked quickly, while the Bedouin fell behind. Some men began to approach the Bedouin and bargain with him for the horse, not knowing that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had already purchased it, until some of them outbid the Bedouin over the price at which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had bought it. When the price was raised, the Bedouin called out to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying: ""If you wish to buy this horse, then buy it, or else I will sell it."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up when he heard the Bedouin's call and came to him. He said: ""Have I not already bought it from you?"" The Bedouin said: ""No, by Allah, I have not sold it to you."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, I have bought it from you."" The people began to gather around the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and the Bedouin as they argued back and forth. The Bedouin kept saying: ""Bring me a witness who can testify that I sold it to you."" Whenever any Muslim came, he would say to the Bedouin: ""Woe to you! The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) does not speak except the truth."" Then Khuzaymah came and listened to what the Bedouin was saying in his argument with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was saying in response. The Bedouin kept saying: ""Bring me witnesses who can testify that I sold it to you."" Khuzaymah said: ""I testify that you sold it to him."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned to Khuzaymah and said: ""On what basis do you testify?"" He said: ""By your truthfulness, O Messenger of Allah."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made the testimony of Khuzaymah equal to the testimony of two men. And Allah knows best. [Sahih. Ahmad 2228]"
"13411","(13405) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Tammar in Hamadan narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us — Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi' informed us — Shu'ayb narrated from al-Zuhri, who said: And 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me that 'A'ishah, Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), said: Hind bint 'Utban ibn Rabi'ah came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, there was no household upon the face of the earth whose humiliation I desired more than the humiliation of your household. But today, there is no household upon the face of the earth whose honouring I desire more than the honouring of your household."" Then she said: ""Indeed, Abu Sufyan is a miserly man. Is there any sin upon me to feed our family from what he possesses?"" He said to her: ""There is no sin upon you to feed them in a reasonable manner (bil-ma'ruf)."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Yaman, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from al-Zuhari. [Bukhari 1761, Muslim 1714]"
"13412","(13406) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed us — Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hamid al-'Attar informed us — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us — Hajjaj narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hakimah bint Umaymah informed me from her mother Umaymah, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) would urinate in a vessel of wood, which was then placed beneath his bed. He urinated and it was placed beneath his bed. Then he came and wanted it, and lo, the vessel contained nothing. He said to a woman called Barakah, who used to serve him — she had come with Umm Habibah from the land of Abyssinia: ""Where is the urine that was in this vessel?"" She said: ""I drank it, O Messenger of Allah."" [Da'if]"
"13413","(13407) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ghalib narrated to us — Musa ibn Isma'il Abu Salamah narrated to us — Hunayd ibn al-Qasim narrated to me — I heard 'Amir ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was cupped and he gave me his blood and said: ""Go and conceal it, so that no beast, dog, or human may discover it."" He said: ""I went aside and drank it. Then I came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and he said: 'What have you done?' I said: 'I did as you commanded me.' He said: 'It seems to me that you have drunk it.' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'What will my Ummah find from you?'"" Abu Ja'far said — and one of the companions of the hadith added to me from Abu Salamah — he said: ""They used to believe that the strength which was in Ibn al-Zubayr came from the strength of the blood of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."" The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And this was also narrated from another chain from Asma' bint Abi Bakr and from Salman regarding Ibn al-Zubayr's (may Allah be pleased with him) drinking of his blood, and it was narrated from Safinah that he drank it. [Da'if]"
"13414","(13408) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us — Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi narrated to us — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar and Ibrahim ibn Asbat both said: Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us — Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us — Burayh ibn 'Umar ibn Safinah narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was cupped and then said to me: ""Take this blood and conceal it from the animals and the birds"" — or he said: ""from the people and the animals."" Ibn Abi Fudayk was uncertain. He said: ""I went away with it and drank it."" He said: ""Then he asked me and I informed him that I had drunk it, and he laughed."" [Da'if Jiddan]"
"13415","(13409) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us — Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stoned the Jamrah and sacrificed his offering, he gave the right side of his head to the barber, who shaved it, and then gave it to Abu Talhah. Then he gave him the left side, which he shaved and ordered him to distribute it among the people. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Abi 'Umar from Sufyan. [Muslim 1305]"
"13416","(13410) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih narrated to us — Salih ibn Muhammad al-Hafiz narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us — 'Abbad ibn al-'Awwam narrated from Ibn 'Awn, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) shaved his hair on the Day of Sacrifice, the people dispersed and took his hair. Abu Talhah took a portion of it. Ibn Sirin said: ""If I had a single hair of his, that would be more beloved to me than the world and all that it contains."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Sa'iqah from Sa'id ibn Sulayman, without the statement of Ibn Sirin. And it is mentioned from Ayyub, Ibn 'Awn, and 'Asim al-Ahwal from Ibn Sirin from 'Ubaydah that he said this statement. [Sahih]"
"13417","(13411) Abu Sahl Muhammad ibn Nasrwayh ibn Ahmad al-Marwazi narrated to us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khanb narrated to us — Abu Ishaq Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us — Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) while the barber was shaving him, and his Companions had surrounded him, wanting no hair to fall except into a man's hand."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from another chain from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah. [Sahih]"
"13418","(13412) And that which I preserve from part of its meaning: Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Durust ibn Ziyad narrated from Aban ibn Tariq, from Nafi', who said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever is invited and does not respond has disobeyed Allah and His Messenger. And whoever enters without an invitation enters as a thief and leaves as a raider."" (Q) And this came regarding a man who enters upon another knowing that he eats so that he may eat with him. And a hadith was narrated negating the restriction which Abu al-'Abbas presumed regarding the food of the Prophet (peace be upon him) in the story of Abu al-Darda'. [Da'if]"
"13419","(13413) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi narrated to us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Sa'd al-Nasawi narrated to us — Muhammad ibn al-Haytham al-'Ukbarri narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us — al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came one day from the mountain pass, having relieved himself, and we had dates upon a leather shield or a small leather shield. We invited him to it, and he ate with us and did not touch water. Abu Dawud narrated it in the book of al-Sunan. [Sahih]"
"13420","(13414) And that was also narrated from 'Amr ibn al-Harith from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: They used to eat dates upon a leather shield. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) passed by us after having come from the privy, and we said: ""Come here!"" So he sat and ate dates with us and did not touch water. Abu Sa'id al-Sayrafi informed us — Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Bushanji narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Hafs narrated to us — Musa ibn A'yan narrated from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, and he mentioned it. [Sahih]"
"13421","(13415) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Sufyān, who said: Zubayd narrated to me from ʿUmārah ibn ʿUmayr from Qays ibn al-Sakan that al-Ashʿath ibn Qays entered upon ʿAbd Allāh on the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ while he was eating. He said: ""O Abā Muḥammad, come near and eat."" He replied: ""I am fasting."" He said: ""We used to fast it, and then it was abandoned."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Yaḥyā. And in this there are many narrations, and all of that negates specification. And Allāh knows best."
"13422","(13417) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Munādī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥbūr al-Dahhān narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiya al-Ḍarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""I entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and he was feverish. I touched him and said: 'O Messenger of Allāh, you are suffering from a severe fever.' He said: 'Yes, I am feverish as two of you are feverish.' I said: 'That is because you will have a double reward.' He said: 'Yes. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, no Muslim on earth is afflicted by any harm—be it illness or otherwise—except that Allāh removes his sins thereby, just as a tree sheds its leaves.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb and others, from Abī Muʿāwiya. And al-Bukhārī narrated it through other chains from al-Aʿmash. And Allāh, the Most High, knows best."
"13423","(13417) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us—and the wording is his: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us: al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Saʿīd ibn Ibrāhīm, who said: I heard ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr say: ʿĀ'ishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, said: ""We used to hear that a Prophet does not die until he is given the choice between this world and the Hereafter."" She said: ""The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was in the illness in which he passed away, and his voice had become hoarse. I heard him saying: {With those upon whom Allāh has bestowed favor—of the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous. And excellent are those as companions.} [al-Nisāʾ 4:69]"" She said: ""So I thought he had been given the choice between this world and the Hereafter."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains from Shuʿbah."
"13424","(13418) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-ʿAbbās al-Rāzī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayd narrated to us: Mihrān ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with him, who said: A man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ said: ""If the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ were to die, I would marry ʿĀ'ishah or Umm Salamah."" So Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And it is not for you to harm the Messenger of Allāh, nor to marry his wives after him, ever. Indeed, that would be, in the sight of Allāh, an enormity.} [al-Aḥzāb 33:53] Sulaymān said: No one narrated it from Sufyān except Mihrān. [Weak]"
"13425","(13419) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us: Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍarawī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr, from Bajālah or someone else, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, passed by a boy who was reciting from the muṣḥaf: {The Prophet is more worthy of the believers than themselves, and his wives are their mothers.} [al-Aḥzāb 33:6] —and he (the Prophet) is a father to them. He said: ""O boy, erase it."" He said: ""This is the muṣḥaf of Ubayy."" So he went to him and asked him. He said: ""That is because the Qur'an keeps me occupied, and the clamor of the marketplace keeps you occupied."""
"13426","(13420) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Bazzār narrated to us in Baghdād: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us: Yūnus narrated from Ṭalḥah, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he used to recite this verse: {The Prophet is more worthy of the believers than themselves} —and he is a father to them— {and his wives are their mothers.} [Very weak]"
"13427","(13421) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from Ṣilah, from Ḥudhayfah, may Allāh be pleased with him, that he said to his wife: ""If it pleases you to be my wife in Paradise, then do not marry after me. For a woman in Paradise will be with the last of her husbands in this world."" It is for this reason that it was forbidden for the wives of the Prophet ﷺ to marry after him, because they are his wives in Paradise. [Ḥasan]"
"13428","(13422) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ibn Abī Qumāsh narrated to us: Ibn ʿĀ'ishah narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Firās, from ʿĀmir, from Masrūq, from ʿĀ'ishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, that a woman said to her: ""O my mother!"" She said: ""I am the mother of your men; I am not your mother."""
"13429","(13423) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me: al-Ḥajjāj ibn Abī Manīʿ narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Usāmah al-Ḥalabī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Abī Manīʿ al-Ruṣāfī narrated to me: my grandfather ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Ziyād narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said:

The first woman the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married was Khadījah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy. He married her during the Jāhiliyyah, and her father Khuwaylid gave her in marriage to him. She bore the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ: al-Qāsim (after whom he was given his kunyah), al-Ṭāhir, Zaynab, Ruqayyah, Umm Kulthūm, and Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with them all.

As for Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, she was married to Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf during the Jāhiliyyah. She bore Abū al-ʿĀṣ a daughter named Umāmah. ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, married Umāmah after the death of Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, may Allāh be pleased with her. ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, passed away while Umāmah was still with him. After ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, al-Mughīrah ibn Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim married Umāmah, and she passed away while with him.

The mother of Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ was Hālah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad, and Khadījah, may Allāh be pleased with her, was her maternal aunt—the sister of her mother.

As for Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, she was married to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, during the Jāhiliyyah. She bore him ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān, after whom he was given his kunyah the first time. He was later also given the kunyah of ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān. He was given a kunyah by each name. Ruqayyah, may Allāh be pleased with her, passed away at the time of Badr. ʿUthmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, stayed behind for her burial, and that prevented him from witnessing Badr.

ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, had migrated to the land of Abyssinia, and Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, migrated with him. Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, passed away on the day Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, the freedman of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, arrived with the glad tidings of the victory of Badr.

As for Umm Kulthūm, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, she was also married to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, after her sister Ruqayyah, may Allāh be pleased with her. She passed away while with him and bore him nothing.

As for Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, she was married to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him. She bore him Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (the elder) and Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, who was killed in Iraq at al-Ṭaff, as well as Zaynab and Umm Kulthūm. These are the children Fāṭimah bore to ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both.

As for Zaynab, she was married to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, and she passed away while with him. She bore him ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar and another brother of his whose name was ʿAwn.

As for Umm Kulthūm, she was married to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him. She bore him Zayd ibn ʿUmar, who was struck during the fighting against Ibn Muṭīʿ with blows from which he never recovered until he passed away. After ʿUmar, ʿAwn ibn Jaʿfar married Umm Kulthūm, and she bore him nothing before he died. Then Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar married Umm Kulthūm after ʿAwn ibn Jaʿfar, and she bore him a girl named Buntah, who was carried on a bier from Makkah to Madīnah. When she arrived in Madīnah, she passed away.

Then ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar married Umm Kulthūm after ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, ʿAwn ibn Jaʿfar, and Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, and she bore him nothing before she passed away while with him.

Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, may Allāh be pleased with her, had been married to two men before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. The first of them was ʿAtīq ibn ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Makhzūm. She bore him a girl, who was the mother of Muḥammad ibn Ṣayfī al-Makhzūmī. Then Abū Hālah al-Tamīmī—who was from Banī Asīd ibn ʿAmr ibn Tamīm—married Khadījah bint Khuwaylid after ʿAtīq ibn ʿĀʾidh. She bore him Hind.

Khadījah passed away in Makkah before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ departed for Madīnah and before the Ṣalāh was made obligatory. She was the first of the women to believe in the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. They claimed—and Allāh knows best—that he was asked about her and he said: ""She has a house made of pearl reeds, in which there is no noise and no fatigue.""

Then the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married ʿĀ'ishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, after Khadījah. He had been shown in his sleep twice that she was his wife. ʿĀ'ishah at that time was six years old. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married her in Makkah when she was six years old. Then the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ consummated the marriage with ʿĀ'ishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, after arriving in Madīnah, and ʿĀ'ishah on the day he consummated the marriage was nine years old. ʿĀ'ishah was the daughter of Abū Bakr ibn Abī Quḥāfah ibn ʿĀmir ibn ʿAmr ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married her as a virgin. The name of Abū Bakr, may Allāh be pleased with him, was ʿAtīq, and the name of Abī Quḥāfah was ʿUthmān.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb ibn Nufayl ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Riyāḥ ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qurṭ ibn Razzāḥ ibn ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr. Before him, she had been married to Ibn Ḥudhāfah ibn Qays ibn ʿAdī ibn Ḥudhāfah ibn Sahm ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥuṣayṣ ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib. She was widowed by him.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Umm Salamah—her name was Hind bint Abī Umayyah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Makhzūm. Before him, she had been married to Abū Salamah—his name was ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Asad ibn Hilāl ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Makhzūm. She bore Abū Salamah: Salamah ibn Abī Salamah, who was born in the land of Abyssinia, and Zaynab bint Abī Salamah. Abū Salamah and Umm Salamah had both migrated to the land of Abyssinia. Umm Salamah was the last of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ to pass away after him. She also had a daughter named Durrah bint Abī Salamah.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Sawdah bint Zamʿah ibn Qays ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Wadd ibn Naṣr ibn Mālik ibn Ḥisl ibn ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr. Before him, she had been married to al-Sakrān ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Wadd ibn Naṣr ibn Mālik ibn Ḥisl ibn ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān ibn Ḥarb ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Quṣayy ibn Kilāb ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr. Before him, she had been married to ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh ibn Riʾāb from Banī Asad ibn Khuzaymah. He died in the land of Abyssinia as a Christian, and she had been with him in the land of Abyssinia. Umm Ḥabībah bore ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh a girl named Ḥabībah, after whom she was given the kunyah Umm Ḥabībah. Her name was Ramlah. ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, arranged the marriage of Umm Ḥabībah to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ because Umm Ḥabībah's mother was Ṣafiyyah bint Abī al-ʿĀṣ, and Ṣafiyyah was the paternal aunt of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him—the sister of ʿAffān by his father and mother. Shurayḥbil ibn Ḥasanah brought Umm Ḥabībah to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Zaynab bint Jaḥsh ibn Riʾāb from Banī Asad ibn Khuzaymah. Her mother's name was Asmāʾ bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim, the paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. Before him, she had been married to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah al-Kalbī, the freedman of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, whose name and affair and the affair of his wife Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, mentioned in the Qurʾān. She was the first of the wives of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to pass away after him. She was the first woman for whom a bier (naʿsh) was made. Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays al-Khathʿamiyyah, the mother of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, made it for her. She had been in the land of Abyssinia and saw them making biers, so she made one for Zaynab on the day she passed away.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Zaynab bint Khuzaymah, who was Umm al-Masākīn. She was from Banī ʿAbd Manāf ibn Mālik ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah. In the narration of Yaʿqūb: Ibn Hilāl ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah. Before him, she had been married to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh ibn Riʾāb, who was killed on the day of Uḥud. Zaynab bint Khuzaymah passed away while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was still alive; she did not remain with him except for a short time.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥazn ibn Buḥayr ibn al-Hizm ibn Ruwaybah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hilāl ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah. She was the one who offered herself to the Prophet ﷺ. She had been married to two men before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. The first of them was Ibn ʿAbd Yālīl ibn ʿAmr al-Thaqafī, who died during her lifetime. Then Abū Raḥm ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Abī Qays ibn ʿAbd Wadd ibn Naṣr ibn Mālik ibn Ḥisl ibn ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr married her.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ captured Juwayriyah bint al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿĀʾidh ibn Mālik ibn al-Muṣṭaliq from Khuzāʿah on the day of the battle against Banī al-Muṣṭaliq at al-Muraysīʿ. The name of al-Muṣṭaliq was Khuzaymah. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ also captured Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab from Banī al-Naḍīr on the day of Khaybar. She was the bride of Kinānah ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq.

These are the eleven women with whom the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ consummated marriage. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, allotted during his caliphate twelve thousand to each of the wives of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. He allotted six thousand each to Juwayriyah and Ṣafiyyah because they had been captives. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had allotted a share to them and had also enjoined ḥijāb upon them.

The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ also married al-ʿĀliyah ibn Ẓabyān ibn ʿAmr from Banī Abī Bakr ibn Kilāb, but he did not consummate the marriage with her and divorced her. In the narration of Yaʿqūb: he consummated the marriage with her and then divorced her."
"13430","(13424) Through this chain of narration from al-Zuhrī: that ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed him that ʿĀ'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allāh be pleased with her, said: ""Aḍ-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Sufyān from Banī Abī Bakr ibn Kilāb came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and I was listening from behind the curtain. He said: 'O Messenger of Allāh, do you have any interest in the sister of Umm Shabīb?' Umm Shabīb was the wife of Aḍ-Ḍaḥḥāk. In the narration of Yaʿqūb: Aḍ-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Sufyān from Banī Abī Bakr ibn Kilāb proposed her to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ."" Then he mentioned the rest.

Al-Zuhrī said regarding the marriage of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to a woman from Banī ʿAmr ibn Kilāb—the brothers of Abī Bakr ibn Kilāb, the people of Zufar ibn al-Ḥārith: he saw some whiteness (in her hair) and divorced her without consummating the marriage. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ also married the sister of Banī al-Jawn al-Kindī, who were allies of Banī Fazārah. She sought refuge from him, and he said: ""You have indeed sought refuge with a great one. Go back to your family."" So he divorced her without consummating the marriage.

He had a Coptic concubine named Māriyah, who bore him a boy named Ibrāhīm. He passed away while still in the cradle. He also had a bondwoman named Rayḥānah bint Shamʿūn from the People of the Book, from Banī Khunāfah—a clan of Banī Qurayẓah. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ manumitted her, and they claim that she observed ḥijāb. [Weak]"
"13431","(13425) Abū al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Aṣbagh ibn Faraj narrated to me, Ibn Wahb narrated from Yūnus, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: It reached us that ʿĀliyah bint Ẓabyān, whom the Prophet (peace be upon him) had divorced, married before Allah had prohibited his wives (from remarrying). She married a paternal cousin of hers and bore children among them. [Weak]"
"13432","(13426) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) had married Asmāʾ ibnat Kaʿb al-Jawniyyah, but he did not consummate the marriage with her until he divorced her. He also married ʿAmrah bint Zayd, one of the women of Banū Kilāb, then Banī al-Waḥīd, and she had previously been married to al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) divorced her before consummating the marriage. He (Ibn Isḥāq) named the two whom al-Zuhrī did not name, and he did not mention ʿĀliyah. [Weak]"
"13433","(13427) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, he said: I heard Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn Sahl saying: I heard Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad saying: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Abān al-Juʿfī saying: My maternal uncle Ḥusayn al-Juʿfī said to me: ""O my son, do you know why ʿUthmān was called Dhū al-Nūrayn?"" I said: ""I do not know."" He said: ""No one has ever been granted the marriage of two daughters of a Prophet since Allah created Adam until the establishment of the Hour, other than ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him). For this reason he was called Dhū al-Nūrayn.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Zaynab ibnat Umm Salamah married ʿAbdullāh ibn Zamʿah, and al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām married Asmāʾ ibnat Abī Bakr, and Ṭalḥah married her other sister — and they were both sisters of the Mother of the Believers — and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf married the daughter of Jaḥsh, who was the sister of the Mother of the Believers Zaynab, that is, Umm Ḥabībah bint Jaḥsh. This is clearly established in the aḥādīth. In all of this there is evidence that the wives of the Prophet (peace be upon him) became Mothers of the Believers, and their daughters did not become their sisters, nor did their sisters become their maternal aunts. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Good]"
"13434","(13428) Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Imām narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Saqaṭī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Thābit narrated to me, my father narrated from al-Hudhayl, from Muqātil ibn Sulaymān, who said — referring to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""Indeed, O wives of the Prophet (peace be upon him), you see the revelation, and you are the most deserving of people of taqwā (piety)."" And He said before that: ""O wives of the Prophet, whoever of you commits a clear immorality…"" Muqātil said: This means disobedience to the Prophet (peace be upon him). ""…the punishment for her will be doubled"" — in the Hereafter. ""And that is easy for Allah"" — meaning, her punishment is easy upon Allah. ""And whoever among you is obedient to Allah and His Messenger"" — meaning, whoever among you obeys Allah and His Messenger — ""and does righteous deeds, We shall give her reward twice"" — in the Hereafter, for every prayer, or fast, or charity, or takbīrah, or tasbīḥah by the tongue, in place of every good deed, twenty good deeds shall be recorded. ""And We have prepared for her a noble provision"" — meaning, a good provision, and that is Paradise. [Weak]"
"13435","(13429) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdad, Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Warrāq narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: We were present with Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) at the funeral of Maymūnah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him), at Sarif. Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""This is Maymūnah (may Allah be pleased with her). When you raise her bier, do not shake it and do not jolt it; be gentle. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) had nine wives; he would divide (his time) among eight, and there was one for whom he would not divide."" ʿAṭāʾ said: The one for whom he would not divide was Ṣafiyyah.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj. ʿAṭāʾ says: The one for whom he did not divide was Ṣafiyyah. However, the connected narrations indicate that it was Sawdah, since she gave her day as a gift to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5067. Muslim 1468]"
"13436","(13430) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us, my grandfather narrated to me, Ibn Abī Uways narrated to me, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, who said: My father narrated to me from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to ask during his illness in which he passed away: ""Where shall I be tomorrow? Where shall I be tomorrow?"" — intending the day of ʿĀʾishah. So his wives gave him permission to be wherever he wished. Thus he was in the house of ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) until he passed away in her presence. ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""He passed away on the day that would have been my turn in my house. He was taken (in death) while his head was between my chest and my throat, and his saliva mixed with my saliva."" She said: ""ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr entered with a siwāk with which he was brushing his teeth. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) looked at it, so I said to him: 'Give me this siwāk, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.' So he gave it to me. I softened it, then chewed it, and gave it to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and he used it as a siwāk while he was leaning against my chest (peace be upon him).""

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim recorded it from another route from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4450. Muslim 1463]"
"13437","(13431) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us, ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal narrated from Muʿādhah al-ʿAdawiyyah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to seek our permission on the day of one of us after the revelation of: 'You may defer any of them you wish and take to yourself any of them you wish.' Muʿādhah said to her: 'What would you say to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) when he sought your permission?' She said: 'I would say: If that is my choice, I would not prefer anyone over myself.'""

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from another route from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, and Muslim recorded it from Surayj ibn Yūnus from ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: When he intended a journey, he would draw lots among them, and whichever of them the lot came out for, he would take her with him. [Bukhārī 4789. Muslim 1476]"
"13438","(13432) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāniʾ narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Shahīd narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ al-ʿAtakī narrated to us, Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, ʿAlqamah ibn Waqqāṣ, and ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUtbah, from ʿľʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""When the Prophet (peace be upon him) intended to travel, he would draw lots among his wives, and whichever of them the lot came out for, he would take her with him.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: This applies to anyone among the people who has wives. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah, the Most High, be pleased with him) also said: Among such instances is that he intended to separate from Sawdah, so she said: ""Do not separate from me, and leave me until Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, resurrects me among your wives. I give my day and my night to my sister ʿľʾishah."" [Bukhārī, Muslim 1476]"
"13439","(13433) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib and ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Jarīr narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿľʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I have not seen any woman in her physical constitution like Sawdah — a woman in whom there was sharpness. When she grew old, she said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have given my day from you to ʿľʾishah.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would allocate for ʿľʾishah two days: her own day and the day of Sawdah.""

Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr, and al-Bukhārī recorded it in abridged form from another route from Hishām. [Bukhārī 5212. Muslim 1463]"
"13440","(13434) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, who said: ʿľʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O son of my sister, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) did not prefer any of us over any other in the matter of division during his stay among us. There was scarcely a day except that he would visit all of us, approaching each woman without touching her, until the day arrived that was her turn, and he would spend the night with her. Indeed, Sawdah bint Zamʿah, when she grew old and feared that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would separate from her, said: 'O Messenger of Allah, my day is for ʿľʾishah.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) accepted that from her."" She said regarding this: Allah, the Most High, revealed — and in similar cases I consider it to be — ""And if a woman fears from her husband ill-treatment or aversion…"" (the verse). [Weak]"
"13441","(13435) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, saying: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) divorced Sawdah. When he went out to the prayer, she held him by his garment and said: ""I have no need of men, but I wish to be gathered among your wives."" So he took her back and assigned her day to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and he would allocate for her a day along with Sawdah's day. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The daughter of Muḥammad ibn Maslamah did something similar when her husband intended to divorce her. [Weak]"
"13442","(13436) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, saying: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us, saying: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The daughter of Muḥammad ibn Maslamah was married to Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj. He came to dislike her, either due to old age or something else, and intended to divorce her. She said: ""Do not divorce me; rather keep me and allocate for me whatever you wish."" So they reconciled upon a settlement, and the Sunnah was established by that. And the Qur'an was revealed: {And if a woman fears from her husband ill-conduct or desertion} [Sūrat al-Nisāʾ, 4:128]. [Authentic]"
"13443","(13437) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from Zaynab bint Abī Salamah, from Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you have any interest in my sister, the daughter of Abū Sufyān?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What should I do?"" She said: ""Marry her."" He said: ""Your sister?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Would you like that?"" She said: ""Yes. I am not unoccupied for you, and I would love for my sister to share in any good that I have."" He said: ""She is not permissible for me."" She said: ""By Allah, I have been told that you are seeking the hand of the daughter of Abī Salamah."" He said: ""The daughter of Umm Salamah?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""By Allah, even if she were not my stepdaughter under my care, she would not be permissible for me, for she is the daughter of my brother through suckling. Thuwaybah suckled me and her father. So do not offer your daughters or your sisters to me in marriage."" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hishām and al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah. [Bukhārī, Muslim 1449]"
"13444","(13438) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me, saying: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us, saying: Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, saying: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, saying: al-Aʿmash narrated to us, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, why do you show such discernment regarding Quraysh and leave us out?"" He said: ""Do you have anything?"" He said: ""We said: 'Yes, the daughter of Ḥamzah.' He said: 'She is not permissible for me; she is the daughter of my brother through suckling.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb. [Muslim 1446]"
"13445","(13439) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated to us, saying: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd say: Ibn Abī Mulkaykah narrated to me that ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr al-Laythī narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer. He mentioned the ḥadīth until he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) remained in his place and sat beside the chambers, warning against tribulations, and said: 'By Allah, nothing will restrain the people from me except that I do not permit except what Allah has permitted in His Book, and I do not prohibit except what Allah has prohibited in His Book.'"" [Weak]"
"13446","(13440) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, with his chain of narration, meaning from Ṭāwūs, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Let not the people be restrained from me by anything, for I do not permit for them except what Allah has permitted for them, and I do not prohibit upon them except what Allah has prohibited."" Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This is disconnected (munqaṭiʿ). Even if it were established, it would be clear in its meaning, as I have described, if Allah the Most High wills. He said: ""Let not the people be restrained from me"" — and he did not say ""let them not be restrained from me."" Rather, he commanded that restraint be exercised regarding him, and Allah, whose praise is exalted, commanded that. [Weak]"
"13447","(13441) Al-Shāfiʿī said: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from Abī al-Naḍr, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Let me not find any of you reclining upon his couch, when a matter comes to him of that which I have commanded or prohibited, and he says: 'I do not know; we only follow what we find in the Book of Allah.'"" Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: For he commanded adherence to what he commanded and avoidance of what he prohibited, and Allah imposed that in His Book upon His creation. What people have in their hands of this is only what they hold fast to from Allah, then from His Messenger (ﷺ), then from his indications. But his statement — if he said it — ""Let not the people be restrained from me by anything"" indicates that when the Prophet (ﷺ) was in the position of exemplar, he had special rulings in which he was permitted what was not permitted for others, and prohibited for him what was not prohibited for the people. So he said: ""Let not the people be restrained from me by anything"" of that which pertains to me or is upon me and not upon them. If it is something that is upon me and for me and not for them, then let them not be restrained by it. An example of this is that Allah permitted him as many wives as He willed, and that he may marry a woman if she offers herself to him. Allah the Most High said: {Exclusive for you, excluding the believers} [Sūrat al-Aḥzāb, 33:50]. So it was not for anyone to say: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) combined more than four wives, and married a woman without a dowry, and took Ṣafiyyah from the war booty, and he had the fifth of the fifth"" — for that is not permissible for the believers after him, nor for their governors, just as it was for him, because Allah has made clear in His Book and upon the tongue of His Messenger (ﷺ) that it was for him exclusively. And Allah obligated him to give his wives the choice between remaining with him or separation. So it was not for anyone to say: ""I am obligated to give my wife the choice, just as Allah obligated His Messenger (ﷺ)."" This is the meaning of the Prophet's statement (ﷺ), if he said it: ""Let not the people be restrained from me by anything."" The Sheikh said: Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) only hesitated regarding the authenticity of the report, saying: ""If he said it,"" because the ḥadīth is mursal and there is nothing with it to corroborate it, unless it is interpreted according to what al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said, in which case it would be clear and in accordance with the established principles. [Authentic]"
"13448","(13442) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us, saying: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, that Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed him, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Jābir narrated to us, that he heard al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib al-Kindī, the companion of the Prophet (ﷺ), say: The Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited certain things on the day of Khaybar, among them the domestic donkey and others. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Soon a man among you will sit upon my couch, narrating my ḥadīth, and he will say: 'Between me and you is the Book of Allah. Whatever we find in it to be permissible, we declare it permissible, and whatever we find in it to be forbidden, we declare it forbidden.' Indeed, what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) has forbidden is just as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has forbidden."" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī likewise narrated it from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ. [Authentic through other chains]"
"13449","(13443) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr, from al-Muṭṭalib, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I have not left anything that Allah has commanded you with except that I have commanded you with it, and I have not left anything that Allah has prohibited you from except that I have prohibited you from it."" Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Whatever was not in it by way of revelation, Allah has imposed in the revelation adherence to His Sunnah. So whoever accepts from him, he has accepted by the obligation of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. [Weak]"
"13450","(13444) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd narrated to us, saying: Ibn Shuʿayb informed me, saying: Shaybān informed me, from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd, who narrated to them from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh al-Asadī, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd said to me: ""What are the ḥafadah?"" He said: ""I said: 'A man's children.' He said: 'No, rather they are the sisters (akhawāt).'"" [Good]"
"13451","(13445) It was narrated by Ibn ʿUyaynah from ʿĀṣim, who said: ""No, they are the in-laws (al-aṣhār)."" Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Sufyān narrated, and he mentioned it. [Ḥasan]"
"13452","(13446) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, al-Aʿmash narrated from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah, who said: ""I was walking with ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿud (may Allah be pleased with him) when ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) met him at Minā and began speaking to him. ʿUthmān said to him: 'O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, shall we not marry you a young maiden, perhaps she may remind you of some of what has passed of your time?' ʿAbdullāh said: 'As for what you have said, indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to us: ""O company of young men, whoever among you has the means (al-bāʾah), let him marry, for it is more effective in lowering the gaze and guarding the private parts. And whoever among you does not have the means, let him fast, for fasting is a shield (wijāʾ) for him.""'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others from Abī Muʿāwiyah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from al-Aʿmash. [Bukhārī 79, Muslim 1400]"
"13453","(13447) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān al-Sukkārī informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash from ʿUmārah ibn ʿUmayr from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, who said: ""We entered upon ʿAbdullāh (ibn Masʿud), and with him were ʿAlqamah and al-Aswad. He narrated a hadith — I do not think he narrated it except on my account, for I was the youngest of the people in age. ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), young men who found nothing (i.e., had no means). He said: ""O company of young men, whoever among you has the means (al-bāʾah), let him marry, for it is more effective in lowering the gaze and guarding the private parts. And whoever among you does not have the means (al-bāʾah), let him fast, for fasting is a shield (wijāʾ) for him.""'"" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their two Ṣaḥīḥs through various chains from al-Aʿmash."
"13454","(13448) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me, Ḥumayd informed me that he heard Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""Three groups (raht) came to the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) asking about the worship of the Prophet (ﷺ). When they were informed of it, it was as though they considered it insufficient. They said: 'Where are we compared to the Prophet (ﷺ), when Allah has forgiven him his past and future sins?' One of them said: 'As for me, I shall pray the night prayer forever.' Another said: 'I shall fast perpetually and never break my fast.' Another said: 'I shall abstain from women and never marry.' The Prophet (ﷺ) came to them and said: 'Are you the ones who said such and such? Indeed, I am the most fearful of you of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and the most devout of you toward Him. Yet I fast and I break my fast, I pray and I sleep, and I marry women. Whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not of me.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Thābit from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"13455","(13449) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām narrated to us, Aswad ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdān al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUthmān al-Lāḥiqī narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us. Thābit informed us from Anas ibn Mālik that a group (nafar) from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) asked the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) about his private devotions. Some of them said: 'I shall not marry women.' Some of them said: 'I shall not eat meat.' Some of them said: 'I shall not sleep on a bed.' Some of them said: 'I shall fast and never break my fast.' This reached the Prophet (ﷺ), so he stood and praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'What is the matter with a people who said such and such? Yet I pray and I sleep, I fast and I break my fast, and I marry women. Whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not of me.' The wording is that of the hadith of Abū ʿUthmān. The hadith of al-Rūdhbārī is abridged and does not contain the narration of their statements. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5063, Muslim 1401]"
"13456","(13450) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us in Marw, Muḥammad ibn al-Layth narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Raqabah from Ṭalḥah al-Iyāmī from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) said to me: ""Marry, for indeed the best of us was the one with the most wives,"" meaning the Prophet (ﷺ). Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13457","(13451) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬāʾiʾ narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Ibrāhīm ibn Maysarah from ʿUbayd ibn Saʿd from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever loves my natural disposition (fiṭrah), then let him follow my Sunnah, and marriage (al-nikāḥ) is of my Sunnah."" That was also narrated from Abī Ḥurrah from al-Ḥasan from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13458","(13452) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬāʾiʾ narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Ibrāhīm ibn Maysarah from Ṭāwūs from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""I have not seen for two who love one another anything like marriage (al-nikāḥ)."" And this is a mursal hadith. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13459","(13453) Muḥammad ibn Muslim al-Ṭāʾifī narrated it from Ibrāhīm ibn Maysarah from Ṭāwūs from Ibn ʿAbbās from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: ""They have not seen for two who love one another anything like marriage (al-tazawwuj)."" Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf (dictation), and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʬAmr all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Tinnīsī Abū Muḥammad informed us, Muḥammad ibn Muslim al-Ṭāʾifī narrated, and he mentioned it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13460","(13454) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿĪsā al-Hilālī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, the Imam, informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Muṭayyin narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd narrated to us. Both said: Salām Abū al-Mundhir narrated from Thābit from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, of your world, women and perfume have been made beloved to me, and the coolness of my eyes has been placed in prayer (al-ṣalāh)."" The wording is that of the hadith of ʿAlī. In the narration of Mūsā, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Of the world, [these things] have been made beloved to me."" Sayyār ibn Ḥātim followed it up from Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān from Thābit from Anas. A group from the weak narrators narrated that from Thābit, and Allah knows best."
"13461","(13455) Abū Ṭāhir, the Imām, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Aṭāʾ reported to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Maymūn, Abū al-Mughallis, narrated to me, from Abī Najīḥ, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: ""Whoever is well-off and able to marry but does not marry is not of us."" [This is a mursal (disconnected) hadith — Weak]"
"13462","(13456) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad, Abū ʿĀṣim, narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān, from al-Maqburī, from Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There are three persons upon whom it is a right of Allah to aid them: the mujāhid in the cause of Allah, the one who marries seeking chastity (ʿafāf), and the mukātab (a slave under a contract of manumission) who seeks to fulfill his obligation."" [Good (Ḥasan)]"
"13463","(13457) Abū Saʿd the Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm al-Thaqafī al-Baṣrī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Thābit al-Baṣrī narrated to us, from Abī Ghālib, from Abī Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Marry, for I shall take pride in your numbers before the nations on the Day of Resurrection, and do not become like the monasticism of the Christians."" There are numerous narrations in this regard whose chains contain weakness, though what we have cited suffices. Imām al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: ""And it has reached us that the Prophet ﷺ said: 'Whoever dies having lost three of his children, the Fire shall not touch him.'"" [Weak]"
"13464","(13458) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Marzūq and Abū Muṣʿab al-Zuhrī — both of them — narrated to us, from Mālik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""No Muslim shall die having lost three of his children, but that the Fire shall not touch him, except as a fulfillment of the oath (taḥillat al-qasam)."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ collections from the ḥadīth of Mālik.

(Note) Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: ""And it is said that a man may be elevated by the supplication of his child after him."" Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: ""And this is the statement of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 6656, Muslim 2634]"
"13465","(13459) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, Allah elevates a servant in rank, and the servant will say: 'My Lord, how have I attained this rank?' Allah will say: 'Through the supplication of your child for you.'"" [Good (Ḥasan)]"
"13466","(13460) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us: Abū al-Naḍr Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to us, from al-Hajjāʿ ibn Qays, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""By Allah, I compel myself to engage in sexual intercourse (jimāʿ), hoping that Allah may bring forth from me a soul that glorifies Him."" [Good (Ḥasan)]"
"13467","(13461) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, that Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, intended not to marry, so Ḥafṣah said to him: ""Marry, for if a child is born to you and lives after you, they will supplicate for you."""
"13468","(13462) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: ʿUqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihāb: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab informed me that he heard Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: ""ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn intended to practice celibacy (tabattul), so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade him from that. Had he permitted it, we would have had ourselves castrated."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 5074, Muslim 1402]"
"13469","(13463) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān al-Ḥakam ibn Nāfiʿ informed us: Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab informed me that he heard Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: ""The Prophet ﷺ rejected that from ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn, and had he permitted him celibacy, we would have had ourselves castrated."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13470","(13464) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: ʿImrān — that is, ibn Mūsā — narrated to us: ʿUthmān — that is, ibn Abī Shaybah — narrated to us: Jarīr narrated to us, from Ismāʿīl, from Qays, who said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh saying: ""We used to go on military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and we had no women with us, so we said: 'Shall we not have ourselves castrated?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us from that. Then he permitted us to marry a woman with a garment (i.e., on a temporary basis), and then he recited to us: {O you who believe, do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you, and do not transgress. Indeed, Allah does not like the transgressors.} [Al-Māʾidah 5:87] Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from Jarīr, and Muslim narrated it from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī, Muslim 1404]"
"13471","(13465) Abū ʿAmr al-Bisṭāmī informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Ḥarmalah informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us that Yūnus informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I am a young man and I fear for myself the hardship (of temptation), and I do not find the means to marry women. Permit me to have myself castrated.' He remained silent from me. Then I said the same to him, and he remained silent from me. Then I said the same to him, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'O Abū Hurayrah, the pen has dried concerning what you are destined to meet. So either castrate yourself on that account, or leave it.'"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: ""And Aṣbagh said: Ibn Wahb informed me, and he mentioned it."" And Allah knows best."
"13472","(13466) Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Naysābūrī informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Both said: Musaddad narrated to us that Yaḥyā narrated to me that ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to me that Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd narrated to me, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Women are married for four things: for their wealth, for their lineage, for their beauty, and for their religion. So marry the one who is religious, may your hands be dusty (i.e., you will prosper)."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd."
"13473","(13467) ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us that Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us that Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us that Isḥāq al-Azraq narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us that ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us, from Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, that he married a woman during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. He met the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and he said to him: ""O Jābir, have you married?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""A virgin or a previously married woman?"" He said: ""A previously married woman."" He said: ""Why not a virgin, so that she may play with you?"" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I had sisters, and I feared that she would come between me and them."" He said: ""That is it. Indeed, a woman is married for her religion, her wealth, and her beauty. So marry the one who is religious, may your hands be dusty."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from another chain from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān."
"13474","(13468) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Marwazī narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us that Ḥaywah informed us that Shuraḥbīl ibn Sharīk informed me that he heard Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥubulī narrating from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, that he said: ""The entire world is provision, and the best provision of the world is a righteous woman."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Numayr, from al-Muqriʾ."
"13475","(13469) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us that Abū Badr narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: 'Do not marry women for their beauty, for their beauty may lead them to ruin. And do not marry women for their wealth, for their wealth may make them rebellious. Rather, marry them for religion. A black, gap-toothed slave woman who is religious is better.'"" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Ibn Bishrān. And in the narration of Abū Zakariyyā, may Allah have mercy on him, it reads: ""kharmāʾ"" (deaf). And Allah knows best."
"13476","(13470) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us that Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated to us that Muḥārib ibn Dithār narrated to us. He said: ""I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with both of them, say: 'I married, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to me: ""What have you married?"" I said: ""I have married a previously married woman."" He said: ""What is the matter with you and the virgin girls and their playfulness?""'"" Shuʿbah said: ""I mentioned that to ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, and ʿAmr ibn Dīnār said: 'I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to me: ""Why not a young girl, so that you may play with her and she may play with you?""'"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from Shuʿbah."
"13477","(13471) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us that ʿĀrim, Sulaymān, and Musaddad — their wordings being close to one another, and the wording being that of Sulaymān — said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: ""ʿAbd Allāh died and left seven daughters, or nine daughters."" Jābir said: ""So I married a previously married woman, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to me: 'Have you married, O Jābir?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'A virgin or a previously married woman?' I said: 'Rather a previously married woman, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Why not a young girl, so that you may play with her and she may play with you, and you may make her laugh and she may make you laugh?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, ʿAbd Allāh died and left seven daughters, or he said nine daughters, and I disliked bringing to them one like them. So I preferred to bring them a woman who would look after them.' He said: 'May Allah bless you,' or he said, 'good.'"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿĀrim and Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others, from Ḥammād ibn Zayd."
"13478","(13472) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbdak al-Rāzī narrated to us in Baghdad that Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us that Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us that my brother narrated to me, from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, tell me: if you came upon a valley with trees from which some had been eaten and you found a tree from which nothing had been eaten, which one would you graze from?' He said: 'From the tree from which nothing had been eaten.'"" She said: ""And that is me, meaning that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, did not marry any virgin other than her."" al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways."
"13479","(13473) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī, Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us that al-Fayḍ ibn Wathīq narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah ibn al-Ṭawīl al-Taymī, who informed me that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sālim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'Marry virgins, for they have sweeter mouths, more fertile wombs, and are more content with little.'"" Abū ʿAbd Allāh and al-Qāḍī said in their narration: ""Ibn ʿUwaym ibn Sāʿidah."""
"13480","(13474) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Khalaf ibn ʿAmr al-ʿUkbariyy narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sālim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUwaym ibn Sāʿidah narrated to me, and he mentioned it with its chain of narration similar to it. And ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUwaym does not have companionship (ṣuḥbah). al-Qutaybī said, as it has reached me from him: ""More fertile wombs"" — meaning more prolific in offspring."
"13481","(13475) Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: al-Muslim ibn Saʿīd informed us: Manṣūr ibn Zādhān narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrath from Maʿqil ibn Yasār, who said:

A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have found a woman of noble lineage, high status, and wealth, but she cannot bear children. Should I marry her?"" He (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade him. Then the man came to him a second time and said the same thing, and he forbade him again. Then the man came to him a third time and said the same thing, whereupon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Marry the affectionate and fertile, for I shall take pride in your numbers over the nations."" [Ḥasan]"
"13482","(13476) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Khalaf ibn Khalīfah narrated to us: Ḥafṣ, the brother of Anas's nephew, narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to command us to marry and used to forbid us from celibacy with a strong prohibition, and he would say: ""Marry the affectionate and fertile, for I shall take pride in your numbers over the Prophets on the Day of Resurrection."" [Ḥasan]"
"13483","(13477) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Ibn ʿAjlān from al-Maqburī from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was asked: ""Which woman is the best?"" He said: ""The one who pleases him when he looks at her, obeys him when he commands her, and does not oppose him regarding herself or his wealth."" [Ḥasan]"
"13484","(13478) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me: Mūsā ibn ʿAlī ibn Rabaḥ narrated from his father from Abī Udhaynah al-Ṣadafī that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said:

""The best of your women are the affectionate, the fertile, the compliant, and the compassionate — when they fear Allah. And the worst of your women are those who display their adornment and walk with haughty pride; they are the hypocrites. None of them shall enter Paradise except in the number of the red-beaked crow.""

It has also been narrated through a sound chain from Sulaymān ibn Yasār from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) in a mursal narration up to his words: ""when they fear Allah."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13485","(13479) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Sayyid Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to me: My father narrated to me: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd from Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrath from his father, who said:

ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed the people and said: ""A servant has not gained anything better after faith in Allah than a woman of good character who is affectionate and fertile. And a servant has not gained anything worse after disbelief in Allah than a woman of sharp tongue and bad character. By Allah, among them there is a source of benefit from which no harm comes, and among them there is a shackle from which there is no escape."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13486","(13480) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated: Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrath — Abū Iyās — informed me, saying: I heard my father narrating from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said — and he had met him — ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said:

""By Allah, a man has not gained any benefit after Islam better than a beautiful woman of good character who is affectionate and fertile. And by Allah, a man has not gained any harm after associating partners with Allah worse than a woman of bad character and sharp tongue. By Allah, among them there is indeed a shackle from which there is no ransom, and a source of benefit from which no harm comes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13487","(13481) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn narrated to us: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Hurmuz al-Fadakī from Saʿīd and Muḥammad, the two sons of ʿUbayd, from Abī Ḥātim al-Muzanī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said:

""When someone comes to you whose religion and character you are pleased with, then marry him [to your women]. If you do not do so, there will be tribulation in the land and widespread corruption."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, even if there is [some deficiency] in him?"" He said: ""When someone comes to you whose religion and character you are pleased with, then marry him."" He said it three times.

Abū Ḥātim al-Muzanī was a Companion, as stated by al-Bukhārī and others. It is also narrated from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) that she said: ""Marriage is a form of bondage, so let each of you look to where he places his freed slave."" This has also been narrated in a marfūʿ chain, but the mawqūf version is more authentic. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13488","(13482) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us: Badl ibn al-Muḥabbar narrated to us: Zāʾidah narrated from ʿĀṣim from Zirr from ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) — regarding the words of the Most High: ""And a master (sayyidan) and a ḥaṣūr,"" he said: ""The ḥaṣūr is the one who does not approach women."" [Ḥasan]"
"13489","(13483) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Warqāʾ narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid, who said: ""The ḥaṣūr is the one who does not approach women."" And this has also been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) and from ʿIkrimah. [Ḥasan]"
"13490","(13484) Abū ʿAbdullāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī, from his original copy, informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baghdādī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Dharr narrated to us: Mujāhid narrated that Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say:

""By Allah, there is no deity but He, I used to lie on the ground with my liver pressed against the earth from hunger, and I used to tie a stone to my stomach because of hunger. One day I sat on the path by which they would go out, and Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by me. I asked him about a verse from the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, only so that he would invite me to follow him, but he passed by and did not do so. Then ʿUmar passed by me, and I asked him about a verse from the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, only so that he would invite me to follow him, but he passed by and did not do so. Then Abū al-Qāsim (peace and blessings be upon him) passed by me, and he smiled when he saw me and recognized what was within me and what was on my face. Then he said: 'O Abā Hurayrah!' I said: 'Here I am, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Follow me.' He went on, and I followed him. He entered, and I sought permission, and he granted me permission. I entered and found milk in a vessel. He said: 'Where did this milk come from?' They said: 'So-and-so or so-and-so presented it to you.' He said: 'O Abā Hurayrah!' I said: 'Here I am, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Go to the People of the Ṣuffah and invite them for me.' He said: 'The People of the ṣuffah are the guests of Islam. They have neither family nor wealth to turn to. When charity came to him, he would send it to them and would not take anything from it. When a gift came to him, he would send it to them and would partake of it and include them in it.' That distressed me. I said: 'What is this milk doing among the People of the ṣuffah? I was hoping to get a drink from this milk to strengthen myself with it, and I am the messenger. When they come, he will command me to give it to them, and what chance do I have of receiving any of this milk?' Yet there was no escape from obeying Allah and obeying His Messenger. So I went to them and invited them, and they came until they sought permission, and he granted them permission, and they took their places in the house. He said: 'O Abā Hurayrah!' I said: 'Here I am, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Take it and give it to them.' So I took the vessel and began giving it to a man, and he would drink until he was satisfied, then return the vessel to me, and I would give it to another, and he would drink until he was satisfied, then return the vessel to me, until I reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and all the people had been satisfied. He took the vessel and placed it on his hand, and he looked at me and smiled, and said: 'O Abā Hurayrah!' I said: 'Here I am, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'I and you remain.' I said: 'You have spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Sit and drink.' So I sat and drank. He said: 'Drink.' So I drank. He said: 'Drink.' So I drank. He kept saying: 'Drink,' and I kept drinking, until I said: 'No, by the One who sent you with the truth, I find no more room for it.' He said: 'Come closer.' So I gave him the vessel, and he praised Allah, said the Name of Allah, and drank the remainder.

al-Bukhārī narrated this in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym. The point intended from this narration in this chapter is his statement: ""The People of the ṣuffah are the guests of Islam. They have neither family nor wealth to turn to."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5375]"
"13491","(13485) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Mughīrah al-Sukkārī in Hamadhān narrated to us: al-Qāsim ibn al-Ḥakam al-ʿUranī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Yamāmī narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said:

A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I am So-and-so, daughter of So-and-so."" He said, ""I recognize you. What is your need?"" She said, ""My need is regarding my cousin So-and-so, the devout worshipper. He has proposed to me, so tell me what the husband's rights are over the wife. If it is something I can fulfill, I will marry him, and if I cannot, I will not marry."" He said, ""Among the husband's rights over the wife is that if blood, pus, and discharge were to flow from his nostrils and she were to lick it with her tongue, she would still not have fulfilled his right. If it were permissible for any human being to prostrate to another human being, I would command the wife to prostrate to her husband when he enters upon her, because of the preference Allah has granted him over her."" She said, ""By the One Who sent you with the truth, I shall never marry as long as I remain in this world."""
"13492","(13486) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn Kaysān, from Abū Ḥāzim, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) when a man came to him and informed him that he had married a woman from the Anṣār. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him, ""Did you look at her?"" He said, ""No."" He said, ""Then go and look at her, for there is something in the eyes of the Anṣār.""

[Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar.]"
"13493","(13487) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Zurʿah ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAmr al-Dimashqī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Khālid narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, from Dāwūd ibn Ḥuṣayn, from Wāqid ibn ʿAmr ibn Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""When one of you proposes to a woman, if he is able to look at that which would please him and invite him to her, let him do so.""

Jābir said, ""I proposed to a woman from Banū Salamah, and I used to hide among the trunks of the palm trees until I saw from her that which pleased me, so I married her.""

[Ḥasan]"
"13494","(13488) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated to us, from Thābit, from Anas, who said:

al-Mughīrah (may Allah be pleased with him) wished to marry a woman, so the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him, ""Go and look at her, for it is more fitting that there be affection between you two."" He said, ""So I looked at her."" He said, ""Then he mentioned her compatibility (in marriage).""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13495","(13489) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān both informed us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from Bakr ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who said:

I proposed to a woman. He said, ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me, 'Have you looked at her?'"" He said, ""I said, 'No.' He said, 'Then look at her, for it is more fitting that there be affection between you two.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13496","(13490) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān narrated to us: Abū Shihāb narrated to us, from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from Bakr ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

I proposed to a woman and mentioned her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said to me, ""Have you looked at her?"" I said, ""No."" He said, ""Then look at her, for it is more fitting that there be affection between you two.""

He said, ""So I came to her, and her parents were with her, and she was in her curtain."" He said, ""I said, 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) has commanded me to look at you.'"" He said, ""They both fell silent."" He said, ""Then the young woman lifted the side of the curtain and said, 'I feel no reluctance, since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) has commanded you to look at me, so look. And even if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had not commanded you to look at me, you could still look.'"" He said, ""So I looked at her, then I married her."" He said, ""No woman has ever held the status she held in my regard, and I have married seventy, or close to seventy, women.""

[Ḥasan]"
"13497","(13491) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us: Abū Yūsuf Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn informed us: Abū Shihāb ʿAbd Rabbih ibn Nāfiʿ informed us, from al-Ḥajjāj, from Ibn Abī Mulkaykah, from Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah, from his paternal uncle Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah, who said:

I saw Muḥammad ibn Maslamah gazing at a woman with his eyes upon a rooftop—she was Thubaytah ibn al-Ḥaḥāk, the sister of Abī Jubayrah. I said, ""Do you do this, when you are a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?"" He said, ""Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said, 'When Allah places in a man's heart the desire to propose to a woman, there is no harm in him looking at her.'""

The chain of this hadith is disputed, and its reliance is upon al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh, and what has preceded is sufficient.

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13498","(13492) Some of the scholars of knowledge have cited as evidence in this regard what Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.

And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Ismāʿīl ibn Najīd ibn Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: al-ʿAtakī and Musaddad both said: Ḥammād narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""I saw you in a dream for three nights. The angel came to me with you wrapped in a piece of silk and said, 'This is your wife.' I uncovered your face, and it was you. So I said, 'If this is from Allah, He will bring it to pass.'""

[al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Rabīʿ al-ʿAtakī.]

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 3895. Muslim 2438.]"
"13499","(13493) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us, from Abū Ḥāzim, from Sahl ibn Saʿd, that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have come to offer myself to you."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) looked at her, raising his gaze upon her and then lowering it, and then he bowed his head. When the woman saw that he had not decided anything regarding her, she sat down.

And he mentioned the rest of the hadith.

[Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah.]

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5030. Muslim 1725.]"
"13500","(13494) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Muslim al-Muʾālī informed us, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding the verse: **""And they should not display their adornment except that which appears thereof""** — he said: ""That refers to kohl and a ring.""

And we have narrated it through another route from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, and that has also been narrated from Anas ibn Mālik.

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"13501","(13495) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Abū al-Azhar informed us, Rūḥ informed us, Ḥammād informed us, Umm Shabīb informed us: She said: I asked ʿĀ'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about apparent adornment (al-zīnah al-ẓāhirah), and she said: ""The anklet and the ring."" And she gathered the edge of her sleeve. [Weak]"
"13502","(13496) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibn Abī Qumāsh informed us, Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us, from al-Walīd ibn Muslim, from Saʿīd ibn Bashīr, from Qatādah, from Khālid ibn Durayk, from ʿĀ'ishah, Mother of the Believers: that Asmā' bint Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with her, came to her while the Prophet ﷺ was with her, wearing thin Syrian garments. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at her and said: ""What is this, O Asmā'? Indeed, when a woman reaches the age of menstruation, nothing should be seen of her except this and this."" And he pointed to his palms and his face. [Weak]"
"13503","(13497) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ informed us, Ibn Lahīʿah narrated to us, from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbdullāh, that he heard Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUbayd ibn Rifāʿah al-Anṣārī narrating from his father — I think — from Asmā' bint ʿUmays, that she said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr, and her sister Asmā' was with her, wearing Syrian garments with wide sleeves. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at her, he stood up and left. ʿĀ'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said to her: ""Move away, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has seen something he dislikes."" So she moved away. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered, and ʿĀ'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, asked him: ""Why did you stand up?"" He said: ""Did you not see her state? It is not permissible for a Muslim woman that anything of her be exposed except this."" And he took his two palms and covered the back of his hands with them, so that nothing of his hands was visible except his fingers. Then he placed his two palms on his temples, so that nothing was visible except its face. Its chain of narration is weak. [Weak]"
"13504","(13498) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed me, Ghiṭfah bint ʿAmr al-Mujāshiʿiyyah informed me, my aunt Umm al-Ḥasan informed me, from her grandmother, from ʿĀ'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Hind bint ʿUtbah said: ""O Prophet of Allah, accept my pledge of allegiance."" He said: ""I will not accept your pledge of allegiance until you change your hands — they are like the paws of a wild beast."" [Very Weak]"
"13505","(13499) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us, ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd informed us, Ṭālūt ibn ʿAbbād informed us, Muṭīʿ ibn Maymūn Abū Saʿīd said: Ṣafiyyah bint ʿIṣmah narrated to us, from ʿĀ'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: A woman came from behind a curtain with a letter in her hand to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ withdrew his hand and said: ""I do not know whether it is the hand of a man or the hand of a woman."" She said: ""Rather, it is the hand of a woman."" He said: ""If you were a woman, you would have dyed your nails with henna."" [Weak]"
"13506","(13500) Abū Saʿd informed us, Abū Aḥmad informed us, Abū Yaʿlā informed us, Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mūsā informed us, Muṭīʿ ibn Maymūn Abū Saʿīd narrated to us, with its chain of narration, similar to it. [Weak]"
"13507","(13501) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl informed us, Hishām ibn ʿAlī informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thābit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet ﷺ intended to marry a woman, so he sent a woman to look at her. He said: ""Smell her cheeks and look at her ankles."" She came to them and they said: ""Shall we give you something to eat, O Mother of so-and-so?"" She said: ""I will not eat except from food that so-and-so brings."" She climbed up into an upper room of theirs and looked at her ankles. Then she said: ""Kiss me, my daughter."" So she began to kiss her while she smelled her cheeks. Then she came and reported. Our shaykh narrated it thus in al-Mustadrak. Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī narrated it in al-Marāsīl from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, as a mursal narration, abbreviated, without mentioning Anas. Abū al-Nuʿmān also narrated it from Ḥammād as a mursal narration. Muḥammad ibn Kathīr al-Ṣanʿānī narrated it from Ḥammād as a mawsūl (connected) narration. ʿUmarah ibn Zādhān narrated it from Thābit, from Anas, as a mawsūl narration. [Denounced (Munkar)]"
"13508","(13502) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibn Milḥān informed us, Yaḥyā informed us, al-Layth narrated to me, from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Anas ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me: that he was ten years of age at the time of the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madīnah. He said: My mother used to urge me to serve the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for ten years in Madīnah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away when I was twenty years of age. I was the most knowledgeable of people regarding the matter of the ḥijāb when it was revealed. The first thing that was revealed concerning it was in connection with the wedding of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, may Allah be pleased with her. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became a bridegroom with her. He invited the people, and they ate from the food, then they departed. Some of them remained with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and lingered for a long time. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up to leave, and I went out with him so that they might also leave. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked, and I walked with him, until he came to the threshold of the chamber of ʿĀ'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ thought that they had left, so he returned, and I returned with him, until he entered upon Zaynab, and there they were, still seated; they had not stood up. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned, and I returned with him, until he reached the chamber of ʿĀ'ishah. He thought that they had left, so he returned, and I returned with him, and they had departed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed the ḥijāb between me and him, and the ḥijāb was revealed. al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and both he and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5166. Muslim 86]"
"13509","(13503) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib informed us, Dāwūd ibn Shabīb informed us, Muʿtamir narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Khalaf Abū Salamah al-Bāhilī and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Ṣanʿānī both said: Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, from his father, Abū Mijlaz narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, he invited the people, and they ate, then they sat conversing. He said: He began to make a show of standing up. He said: But they did not stand. He said: When he saw that, he stood up, and some of the people stood, but three of them remained seated. The Prophet ﷺ came to enter, and there the people were, still seated. Then they stood up and departed. I came to enter, and the ḥijāb was placed between me and him. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: ""O you who believe, do not enter the houses of the Prophet unless you are given permission to a meal, not waiting for its preparation. But when you are invited, enter; and when you have eaten, disperse..."" until: ""Indeed, that was [a matter] grave in the sight of Allah."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Nuʿmān and others, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā and others, from Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān. A group also narrated it with the same meaning from Anas ibn Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13510","(13504) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Zuhrī informed us in Makkah, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣā'iğ informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī informed us, Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdūs ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Naysābūrī informed us, Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī informed us, al-Anṣārī informed us, Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""My Lord agreed with me in three matters. I said: 'If only we took the Station of Ibrāhīm as a place of prayer.' So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: 'And take the Station of Ibrāhīm as a place of prayer.' And I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, the righteous and the wicked enter upon you. If only you would veil the Mothers of the Believers.' So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed the verse of Ḥijāb. And I learned of something that had occurred between the Mothers of the Believers and the Prophet ﷺ, so I went to them and said: 'You must desist from [bothering] the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, or Allah will give him wives better than you.' I went to the last of the Mothers of the Believers, and Umm Salamah said: 'O ʿUmar, is it not that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ admonishes his wives, that you admonish them?' So I fell silent. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: 'It may be that his Lord, if he divorces you, will give him in exchange wives better than you...'"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from another route from Ḥumayd, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar from ʿUmar, in abbreviated form, except that he said in place of the third matter: ""The captives of Badr."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 4483. Muslim 2399]"
"13511","(13505) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ibn Milḥān informed us: Yaḥyā informed us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀ'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that the wives of the Prophet ﷺ used to go out at night when they needed to answer the call of nature to al-Manāṣiʿ, which was an open, spacious area. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ""Veil your wives."" But the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not do so. One night Sawdah bint Zamʿah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, went out for the evening prayer time, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) called out to her: ""We have recognized you, O Sawdah!"" — out of eagerness that the command of Ḥijāb might be revealed. ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Then the Ḥijāb was revealed.""

[Sahih. Bukhari 147. Muslim 2170]"
"13512","(13506) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāyinī informed me from the original of his book: Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn, who is al-Khusrawjirdī, informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth informed me: my father informed me, from my grandfather, who said: ʿUqayl ibn Khālid narrated it with the same wording, and added: ""She was a tall woman."" So ʿUmar called out: ""Indeed, we have recognized you."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth, from his father, from his grandfather. Thus narrated al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah.

[Sahih]"
"13513","(13507) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: ʿImrān ibn Mūsā narrated to us: ʿUthmān, who is Ibn Abī Shaybah, narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated from Hishām, from his father, from ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""Sawdah (may Allah be pleased with her) went out after the Ḥijāb had been enjoined upon us, to attend to some of her needs. She was a large-bodied woman, towering above other women in stature, and she could not be hidden from anyone who knew her. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) saw her and said: 'By Allah, you cannot hide yourselves from us! See how you go out!' She said: 'So I turned back, returning, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in my room, taking his supper, and in his hand was a bone. She entered and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I went out and ʿUmar said such and such."" Then Allah revealed to him, and then the state was lifted from him, while the bone was still in his hand — he had not put it down. He said: ""You have been given permission to go out for your needs.'"" Hishām said: He meant answering the call of nature.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā, and Muslim narrated it from Abī Kurayb and others, all from Abū Usāmah.

[Bukhari 4795]"
"13514","(13508) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Maʿqil informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated — Aḥmad ibn Shabīb said: my father narrated from Yūnus — who said: Ibn Shihāb narrated from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""May Allah have mercy on the early Muhājir women. When Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: 'And let them draw their head-coverings over their bosoms' [al-Nūr 24:31], they tore their outer garments and covered themselves with them.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it thus.

And Abū ʿAmr al-Bisṭāmī informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Ḥibbān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh, who is Ibn al-Mubārak, informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Nāfiʿ narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim, from Ṣafiyyah bint Shaybah, from ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""When this verse was revealed — 'And let them draw their head-coverings over their bosoms' — the women took their lower garments and tore them from the edges and covered themselves with them.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym, from Ibrāhīm ibn Nāfiʿ. And we have narrated it through a higher chain in the book of Ṣalāh.

[Sahih. Bukhari 4759]"
"13515","(13509) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from Ibn Ṭāwūs, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""I have not seen anything more resembling the minor sins (lamam) than what Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Allah has decreed for the son of Ādam his share of zinā (adultery/fornication), which he will inevitably attain. The zinā of the eyes is looking; the zinā of the tongue is speech; the soul desires and yearns; and the private part confirms it or denies it.'""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm.

[Sahih. Bukhari 6243. Muslim 2657]"
"13516","(13510) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr informed us: al-Makhzūmī, meaning Abū Hishām, informed us: Wuhaib narrated: Suhayl narrated from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""It has been written for the son of Ādam his share of zinā, which he will inevitably attain. The zinā of the two eyes is looking; the zinā of the two ears is listening; the zinā of the tongue is speech; the zinā of the hand is seizing; the zinā of the foot is walking; the heart desires and yearns; and the private part confirms it or denies it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr.

[Sahih]"
"13517","(13511) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Abū Muslim informed us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl informed us: Ḥammād narrated from Suhayl, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Every son of Ādam has his share of zinā. The two eyes commit zinā, and their zinā is looking. The two hands commit zinā, and their zinā is seizing. The two feet commit zinā, and their zinā is walking. The mouth commits zinā, and its zinā is kissing. The heart desires and yearns, and the private part confirms it or denies it."" Abū Hurayrah testified to that by his hearing and his sight.

[Sahih]"
"13518","(13512) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Asadī informed us: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, from Zayd ibn ʿAlī, from his father, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet ﷺ took al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās up behind him, then came to the Jamrah and pelted it. A young woman from Khathʿam came before him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my father is an old, decrepit man, and the obligation of Allah regarding Hajj has caught up with him. Will it suffice if I perform Hajj on his behalf?"" He said: ""Perform Hajj on behalf of your father."" And he turned the neck of al-Faḍl. al-ʿAbbās said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, why did you turn the neck of your nephew?"" He said: ""I saw a young man and a young woman, and I did not feel safe from Satan regarding them.""

And we have narrated it in the book of Hajj from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās with part of its meaning.

[Sahih]"
"13519","(13513) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭā' ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Beware of sitting on the roads."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we have no choice but to sit and converse."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""If you insist, then give the road its right."" They said: ""And what is the right of the road, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Lowering the gaze, refraining from harm, returning the greeting of peace, enjoining what is good, and forbidding what is evil.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. And al-Bukhārī narrated it through two other routes from Zayd ibn Aslam.

[Sahih. Bukhari 622. Muslim 1211]"
"13520","(13514) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab in Wāsiṭ informed us: Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Sufyān narrated — (ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī informed us: Abū Nuʿaym informed us: Sufyān narrated from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd, from Abū Zurʿah, from Jarīr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I asked the Prophet ﷺ about a sudden, unintended glance. He commanded me to avert my gaze.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Wakīʿ, from Sufyān al-Thawrī.

[Sahih. Muslim 2159]"
"13521","(13515) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym and Abū Ghassān both said: Sharīk narrated to us, from Abū Rabīʿah al-Iyādī, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): ""O ʿAlī, do not follow one glance with another, for the first is yours, but the last is not."" [Ḥasan for other than its chain]"
"13522","(13516) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq and Abū Muslim — who is Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq in Baghdād informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdillāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Ayyūb al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Baṣrī said: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us: Abū al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ﷺ) saw a woman and went to Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, fulfilled his need with her, and then came out to his Companions and said: ""Indeed, a woman comes in the form of a devil and goes away in the form of a devil. Whoever finds that, let him go to his wife, for it will extinguish what is within himself."" Ismāʿīl did not mention his words: ""for it will extinguish what is within himself."" Muslim narrated it from another route from Hishām al-Dastawāʾī and said: ""for that will repel what is within himself."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1403]"
"13523","(13517) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us orally: Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Marwazī narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Ādam al-Marwazī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah al-Hilālī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated to us: Abū Maʿbad, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: ""Let no man be alone with a woman, and let no woman travel except with a mahram."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, all from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Bukhārī 1864. Muslim 827]"
"13524","(13518) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mātī al-Kūfī in Baghdād narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah al-Ghifārī informed us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us: al-Layth informed us, from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Abū al-Khayr, from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Beware of entering upon women."" A man from the Anṣār said: ""What about the husband's brother (al-ḥamu)?"" He said: ""The husband's brother is death."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Bukhārī 5232. Muslim 2172]"
"13525","(13519) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā — (ḥ) and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Abū al-Ṭāhir said — Ibn Wahb informed us: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that Bakr ibn Sawādah narrated to him that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr narrated to him that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated to him that a group of Banī Hāshim entered upon Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, and Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) also entered while she was his wife at that time. He saw them and disliked that, and he mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""I see nothing but good."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has declared her innocent of that."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood upon the minbar and said: ""No man shall enter upon a woman whose husband is absent after this day of mine except that he be accompanied by one or two men."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Muqrīʾ. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2173]"
"13526","(13520) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from al-Ḥakam, who said: I heard Dhakwān narrating from a freedman of ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, that he sent him to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) to seek permission to enter upon Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays. He gave him permission, and when he had finished his need, the freedman asked ʿAmr about that. He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade us — or forbade — from entering upon women without the permission of their husbands."" [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than its chain]"
"13527","(13521) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Quraysh al-Marwazī, who came to us as a warrior, narrated to us: Ḥāmid ibn Maḥmūd al-Marwazī narrated to us: ʿAbdān ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Jabalah informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us: Muḥammad ibn Sūqah narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb delivered a sermon at al-Jābiyah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood among us as I stand among you and said: ""Treat my Companions well, then those who come after them, then those who come after them. Then falsehood will spread so much that a man will begin giving testimony before being asked for it, and swearing an oath before being asked for it. Whoever among you desires the spaciousness of Paradise, let him hold fast to the congregation, for the devil is with the one who is alone, and he is farther from two. Let none of you be alone with a woman, for the devil is the third of them. And whosever is pleased by his good deeds and displeased by his bad deeds, he is a believer."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13528","(13522) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Aṭāʾ informed us: Sulaymān al-Taymī — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Sulaymān al-Taymī, who said: I heard Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī narrating from Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I have not left after me a trial more harmful to men than women."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it through various routes from al-Taymī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5006. Muslim 2740]"
"13529","(13523) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAttāb al-ʿAbdī in Baghdād narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Rūḥ al-Madāʾinī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Shuʿbah — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAbdillāh informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Bundār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Abū Maslamah, who said: I heard Abā Naḍrah narrating from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Indeed, the world is green and sweet, and Allah will place you as successors therein to see how you act. So beware of the world and beware of women, for the first trial of the Children of Israel was in women."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ghundar. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bundār Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2742]"
"13530","(13524) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustūyah narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us: Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd narrated to us: ʿUqayl ibn Khālid narrated to me: Ibn Shihāb informed me, from Nabhān, the freedman of Umm Salamah, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered while I and Maymūnah were sitting. He sat, and Ibn Umm Maktūm, the blind man, sought permission. He said: ""Veil yourselves from him."" We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is he not blind and cannot see us?"" He said: ""But you cannot see him."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13531","(13525) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ informed us, Ibn al-Mubārak reported from Yūnus, from al-Zuhrī, that Nabhān, the freed slave of Umm Salamah, narrated to me, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and Maymūnah was with him when Ibn Umm Maktūm came in, and that was after we had been commanded to observe hijāb. He entered upon us, so the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Observe hijāb from him.' We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, is he not blind? He cannot see us nor recognize us.' The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Are you two blind? Do you not see him?'"" [Weak]"
"13532","(13526) As for the ḥadīth which Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar reported from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""By Allah, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) standing at the door of my chamber while the Abyssinians were playing with spears in the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was screening me with his garment so that I could watch their play between his ear and his shoulder. Then he would stand up for my sake until I was the one who departed. So estimate the measure of a young girl of recent years who is eager for amusement."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through various chains from Maʿmar, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Authentic. Bukhārī 5236, Muslim 892]"
"13533","(13527) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk al-Bazzāz narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth reported from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) entered upon her while she had two young slave girls with her during the days of Minā, singing and playing the duff and beating it, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was wrapped in his garment. Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) rebuked them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) uncovered his face and said: 'Leave them, O Abū Bakr, for these are the days of ʿĪd.' And those were the days of Minā while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was in Madīnah. ʿĀʾishah said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) screening me with his garment while I watched the Abyssinians playing in the mosque, and I was a young girl.' Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr with an additional phrase at the end and another omission. In this additional phrase, his words 'and I was a young girl' serve as evidence that she had not yet reached puberty. This also indicates the same. [Authentic. Muslim 892]"
"13534","(13528) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar reported from Thābit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) arrived in Madīnah, the Abyssinians played with their spears out of joy at his arrival."" If this incident and what ʿĀʾishah narrated are one and the same, then it contains evidence that she had not yet reached puberty at that time, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) consummated marriage with her when he arrived in Madīnah and she was nine years old. It is also possible that this occurred before the command of hijāb was revealed. [Authentic]"
"13535","(13529) In what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr reported from Ibn Isḥāq, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sahl narrated to me, from ʿĿʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she was in the fortress of Banī Ḥārithah on the day of the Khandaq, and Umm Saʿd ibn Muʿādh was with her in the fortress, and that was before the command of hijāb was revealed to them. [Weak] And from Ibn Isḥāq, Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qusayṭ narrated to me in the story of the revelation of the repentance of Abū Lubābah in the story of Banī Qurayẓah. Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Shall I not give him glad tidings of that, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Yes, if you wish."" She said: ""So I stood at the door of my chamber and said — and that was before the command of hijāb was revealed to us — 'O Abū Lubābah, receive glad tidings, for Allah has accepted your repentance.'"" The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The expedition of Banī Qurayẓah took place immediately after the Khandaq in the fifth year, so the revelation of the command of hijāb came after that, and Allah knows best."""
"13536","(13530) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Marwazī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid reported from his father, from Yazīd al-Naḥwī, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said regarding the verse: ""And tell the believing women to lower their gaze"" (al-Nūr 24:31) — this was abrogated and an exception was made from it: ""And the women past childbearing who do not expect marriage"" (al-Nūr 24:60). [Hasan due to supporting narrations]"
"13537","(13531) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ reported from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding His Exalted words: ""And the women past childbearing who do not expect marriage"" (al-Nūr 24:60) — she is the woman upon whom there is no sin if she sits in her house wearing a dirʿ (long shirt) and a khimār (head covering), and removes from herself the jilbāb (outer garment), so long as she does not display adornment in a manner that Allah disapproves of. And that is His words: ""There is no blame upon them that they put aside their outer garments, not displaying adornment"" (al-Nūr 24:60). Then He said: ""And that they refrain [from removing them] is better for them"" (al-Nūr 24:60)."
"13538","(13532) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Miṣrī informed us, Mālik ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us — both said: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim reported from al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrīt, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he used to recite: ""that they put aside [min] their garments"" — he said: meaning the jilbāb (outer garments). [Hasan]"
"13539","(13533) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Abū al-Azhar narrated to us, Rūḥ narrated to us, Shuʿbah reported — he said: I heard al-Ḥakam say: I heard Abū Wāʾil say: I heard ʿAbd Allāh, that is Ibn Masʿūd, say regarding the words: ""There is no blame upon them that they put aside their garments"" (al-Nūr 24:60) — he said: meaning the jilbāb (outer garments). And it has been narrated to us from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: ""She puts aside the jilbāb."" And from Mujāhid regarding the words: ""And that they refrain is better for them"" (al-Nūr 24:60) — he says: that they wear their jilbāb is better for them. [Authentic]"
"13540","(13534) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us — both said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah reported from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, who said: ""We used to enter upon Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn after she had arranged the jilbāb thus and veiled herself with it, and we would say to her: 'May Allah have mercy on you! Allah the Exalted said: ""And the women past childbearing who do not expect marriage — there is no blame upon them that they put aside their garments, not displaying adornment"" (al-Nūr 24:60)' — meaning the jilbāb."" She would then say to us: ""What comes after that?"" So we would say: ""And that they refrain is better for them"" (al-Nūr 24:60). She would say: ""That means affirming the wearing of the jilbāb."" [Hasan]"
"13541","(13535) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Naḍr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from his father, from Sahl ibn Saʿd, who said: ""We used to rejoice on the day of Jumuʿah."" I said, ""Why?"" He said, ""We had an old woman who would send to a place called Buḍāʿah. She would take the roots of the chard (silq), throw them into a pot, and crush some barley grains into it. When we had prayed, we would go to her, greet her, and she would serve it to us. We used to rejoice on the day of Jumuʿah because of that, and we would neither take our siesta nor eat our midday meal until after Jumuʿah."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī. And it has been narrated to us from Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that they used to visit Umm Ayman after the passing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she had been the nursemaid of the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 6248. Muslim 869]"
"13542","(13536) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym and Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr al-Jārūdī said, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn ʢĀṣim al-Kilābī narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated from Thābit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to visit Umm Ayman, and I went with him. She brought him a drink. He was either fasting or did not want it, so he returned it. She began to scold the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. After the passing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, 'Let us go to visit Umm Ayman.' When we reached her, she wept. They said to her, 'What makes you weep? What is with Allah is better for His Messenger ﷺ.' She said, 'By Allah, I do not weep because I do not know that what is with Allah is better for His Messenger ﷺ, but I weep because the revelation from the heavens has ceased.' This moved them both to tears, and they began to weep."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from ʿAmr ibn ʢĀṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2454]"
"13543","(13537) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding the words of Allah, exalted be His praise: {And they should not display their adornment except that which appears thereof} — the apparent adornment being the face, the kohl of the eye, the henna of the hand, and the ring. This is what she displays in her home for whoever enters upon her. Then He said: {And they should not display their adornment except to their husbands, or their fathers, or the fathers of their husbands, or their sons, or the sons of their husbands, or their brothers, or the sons of their brothers, or the sons of their sisters, or their women, or those whom their right hands possess, or the followers among men who have no desire} — the adornment which she displays to these people being her earrings, her necklaces, and her bracelets. As for her anklets, her armlets, her neck area, and her hair, she does not display these except to her husband.

And it has been narrated to us from Mujāhid that he said it refers to the two earrings, the neck area, the forearms, and the feet.

(Q) And it is preferable that she not display any of her hidden adornment to anyone other her husband, except that which is apparent in her daily work. If something appears to her maḥram relatives above her navel and below her knee, it has been said there is no harm in that, based on what has been narrated to us in the Book of Prayer from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When one of you marries off his slave, his female servant, or his hired worker, let him not look at her ʿawrah."" And in another narration: ""Let him not look at what is between the navel and the knee."" When the second narration is taken together with the first, they indicate that the meaning of the hadith is the prohibition of the master from looking at her ʿawrah when he marries her off, which is the area between the navel and the knee. The master, when he marries her off, is in the position of her maḥram relatives, except that al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated it from Sawwār Abī Ḥamzah from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet ﷺ: ""When one of you marries off his slave, his female servant, or his hired worker, the female servant should not look at any part of his ʿawrah, for what is between the navel and the knee is ʿawrah."" And all the other chains of narration point to this. This does not contradict what the first narration indicates.

The correct position is that she does not display anything to her master after he has married her off, nor does a free woman display anything to her maḥram relatives, except that which is apparent during the course of her work. And with Allah lies success.

As for the husband, he may look at her ʿawrah, and she may look at his ʿawrah, apart from the private part (farj), concerning which there is a difference of opinion. Similarly, the master with his female slave, if she is permissible for him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13544","(13538) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firyābī narrated — Sufyān mentioned from Bahz ibn Ḥakīm, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what of our ʿawrāt — what of them may we approach and what of them should we avoid?"" He said, ""Guard your ʿawrah except from your wife or what your right hand possesses."" He said, ""I said, 'What if we are among one another?' He said, 'If you can ensure that no one sees it, then let no one see it.' I said, 'What if one of us is alone?' He said, 'Allah has more right that one should be shy before Him than before people.'"" [Ḥasan]"
"13545","(13539) As for the private part (farj), Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Mūsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd, from a freed slave woman of ʢĀʾishah, from ʢĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""I never saw the private part (farj) of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at all."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13546","(13540) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Muḥammad ibn ʢAbd al-Ṣamad al-Dimashqī narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʢAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Let none of you look at the private part (farj) of his wife or the private part of his female slave when he has intercourse with her, for that causes blindness.""

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ said: It is likely that there is some unknown narrator or some weak narrator between Baqiyyah and Ibn Jurayj in this hadith, except that Hishām ibn Khālid said, from Baqiyyah, ""Ibn Jurayj narrated to me."" [Mawḍūʿ]"
"13547","(13541) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us, Ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Hishām ibn Khālid narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to me, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, and he mentioned it with the same meaning. [Mawḍūʿ]"
"13548","(13542) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Naḍrawī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Hishām ibn al-Ghāz ibn Rabīʿah al-Jurashī narrated from ʿUbādah ibn Nusayy al-Kindī, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, may Allah be pleased with him: ""To proceed: It has reached me that some Muslim women are entering the public baths along with women of the People of the Book. Prevent this and block it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13549","(13543) Abū Naṣr informed us, Abū Manṣūr informed us, Aḥmad narrated to us, Saʿīd narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from Hishām ibn al-Ghāz, from ʿUbādah ibn Nusayy, from his father, from al-Ḥārith ibn Qays, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abū ʿUbaydah, may Allah be pleased with him: ""To proceed: It has reached me that some Muslim women are entering the public baths along with women of the polytheists. Forbid this in your jurisdiction, for it is not permissible for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day that anyone look at her ʿawrah except the people of her own religion (millah)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13550","(13544) He said, and Saʿīd narrated to us, Jarīr narrated from Layth, from Mujāhid, who said: ""A Muslim woman should not remove her headcover (khimār) in the presence of a polytheist woman, nor should she kiss her, because Allah, the Most High, says: {or their women} — and she is not of their women."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13551","(13545) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasa narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Isa narrated to us; Abu Jami' — Salim ibn Dinar narrated from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ﷺ) came to Fatimah with a slave whom he had given to her as a gift. Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) had a garment which, when she covered her head with it, it did not reach her feet, and when she covered her feet with it, it did not reach her head. When the Prophet (ﷺ) saw her predicament, he said: ""There is no hardship upon you; it is only your father and your slave."" Salam ibn Abi al-Sahba' followed him in narrating from Thabit. [Hasan]"
"13552","(13546) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us; Abu Mu'awiyah — Muhammad ibn Khazim al-Darir narrated from 'Amr ibn Maymun ibn Mihran, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I sought permission to enter upon her, and she said: 'Who is this?' I said: 'Sulayman.' She said: 'How much remains of your mukataba (contract of manumission)?' I said: 'Ten awqiyyah.' She said: 'Then enter, for you are still a slave so long as a dirham remains upon you.'""

(T) And it has been narrated to us from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad that he said: ""If any of the Mothers of the Believers had a mukatab (slave under a manumission contract), she would lower the veil for him so long as a dirham remained upon him; but when he had fulfilled it, she would draw the veil between herself and him."" Al-Hasan, al-Sha'bi, Tawus, and Mujahid disliked that a slave should look at the hair of his mistress, as though they counted the hair among the adornments that she should not display to her slave, just as Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) counted it among the adornments that she should not display to her mahrams, as we have narrated from him. And it has been narrated to us from Ibrahim al-Sa'igh, who said: ""I said to Nafi': 'Does her slave take her out?' He said: 'No, because they consider the slave insignificant, and the apparent meaning of the Book is more deserving of being followed, along with what is in it of the Sunnah.'"" [Sahih]"
"13553","(13547) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; Abu al-Hasan — Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-'Anazi narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he said: ""He is the man who follows the people while being deficient in his intellect, having no concern for women and no desire for them."" [Hasan li-ghayrihi]"
"13554","(13548) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Bakr al-Qattan narrated to us; Abu al-Azhar narrated to us; Rawh narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, regarding His statement {ghayri uli al-irbati min al-rijal} (""other than those having no physical need of women""), he said: ""He who has no need — that is, no desire — for women."" [Hasan]"
"13555","(13549) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us; Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us; Warqa' narrated from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, regarding His Most High's statement {aw al-tabi'ina ghayri uli al-irbati min al-rijal} (""or those who have no physical need of women""), he said: ""He is one whom nothing concerns except his belly, and who poses no threat to women.""

(T) And it has been narrated to us from Tawus that he said: ""He is the fool who has no need — that is, no desire — for women."" And from al-Hasan, who said: ""He is one who has no intellect, who does not desire women, and whom women do not desire."" [Sahih li-ghayrihi]"
"13556","(13550) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us; Adam narrated to us; Warqa' narrated from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: ""They are those who, due to their young age, do not know what women are."" [Mawdu']"
"13557","(13551) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abd Allah ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah and Muhammad ibn Ishaq both said: Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razaq informed us; Ma'mar narrated from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""There used to enter upon the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) a mukhannath (effeminate man), and they considered him among those having no physical need. One day the Prophet (ﷺ) entered while he was with one of his wives, describing a woman, saying: 'When she comes forward, she comes forward with four, and when she turns her back, she turns her back with eight.' The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Do I not see that this one knows what is here! Let him not enter upon you.' So they veiled themselves from him."" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abd ibn Humayd from 'Abd al-Razaq. Thus the Prophet (ﷺ) inferred from what the mukhannath said that he was among those having physical need, and so he veiled him. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 5887. Muslim 2180]"
"13558","(13552) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-'Anbari informed us; my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us; Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Isma'ili narrated to us; 'Isa ibn Hammad ibn Zughbah informed us; al-Layth narrated from al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for cupping, and the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered Abu Taybah to cup her. He (Jabir) said: ""I think he said: 'He was her foster-brother, or a boy who had not yet reached puberty.'"" [Sahih. Muslim 2206]"
"13559","(13553) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; Abu al-Hasan — Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-'Anazi narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding His Most High's statement {liyastadhinkum alladhina malakat aymanukum wa-alladhina lam yablughu al-hulum minkum thalatha marratin min qibli salati al-fajr wa-hina tada'una thiyabakum min al-zahirah wa-min ba'di salati al-'isha'i thalatha 'awratun lakum} (""Let those whom your right hands possess and those who have not yet reached puberty ask your permission at three times: before the dawn prayer, when you put aside your clothing at midday, and after the night prayer — three times of privacy for you""), he said: ""When a man is alone with his family after the night prayer, no servant or boy should enter upon him without permission until he has prayed the morning prayer. And when he is alone with his family at midday, it is the same. Then they were permitted to enter without permission in the times between these, and that is His Most High's statement {laysa 'alaykum wa-la 'alayhim junahun ba'dahunna} (""There is no blame upon you or upon them after these""). As for one who has reached puberty, he may not enter upon a man and his family without permission at any time, and that is His statement {wa-idha balagha al-atfalkum al-hulum falyastadhinu kama istadhina alladhina min qablihim} (""And when among you the children reach puberty, let them ask permission, as those before them asked permission')."" [Da'if]"
"13560","(13554) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu Mansur al-Nadrawi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ata', who said: ""I said to Ibn 'Abbas: 'I have two sisters under my care whom I maintain and provide for; should I seek permission from them (when entering)?' He said: 'Yes.' I pressed him, saying: 'This is difficult for me.' He said: 'Allah the Most High says: {Ya ayyuha alladhina amanu liyastadhinkum alladhina malakat aymanukum wa-alladhina lam yablughu al-hulum minkum thalatha marratin min qibli salati al-fajr wa-hina tada'una thiyabakum min al-zahirah wa-min ba'di salati al-'isha'i thalatha 'awratun lakum} (""O you who believe, let those whom your right hands possess and those who have not yet reached puberty ask your permission at three times..."") to the end of the verse.' Ibn 'Abbas said: 'These were not commanded to seek permission except in these three times of privacy.' Then he recited: {Wa-idha balagha al-atfalkum al-hulum falyastadhinu kama istadhina alladhina min qablihim} (""And when among you the children reach puberty, let them ask permission, as those before them asked permission')."" [Al-Adab al-Mufrad 1063]"
"13561","(13555) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad narrated to us; Saʿīd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd, who heard Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: ""There is a verse which most people do not believe in (i.e., do not act upon) — the verse of seeking permission (Āyat al-Idhān). I would command this young servant girl of mine, who is short and stands at his head, to seek permission before entering upon me."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13562","(13556) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from al-Zuhrī, who said: I heard Hudhayl al-Aʿmā say: I heard Ibn Masʿūd say: ""Upon you is to seek permission even upon your mothers."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13563","(13557) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Muslim ibn Nuḍayr, that Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked: ""Does a man seek permission upon his mother?"" He said: ""Yes. If he does not do so, he may see from her that which he would dislike."" [Ḥasan]"
"13564","(13558) A mursal ḥadīth has been narrated regarding this. Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated from Mālik, from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): ""Shall I seek permission upon my mother, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Yes."" The man said: ""I live with her in the house."" He said: ""Then seek permission upon her."" The man said: ""I am her servant (i.e., I attend to her needs)."" He said: ""Would you like to see her naked?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then seek permission upon her."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13565","(13559) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr, from ʿIkrimah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, that two men asked him about seeking permission during the three times of privacy which Allah has commanded in the Qurʾān. Ibn ʿAbbās said to them: ""Indeed, Allah is the One who covers (al-Sattīr), and He loves covering (al-satr). The people had no curtains on their doors nor screens in their homes, so it might happen that a man's servant, his child, or the orphan under his care would suddenly come upon him while he was with his wife. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded them to seek permission during those times of privacy which Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, specified. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, later provided them with curtains and expanded their provision. They took curtains and made screens, and the people thought that these were sufficient for them from the seeking of permission which He had commanded."" The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd and ʿAṭāʾ weakens this narration. And Allah knows best."" [Ḥasan]"
"13566","(13560) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qays greeted ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) three times, but he was not given permission, so he turned back. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) sent someone after him and said: ""Why did you turn back?"" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'When one of you greets three times and is not answered, let him return.'"" ʿUmar said: ""You must bring me proof for what you say, or I will do such-and-such to you"" — and he threatened him. Abū Mūsā came, his colour changed, while I was sitting in a circle. We said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I greeted ʿUmar, and he told me such-and-such. Has any of you heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?"" They said: ""Yes, we have all heard it."" So they sent a man with him from among them until he came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him of that. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from al-Jurayrī, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Abū Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2062. Muslim 3153]"
"13567","(13561) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Hushaym informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Naṣr narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me; Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar narrated to us; Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us; Abū Khaythamah and ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid both said: Hushaym narrated to us; Abū al-Zubayr informed us from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Beware! Let no man spend the night in the presence of a woman unless he is her husband or a maḥram (unmarriageable relative)."" Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā added in his narration: ""Three times,"" and he said: ""from Abū al-Zubayr."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Abū Khaythamah. And in Yaḥyā's narration it says: ""in the presence of a thayyib (previously married woman)"" — he did not say ""three times."" Thus it is in my copy of Muslim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2171]"
"13568","(13562) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Wakīʿ narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Let no woman travel for three days or more except with her father, her son, her brother, her husband, or a maḥram."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb and others from Wakīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 827]"
"13569","(13563) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūq al-Shaybānī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Rajāʾ ibn al-Sindī narrated to us; Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us; Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Manṣūr, from Abū Wāʾil, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade a woman to lie in close bodily contact with another woman under a single garment, lest she then describe her to her husband as though he were looking at her. And He forbade that when we are three, two should converse privately to the exclusion of the third, lest it grieve him, until he mingles with other people. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hannād ibn al-Sarī, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through two other chains from Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5240. Muslim 2184]"
"13570","(13564) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us; al-Ḍaḥḥāk narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Saʿīd, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Let no man look at the nakedness of another man, and let no woman look at the nakedness of another woman. And let no man lie in close bodily contact with another man under a single garment, and let no woman lie in close bodily contact with another woman under a single garment."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 338]"
"13571","(13565) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us; Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated from al-Jurayrī, from Abū Naḍrah, from a man of al-Ṭufāwah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Let no man lie with a man, nor a woman with a woman, except in the case of a child or a parent."" He said: ""He mentioned the third, but I have forgotten it."" [Weak]"
"13572","(13566) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Salmān informed me, from ʿAmr, the mawlā of al-Muṭṭalib, from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""It has reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Allah has cursed the one who looks and the one who is looked at.'"" This is a mursal hadith. [Weak]"
"13573","(13567) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Shammās narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated from al-Waḍīn, from some of the shaykhs, who said: ""He used to dislike one's gaze being directed toward a handsome-faced beardless youth."" This has also been narrated from Baqiyyah from al-Wāziʿ ibn Nāfiʿ, and he is weak, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), marfūʿ, with part of its meaning. The well-known narration from Baqiyyah is what we have mentioned. Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmara al-Ṭaḥḥān also narrated a fabricated hadith in its meaning from al-Thawrī, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), marfūʿ. In what we have mentioned of the verse there is sufficiency from other than it, and its trial is manifest and does not need a report to explain it. And with Allah, the Most High, lies success. [Weak]"
"13574","(13568) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Qatādah, who said: ""I asked Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him): 'Was handshaking practiced among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?' He said: 'Yes.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim, from Hammām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 6263]"
"13575","(13569) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr al-Ḍabbī narrated to us; Hushaym ibn Bashīr narrated from Abū Balj; Zayd ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ narrated to me, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When two Muslims meet and shake hands, and praise Allah and seek His forgiveness, they are both forgiven."" [Weak]"
"13576","(13570) Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn, from Hushaym, except that he said: ""From Zayd, Abū al-Ḥakam al-ʿAnazī — Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn narrated to us,"" and he mentioned it. [Weak]"
"13577","(13571) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū Khālid and Ibn Numayr narrated from al-Ajlaj, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There are no two Muslims who meet and shake hands except that they are both forgiven before they part."" [Weak]"
"13578","(13572) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ḥammād informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn, meaning Khālid ibn Dhakwān, narrated from Ayyūb ibn Bishr ibn Kaʿb al-ʿAdawī, from a man of ʿAnazah, that he said to Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) when he was expelled from Syria: ""I wish to ask you about a hadith from the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."" He said: ""Then I shall inform you of it, unless it be a secret."" I said: ""It is not a secret. Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to shake your hands when you met him?"" He said: ""I never met him except that he shook my hand. One day he sent for me while I was not at home. When I came, I was informed that he had sent for me. So I went to him while he was on his bed, and he embraced me, and that was the most generous and most generous."" [Weak]"
"13579","(13573) As for the hadith which Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ informed us of — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ḥanẓalah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated: I heard Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, should some of us bow to one another when we meet?"" He said: ""No."" It was said: ""Should some of us embrace one another?"" He said: ""No."" It was said: ""Should some of us shake hands with one another?"" He said: ""Yes."" This is narrated uniquely by Ḥanẓalah al-Sadūsī, and he had become confused; Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān abandoned his narrations due to his confusion. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"13580","(13574) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Shabābah ibn Sawwār narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ismāʿīl al-Asadī narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Shaʿbī narrating from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) that he was at a watering-place of his when it reached him that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) had set out for Iraq. He caught up with him and mentioned the hadith regarding his command to return, but he refused to return. So Ibn ʿUmar embraced him and wept, and said: ""I entrust you to Allah as one who will be killed."" This is how Shabābah narrated it.

(T) Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Sālim, from his father, from al-Shaʿbī. [Ḥasan]"
"13581","(13575) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sarrāj informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Ghālib al-Tammār, who said: Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn used to disapprove of handshaking. I mentioned that to al-Shaʿbī, and he said: The Companions of Muḥammad (peace be upon him) used to shake hands when they met, and when they returned from a journey, they would embrace one another. [Ḥasan]"
"13582","(13576) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥassān al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) kissed al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both), while al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis al-Tamīmī was sitting with him. Al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have ten children and I have never kissed any of them."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) looked at him and said: ""Whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from al-Razzāq, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5997. Muslim 2318]"
"13583","(13577) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated, saying: Sufyān mentioned from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and said: ""You kiss children, but we do not kiss them."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""I cannot help you if Allah has removed mercy from your heart.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firyābī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5998. Muslim 2317]"
"13584","(13578) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥassān al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Maysarah ibn Ḥabīb, from al-Minhāl ibn ʿAmr, from ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah, from Umm al-Muʾminīn ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I have not seen anyone whose speech and discourse more closely resembled that of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) than Fāṭimah. When she would enter upon him, he would welcome her, rise for her, take her hand, kiss her, and seat her in his place. And when he would enter upon her, she would welcome him, rise, take his hand, and kiss him."" And he mentioned the hadith. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13585","(13579) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ḥammād informed us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said, in the story of al-Ifk: Then the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Rejoice, O ʿĀʾishah, for Allah has revealed your innocence."" And he recited the Qurʾān to her. Then my parents said: ""Rise and kiss the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."" I said: ""I praise Allah, not you two."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13586","(13580) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ in Kūfah both informed us, saying: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān, from al-Ajlaj, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: When Jaʿfar (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived from Abyssinia, the Prophet (peace be upon him) embraced him and kissed between his eyes, and said: ""I do not know which makes me happier — the conquest of Khaybar or the arrival of Jaʿfar."" This is a mursal narration. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13587","(13581) Abū Saʿd al-Zāhid narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Bundār al-Ṣūfī informed us; ʿAbdān al-Jawālīqī narrated to us; Khalīfah ibn Khayyāṭ narrated to us; Ziyād ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us; Mujālid ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, who said: When Jaʿfar arrived from Abyssinia, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) went out to meet him and kissed him. The preserved narration is the first one, as mursal. [Munkar]"
"13588","(13582) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Sālim narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Yūsuf narrated from his father, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ, who said: I entered with Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) when he first arrived in Madīnah, and there was his daughter ʿĀʾishah, lying down, afflicted with fever. Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) came to her and said: ""How are you, my dear daughter?"" and he kissed her cheek. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13589","(13583) Abū ʿAlī informed us; Abū Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; al-Muʿtamir narrated from Iyās ibn Daghfal, who said: I saw Abū Naḍrah kissing the cheek of al-Ḥasan, meaning al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī (may Allah have mercy upon him). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13590","(13584) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād narrated that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā narrated to him that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated to him, and he mentioned a story, saying: We drew near to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and kissed his hand. [Very weak]"
"13591","(13585) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: al-Thawrī narrated from Ziyād ibn Fayyāḍ from Tamīm ibn Salamah, who said: When ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived in Shām, Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to meet him and kissed his hand. Then they went aside and wept. Tamīm used to say: Kissing the hand is a Sunnah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13592","(13586) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn narrated to us: Khālid narrated from Ḥuṣayn from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā from Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr, a man from the Anṣār (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: While he was speaking to the people — and there was some humour in him, making them laugh — the Prophet (ﷺ) poked him in his side with a stick. He said: ""Let me retaliate."" He said: ""Retaliate."" He said: ""You have a shirt on, and I have no shirt on."" So the Prophet (ﷺ) raised his shirt, and he embraced him and began kissing his flank, saying: ""This is what I wanted, O Messenger of Allah.""

His saying ""Let me retaliate"" means: ""Grant me retribution from yourself."" And his saying ""Retaliate"" means: ""Seek retribution."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13593","(13587) And Abū ʿAlī informed us: Abū Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Maṭar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿnaq said: Umm Abān bint al-Wāziʿ ibn Zāriʿ narrated to me from her grandfather Zāriʿ, who was among the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays. He said: We hastened down from our mounts to kiss the hand and foot of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). al-Mundhir al-Ashajj waited until he came to his saddlebag, put on his two garments, and then came to the Prophet (ﷺ). The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: ""You possess two qualities that Allah loves: forbearance (al-ḥilm) and deliberation (al-anāh)."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, have I acquired them by my own effort, or has Allah created me with them?"" He said: ""Rather, Allah has created you with them."" He said: ""All praise is due to Allah, who has created me with two qualities that Allah and His Messenger love."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13594","(13588) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn Sinān narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Raddād, a shaykh from the people of Madīnah, narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār narrated from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Travel, for you will be healthy and you will gain wealth."" [Munkar]"
"13595","(13589) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Khālid, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Dāmaghānī in Naysābūr, narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us: Bisṭām ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from Abī Ḥāzim from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Travel, for you will be healthy and you will gain wealth.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: This is an indication, not an obligation, that one should travel seeking health and provision. It is possible that the command regarding marriage is an obligation, and in all that which is obligatory from Allah is guidance. Some of the scholars said: The entire matter is one of permissibility, and the indication is toward guidance, until evidence is found that the command constitutes an obligation, and whatever Allah has forbidden is prohibited until evidence is found that the prohibition is not one of prohibition. [Ḍaʿīf Jiddan]"
"13596","(13590) The one who holds this view cited as evidence what Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ibn ʿAjlān from his father from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Leave me as I have left you, for indeed those who came before you were destroyed by their excessive questioning and their disagreement with their prophets. So whatever I command you of a matter, do of it what you are able, and whatever I forbid you from, refrain from it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 7288. Muslim 1337]"
"13597","(13591) He said: And al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Abī al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ﷺ) with a similar meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13598","(13592) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Abī al-Zinād, and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13599","(13593) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash from Abī Ṣāliḥ from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Leave me as I have left you, for indeed those who came before you were destroyed by their questioning and their disagreement with their prophets. So when I command you with something, take from it what you are able, and when I forbid you from something, refrain from it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr from his father.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: It is possible that the command carries the meaning of prohibition, in which case both would be binding, unless there is evidence that they are not binding. His saying (ﷺ) ""do of it what you are able"" means that the obligation of fulfilling the command is according to one's ability, since people are only obligated according to their capacity. It is upon the people of knowledge to seek the evidences in order to distinguish between what is obligatory, what is permissible, and what is guidance that is not obligatory, in both command and prohibition. [Ṣaḥīḥ — previously cited]"
"13600","(13594) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Sharqī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ and al-Farrāʾ — meaning ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad — and Qaṭn said: Ḥafṣ narrated to me: Ibrāhīm — meaning Ibn Ṭahmān — narrated from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd from al-Ḥasan that he said regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""Do not prevent them from marrying their (former) husbands"" (the verse): Maʿqil ibn Yasār al-Muzanī (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that this verse was revealed concerning him. He said: I had married my sister to a man, and he divorced her. When her waiting period elapsed, he came to propose to her again. I said to him: ""I married you to her, I provided you with her bedding, and I honoured you, yet you divorced her, and now you come to propose to her again? By Allah, she will never return to you."" He said: ""There is no harm in it."" The man was not a bad person, and the woman wished to return to him. So Allah revealed this verse. I said: ""Now I will do it, O Messenger of Allah."" So I married her to him.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4529]"
"13601","(13595) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb al-ʿAdl informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Saʿīd —
(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bisṭāmī al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and Bundār said: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā informed us; Saʿīd, from Qatādah; al-Ḥasan informed us that Maʿqil ibn Yasār — may Allah be pleased with him — had a sister who was married to a man. He divorced her and then left her alone until her ʿiddah had expired. Then he came near to propose to her again, but Maʿqil was filled with indignation over that. He said: ""He had the option of taking her back, yet he left her. Now he comes to propose to her again!"" So he stood between him and her. Then Allah, the Most High, revealed: **""And when you have divorced women and they have reached their term, do not prevent them from marrying their husbands""** (the verse). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called him and recited the verse to him. He then abandoned his indignation and submitted to the command of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. al-Kalbī surmised that his sister's name was Jamīl bint Yasār. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13602","(13596) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; ʿAbbād ibn Rāshid said: I heard al-Ḥasan say: Maʿqil ibn Yasār al-Muzanī informed me —
(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Isḥāq al-Baghawī in Baghdād informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī informed us; ʿAbbād ibn Rāshid, from al-Ḥasan; Maʿqil ibn Yasār — may Allah be pleased with him — informed me, saying: I had a sister who was proposed to, and I used to prevent her from people. Then a paternal cousin of mine came and proposed to her, so I married her to him. They remained together as long as Allah willed. Then he divorced her with a revocable divorce, but he left her until her ʿiddah expired. When she was proposed to again, he came to me along with the other suitors and proposed to her. I said: ""I prevented people from her and gave you preference over them, yet you divorced her with a revocable divorce and left her until her ʿiddah expired. Now that she is being proposed to, you come to me along with the other suitors — I will never marry her to you!"" Then Allah, the Most High, revealed: **""And when you have divorced women and they have reached their term, do not prevent them from marrying their husbands""** (the verse). So I expiated for my oath and married her to him. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-ʿAqadī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13603","(13597) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā informed me; Abū ʿĀmir mentioned it with the same meaning in an abbreviated form, except that he said: I said, ""By Allah, I will never marry her to you!"" He said: ""Concerning me this verse was revealed,"" and then he mentioned the ḥadīth. al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Saʿīd from Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī.
(Sh) al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: ""This is the clearest thing in the Qurʾān indicating that the walī has a right alongside the woman regarding her own person, and that it is incumbent upon the walī not to prevent her if she consents to be married in accordance with what is customary."" He said: ""And the Sunnah came with the same meaning as the import of the Book of Allah, the Most High."""
"13604","(13598) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: ""A woman is not to be married without the command of her walī. If she marries, her marriage is invalid"" — he repeated it three times — ""If he has consummated the marriage, then she is entitled to the mahr of her like, on account of what he has had of her. If they dispute, then the sulṭān is the walī of the one who has no walī."""
"13605","(13599) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Fāris informed us; Abū Masʿūd Aḥmad ibn al-Furāt informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, that Sulaymān ibn Mūsā informed him —
(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Sulaymān ibn Mūsā informed me that Ibn Shihāb informed him that ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed him that ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — informed him that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Any woman who is married without the permission of her walī — her marriage is invalid, her marriage is invalid, her marriage is invalid. She is entitled to her mahr on account of what he has had of her. If they dispute, then the sulṭān is the walī of the one who has no walī."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ḥajjāj. In the narration of ʿAbd al-Razzāq it is: ""without the permission of her mawālī — her marriage is invalid, her marriage is invalid, her marriage is invalid, and she is entitled to the mahr on account of what he has had of her."" The remainder is the same. Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated it from ʿAbd al-Razzāq: ""Any woman who is married without the permission of her walī."" And likewise al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from Muslim ibn Khālid and ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, from Ibn Jurayj."
"13606","(13600) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: Abū ʿUbayd said: ""The mawlā, according to most people, is specifically the paternal cousin, but it is not so. Rather, it is the walī. Every walī of a person is his mawlā — such as the father, the brother, the brother's son, the paternal uncle, the paternal uncle's son, and beyond them among all the ʿaṣabah. Among this is His statement, the Most High: **'Indeed, I fear the mawālī after me.'**"" He said: ""What makes it clear to you that the mawlā is every walī is the ḥadīth of the Prophet ﷺ: 'Any woman who is married without the permission of her mawlā — her marriage is invalid.' He intended by mawlā the walī. And Allah, the Most High, said: **'A day when no mawlā will avail another mawlā at all.'** Do you think He meant only the paternal cousin to the exclusion of the rest of his household?"""
"13607","(13601) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Rajāʾ informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muṣaffā informed us; Baqiyyah informed us; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah said: al-Zuhrī said to me: ""Makhḥūl used to come to us, and Sulaymān ibn Mūsā — and by Allah, Sulaymān ibn Mūsā is certainly the more reliable of the two men."""
"13608","(13602) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ushnānī said: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us. He said: I heard ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī say: I asked Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, ""What is the status of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā with regard to al-Zuhrī?"" He said: ""Thiqah (trustworthy)."""
"13609","(13603) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī informed us; Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Rāzī said: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say — when it was mentioned to him that Ibn ʿUlayyah narrates the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj: ""There is no marriage except with a walī"" — Ibn Jurayj said: ""I met al-Zuhrī and asked him about it, but he did not know it."" He praised Sulaymān ibn Mūsā. So Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal said: ""Ibn Jurayj had compiled books, and this is not in his books"" — meaning the report of Ibn ʿUlayyah from Ibn Jurayj."
"13610","(13604) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb said: I heard al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad say: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn say regarding the ḥadīth ""There is no marriage except with a walī,"" which Ibn Jurayj narrates: I said to him, ""Ibn ʿUlayyah says: Ibn Jurayj said, 'I asked al-Zuhrī about it,' and he said, 'I do not preserve it.'"" Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn said: ""No one says this except Ibn ʿUlayyah. Rather, Ibn ʿUlayyah presented the books of Ibn Jurayj to ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Rāwād, and he corrected them for him."" I said to Yaḥyā, ""I did not think that ʿAbd al-Majīd was like this."" He said: ""He was the most knowledgeable of people regarding the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj, but he did not devote himself to the pursuit of ḥadīth."""
"13611","(13605) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: I heard Abū Isḥāq al-Muzakkī say: I heard Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Hārūn say: I heard Jaʿfar al-Ṭayālisī say: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn weaken the narration of Ibn ʿUlayyah from Ibn Jurayj, for he denied knowledge of the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, and he said: ""No one other than Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated it from Ibn Jurayj, and Ibn ʿUlayyah only heard from Ibn Jurayj a hearing that was not of that [reliable] calibre; rather, he corrected his books against the books of ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz."" And Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn severely weakened the narration of Ismāʿīl from Ibn Jurayj."
"13612","(13606) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ al-Saʿdī say: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal — raḥimahu Allāh — was asked, while he was present, about the ḥadīth of al-Zuhrī concerning marriage without a guardian (walī). Rūḥ al-Karābīsī said: ""Al-Zuhrī has forgotten this,"" and he cited as evidence a ḥadīth that Ibn ʿUyaynah heard from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, then met al-Zuhrī and said: ""I do not know it."" He said: ""So I asked ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, and he said: 'He narrated it to me regarding the touching of the armpit.'""

The Shaykh — raḥimahu Allāh — said: ""That has been narrated from two other routes from al-Zuhrī, even though reliance is placed on the narration of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā."""
"13613","(13607) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Muʿallā and Ibn Abī Maryam informed us, saying: Ibn Luḥayʿah narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Rabīʿah narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhā — from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian. If there is no guardian and they dispute, then the sulṭān (ruler) is the guardian of the one who has no guardian.""

Al-Qaʿnabī narrated it from Ibn Luḥayʿah with the wording of the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā."
"13614","(13608) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; al-Aswad ibn ʀāmir informed us; Ibn al-Mubārak informed us, from al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhā — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian, and the sulṭān is the guardian of the one who has no guardian."""
"13615","(13609) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Abū Kurayb narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhā — and from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhumā — who both said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian.""

In the ḥadīth of al-Zuhrī: ""And the sulṭān is the guardian of the one who has no guardian.""

As for what was narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn that he rejected the ḥadīth: ""There is no marriage without a guardian"" — he did not reject the narration of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā; rather, he rejected…"
"13616","(13610) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in the Tārīkh of Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: I heard al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī say: It was said to Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn regarding the ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhā — ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" Yaḥyā said: ""Nothing is authentic in this regard except the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā. As for the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Saʿd, they differ concerning it. And al-Khayyāṭ — meaning Ḥammād al-Khayyāṭ — and Ibn Mahdī narrated it; some of them raised it [to the Prophet] and some of them did not raise it."" He said: ""And I heard Yaḥyā say: Mandal narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhā — that the Prophet ﷺ said: 'There is no marriage without a guardian.'"" Yaḥyā said: ""And this ḥadīth is nothing."" Thus, Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn only rejected what he clarified in the narration of al-Dūrī from him, and he made an exception for the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, declaring it authentic. He rejected the report of Ibn ʿUlayyah from Ibn Jurayj in his narration, and likewise in the narration of Jaʿfar al-Ṭayālisī from him, as has been mentioned previously. And he declared Sulaymān ibn Mūsā reliable in the narration of al-Dārimī from him. Therefore, the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā is authentic, and the rest of the narrations from ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhā — if anything from them is established, it has a corroborating witness in his ḥadīth. And in Allah is the success."
"13617","(13611) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us in Marw; al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated to us; Isrāʾīl ibn Yūnus informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Khālid ibn Khalī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī narrated to us; Isrāʾīl informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Faqīh al-Ṭūsī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; Ṭalq ibn Ghannām narrated to us; Isrāʾīl ibn Yūnus narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhu — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" Thus narrated ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī and a group of imāms from Isrāʾīl."
"13618","(13612) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zibriqān informed us; Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Abū ʿAwwānah informed us, from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" Muʿallā said: ""Then Abū ʿAwwānah said afterwards: 'I did not hear it from Abū Isḥāq; between me and him was Isrāʾīl.'"" The Shaykh said: ""It has also been narrated from Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah, Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and others, from Abū Isḥāq, likewise as a connected (mawṣūl) narration."""
"13619","(13613) Abū Yaʿlā Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Ṣaydalānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Dallawayh al-Daqqāq narrated to us; Abū al-Azhar Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān al-Raqqī narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; Abū Isḥāq narrated to us, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" And thus narrated Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah from Abū al-Azhar, and thus narrated others besides Abū al-Azhar from ʿAmr; ʿAmr was alone in narrating it."
"13620","(13614) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī — raḥimahu Allāh — informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Marwazī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Qāsim al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥabīb al-Mufassir informed us from the original of his hearing; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣamm narrated to us; my father narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr narrated to us; Sharīk narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī — raḍiya Allāh ʿanhu — from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."""
"13621","(13615) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd al-Sukkariyy informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us; Abū Jaʿfar ibn al-Munādī narrated to us; Shabābah narrated to us; Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Qays — meaning Ibn al-Rabīʿ — narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian (walī).""

And in the narration of Shabābah, it is from Abū Burdah, from Abī Mūsā."
"13622","(13616) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Maḥmūd ibn Muḥammad al-Marwazī informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī affirming Isrā'īl in the chain of Abū Isḥāq, saying: ""He used to narrate them completely, and whatever I missed of the ḥadīths of Sufyān from Abū Isḥāq was only because I relied on Isrā'īl for them."""
"13623","(13617) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: I heard Zakariyyā al-Sājī say: I heard al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAẓīm say: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī say: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus said: ""Isrā'īl memorizes the ḥadīths of Abū Isḥāq just as a man memorizes a sūrah from the Qur'ān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13624","(13618) He said, and Abū Aḥmad informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Sufyān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Makhlad narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī say: ""Isrā'īl is more reliable (athbat) than Shuʿbah and al-Thawrī in the narrations of Abū Isḥāq."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13625","(13619) He said, and Abū Aḥmad informed us; Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Naffāḥ narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Khālid narrated to us; Ḥajjāj narrated to us, saying: We said to Shuʿbah: ""Narrate to us the ḥadīths of Abū Isḥāq."" He said: ""Ask Isrā'īl about them, for he is more reliable (athbat) than I am regarding them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13626","(13620) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, and Abū Bakr al-Ashnānī informed us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ṭarā'ifī narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī say: I said to Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn: ""Who is more preferable to you in the narrations of Abū Isḥāq — Sharīk or Isrā'īl?"" He said: ""Sharīk is more preferable to me, for he is earlier (aqdam), and Isrā'īl is ṣadūq (truthful)."" I said: ""Is Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq more preferable to you, or Isrā'īl?"" He said: ""Both are trustworthy (thiqah)."""
"13627","(13621) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāni' narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Mundhir ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Jabalah narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī say: ""The ḥadīth of Isrā'īl is sound (ṣaḥīḥ) regarding: 'There is no marriage without a guardian (walī).'"" [Ḥasan]"
"13628","(13622) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: I heard Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā say: I heard Muḥammad ibn Hārūn al-Miskī say: I heard Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī, and he was asked about the ḥadīth of Isrā'īl from Abū Isḥāq from Abū Burdah from his father from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian (walī)."" He said: ""An addition from a trustworthy narrator is acceptable.""

And Isrā'īl ibn Yūnus is trustworthy (thiqah), and even though Shuʿbah and al-Thawrī narrated it in a more concise form (arsalāhu), that does not harm the ḥadīth. [Ḥasan]"
"13629","(13623) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr say: I heard al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān say: I heard Abū Kāmil — al-Fuḍayl ibn al-Ḥusayn — say: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us from Shuʿbah, saying: Sufyān al-Thawrī said to Abū Isḥāq: ""Did you hear Abū Burdah narrate from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: 'There is no marriage without a guardian (walī)'?"" He said: ""Yes."" Al-Ḥasan said: ""Had he said 'from his father,' he would have said yes.""

The Shaykh — may Allah have mercy on him — said: And similarly, Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān and Abū Mūsā narrated it from Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī.

Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī — may Allah have mercy on him — said in the Kitāb al-ʿIlal: ""The ḥadīth of Abū Burdah from Abī Mūsā is, to me — and Allah knows best — more authentic (aṣaḥḥ), even though Sufyān al-Thawrī and Shuʿbah do not mention Abī Mūsā in it, because it is indicated in the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah that their hearing (samāʿ) was all at one time. And those who narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Abū Burdah from Abī Mūsā heard at different times.""

He said: ""And Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated this from his father, and Yūnus met some of his father's shaykhs, so his hearing is early (qadīm al-samāʿ). And Isrā'īl narrated it, and he is the most reliable (athbat) of the companions of Abū Isḥāq after Shuʿbah and al-Thawrī."""
"13630","(13624) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us; al-Ḥārith ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from Abī Mūsā — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian (walī)."" Thus he said: ""from Abū Isḥāq.""

And similarly, Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad and Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated it from Yūnus. And thus ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus said it from his father from Abū Isḥāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13631","(13625) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Mukram ibn Aḥmad ibn Mukram al-Qāḍī in Baghdād narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Burd al-Anṭākī narrated to us: al-Haytham ibn Jamīl al-Anṭākī narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated from his father, from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsá (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13632","(13626) And it has been narrated from Yūnus, from Abū Burdah himself — Abū ʿAlī Rūḥ ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Tamīmī al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Yaʿlá al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Zubayrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manīʿ narrated to us: Abū ʿUbaydah al-Ḥaddād narrated to us, from Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsá, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" And thus did Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrate it, and likewise did Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām narrate it from Aḥmad ibn Manīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13633","(13627) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Qudāmah ibn Aʿyan narrated to us: Abū ʿUbaydah al-Ḥaddād narrated to us, from Yūnus, from Abū Burdah, and Isrāʾīl, from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsá, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" Then Abū Dāwūd (may Allah have mercy on him) said in some copies of the book *al-Sunan*: ""He is Yūnus ibn Abī Kathīr."" Thus it was reported from Abū Dāwūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13634","(13628) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in *al-Mustadrak*: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Ḍubaʿī in Baghdād narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sahl ibn ʿAskar narrated to us: Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsá, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" Ibn ʿAskar said: Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah said to me: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī came to me and asked me about this hadith, so I narrated it to him. Then ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī said: ""We are now relieved of any disagreement with Abū Isḥāq."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13635","(13629) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in *al-Mustadrak*: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: al-Ḥārith ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq (H) — and Abū Qutaybah Salm ibn al-Faḍl al-Ādamī in Makkah informed us: al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us: Asbāṭ ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsá (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" This is contrary to the narration of Abū Zakariyyā from Aḥmad ibn Salmān, as though our shaykh Abū ʿAbdillāh conflated the hadith of Ibn Qutaybah with the hadith of Asbāṭ. The hadith of Asbāṭ was thus narrated by Abū Bakr ibn Ziyād from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ, without mentioning Abū Isḥāq in it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13636","(13630) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil ibn Khalaf al-Qāḍī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd narrated to me: al-Nuʿmān ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, from Shuʿbah and Sufyān al-Thawrī, from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsá al-Ashʿarī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Shādhakūnī is alone in narrating it from al-Nuʿmān ibn ʿAbd al-Salām.

And it has been narrated from Muʾammal ibn Ismāʿīl and Bishr ibn Manṣūr from al-Thawrī as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, and from Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ from Shuʿbah as a connected narration. However, the preserved narration from both of them is unconnected (ghayr mawṣūl). Reliance is placed upon what has preceded from the narration of Isrāʾīl and those who followed him in connecting the hadith. And Allah knows best."
"13637","(13631) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Ahwāzī narrated to us: Sahl ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from al-Ḥajjāj, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""There is no marriage without a guardian."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13638","(13632) Abū Saʿīd ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdūs ibn Maḥfūẓ al-Faqīh narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Muslim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Jurmī narrated to us: Makhlad ibn Ḥusayn narrated to us, from Hishām ibn Ḥassān, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""A woman shall not marry off another woman, nor shall a woman marry herself off. Indeed, the woman who marries herself off is the fornicatress."" Al-Ḥasan said: I asked Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn about the narration of Makhlad ibn Ḥusayn from Hishām ibn Ḥassān, and he said: ""He is trustworthy (thiqah)."" Then I mentioned this hadith to him, and he said: ""Yes, there was a shaykh among us who used to raise it (as marfūʿ) from Makhlad."" The Shaykh said: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb and Muḥammad ibn Marwān al-ʿUqaylī followed him (in narrating) from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13639","(13633) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us by dictation: al-Qāḍī Abū Muḥammad Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Abū ʿAmr Aḥmad ibn al-Mubārak al-Mustamlī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Mūsá Khatt narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad al-Muḥāribī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb al-Mulāʾī narrated to us, from Hishām ibn Ḥassān, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A woman shall not marry off another woman, nor shall a woman marry herself off."" Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""We used to consider the woman who marries herself off to be a fornicatress.""

(T) And likewise did Hannād ibn al-Sarī and ʿUbayd ibn Yaʿīsh narrate it from al-Muḥāribī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13640","(13634) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Jamīl ibn al-Ḥasan al-Jahḍamī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Marwān al-ʿUqaylī narrated to us: Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A woman shall not marry off another woman, nor shall a woman marry herself off. For indeed, the fornicatress is the one who marries herself off."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13641","(13635) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, al-Awzāʿī informed us from Ibn Sīrīn from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""A woman should not arrange the marriage of another woman, nor should a woman arrange her own marriage, for indeed the fornicatress is the one who arranges her own marriage."" This is a mawqūf narration. And Ibn ʿUyaynah similarly reported it from Hishām ibn Ḥassān from Ibn Sīrīn, and ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb distinguished the musnad from the mawqūf, so it is as though he preserved it. And Allah, the Most High, is most knowing. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13642","(13636) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar Abū al-Shaykh narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Dāwūd narrated to us from his wording, Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us.
(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Mūsā narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, ʿAnbasah narrated to us — all from Yūnus ibn Yazīd, and this is the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿAnbasah. Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated to me, Muḥammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihāb said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), informed him: ""Marriage in the Jāhiliyyah was of four types. One of them was the marriage practiced by people today: a man would propose to another man regarding his ward, and in the narration of Ibn Wahb, his daughter, and he would give her a dowry and then marry her. Another type was that a man would say to his wife, when she became pure from her menses: 'Send to so-and-so and seek offspring from him.' Her husband would then separate from her and not touch her at all until her pregnancy from that man from whom she sought offspring became apparent. Once her pregnancy was evident, her husband would have intercourse with her if he wished. They did this out of desire for the nobility of the child. This marriage was called the marriage of istibḍāʿ (seeking noble offspring). Another type was that a group of less than ten men would gather and all have intercourse with a woman. When she became pregnant and gave birth, and some nights had passed after she delivered her child, she would send for them, and none of them could refuse until they all gathered before her. She would say to them: 'You know what has transpired among you. I have given birth. This is your son, O so-and so.' She would name whomever she wished from among them, and her child would be attributed to him. The fourth type was that many men would gather and have intercourse with a woman who would not refuse anyone who came to her. These were the prostitutes who would set up flags at their doors as signs for whoever wished them. They would have intercourse with them. When one of them became pregnant and gave birth, they would gather for her and call the qāfah (those skilled in physiognomy), and they would attribute her child to whomever they considered resembled the child. The child would be attached to him and called his son, and he could not refuse this.
When Allah sent Muḥammad (ﷺ) with the truth, he abolished all the marriages of the people of Jāhiliyyah except the marriage practiced by the people of Islam today.""
al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ from ʿAnbasah. He said: And Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5217]"
"13643","(13637) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibnbishrān informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us from ʿImrān al-Qaṣīr from al-Ḥasan, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Any woman whose marriage is not arranged by the guardian or guardians — her marriage is void."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13644","(13638) And Abū Zakariyyā informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Maʿbad ibn ʿUmayr that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) annulled the marriage of a woman who was married without a guardian. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13645","(13639) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Rūḥ narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jubayr ibn Shaybah narrated to me from ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid, who said: ""The road brought together a caravan, and among them was a thayyib (previously married) woman who gave her hand to a man other than her guardian, and he performed her marriage. This reached ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), so he flogged both the one who performed the marriage and the one who arranged it, and he annulled their marriage."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13646","(13640) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, Yūnus ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj that he heard Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib say, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""A woman should not be married except with the permission of her guardian, or the one of sound judgment from her family, or the sulṭān (ruling authority)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13647","(13641) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us, Abū Usāmah narrated to us from Sufyān from Salamah ibn Kuhayl from Muʿāwiyah ibn Suwayd, meaning ibn Muqarrin, from his father, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Any woman who is married without the permission of her guardian — her marriage is void. There is no marriage except with the permission of a guardian."" This is a ṣaḥīḥ isnād. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13648","(13642) And it has been narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) through other chains, though reliance is placed upon this one rather than those. Among them is what Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, Abū Isḥāq ibn Rajāʾ al-Buzārī narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Ghāzī al-Ṭabarī narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us from Hushaym from Mujāld from al-Shaʿbī that ʿUmar, ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both), Sharīḥ, and Masrūq (may Allah have mercy on them) said: ""There is no marriage except with a guardian."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13649","(13643) And Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Isfarāʾinī informed us, Zāhir ibn Aḥmad narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Ziyād al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us, he said: Mujālid narrated to us from al-Shaʿbī, who said: ʿAlī, ʿAbdullāh, and Sharīḥ said: ""There is no marriage except with a guardian."" [Ḥasan]"
"13650","(13644) And it has been narrated from another route from Mujālid from al-Shaʿbī that he said: ""None of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) was more severe regarding marriage without a guardian than ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), to the extent that he would carry out the punishment for it.""
Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Walīd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Abū Khālid narrated to us, Mujālid narrated to us from al-Shaʿbī, and he mentioned it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13651","(13645) Abū al-Fatḥ Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Rayy informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Hārūn narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar and ʿUbayd ibn Ziyād al-Farrāʾ narrated from Ḥajjāj, from Ḥuṣayn, from al-Shaʿbī, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""There is no marriage (nikāḥ) except with a guardian (walī), and there is no marriage except with witnesses."" Yazīd ibn Hārūn also narrated it from Ḥajjāj, and he said: ""There is no marriage except with a guardian and two upright witnesses."" This is a corroborating witness for the narration of Mujālid. And we have narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ from ʿAlī. [Hasan due to supporting evidence, except for the words ""and there is no marriage except with witnesses.""]"
"13652","(13646) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us: Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥakīm informed us: Sufyān narrated from Juwaybir, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk, from al-Nazzāl ibn Sabrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""There is no marriage except with the permission of a guardian. Whoever marries or gives in marriage without the permission of a guardian, his marriage is void (bāṭil).""

And it has been narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he permitted the marriage (conducted by) the maternal uncle or the mother. [Hasan]"
"13653","(13647) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad ibn Makhlad ibn al-Najjār in Kūfah informed us, both saying: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq informed us: Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān, from Abū Qays, from Huzayl, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) permitted the marriage (conducted by) the maternal uncle (khāl). He said ""the maternal uncle"" thus. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13654","(13648) And it has been narrated from Abū Qays al-Awdī, from one who informed him, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he permitted the marriage of a woman whom her mother had married off with her consent. Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥamzah al-Harawī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Abū Muʿāwiyah informed us: Abū Isḥāq al-Shaybānī narrated from Abū Qays al-Awdī, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13655","(13649) And it has been reported from al-Shaybānī, from Abū Qays al-Awdī, that a woman from ʿĀʾidh Allāh, called Salamah, was married off by her mother and her family. This matter was raised before ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: ""Has he not consummated the marriage with her?"" So the marriage was deemed permissible. Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥamzah informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Hushaym informed us: al-Shaybānī narrated, and he mentioned it. [Ḍaʿīf]

And Abū ʿAwanah and Ibn Idrīs narrated it from al-Shaybānī, from Baḥriyyah bint Hāniʾ ibn Qabīṣah, that she married herself to al-Qaʿqāʿ ibn Shawr, and he spent a night with her. Her father came and complained to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He asked: ""Did he consummate the marriage with her?"" He said: ""Yes."" So he permitted the marriage.

This is a narration whose chain of narration (isnād) and text (matn) are disputed, and its chain revolves around Abū Qays al-Awdī, whose uprightness (ʿādālah) is disputed, and Baḥriyyah is unknown (majhūlah). The condition of consummation (dukhūl) for validating the marriage—if it were established—does not render the impermissible permissible. The first narration from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the condition of the guardian has a sound chain of narration, and reliance is placed upon it. And with Allah lies success."
"13656","(13650) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us: Muslim ibn Khālid narrated from Ibn Khuthaym, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""There is no marriage except with a prudent guardian (walī murshid) and two upright witnesses."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13657","(13651) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Tamtām informed us: Shujaʿ informed us: ʿAbbād, who is Ibn al-ʿAwwām, narrated from Hishām, who is Ibn Ḥassān, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""They used to say: The woman who marries herself off is a fornicator (zāniyah)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13658","(13652) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us: al-Thiqah (the trustworthy narrator) narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, who said: ""ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) would be approached regarding the marriage of women from her family, and she would witness it. When the time for the marriage contract came, she would say to some of her family: 'Perform the marriage, for a woman does not preside over the marriage contract.'""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""This narration indicates that the one [who presides over the contract is the guardian]."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13659","(13653) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Najayd informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī informed us: Ibn Bukayr informed us: Mālik narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she married Ḥafṣah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān to al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr while ʿAbd al-Raḥmān was absent in Shām. When ʿAbd al-Raḥmān arrived, he said: ""Is something like this done to someone like me, and is it decided without my consultation?"" So ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) spoke to al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr, and al-Mundhir said: ""That is in the hands of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān."" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: ""I would not reject a matter that you have already decided."" So Ḥafṣah remained with al-Mundhir, and that was not a divorce. What is meant by this is that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) facilitated the marriage, and then someone else carried out the marriage contract. The attribution of the marriage arrangement is to her because of her permission and her facilitation of its means. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 1182]"
"13660","(13654) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Raffāʾ informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishr informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways and ʿĪsā ibn Mīnāʾ both said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated from his father, from the jurists whose word was followed among the Successors (Tābiʿūn) of the people of Madīnah, who used to say: ""A woman may not contract the marriage bond for herself or for anyone else."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13661","(13655) By way of inference, Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj reported to us, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""A woman is not to be married except with the permission of her guardian. If she is married [without it], then it is void, void, void. If he consummates the marriage with her, then she is entitled to the mahr on account of what he has had of her. If they dispute, then the sulṭān is the guardian for one who has no guardian."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13662","(13656) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū Khaythamah narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq: ʿUmar ibn Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbd Allāh, the freedman of the family of Ḥāṭib, narrated to me, from Nāfiʿ, the freedman of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (may Allah be pleased with him) passed away and left behind a daughter from Khuwaylah bint Ḥakīm ibn Umayyah ibn Ḥārithah ibn al-Awqāṣ. He had bequeathed [her guardianship] to his brother Qudāmah ibn Maẓʿūn."" ʿAbd Allāh said: ""They are both my maternal uncles."" He said: ""So I proposed to Qudāmah ibn Maẓʿūn for the daughter of ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn, and he married her to me. Then al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah (may Allah be pleased with him) went to her mother and enticed her with wealth, so she inclined toward him, and the girl inclined toward her mother's desire. They both refused [me] until their matter was raised to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" He said: ""Qudāmah ibn Maẓʿūn said: 'My brother's daughter — he bequeathed her to me, so I married her to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. I did not fall short in her welfare or in [ensuring] her compatibility. However, she is a woman, and she inclined toward her mother's desire.'"" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'She is an orphan, and she is not to be married except with her permission.'"" He said: ""So she was taken from me, by Allah, after I had taken possession of her, and they married her to al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah."" [Ḥasan]"
"13663","(13657) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me: al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dārimī narrated to me: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) married me when I was six years old, and he consummated the marriage with me when I was nine years old."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1423]"
"13664","(13658) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) married ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) three years after the death of Khadījah, and ʿĀʾishah was at that time six years old. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) consummated the marriage with her when she was nine years old, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away when ʿĀʾishah was eighteen years old.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd ibn Ismāʿīl from Abī Usāmah as a mursal narration, and Muslim narrated it from Abī Kurayb as a mawṣūl narration. Sufyān al-Thawrī, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān, ʿAlī ibn Mushhir, Abū Muʿāwiyah, and others connected it, and both [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] narrated it as mawṣūl through various routes. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13665","(13659) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed me: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ḥajjāj al-Warrāq narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) married her when she was six years old, and consummated the marriage with her when she was nine years old, and passed away when she was eighteen years old."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""And ʿAlī married Umm Kulthūm to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) without her permission."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13666","(13660) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us: Dalaj ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us: Sufyān ibn Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbadah narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: Ibn Abī Mulaykah informed me: Ḥasan ibn Ḥasan informed me, from his father, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb proposed to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) for Umm Kulthūm (may Allah be pleased with her). ʿAlī said to him: ""She is too young for that."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Every tie of relationship and lineage will be severed on the Day of Resurrection except my relationship and my lineage. So I would like there to be for me a relationship and lineage with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).'"" ʿAlī said to Ḥasan and Ḥusayn (may Allah be pleased with them both): ""Marry your uncle [to her]."" They said: ""She is a woman from among the women; she chooses for herself."" ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up in anger. Ḥasan held onto his garment and said: ""I cannot bear your estrangement, O father!"" He said: ""So they married her [to ʿUmar].""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""And al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) married his daughter while she was young, and others among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) married their daughters while they were young. If marriage were not valid for a virgin except with her command, it would not be permissible to marry her until she had a command over herself."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13667","(13661) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Mālik ibn Anas informed me: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl narrated to me, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The previously married woman (al-ayyim) has more right over herself than her guardian, and the virgin (al-bikr) is to be consulted regarding herself, and her permission is her silence.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1421]"
"13668","(13662) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated. He said: ""I said to Mālik: 'ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl narrated to you…' and he mentioned it with the same hadith as that of Ibn Wahb.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13669","(13663) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: Sufyān narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated, from Ziyād ibn Saʿd, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The previously married woman (al-thayyib) has more right over herself than her guardian, and the virgin (al-bikr) — her father seeks her permission regarding herself, and her permission is her silence."" And he sometimes said: ""And her silence is her consent (iqrāruhā).""

The wording is that of Ibn Abī ʿUmar. In the narration of Aḥmad: ""The previously married woman has more right over herself than her guardian, and the virgin — her father commands her.""

Abū Dāwūd (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""Her father"" is not preserved [as authentic].

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar and mentioned this addition. And Allah knows best.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""Ibn ʿUyaynah added in his hadith: 'And the virgin — her father marries her off.' This makes clear that the matter rests with the father in the case of the virgin.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""And mutual consultation (al-muʾāmarah) may be upon the inclination of the soul, because it is narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'And consult the women regarding their daughters.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13670","(13664) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed him: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Muʿāwiyah ibn Hishām narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah. He said: ""A trustworthy person narrated to me, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'And command the women regarding their daughters.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13671","(13665) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Mu'addib narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated from Makhul from Salamah ibn Abi Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from his father, that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) proposed to Nu'aym ibn 'Abdullah — who was called al-Nahham, one of the Banu 'Adi — for his daughter, who was a virgin. Nu'aym said to him: ""I have an orphan under my care whom I would not favour anyone over."" The girl's mother, the wife of Nu'aym, went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""Ibn 'Umar has proposed for my daughter, but Nu'aym has refused him and wishes to marry her off to an orphan under his care."" She informed the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), so he sent for Nu'aym. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: ""Seek her consent and seek your daughter's consent.""

We have also narrated it through another chain from 'Urwah from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), as a connected (mawsul) narration. Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""The people do not differ that the mother has no authority in the matter; rather, it is in the sense of seeking what is agreeable to one's own conscience.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: Salih ibn Kaysan narrated it from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl with his chain, and he said: ""The orphan girl is to be consulted (musta'marah)."" Muhammad ibn 'Amr also narrated it from Abu Salamah from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abu Burdah narrated it from Abu Musa from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). Thus, the intended meaning of ""the virgin"" mentioned in the hadith is the orphan virgin, and the addition of Ibn 'Uyunah is not preserved. And Allah knows best. Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""Ibn 'Umar, al-Qasim, and Salim used to marry off virgins without consulting them."" [Hasan]"
"13672","(13666) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajanī informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated that it reached him that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Salim ibn 'Abdullah used to marry off their virgin daughters without consulting them. And it reached him that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, Salim ibn 'Abdullah, and Sulayman ibn Yasar used to say regarding the virgin whom her father marries off without her permission: ""That is binding upon her."" [Da'if]"
"13673","(13667) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Baghdadi al-Raffa' informed us: Abu 'Amr 'Uthman ibn Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Abi Uways and 'Isa ibn Mīnā' narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad from his father, from one of the jurists of theirs whose opinion was followed — among them Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah, and Sulayman ibn Yasar, along with a group of eminent scholars among their peers. He said: ""And they sometimes differed on a matter, and I took the view of the majority among them."" He said: They used to say: ""A man has the greatest right to arrange the marriage of his virgin daughter without her command, but if she is a non-virgin (thayyib), then her father has no authority over her marriage except with her permission."" [Da'if]"
"13674","(13668) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us: al-Shafi'i informed us: 'Abd al-Majid narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': ""Is it permissible for a man to marry off his virgin daughter while she is averse to it?"" He said: ""Yes."" I said: ""And a non-virgin who is averse?"" He said: ""No. The non-virgin has authority over her own affair.""

It has been narrated from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i: ""The father may compel the virgin (yujbiruha)."" And from al-Sha'bi: ""No one may compel except the parent."" [Hasan]"
"13675","(13669) As for the hadith: Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Shakir and Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Jarir narrated from Ayyub from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that a virgin girl came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and mentioned to him that her father had married her off while she was averse to it. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) gave her the choice.

This is a hadith in which Jarir ibn Hazim erred regarding Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani. [Munkar]"
"13676","(13670) The preserved narration from Ayyub from 'Ikrimah is from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal (disconnected) narration. Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated from Hammad ibn Zayd from Ayyub from 'Ikrimah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) with this hadith, without mentioning Ibn 'Abbas.

Abu Dawud said: ""Likewise, it is narrated as a well-known mursal narration.""

The Shaykh said: It has also been narrated through another chain from 'Ikrimah as a connected (mawsul) narration, but that too is an error. [Da'if]"
"13677","(13671) Abu al-Qasim Talhah ibn 'Ali ibn al-Saqr informed us in Baghdad: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Qadi in 'Asqalan narrated to us: Abu Salamah al-Muslim ibn Muhammad ibn Ammar al-San'ani narrated to us: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Dhimari narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated from Hisham al-Dastawa'i from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) annulled the marriage of a virgin and a non-virgin whom their father had married off while both were averse, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) annulled their marriage. [Munkar]"
"13678","(13672) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz said: ""This is an error. The correct narration is from Yahya from al-Muhajir ibn 'Ikrimah, as a mursal narration. al-Dhimari erred regarding al-Thawri, and he is not strong.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: It is in the Jami' of al-Thawri from al-Thawri, as Abu al-Hasan al-Daraqutni (may Allah have mercy upon him) mentioned, as a mursal narration. And likewise, the generality of his companions narrated it from him. And others besides al-Thawri narrated it from Hisham.

It has also been narrated through another chain in which the narrator erred. [Sahih]"
"13679","(13673) Abu Tahir al-Faqih and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us: Shu'ayb ibn Ishaq narrated from al-Awza'i from 'Ata' from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) that a man married off his daughter, who was a virgin, without her command. She came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he separated them.

This is an error. The correct narration is from al-Awza'i from Ibrahim ibn Murrah from 'Ata' from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal narration. Thus narrated Ibn al-Mubarak, 'Isa ibn Yunus, and others from al-Awza'i. [Munkar]"
"13680","(13674) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: I heard Abu 'Ali al-Hafiz al-Naysaburi, and he was asked about this hadith of Shu'ayb ibn Ishaq. He said: ""Abu 'Ali al-Hafiz: al-Awza'i did not hear it from 'Ata'. The hadith in its original form is mursal from 'Ata'. The trustworthy narrators narrated it from al-Awza'i from Ibrahim ibn Murrah from 'Ata' from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal narration."" [Sahih]"
"13681","(13675) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī, the Ḥāfiẓ, narrated to us, saying: The correct view is that it is mursal, and the statement of Shuʿayb is an error. Al-Athram mentioned it to Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, and he rejected it.

It has also been narrated through another weak chain from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir, but it is not well-known (mashhūr). Even if that is authentic, it is as though he had placed her in a marriage to one who was not her equal (ghayr kuf'), so the Prophet ﷺ gave her the choice. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13682","(13676) In a similar vein, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, and ʿUbayd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Mahdī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Kahmas al-Qaysī informed us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah, who said:

A young woman came to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) and said: ""My father has married me to his brother's son in order to raise his low status through me, and I dislike that."" ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Sit down until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ comes, and mention it to him."" When the Prophet ﷺ came, she mentioned it to him, so he ﷺ sent for her father. When her father came, he placed the matter in her hands. When she saw that the matter had been given to her, she said: ""I have accepted what my father arranged; I only wished to know whether women have any say in the matter or not.""

This is mursal, for Ibn Buraydah did not hear from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13683","(13677) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:

""The previously married woman (al-ayyim) has more right over herself than her guardian (walī), and the virgin (al-bikr) is to be asked permission concerning herself, and her permission is her silence.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ, as has been mentioned above. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1421]"
"13684","(13678) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Isḥāq al-Ḥarbī narrated to us, Muslim narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Shuʿbah informed us, from Mālik ibn Anas, and he narrated it with the same meaning, except that both of them said: ""The previously married woman (al-thayyib) has more right over herself.""

A group narrated it thus from Mālik, and likewise Ziyād ibn Saʿd narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl, and it has been mentioned above. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13685","(13679) Likewise, Abū Uways al-Madanī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl. ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed it to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to us, my father narrated to me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl, and he narrated it with a similar wording, and said: ""The previously married woman (al-thayyib) has more right over herself."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13686","(13680) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Anas al-Qurashī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Makkī al-Marwazī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Maʿmar.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, al-Maḥāmilī and Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us, from Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:

""The guardian has no authority over the previously married woman (al-thayyib), and the orphan girl (al-yatīmah) is to be consulted, and her silence is her approval.""

ʿAlī said: I heard al-Naysābūrī say: ""In my view, Maʿmar made an error in this."" And ʿAlī said likewise.

He cited as evidence for that the narration of Ibn Isḥāq and Saʿīd ibn Salamah al-Ḥadīth from Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), with a wording similar to the first narration in its beginning, except that both of them also said: ""And the orphan girl (al-yatīmah) is to be consulted.""

It is possible that what is meant by his statement in these narrations, ""and the virgin (al-bikr) is to be consulted,"" is the orphan virgin. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13687","(13681) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Ibn Idrīs narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, from Abū ʿAmr, the mawlā of ʿĀʾishah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:

""Women are to be consulted regarding their private parts (i.e., their marriages)."" He said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, they feel shy."" He said: ""The previously married woman (al-ayyim) has more right over herself, and the virgin (al-bikr) is to be asked permission, and her silence is her approval.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah."
"13688","(13682) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū Muslim narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq narrated to us in Baghdad, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Ayyūb al-Bazzāz narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Hishām narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet ﷺ, who said:

""The previously married woman (al-thayyib) is not to be married until she is consulted, and the virgin (al-bikr) is not to be married until she is asked permission."" It was said, ""O Messenger of Allah, how is her permission?"" He said: ""If she remains silent, that is her consent.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Hishām al-Dastawāʾī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13689","(13683) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Mālik ibn Anas informed me.

And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, Mālik narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and Mujammiʿ, the two sons of Yazīd ibn Jāriyah, from Khansāʾ bint Khidhām al-Anṣāriyyah, that her father married her off while she was a previously married woman (thayyib), and she disliked that, so she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he annulled her marriage.

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Uways and others from Mālik.

Likewise, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated it from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 6945]"
"13690","(13684) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Qāsim — meaning Ibn Zakariyyā — narrated to us, Ibn al-Muthannā, Yaʿqūb, and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us, saying: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd and Mujammiʿ ibn Yazīd informed him that a man among them called Khidhām married his daughter to a man, and she disliked his marriage, so she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned that to him, so he annulled her father's marriage for her, and she then married Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir.

Abū Bakr said: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated it to me, ʿAmmār ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us something similar, and Yaḥyā added: ""It reached him that she was a previously married woman (thayyib).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq, from Yazīd ibn Hārūn. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13691","(13685) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ibn Manīʿ narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar al-Kūfī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Ḥajjāj ibn al-Sāʾib, meaning Ibn Abī Lubābah, from his father, from his grandmother Khansāʾ bint Khidām ibn Khālid, who said: She had been widowed by a man, and her father married her off to a man from Banū ʿAwf. She inclined toward Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir, so her matter was raised to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded her father to follow her wishes, and so she married Abū Lubābah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13692","(13686) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abū al-Ḥuwayrith, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, who said: Khansāʾ bint Khidām was widowed, and her father married her off while she was averse to it. She came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""My father married me off while I was averse, and I have authority over my own affair, and he did not inform me."" He said: ""There is no marriage for him; marry whom you wish."" So she married Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir. This is a mursal narration, and it serves as a witness for what preceded it. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its own chain]"
"13693","(13687) Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Ibn Abī ʿĀṣim narrated to us; Duḥaym narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us; Shaybān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah: that during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a man married off his daughter, a previously married woman who had been with a husband, and she disliked that. She came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him, so he annulled her marriage.

It was also narrated by ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah from his father, and he named the woman as Khansāʾ bint Khidām, mentioning it as mursal. It has also been said as connected (mawṣūl), but the mursal version is more authentic, and what has preceded in the connected narrations is sufficient. [Munkar]"
"13694","(13688) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī in Marw informed us; Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Hilāl al-Būzanjirdī informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Abū Ḥanīfah, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿAbbāy — may Allah be pleased with them both: A woman's husband died and she had a child by him. The paternal uncle of her child proposed to her father, saying: ""Give her to me in marriage."" He refused, so he married her off to someone else without her consent. She came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him. The Prophet ﷺ sent for him and said: ""Did you marry her off to someone other than the paternal uncle of her child?"" He said: ""Yes, I married her to one who is better for her than the paternal uncle of her child."" So the Prophet ﷺ separated them and married her to the paternal uncle of her child. This is what he said. [Munkar]"
"13695","(13689) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us; Abū Qilābah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, from Abū Salamah: A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""My father married me off while I was averse, and I wish to marry the paternal uncle of my child."" So the Prophet ﷺ annulled his marriage. This is the correct version, mursal from Abū Salamah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13696","(13690) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us by dictation; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Asbāṭ ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The orphan girl shall be consulted concerning herself; if she remains silent, that is her permission, and if she refuses, there shall be no compulsion upon her."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its own chain]"
"13697","(13691) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Maymūn narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Yūnus, meaning Ibn Abī Isḥāq, narrated to us; Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā narrated from Abū Mūsā — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The orphan girl shall be consulted concerning herself; if she remains silent, she has given permission, and if she refuses, she shall not be compelled."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13698","(13692) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; it was read to Ibn Ṣāʿid while I was listening: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Saʿd al-Zuhrī narrated to you; he said: My paternal uncle narrated to me; my father narrated to me from Ibn Isḥāq; ʿUmar ibn Ḥusayn, the freedman of the family of Ḥāṭib, narrated to me from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn passed away and left a daughter by Khawlah bint Ḥakīm ibn Umayyah. He bequeathed her guardianship to his brother Qudāmah ibn Maẓʿūn — and they were both my maternal uncles. I proposed to Qudāmah for ʿUthmān's daughter, and he married her to me. Then al-Mughīrah went to her mother and tempted her with wealth, so she inclined toward him, and the girl inclined toward her mother's desire. Their matter was raised to the Prophet ﷺ. Qudāmah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, she is my brother's daughter, and he bequeathed her to me. I married her to Ibn ʿUmar, and I did not fall short in terms of rectitude and compatibility. But she is a woman, and she inclined toward her mother's desire."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""She is an orphan, and she shall not be married except with her permission."" So she was taken from me — by Allah — after I had taken possession of her, and they married her to al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah. [Ḥasan]"
"13699","(13693) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from ʿUmar ibn Ḥusayn, from Nāfiʿ: that Ibn ʿUmar married the daughter of his maternal uncle, ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn. Her mother went to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""My daughter dislikes that."" So the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to separate from her and said: ""Do not marry off the orphans until you consult them; if they remain silent, that is their permission."" After ʿAbdullāh, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah married her. [Ḥasan]"
"13700","(13694) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us with it in another place through this chain, and he said, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he married.

And similarly, Ibn Ṣāʿid narrated it from Ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam and from Abū ʿUtbah, from Ibn Abī Fudayk, with his chain, and he said, from Ibn ʿUmar. And Allah knows best. [Ḥasan]"
"13701","(13695) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd informed us; Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Suwayd, who said: I found in my father's book, on the authority of ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: ""When women reach the age of full discernment (naṣṣ al-ḥaqāʾiq), then the ʿaṣabah (agnatic heirs) is more entitled [to guardianship], and whoever is present should intercede with goodness."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13702","(13696) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us; he said: Abū ʿUbayd (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Some of them say ""al-ḥiqāq,"" and it is from al-muḥāqqah, meaning disputation — that the mother disputes with the ʿaṣabah regarding them [the women]. The naṣṣ of al-ḥiqāq is merely the attainment of discernment, for it is the extremity of childhood. When women reach that stage, the ʿaṣabah is more entitled to the woman than her mother, provided they are maḥram (unmarriageable kin), and also to marry her off if they wish. He said: This makes clear to you that the ʿaṣabah and the guardians other than the fathers do not have the authority to marry off the orphan girl until she reaches maturity (tudrika). Had that authority been theirs, they would not have waited for her to reach the naṣṣ of al-ḥiqāq. He said: And whoever narrated it as naṣṣ al-ḥaqāʾiq intended the plural of ḥaqīqah (reality). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13703","(13697) As for the ḥadīth: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Jahm informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faraj informed us; al-Wāqidī informed us; Ibn Abī Ḥabībah narrated to me, from Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that ʿĀmah bint Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib was in Makkah. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived — meaning during the ʿUmrah of al-Qaḍiyyah — ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) brought her out and said to the Prophet (ﷺ): ""Marry her."" He said: ""She is the daughter of my foster brother."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) married her to Salamah ibn Abī Salamah. The Prophet (ﷺ) used to say: ""Have I repaid Salamah [adequately]?""

This isnād is weak, and there is nothing in it to indicate that she was a minor. The Prophet (ﷺ) had prerogatives in the matter of marriage that no one else had, and he was more entitled to the believers than they were to themselves. It was for this reason that he undertook her marriage himself, rather than her uncle al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, if indeed he did so. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"13704","(13698) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; al-Qāḍī Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Khurruzādh al-Ahwāzī informed us; it was recited to Buhlūl ibn Isḥāq and ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥasan, while I was present: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to you; Mālik ibn Anas narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The previously married woman (al-ayyim) is more entitled to herself than her guardian, and the virgin (al-bikr) is consulted regarding herself, and her consent is her silence.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr and others. [Muslim 1421]"
"13705","(13699) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Tanūkhī informed us; Bishr ibn Bakr informed us.

(Ḥ) And al-ʿAbbāb ibn al-Walīd informed us; my father informed us; they both said: al-Awzāʿī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr informed us; Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The previously married woman (al-thayyib) is not married until she is consulted, and the virgin (al-bikr) is not married until she is asked permission."" They said: ""How is her permission, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Silence.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Awzāʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1419]"
"13706","(13700) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah al-Shaybānī informed us in al-Kūfah; al-Haytham ibn Khālid informed us; Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn informed us; Abū Muʿāwiyah Shaybān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The previously married woman (al-ayyim) is not married until she is consulted, and the virgin (al-bikr) is not married until she is asked permission."" They said: ""And how is her permission?"" He said: ""That she remain silent.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from Shaybān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13707","(13701) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Abū Kāmil informed us; Yazīd ibn Zuraʿayy informed us. Abū Dāwūd said: And Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us; Ḥammād — with the same meaning — informed us. They both said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated to me; Abū Salamah narrated to me, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The orphan girl (al-yatīmah) is consulted regarding herself. If she remains silent, that is her permission, and if she refuses, there is no compulsion upon her.""

Abū Dāwūd said: The narration (al-ikhbār) in the ḥadīth of Yazīd — and likewise Abū Khālid Sulaymān ibn Ḥayyān and Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr.

Abū Dāwūd said: And Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ informed us; Ibn Idrīs informed us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, with this ḥadīth by its isnād, and he added in it: ""And if she weeps or remains silent"" — he added ""if she weeps."" Abū Dāwūd said: ""If she weeps"" is not preserved; it is a confusion (wahm) in the ḥadīth, the error being from Ibn Idrīs or from Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13708","(13702) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Zāhid informed us; Aḥmad ibn Mihrān informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us; Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, who heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: ""The orphan girl (al-yatīmah) is consulted regarding herself. If she remains silent, that is her consent, and if she dislikes it, there is no compulsion upon her."""
"13709","(13703) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Ḥajjāj informed us; Ibn Jurayj said: I heard Ibn Abī Mulaykah say: Dhakwān, the freedman of ʿĀʾishah, said: I heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about a young girl whose family marries her off — is she consulted or not? The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to her: ""Yes, she is consulted."" ʿĀʾishah said: ""But she feels shy and remains silent."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""That is her permission, when she remains silent.""

al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5949. Muslim 1420]"
"13710","(13704) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī informed us; Ibn Abī Maryam informed us; al-Firyābī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Qāsim informed us; Ibn Kaysān informed us; Abū Ḥudhayfah informed us; they both said: Sufyān informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, from Abū ʿAmr, the freedman of ʿĀʾishah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, are women consulted regarding their private parts (i.e., their marriages)?"" He said: ""Yes."" I said: ""But the virgin feels shy."" He said: ""She is consulted, and if she remains silent, her silence is her permission.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Firyābī."
"13711","(13705) It was narrated that Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz said: “Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us, and Abu al-‘Abbas narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, ‘Amr ibn ar-Rabi‘ ibn Thariq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from his father, from ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn al-Makkī, who narrated to him from ‘Adiyy ibn ‘Adiyy al-Kindi, from his father, from ‘Urw ibn ‘Amirah al-Kindi, a Companion of the Prophet (peace be upon him), that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ‘Advise the women about their own selves, for the married woman will speak in defense of her own self, and the virgin’s consent is her silence.’ [Sahih li Ghayrihi]"
"13712","(13706) It was narrated that Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakkī and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi said: “Abu al-‘Abbas narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn ‘Abdillah ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa‘d narrated to me, ‘Abdillah ibn ‘Abdur-Rahman al-Qurashi narrated from ‘Adiyy ibn ‘Adiyy al-Kindi, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), that he said: ‘Consult the women about their own selves.’ It was said to him: ‘O Messenger of Allah, the virgin is ashamed.’ He said: ‘The married woman will speak in defense of her own self, and the virgin’s consent is her silence.’ The marriage was not mentioned in its chain of narration. [Sahih li Ghayrihi]"
"13713","(13707) It was narrated that Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadhl al-Qattan in Baghdad narrated: Isma‘il ibn Muhammad as-Saffar narrated to us, ‘Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Hisham ibn Bahram narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma‘il narrated from Abu al-Asbat, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salah, from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), and from ‘Ikrimah, from Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), when some of his daughters were proposed to, would go to al-Khidr and say: ‘Indeed, so-and-so is proposing to so-and-so. If she speaks against him in al-Khidr, he will not marry her; and if she does not speak against him, he will marry her.’

This is also narrated by Abu al-Asbat al-Harithiyy, but he is not to be trusted. The trustworthy one is the one who narrated from Yahya, which is mursal. [Da‘if]"
"13714","(13708) As narrated by Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa‘id ibn Abi ‘Amr, they said: “Abu al-‘Abbas narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Jabbar narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Hisham ibn Sanbar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Muhajir ibn ‘Ikrimah al-Makhzumi, who said: ‘The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), when he intended to marry one of his daughters, would sit by al-Khidr and say: ‘Indeed, so-and-so wants to marry so-and-so.’ [Da‘if]"
"13715","(13709) It was narrated that Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz said: “‘Ali ibn Hamshad narrated to me: Yazid ibn al-Haytham narrated to him, that Ibrahim ibn Abi Layth narrated to them, that al-Ashja‘iyy narrated from Sufyan, from Hisham al-Dastawayi, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhajir ibn ‘Ikrimah, who said: ‘Whenever a proposal was made to the Prophet (peace be upon him) concerning some of his daughters, he would go to al-Khidr and say: ‘Indeed, so-and-so is proposing to so-and-so. If she moves him, he will not marry her; and if she does not move him, he will marry her.’ This is also narrated from another perspective, mursal. [Da‘if]"
"13716","(13710) It was narrated to us by Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz: “Abu al-‘Abbas narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Jarir ibn Hazim narrated from Humayd at-Tawil, from Jubayr ibn Hayyah ath-Thaqafi, who said: ‘The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), when he intended to marry one of his daughters, would sit by al-Khidr and say to her: ‘Indeed, so-and-so mentions so-and-so.’ If she spoke, he would dislike it and would not marry her; and if she remained silent, he would marry her.’

It is also narrated by Abu Hariz, the judge of Sijistan, from ash-Sha‘bi, from ‘Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and from ‘Ikrimah, from Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them). [Da‘if]"
"13717","(13711) It was narrated by Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and Abu Sa‘id ibn Abi ‘Amr, they said: “Abu al-‘Abbas narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, ar-Rabi‘ ibn Sulaiman narrated to us, ash-Shafi‘iyy narrated to us, Malik narrated to ‘Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from ‘Abdur-Rahman and Mujammi‘, the two sons of Yazid ibn Jariyah, from Khansa’ bint Khidham, that her father married her while she was thayyib and karihah, so she came to the Prophet (peace be upon him), and he annulled her marriage.”

Abu Sa‘id added in his narration: “ash-Shafi‘iyy (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ‘He did not say anything except that if she wishes to absolve her father, then he can permit the marriage, if her permission for the marriage is valid, it is more likely that he would command her to permit her father’s marriage, and not to reject it.’

It is related by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Qaza‘ah and others from Malik. [Sahih, al-Bukhari 6945]"
"13718","(13712) It was narrated to us by Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz: “Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih said: ‘It was recited upon Muhammad ibn Isma‘il as-Sulamiyy, and I am listening: Sa‘id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, that Sulaiman ibn Musa ad-Dimashqiyy narrated to him, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, ‘Urwah narrated from ‘Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ‘The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ‘A woman shall not be married without the permission of her guardian. If she is married without permission, her marriage is void three times. If she enters into it, she has her mahr from what she obtained from it. If they dispute, the authority is with the guardian, even if he has no guardian.’ [Sahih]"
"13719","(13713) It was narrated by Abu al-Husayn: Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadhl al-Qattan in Baghdad narrated: Abu Sahl: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn ‘Abdillah ibn Ziyad al-Qattan narrated to us, Mu‘adh ibn al-Muthannay narrated to us, ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Umar al-Qawaririyy narrated to us, ‘Abdillah ibn Dawud heard it from Sufyan, who mentioned it from Ibn Khuthaym, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: ‘Ubaydullah said, and he narrated to us from Bashir ibn Mansur and ‘Abdur-Rahman ibn Mahdi, all of them said: Sufyan narrated from Ibn Khuthaym, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ‘Abbas, from the Prophet (peace be upon him): ‘If Allah wills, he said: ‘There is no marriage except with the permission of a guardian who is wise, or with the authority of a ruler.’

This is also said by Abu al-Muthannay, Mu‘adh ibn al-Muthannay.

It is also narrated by others from ‘Ubaydullah al-Qawaririyy, who said: ‘The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ‘Without exception, the Qawaririyy narrated it alone, elevated.’ The Qawaririyy is trustworthy, except that the famous with this chain is stopped at Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them). [Da‘if]"
"13720","(13714) It was narrated to us by ‘Ali ibn Ahmad ibn ‘Abdun: Abu al-Qasim at-Tabarani narrated to us, Ishaq ad-Dabari narrated from ‘Abdur-Razzaq, from ath-Thawri, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), similar to it, and he did not elevate it. [Sahih]"
"13721","(13715) It was narrated that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""There is no marriage except with the permission of a wali (guardian) or a ruler. If a foolish person marries her and expresses regret over it, then her marriage is invalid."""
"13722","(13716) It was narrated that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage except with the permission of a wali and two just witnesses. If a wali marries her and expresses regret over it, then her marriage is void."" 
This was narrated by Adi ibn al-Fadl, who is weak, and the correct version is interrupted."
"13723","(13717) It was narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Any woman who is married without the permission of her wali and two just witnesses—her marriage is void. If he enters upon her, she is entitled to the mahr; and if the walis dispute, the sultan is the wali of whoever has no wali."" 
Abu Al-Hasan (the Naysaburi) said: Abu Yufir al-Raqiyy is among the reliable narrators of Ahl al-Jazirah and their most precise scholars."
"13724","(13718) It was narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage except with the permission of a wali and two just witnesses. If they dispute, the sultan is the wali of whoever has no wali."" 
Ali (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Abdur-Rahman ibn Yunus narrated it from Isa ibn Yunus in the same way. 
Similarly, it was narrated by Sa'id ibn Khalid ibn Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, Yazid ibn Sinan, and others, and in some versions it includes ""and two just witnesses."""
"13725","(13719) It was narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage except with the permission of a wali and two just witnesses."" 
Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It was narrated from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no marriage except with the permission of a wali and two just witnesses."""
"13726","(13720) It was narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""It is not permissible to marry except with the permission of a wali, with a dower, and with two just witnesses."" 
Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Even if this hadith is interrupted before the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), most of the scholars affirm it, and the distinction between marriage and fornication lies in the witnesses. 
Muzani said: It was narrated by others besides Shafi'i, from al-Hasan through Imamran ibn Husayn, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."
"13727","(13721) The Sheikh said: This hadith was narrated by Abdillah ibn Muharrar from Qatadah from al-Hasan through Imamran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""It is not permissible to marry except with the permission of a wali and two just witnesses."" 
It was narrated by Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran al-Adli from Abu Bakr al-Faqih, from Abu al-Fadl Ahmad ibn Mulayib, from al-Fadl ibn Dukayn, from Abdillah ibn Muharrar, who narrated it in full. 
Abdillah ibn Muharrar is rejected and cannot be used as evidence. 
It is also said that he narrated it from Qatadah through al-Hasan through Imamran from ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), but this is not authentic. 
It has also been narrated from another source, in full and elevated."
"13728","(13722) It was narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""There is no marriage except with the permission of a wali, a caller (khutbah), and two just witnesses."" 
Abu Ahmad and al-Junaydi narrated it; al-Bukhari said: Mughirah ibn Musa of Basra is rejected in hadith. Abu Ahmad said: Mughirah ibn Musa is trustworthy in himself."
"13729","(13723) It was narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""The wicked women are those who marry themselves without proof."""
"13730","(13724) It was narrated in a similar way by Yousuf ibn Hammâd, and the correct version is interrupted, and Allah knows best. 
Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It is firmly established from Ibn Abbas and others among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."
"13731","(13725) It was narrated that Abu Zakariyya reported: Yahya ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Abu al-‘Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us: Rab‘i ibn Sulaiman narrated to us: al-Shafi‘i narrated to us: Muslim ibn Khalid and Sa‘id ibn Salim al-Qaddah narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, from ‘Abd Allah ibn ‘Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr and Mujahid, from Ibn ‘Abbas, who said: “There is no marriage except with the permission of two just witnesses and a guardian who is rightly guided.” 
Al-Shafi‘i, may Allah have mercy on him, said: I believe that Muslim ibn Khalid heard it from Ibn Khuthaym. [Hasan]"
"13732","(13726) It was narrated that Abu Zakariyya ibn Ibrahim and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan reported: Abu al-‘Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us: Rab‘i ibn Sulaiman narrated to us: al-Shafi‘i narrated to us: Malik narrated to us from Abu al-Zubayr, who said: “‘Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was informed of a marriage in which there was only one man and one woman as witnesses. He said: ‘This is a marriage in secret, and it is not permissible; and if I had been the first to hear of it, I would have stoned them.’” 
[Da‘if]"
"13733","(13727) It was narrated that Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn ‘Ali al-Hafiz reported: Zahir ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Ziyad al-Naysaburi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: ‘Abd al-Wahhab ibn ‘Ata’ narrated to us from Sa‘id, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Sa‘id ibn al-Mu‘sib, that ‘Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “There is no marriage except with the permission of a guardian and two just witnesses.” 
This chain is authentic. Ibn al-Mu‘sib was known as the narrator of ‘Umar, and ibn ‘Umar would send him to inquire about certain matters concerning ‘Umar. [Da‘if]"
"13734","(13728) As for what was narrated to us by Abu Hamid al-Hafiz, he reported: Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayr narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Sa‘id ibn Mansur narrated to us: Husayn narrated to us: Hajjaj narrated to us from ‘Ata’, from ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he permitted the testimony of women together with men in marriage. 
This chain is broken. Hajjaj ibn ‘Arta’ is not to be relied upon. 
It has also been narrated to us concerning the conditions of witnesses from ‘Ata’ ibn Abi Rabah, al-Hasan, and az-Zuhri. [Da‘if]"
"13735","(13729) It was narrated that Abu al-Hasan ‘Ali ibn Ahmad ibn ‘Abdun reported: Ahmad ibn ‘Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Hisham ibn ‘Ali narrated to us: Ibn Raji narrated to us: al-Hasan, meaning ibn Salih ibn Hayy, narrated to us from ‘Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn ‘Aqil, who said: “I heard Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: The Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—said: ‘Whoever marries a slave without the permission of his master is an adulterer.’” [Da‘if]"
"13736","(13730) It was narrated that Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran reported: Abu al-Hasan ‘Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri narrated to us: Malik ibn Yahya narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us from Ibn ‘Abd al-Wahid al-Makki, from ‘Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn ‘Aqil, from Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah, from the Prophet—peace and blessings be upon him—who said: “Whoever marries a slave without the permission of his master is an adulterer.” 
This is the narration of al-Qasim ibn ‘Abd al-Wahid. [Da‘if]"
"13737","(13731) It was narrated that Abu ‘Ali al-Rudhbari reported: Abu Bakr ibn Dassah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: ‘Uqba ibn Mukrim narrated to us: Abu Qutaybah narrated to us from ‘Abd Allah ibn ‘Umar, from Nafi‘, from ibn ‘Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet—peace and blessings be upon him—who said: “When a slave marries without the permission of his master, his marriage is void.” [Da‘if]"
"13738","(13732) It was narrated that ‘Ali ibn Muhammad ibn ‘Abd Allah ibn Bashran reported: Isma‘il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali ibn ‘Affan narrated to us: ‘Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us from ‘Ubayd Allah ibn ‘Umar, from Nafi‘, from ibn ‘Umar, who said: “He used to consider the marriage of a slave without the permission of his master to be fornication, and he would punish whoever married such a person.” [Hasan]"
"13739","(13733) And through a similar chain from ibn ‘Umar, it is narrated that he said: “When a slave marries with the permission of his master, the divorce is in the hand of the slave.” 
It has also been narrated from ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in a similar sense, and from ibn ‘Umar, who said: “In a case where a slave married a free woman without the permission of his master, he said: ‘She has made her private parts lawful.’” [Hasan]"
"13740","(13734) It was narrated that Abu Bakr al-Ardistani reported: Abu Nasr al-‘Iraqi narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us: ‘Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us: ‘Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us from Sufyan, who narrated to us from ‘Abd al-Malik ibn Jurayj, from ‘Ata’, from ibn ‘Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: “There is no harm in a man marrying his slave woman without a dowry.” [Sahih]"
"13741","(13735) It was narrated that Abu Hazim al-‘Abdawi al-Hafiz said: It was narrated that Abu al-Hasan ibn Hamzah al-Harawi said: It was narrated that Ahmad ibn Najdah said: It was narrated that Sa‘id ibn Mansur said: It was narrated that Hushaym said: It was narrated that Husayn narrated from Bakr ibn ‘Abd Allah al-Muzani that ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a woman who had married a slave of hers. The woman said: “Does not Allah, the Exalted, say in His Book: {or what your right hands own}?” So he flogged them both, separated them, and wrote to the governors of the provinces: “Whoever among the women marries a slave of hers, or marries without clear proof, or without a wali, then flog them both.” [Hasan, for others]"
"13742","(13736) It was narrated that Abu Hazim said: It was narrated that Abu al-Hasan said: It was narrated that Ahmad said: It was narrated that Sa‘id said: It was narrated that Hushaym said: It was narrated that Yunus narrated from al-Hasan that ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a woman who had married her slave. He punished her, separated her from her slave, and forbade her from marrying again as a punishment for her. [Two narrators, one confirms the other. Hasan, for others]"
"13743","(13737) It was narrated that Abu Muhammad ‘Abd Allah ibn Yūsuf said: It was narrated that Abu Sa‘id ibn al-A‘rabi said: It was narrated that al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za‘farani said: It was narrated that Sa‘id ibn Sulaiman said: It was narrated that ‘Abbad narrated from ‘Umar ibn ‘Amir from Qatadah from Khilas from ‘Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that a woman inherited a share of her husband’s property, and it was brought to ‘Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: “Did you have sexual relations with her?” He said: “No.” He said: “If you had had sexual relations with her, I would have stoned you. Then he said: “He is your slave; if you wish, sell him; if you wish, give him in marriage; if you wish, free him and marry him.” [Hasan, for others]"
"13744","(13738) It was narrated that Abu ‘Abd Allah al-Hafiz said: It was narrated to me that Abu al-Nadhr Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Yūsuf al-Tusi said: It was narrated that ‘Uthman ibn Sa‘id al-Darimi said: It was narrated that Yahya ibn Yahya said: It was narrated that Hushaym narrated from Salih ibn Salih al-Hamdani who said: I saw a man from the people of Khurāsān ask al-Sha‘bi, who said: “O Abu ‘Amr, the people of Khurāsān say about a man who frees his slave and then marries her, that he is like one who rides his own body.” Al-Sha‘bi said: “Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa narrated to me from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ‘Three people will be given a double reward: A man from the People of the Book who believed in his Prophet and met him, then believed in him and followed him and testified to him – he will have a double reward. And a slave who paid the rights of Allah and the rights of his master – he will have a double reward. And a man who had a female slave, fed her well, educated her well, then freed her and married her – he will have a double reward.’” Then al-Sha‘bi said to the Khurāsānī: “Take this hadith without any addition, for the man is traveling to Medina in any case.” 
Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from other chains of narration through Salih, and narrated by Muslim through Yahya ibn Yahya. [Muslim 154]"
"13745","(13739) It was narrated that Abu Muhammad ‘Abd Allah ibn Yūsuf al-Asbahani said: It was narrated that Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq ibn Ayyub said: It was narrated that Abu al-Muthannā said: It was narrated that Muhammad ibn Kathir said: It was narrated that Sufyan narrated from Salih from al-Sha‘bi from Abu Burdah from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Whoever had a female slave, educated her well, taught her well, then freed her and married her, will have a double reward. And whoever is a slave who paid the rights of Allah, the Exalted, and the rights of his master, will have a double reward.” 
Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through Muhammad ibn Kathir, who said: “Abu Bakr means ibn ‘Ayyash, from Abu Hāsin, from Abu Burdah from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): He freed her, then gave her a mahr.” [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13746","(13740) It was narrated that Abu Bakr ibn Furak said: It was narrated that ‘Abd Allah ibn Ja‘far said: It was narrated that Yunus ibn Habib said: It was narrated that Abu Dawood said: It was narrated that Abu Bakr al-Khayyāt narrated: 
It was narrated that Abu ‘Abd Allah al-Hafiz said: It was narrated that Abu al-‘Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya‘qūb said: It was narrated that al-‘Abbās ibn Muhammad al-Duri said: It was narrated that Ahmad ibn Yunus said: It was narrated that Abu Bakr ibn ‘Ayyash narrated from Abu Hāsin from Abu Burdah from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “When a man frees his slave and then marries her with a new mahr, he will have a double reward.” 
The wording of Ahmad’s narration, and in the narration of Abu Dawood: “When a man frees his slave and then gives her a new mahr, he will have a double reward.” [Munkar]"
"13747","(13741) It was narrated that Abu Muhammad ‘Abd Allah ibn Yūsuf said: It was narrated that Abu Sa‘id ibn al-A‘rabi said: It was narrated that al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za‘farani said: It was narrated that Khalaf ibn Hisham said: It was narrated that Abu ‘Awanah narrated from Qatadah from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) freed Safiyyah and made her emancipation a mahr for her. 
Narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through Qutaybah from Abu ‘Awanah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1365]"
"13748","(13742) It was narrated that Abu ‘Ali al-Rudhbarī said: It was narrated that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahmud al-Askarī said: It was narrated that Ja‘far ibn Muhammad al-Qalānisi said: It was narrated that Adam ibn Abi Iyas said: It was narrated that Shu‘bah said: It was narrated that ‘Abd al-‘Aziz ibn Suhaib said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: “The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) made Safiyyah a captive, then freed her and married her.” 
Thābit al-Bunānī said about Anas: “Did he give her a mahr?” He said: “Her own self – he freed her and married her.” 
Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through Adam. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13749","(13743) It was narrated that Abu Sa‘id ibn Abi ‘Amr said: It was narrated that Abu ‘Abd Allah al-Saffār said: I heard al-Qadi Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Birti say: I asked Yahya ibn Aktham about this hadith, and he said: “This was specific to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him).” 
The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This is also narrated from al-Muzanī (may Allah have mercy on him) that he mentioned this hadith to al-Shāfi‘ī (may Allah have mercy on him), who took it as a special case. The occasion for the special case was that he freed her completely, then married her without a mahr, and the marriage is not without a mahr. Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13750","(13744) It was narrated that Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashrān said: It was narrated that Ismā‘īl al-Saffār said: It was narrated that al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali ibn ‘Affān said: It was narrated that ibn Numayr narrated from ‘Ubaid Allah ibn ‘Umar from Nāfi‘ who said: “Ibn ‘Umar disliked that the emancipation of a woman be considered her mahr until he imposed a sadaqah upon her.” 
The Sheikh said: This is indicated by the hadith of Abū Mūsā narrated by Abū Bakr ibn ‘Ayyāsh. May Allah grant us success. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"13751","It has been narrated through a weak chain of transmission that Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdān reported to us, Ahmad ibn Ubayd reported to us, Hasan ibn Ali al-Sukkari reported to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri reported to us, saying: Ulayla bint al-Kumayt al-‘Atakiyya reported to her mother Umayma, from her handmaiden Ruqayna, from her mother Ruqayna, who said: When the day of Quraizah and Nadir arrived, a slave girl named Safiyya was brought bound, her forearm in the hand of her master until Allah granted victory over them. When the Prophet saw her, she declared: “I bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah.” He then released her forearm from his hand, freed her, gave her in marriage, and paid her dower, which was Ruqayna."
"13752","Abu al-Hasan ibn Abdān reported to us, Ahmad ibn Ubayd reported to us, Ayyash al-Sukkari reported to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj reported to us, Hammad reported from ‘Ammār ibn Abī ‘Ammār, from Ibn ‘Abbās—may Allah be pleased with them—as Hammad believed: The Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—mentioned Khadīja bint Khuwaylid, and her father wished not to give her in marriage. She prepared food and drink, and invited her father and a group from Quraysh. They ate and drank until they became intoxicated. Khadīja—may Allah be pleased with her—then said to her father: Indeed, Muḥammad ibn ‘Abd Allāh is proposing to me; give me in marriage. He gave her in marriage to him. She clothed him and dressed him in a garment. They used to do as the fathers did when they married their daughters. When the intoxication left him, he awoke and saw that he was clothed in a garment. He said: What is the matter with me? She said: You married me, Muḥammad ibn ‘Abd Allāh. He said: I am marrying an orphan of my uncle Abū Ṭālib. She said: No, by my life! Do you feel ashamed to appear intoxicated before Quraysh, to tell people that you were drunk and remained so until you acknowledged it?"
"13753","Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān reported to us, ‘Abd Allāh ibn Ja‘far reported to us, Ya‘qūb ibn Sufyān reported to us, al-Hajjāj ibn Abī Manī‘ reported to us, my grandfather reported from al-Zuhrī: 
(أ) ‘Umar ibn Abī Bakr al-Mu’ammalī reported to me, ‘Abd Allāh ibn Abī ‘Ubayda ibn Muḥammad ibn ‘Ammār ibn Yāsir reported from his father, from Miqsam al-Qāsim—my master ‘Abd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal—who said that ‘Abd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith reported to him: ‘Ammār ibn Yāsir mentioned the story of Khadīja’s marriage—may Allah be pleased with her—and she said: She spoke to her brother, he spoke to her father, who had already drunk wine. He mentioned the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—and his station, and asked him to marry her. The Messenger of Allah married her, Khadīja. Then he slept, and awoke shouting, denying that he had married her. He said: Where is the man among you whom you claim I married? The Prophet—peace and blessings be upon him—then appeared before him. When he looked at him, he said: If I have married her, then that is correct; and if I have not, then I have now married her. 
It is also narrated from al-Zuhrī that the Prophet—peace and blessings be upon him—married Khadīja during the Jahiliyya period, and her father, Khuwaylid ibn Asad, gave her in marriage."
"13754","Abu al-Hasan—‘Alī ibn Ahmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ar-Razzāz of Baghdad—reported to us, Abu Saḥl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān reported to us, Ahmad ibn al-Jabbār reported to us, ‘Abd Allāh ibn Idrīs al-Awdī reported from Muḥammad ibn ‘Amr, from Yaḥyā ibn ‘Abd ar-Raḥmān ibn Ḥāṭib, who said: ‘Ā’isha—may Allah be pleased with her—said: When Khadīja bint Khuwaylid—may Allah be pleased with her—died, Khawla bint Ḥakīm came to the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—and said: “O Messenger of Allah, will you not marry?” He asked: “And whom?” She said: “If you wish, a virgin; if you wish, a previously married woman.” He asked: “And who is the virgin, and who is the previously married woman?” She said: “As for the virgin, it is ‘Ā’isha bint Abī Bakr—may Allah be pleased with her—whom Allah loves most among His creation. As for the previously married woman, it is Sūda bint Zam‘a, who believed in you and followed you.” He said: “Then mention them to me.” She said: “So I went to Umm Rūmān, who said: ‘O Umm Rūmān, what has Allah brought into your house of goodness and blessing?’ She said: ‘And what is that?’ The Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—mentioned ‘Ā’isha. She said: “Wait, for Abū Bakr is coming.” Abū Bakr—may Allah be pleased with him—arrived, and she mentioned that to him. He said: “Is it fitting that she should marry while she is the daughter of my brother?” The Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—said: “I am her brother, and she is my brother’s daughter, and her marriage to me is fitting.” He narrated the hadith until he said: Then Abū Bakr—may Allah be pleased with him—said to her: “Tell the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—to come.” The Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—came and married her. Then Khawla said: I went to Sūda, whose father was an old man who had retired from worldly affairs. I greeted him with the greeting of the Jahiliyya period and said: “Good morning.” He asked: “Who are you?” I said: “Khawla bint Ḥakīm.” He welcomed me and said: “May Allah grant what He wills.” I said: “Muḥammad ibn ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Abd al-Muṭṭalib mentions Sūda bint Zam‘a.” He said: “A noble lineage. What does your mistress say?” I said: “She loves her.” He said: “Go and tell her to send for him.” She said: “So the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—came and married her. When ‘Abd ibn Zam‘a arrived, he began strewing dust on his head, saying: ‘May the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—marry Sūda.’” He narrated the rest of the hadith."
"13755","Muḥammad ibn ‘Abd Allāh al-Hāfiẓ reported to me, Abu al-Naḍr al-Faqīh reported to me, ‘Uthmān ibn Sa‘īd ad-Darimī reported to us, Abu al-Yamān—al-Hakam ibn Nāfi‘—reported to us: Shu‘ayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah reported to him from al-Zuhrī. 
(ح) Abu ‘Abd Allāh al-Hāfiẓ and Abu Sa‘īd ibn Abī ‘Amr reported to us. Abu ‘Abd Allāh said: Abu ‘Abd Allāh reported to me; Abu Sa‘īd said: Ahmad ibn ‘Abd Allāh al-Muzanī reported to us, ‘Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn ‘Īsā reported to us, Abu al-Yamān reported to me: Shu‘ayb reported from al-Zuhrī, who reported to me: Salīm ibn ‘Abd Allāh reported to me that he heard ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Umar narrating: When Ḥafṣa bint ‘Umar was widowed by Khunays ibn Ḥuzāfah al-Saḥmī—who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah—and had witnessed Badr, and died in Medina, ‘Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb—may Allah be pleased with him—said: I met ‘Uthmān ibn ‘Affān and proposed Ḥafṣa to him, saying: “If you wish, I will marry you Ḥafṣa bint ‘Umar.” He said: “I will consider my affair.” I waited for several nights, then he came to me and said: “It has become clear to me that I will not marry today.” ‘Umar said: I met Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, and proposed Ḥafṣa to him, saying: “If you wish, I will marry you Ḥafṣa bint ‘Umar.” Abū Bakr was silent and did not respond to me. I felt more distressed toward Abū Bakr than toward ‘Uthmān. I waited several nights, then the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—sent to me to marry her to him. I married her to him. Then Abū Bakr came to me and said: “Perhaps you found me silent when I proposed Ḥafṣa to you, and I did not respond to you.” He said: “Indeed, nothing prevented me from responding to you in what you proposed except that I knew the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—had mentioned Ḥafṣa. I did not want to disclose the private matter of the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—even if the Messenger had left her, I would have accepted her.” 
This is narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abu al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ: al-Bukhārī 4145, 4005, 5122, 5129]"
"13756","Abu ‘Abd Allāh al-Hāfiẓ reported to me, Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-‘Adl reported to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib reported to us, Ibn Abī ‘Umar reported to us, ‘Abd al-Wahhāb ath-Thaqafī reported from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, from Maqil ibn Yāsir: 
(ح) Abu ‘Amr al-‘Adīb reported to us, Abu Bakr al-Ismā‘īlī reported to us, Ibn Nājiyya and ‘Imrān reported to me: Wahb ibn Baqīyya reported to us, Khālid reported from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan: That Maqil ibn Yāsir married his sister to a man, who then divorced her once. She became divorced from him. Then he came to propose to her again, but he refused him, saying: “Did you not disgrace my honor, then divorce her, and now come to propose to her? By Allah, I will not marry her to you.” And the woman had already inclined toward returning to him. Then Allah, the Exalted, revealed: {And when you divorce women and they have fulfilled their term, either retain them according to acceptable terms or release them according to acceptable terms} [al-Baqara 2:232]—until the end of the verse. 
Maqil said: Yes, I will marry her to you. This is the wording of Khālid’s narration. 
This is narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ‘Abd al-Wahhāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ: al-Bukhārī 4230]"
"13757","Muḥammad ibn ‘Abd Allāh al-Hāfiẓ reported to me, Abu Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-‘Adl reported to us, Muḥammad ibn Ishāq reported to us, Salm ibn Junādah reported to us, Waki‘ reported from Hishām ibn ‘Urwah, from his father, from ‘Ā’isha—may Allah be pleased with her—concerning the verse: {And never will you find, in the track of the Messenger of Allah, a change in the practice of Allah. This is so. And if a female is deprived of her offspring, and she has no husband—you being her nearest kin—and she wishes to marry a believer, then it is permissible for her to marry whom she likes among the believers} [an-Nisā’ 127]. She said: This orphan girl was in the care of a man who was her guardian, perhaps she would be a partner in his wealth, and he preferred that she marry and manage his wealth. But he disliked marrying her because he feared that someone else might share in her wealth. 

This is narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Waki‘, and Muslim narrated it from various chains through Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ: al-Bukhārī in numerous places; Muslim 3018]"
"13758","Abu ‘Abd Allāh al-Hāfiẓ reported to us, Abu al-‘Abbās—Muḥammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbūbī of Marw—reported to us, Sa‘īd ibn Mas‘ūd reported to us, Yāzid ibn Hārūn reported to us, Hammad ibn Salama reported to us: 
(ح) Abu Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh reported to us, Abu Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Asbahānī reported to us, Abu Ya‘la al-Mawsilī reported to us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Hajjāj as-Sāmī reported to us, Hammad ibn Salama reported from Thābit al-Bunānī, who reported to me: Ibn ‘Umar ibn Abī Salama reported from his father, from Umm Salama, who said: The Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—said: “Whoever experiences a trial, let him say: Indeed we belong to Allah, and indeed to Him we return. O Allah, I place my trust in You concerning my trial, so grant me reward in it and replace it with something better.” When Abū Salama died, I began to say it, and every time I mentioned replacing it with something better, I would think within myself: “Who is better than Abū Salama?” Then I continued to say it. When her period ended, the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—sent ‘Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb—may Allah be pleased with him—to propose to her on his behalf. She said to her son: “O ‘Umar, arise and marry the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him.” ‘Umar married him to her. 
The wording of Abu ‘Abd Allāh’s narration is as above, but in the narration of al-Asbahānī, there is no mention of ‘Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb—may Allah be pleased with him—nor of the waiting period (‘iddah). Rather, it says: She said: “The Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him—proposed to me.” I said: “Indeed, none of my guardians are present.” He said: “Indeed, none of them are present or absent except that I am pleased with it.” I said: “O ‘Umar, arise and marry the Messenger of Allah—peace and blessings be upon him.” 
The sheikh—may Allah have mercy on him—said: ‘Umar ibn Abī Salama was also a clan member, because Umm Salama is Hind bint Abī ‘Umayyah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Umar ibn Makhzūm, and ‘Umar is the son of Abū Salama, and Abū Salama’s name was ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Abd al-Asad ibn Ḥilāl ibn ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Umar ibn Makhzūm. [Weak: The text in the authentic hadith is different; see Ar-Rawḍa 1819]"
"13759","Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān reported to us, ‘Abd Allāh ibn Ja‘far reported to us, Ya‘qūb ibn Sufyān reported to us, al-Hajjāj ibn Abī Manī‘ reported to us, my grandfather reported from al-Zuhrī, and he mentioned it. 
I heard Abū Bakr ar-Rādistānī say: I heard Abū Naṣr al-Kalābadhī—may Allah have mercy on him—say: ‘Umar ibn Abī Salama died when the Prophet—peace and blessings be upon him—died, and he was nine years old, and he died during the caliphate of ‘Abd al-Malik ibn Marwān. [Weak: See earlier mention]"
"13760","Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashrān reported to us, Abu Ja‘far ar-Razzāz reported to us, Ahmad ibn al-Khalīl reported to us, al-Wāqidī reported to us, ‘Umar ibn ‘Uthmān al-Makhzūmī reported from Salama ibn ‘Abd Allāh ibn Salama ibn Abī Salama, from his father, from his grandfather: That the Prophet—peace and blessings be upon him—proposed to Umm Salama, saying: “Command your son to marry you.” Or he said: “Your son married her.” He was then a small child who had not yet reached maturity. 
The sheikh—may Allah have mercy on him—said: This occurred when there was no one else available except him in the matter of marriage. [Weak: See earlier mention]"
"13761","It was narrated that Anas said: Abu Talh addressed Umm Sulaym and said: ""Do you not know that your Lord, whom you worship, is a piece of wood that grows from the earth? The Abyssinians of Banu Fulan have made an incision in it. If you accept Islam, I will not claim any dower from you other than that."" He said: ""I will consider it."" Then he went away and came back and said: ""I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."" She said: ""O Anas, marry Abu Talh to me."" 
The Sheikh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Anas ibn Malik is her son and her clan. Indeed, Anas ibn Malik ibn Nadr ibn Dhamdham ibn Zayd ibn Haram from Banu Adi ibn Najjar, and Umm Sulaym is the daughter of Milhan ibn Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Haram from Banu Adi ibn Najjar."
"13762","It was narrated that Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Barirah stipulated nine *waqas* annually for her freedom. She came to Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, seeking her help. Aisha said: ""I will not do anything except that Allah wills, and I will pay the price once and grant her freedom, and the guardianship will be mine."" Barirah went and spoke to her people about it, but they refused except on the condition that the guardianship remain with them. Then Barirah came to Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was present at that time. She told Aisha what her people had said, and Aisha said: ""By Allah, that is only possible if the guardianship is mine."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Buy her and stipulate the guardianship for them, and free her, for the guardianship belongs only to the one who frees her."" Then he stood and addressed the people, praised Allah, and extolled Him, then said: ""What is the matter with a people who stipulate conditions that are not in the Book of Allah? They say: 'Free her, O so-and-so, and let the guardianship be mine.' The Book of Allah is more entitled to be followed, and the condition of Allah is more binding. Every condition that is not in the Book of Allah is void, even if there are a hundred such conditions."" 
Aisha said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) granted Barirah the choice between her husband and herself, and he was a slave, so she chose herself."" Urwah said: ""Even if he had been free, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would not have granted Barirah the choice."" 
This is narrated by Muslim in his Sahih on the authority of Isaac ibn Ibrahim. It contains the indication we intended regarding the guardianship belonging only to the one who frees her, and that there is no guardianship for anyone else. One of the rulings on guardianship is that the marital guardianship is established for the one who has guardianship, even in the absence of a suitable match. Allah knows best. 
There are other hadiths in *Al-I'rab al-Kafi* that do not provide the majority of the evidence, and Allah knows best. (Sahih, Muslim 2168)"
"13763","Among them is a hadith similar to this, which was narrated by Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan, who said: It was narrated by Ahmad ibn Ubaid al-Saffar, who said: It was narrated by Ahmad ibn Ali and Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firaybi, who differed in their narration. They said: It was narrated by Qutaybah ibn Sa'id, who said: It was narrated by Abd Allah ibn Wahb on the authority of Sa'id ibn Abd Allah al-Juhani on the authority of Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib on the authority of his father on the authority of his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to him: ""O Ali, do not delay these three things: the prayer when it comes, the funeral when it arrives, and the marriage when you find a suitable match."" (Da'if)"
"13764","Among them is a hadith narrated by Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz, who said: It was narrated by Ali ibn Isa, who said: It was narrated by Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib, who said: It was narrated by Abd Allah ibn Sa'id al-Kindi, who said: It was narrated by al-Harith ibn Imran al-Ja'fari on the authority of Hisham ibn Urwah on the authority of his father on the authority of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Choose for us so that we may look at you, and marry off the orphaned girls, and marry them to others."" (Da'if, very weak)"
"13765","It was narrated by Abu Abd Allah, who said: It was narrated by Ali ibn Isa, who said: It was narrated by Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib, who said: It was narrated by Ziyad ibn Ayyub, who said: It was narrated by Ikrimah ibn Ibrahim on the authority of Hisham ibn Urwah, and he mentioned it with a chain similar to the previous one. 
It was also narrated by Abu Umayyah ibn Ya'la on the authority of Hisham. (Da'if, very weak)"
"13766","As for the hadith of Mubashir ibn Ubaid on the authority of al-Hajjaj ibn Artath on the authority of Atay and Amr ibn Dinar on the authority of Jaber, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not marry women except to the guardians, and do not marry them except to the orphaned, and do not give a dowry of less than ten dirhams."" This hadith is weak, and it has been narrated by Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih, who said: It was narrated by Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz, who said: It was narrated by Ahmad ibn Isa ibn al-Sukain al-Baladi, who said: It was narrated by Zakariya ibn al-Hakam al-Ras'ani, who said: It was narrated by Abu al-Mughirah: Abd al-Quddus ibn al-Hajjaj, who said: It was narrated by Mubashir ibn Ubaid, who said: It was narrated by al-Hajjaj ibn Artath, who mentioned it. 
Ali, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Mubashir ibn Ubaid is rejected in hadith; his narrations are not transmitted by others. 
The Imam Ahmad, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Some of the remaining narrators of Mubashir ibn Ubaid narrated it on the authority of al-Hajjaj on the authority of Abu al-Zubair on the authority of Jaber, and it is weak, and no proof can be established from it. It was also said of Bqiyyah that it is similar to the first. (Da'if, very weak)"
"13767","It was narrated by Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz, who said: It was narrated by Abu Ali: al-Husayn ibn Ali al-Hafiz, who said: It was narrated by Muhammad ibn Isaac ibn Khuzaymah, who said: It was narrated by Ali ibn Hujr, who said: It was narrated by Bqiyyah, who said: It was narrated by Mubashir, and I am the first to narrate it from his *uhdah* on the authority of al-Hajjaj ibn Artath on the authority of Amr ibn Dinar on the authority of Jaber, may Allah be pleased with him, and on the authority of Atay on the authority of Jaber, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not marry women except to the guardians, and do not marry them except to the orphaned, and do not give a dowry of less than ten dirhams."" 
He said: ""I am the first to narrate it from the *uhdah* of Khuzaymah ibn Khuzaymah."" (Da'if, very weak)"
"13768","It was narrated by Abu Zakariya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki, who said: It was narrated by Abu Abd Allah: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, who said: It was narrated by Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab, who said: It was narrated by Ja'far ibn Awn, who said: It was narrated by Misar on the authority of Sa'd ibn Ibrahim on the authority of Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Talhah, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I will prevent women of high status from marrying except to the orphaned."" 
The Sheikh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, interpreted the meaning of the stipulation of guardianship in marriage so that a woman does not marry without a guardian. He said: ""It has no meaning to say 'the first of them' rather than 'do not marry except to the orphaned.' Rather, I do not think he intended that there is a ruling concerning the woman herself except to prevent her from marrying without a guardian."" 
It was narrated by this by Abu Said ibn Abi Amr, who said: It was narrated by Abu al-Abbas, who said: It was narrated by Rab'i, who said: It was narrated by the Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, who mentioned it. (Da'if, very weak)"
"13769","It was narrated by Abu Ali al-Rudhbar, who said: It was narrated by Abu Bakr ibn Dassah, who said: It was narrated by Abu Dawood, who said: It was narrated by Ahmad ibn Hanbal, who said: It was narrated by Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: It was narrated by Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, who said: It was narrated by Qatadah on the authority of al-Hasan on the authority of Qays ibn Ubad, who said: ""I went with al-Ashtar to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and we said: 'Has the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) given you any promise that he did not give to the people?' He said: 'No, except what is in my Book, and in it is: ""The believers have made their bloods equivalent, and they are a hand against those other than them.""'"" (Da'if)"
"13770","It was narrated by Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Abd Allah: Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Sussi, who said: They said: It was narrated by Abu al-Abbas: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, who said: It was narrated by Rab'i ibn Sulayman and Sa'id ibn Uthman, who said: They said: It was narrated by Bishr ibn Bakr on the authority of al-Awzai, who said: It was narrated by Abu Ammar: Shaddad on the authority of Wathilah ibn al-Asq'aq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Indeed, Allah has chosen Banu Kinanah from Banu Isma'il, and has chosen from Banu Kinanah Quraysh, and has chosen from Quraysh Banu Hashim, and has chosen me from Banu Hashim."" 
Rab'i said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Indeed, Allah has chosen from Quraysh Banu Hashim, and has chosen me from Banu Hashim."" 
Muslim narrated this in his Sahih on the authority of al-Awzai. (Sahih, Muslim 2276)"
"13771","(13765) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, Allah chose the Arabs, and from them He chose Kinānah""—or he said, ""Naḍr ibn Kinānah""—Ḥammād expressed doubt. ""Then He chose from them Quraysh, then He chose from them Banū Hāshim, then He chose me from Banū Hāshim.""

This is a ḥasan mursal [weak].

---"
"13772","(13766) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū al-Jawāb narrated to us; ʿAmmār, meaning ibn Ruzayq, narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq from Aws ibn Ḍamʿaj from Salmān, who said: ""There are two matters in which you have been given preference over us, O company of Arabs: we do not marry your women, and we do not lead you in prayer.""

This is the preserved version, and it is mawqūf [weak].

---"
"13773","(13767) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥāmid al-Balkhī narrated to us; Maʿmar ibn Muḥammad al-Balkhī narrated to us; Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Sharīk ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq from al-Ḥārith from Salmān, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us from leading you in prayer and from marrying your women.""

That has also been narrated through another weak chain from Salmān. [Weak]

---"
"13774","(13768) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us; Zāʾidah narrated to us from Simāk from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim from his father from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she purchased Barīrah from a group of the Anṣār, and they stipulated the right of walāʾ (clientage). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The walāʾ belongs to the one who confers the favour (of manumission)."" She said: ""And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave her the choice, and her husband was a slave.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Zāʾidah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]

---"
"13775","(13769) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Shujāʿ ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; one of our brothers narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Mulaykah from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The Arabs are one another's equals (kufʾ)—tribe to tribe and man to man. And the freedmen (mawālī) are one another's equals—tribe to tribe and man to man—except for a weaver or a cupper (ḥajjām).""

This is munqaṭiʿ between Shujāʿ and Ibn Jurayj, since Shujāʿ did not name one of his companions. And it was narrated by ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from ʿAlī ibn ʿUrwah al-Dimashqī from Ibn Jurayj from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, and it is weak.

It has also been narrated through another chain from Nāfiʿ, and it is likewise weak. [Mawḍūʿ]

---"
"13776","(13770) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Isfarāʾīnī, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Ṣaydalānī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated to us; Zurʿah ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Zubaydī narrated to us from ʿImrān from Abī al-Faḍl from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The Arabs are one another's equals—tribe to tribe and man to man. And the freedmen (mawālī) are one another's equals—tribe to tribe and man to man—except for a weaver or a cupper (ḥajjām).""

That has also been narrated through another chain from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and it is likewise weak. [Mawḍūʿ]

---"
"13777","(13771) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Azdī narrated to us; al-Zuhrī narrated to me from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The Arabs are equals for the Arabs, and the freedmen (mawālī) are equals for the freedmen—except for a weaver or a cupper (ḥajjām)."" [Mawḍūʿ]

---"
"13778","(13772) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī al-Sayyid informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad al-Anjadhānī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Salīm ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn Mīnāʾ from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no contagion (ʿadwā), no omen of evil (hāmah), no ṣafar (superstition about the month of Ṣafar), and flee from the leper as you would flee from a lion""—or he said, ""from a black snake.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: ""And ʿAffān said: Salīm narrated to us, and he mentioned it.""

And it has been narrated to us from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""A sick camel should not be brought near a healthy one."" This will be cited alongside what we use as evidence for annulling marriage on account of the five defects, if Allah wills. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2220]

---"
"13779","(13773) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us; Shuʿbah narrated to us from Yaḥyā from Saʿīd, meaning Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: ʿUmar said: ""When a man marries a woman and she has insanity (junūn), leprosy (judhām), leukoderma (baraṣ), or a horn-like growth (qarn), then if he has consummated the marriage with her, she is entitled to the dower by virtue of his touching her, and it is incumbent upon the guardian (walī).""

---"
